/* Subscriber content. */
Object.assign(window.TPR_X=window.TPR_X||{},{"c-m-01":{"obj":["Told her within the first minute, in plain words, that Jamie has died","Answered what is known about how he died honestly, with no speculation or defence of the team","Explained the coroner's officer, post-mortem and inquest, and named a trust family liaison contact","Given Help is at Hand, Support After Suicide Partnership and Cruse details in writing, and asked about her own safety","Cancelled his appointments, reported the death and booked a same-day team debrief"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"No. He texted me last night. He can't be dead.\"","r":"I'm so sorry. I know this is impossible to take in. He did die, at ten past twelve. Take all the time you need. We're staying right here with you."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"He saw you lot nine days ago. How did nobody see this coming?\"","r":"You have every right to ask that. I don't have the answer today. The trust will review his care, you'll have a named contact, and we'll tell you what we find."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Did he suffer?\"","r":"I don't know for certain, and I won't guess. The coroner's post-mortem will tell us more. I'll make sure the coroner's officer knows you want to ask about that."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have gone round. I knew he was low.\"","r":"It's natural to go over and over what you could have done. You were a loving mum who was in touch with him. His illness hid a lot, even from us."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I have the note? It's mine.\"","r":"Of course you want it. The police are holding it for the coroner because his death was sudden. It will come back to you, and the coroner's officer can tell you when."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I don't want to be here without him.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me. That's a really important thing to say. Can I ask a bit more about those thoughts, so we can make sure you're safe tonight?"}],"bps":{"bio":["Ask her GP (with her consent) to see her within a week; on the day, avoid starting sleeping tablets in A&E","Tell Jamie's GP and community pharmacy today so repeat prescriptions and reminders stop"],"psych":["Help is at Hand booklet, printed, before she leaves","Support After Suicide Partnership (local groups bereaved by suicide) and Cruse Bereavement Support helpline 0808 808 1677","Referral to the local suicide bereavement support service, with her consent, by the care coordinator"],"social":["Coroner's officer phones her, usually within 1 to 2 working days, about the post-mortem and release of Jamie's body","Trust family liaison lead (patient safety team) is her single named contact for the review","Care coordinator cancels all appointments and letters today and phones her within 2 working days","Her sister takes her home; she should not drive today"],"safety":["Ask directly about her own suicidal thoughts; give Samaritans 116 123 and NHS 111 option 2","Plan with her how Jamie's brother will be told so he is not alone when he hears","Incident report on the trust system today; CMHT consultant and GP informed before you leave A&E","Team debrief at 16:30, facilitated by the CMHT psychologist; staff wellbeing service offered"]},"facts":[{"h":"Say 'died' in the first minute","x":"Warning shot, then the word 'died' or 'dead'. Euphemisms prolong hope and confusion. Then stop and wait; silence is part of the conversation."},{"h":"Sudden deaths go to the coroner","x":"Unnatural or unexplained deaths are reported to the coroner; police attend on the coroner's behalf. No death certificate until the coroner decides; a post-mortem is likely."},{"h":"Tubes stay in for the coroner","x":"She can usually see and touch him in A&E with a nurse present, but lines and tubes stay in place until the coroner allows removal. Tell her before she walks in."},{"h":"Ask about her own safety","x":"Close family bereaved by suicide have a raised risk of suicide. Ask kindly before she leaves, and give Samaritans 116 123 in writing."},{"h":"Stop the letters today","x":"Cancel appointments, texts and repeat prescriptions straight away. A clinic letter arriving for a dead son is a common, avoidable harm."},{"h":"Debrief is support, not investigation","x":"Offer a short team debrief the same day for everyone involved, including you, then signpost staff wellbeing support. The formal review is separate."}],"type":"Breaking bad news","do":"Tell his mother clearly that Jamie has died, answer her questions, and give coroner and support details."},"c-m-02":{"obj":["Told him in person, the same day, that he was given his 300 mg night dose of clozapine twice","Given a clear apology on behalf of the ward and the trust, without blaming any individual","Explained what was done, today's monitoring and the warning signs he should report","Confirmed clozapine continues tonight at his usual dose, if his checks are stable","Agreed the written follow-up, the review findings, and whether his sister is told"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"So you poisoned me. That's what you're saying.\"","r":"I can see why it feels like that, and it's frightening to hear. It was a mistake, not something done on purpose, and I'm truly sorry. Let me tell you exactly what happened."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Who was it? I want their name.\"","r":"I understand why you'd want that. I'm not going to name staff today. The review will look at how the checks failed, so it can't happen to anyone again, and you'll hear what it finds."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you stopping my clozapine now? It's the only thing that's worked.\"","r":"I can hear how much it matters to you. We're not stopping it. If your checks are fine today, you'll have your usual dose tonight."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Has it damaged my heart?\"","r":"Your heart tracing and blood tests last night were normal, and your blood pressure has recovered. We don't expect lasting harm, and we'll do another tracing this afternoon to be sure."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not taking anything from that trolley again.\"","r":"That's an understandable reaction after last night. Would it help to watch the nurse check your chart and sign tonight, with me there?"},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I'm going to sue you lot.\"","r":"That's your right, and it won't affect your care here. You'll get everything I've told you in writing. PALS or an advocate can explain your options."}],"bps":{"bio":["Morning clozapine 150 mg held today (consultant and pharmacist); usual 300 mg at 20:00 tonight if obs stable","NEWS2 with lying and standing BP 4-hourly for 24 h; doctor review if pulse over 120, systolic under 90 or new drowsiness","ECG this afternoon (QTc, rate); clozapine plasma level today; FBC as per his routine clozapine monitoring","Stool chart daily and continue his regular laxatives; constipation risk rises with higher clozapine exposure","Sit him upright if drooling with drowsiness (aspiration risk); seizure or temperature means urgent medical review"],"psych":["Consultant review tomorrow to answer new questions and rebuild trust","Offer him the chance to watch tonight's dose being checked and signed"],"social":["With his consent, Jo phones his sister Carol today with the same account","Ward manager sends the candour letter within 10 working days; findings shared when the review is complete","PALS and independent advocacy offered in writing"],"safety":["Incident on Datix graded moderate harm; candour conversation recorded on it","Interim ward change: clozapine signed immediately after each dose; second check of chart before any night dose","Day team handover: warning signs and escalation triggers written in his notes"]},"facts":[{"h":"Moderate harm triggers statutory candour","x":"Death, severe or moderate harm, or prolonged psychological harm: tell in person, apologise, give the facts, explain the review, then write. Transfer for monitoring counts as moderate."},{"h":"Sorry is not liability","x":"The Compensation Act 2006 says an apology is not, by itself, an admission of negligence. Say 'I'm sorry this happened' clearly and early."},{"h":"Missed for over 48 hours: re-titrate","x":"If clozapine has been missed for more than 48 hours, restart at 12.5 mg and re-titrate because of collapse risk. One held dose does not need that."},{"h":"What excess clozapine does","x":"Sedation, low BP on standing, fast pulse, drooling with aspiration risk, seizures (dose-related), delirium, constipation. Watch NEWS2, lying and standing BP, ECG and bowels."},{"h":"Facts, not fault","x":"Share only what is known; answer 'the review will look at that' about why. Do not name, blame or guess. Write down exactly what you said."}],"type":"Breaking bad news + Documentation and reporting","do":"Disclose the double dose to him honestly, apologise, explain actions taken, and agree written follow-up and support."},"c-m-03":{"obj":["Heard her whole complaint without interrupting and summarised all six concerns back to her","Apologised for the specific failures without blaming A&E colleagues","Arranged today: medicines checked, glasses returned, a daily phone update and a consultant meeting","Explained PALS, the formal complaints process with its 3-working-day acknowledgement, and the Ombudsman","Reported the wait and the missed medicines, informed the ward manager and offered a carer's assessment"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Nine hours on a plastic chair! Is that how you treat people here?\"","r":"It's not how anyone should be treated, and I'm sorry it happened to Brian. Please tell me everything, from the start. I want to understand it properly."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I sat at home all night thinking he'd walk out and do something.\"","r":"That sounds terrifying, being at home with no news while he was so unwell. Anyone would be frightened and angry. I'm sorry you were left like that."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Don't give me a leaflet and a pat on the head.\"","r":"I won't. I'll do some things today, and then I'll explain how to make sure your complaint is formally recorded and answered in writing."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why did it take nine hours?\"","r":"Honestly, there were no free beds until someone was discharged this morning. That explains it, but doesn't excuse it, and your complaint helps the trust see the effect."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I complain, will they treat him differently?\"","r":"No. Complaining won't affect his care in any way, and staff are expected to welcome it. I'd rather know when we've got things wrong."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"And what about that nurse who said he was lucky?\"","r":"That wasn't acceptable, and I'm sorry. I'll tell the ward manager today so it's dealt with properly."}],"bps":{"bio":["Check today's drug chart: morning sertraline and amlodipine given; missed evening doses recorded, next doses as normal, not doubled","Full obs with NEWS2 after a night with little sleep and food; meal and fluids offered now"],"psych":["Consultant meets Mr and Mrs Doyle at Wednesday ward round to agree the treatment plan","Named nurse phones Mrs Doyle every evening at 18:00 with an update"],"social":["Ask A&E to send his glasses up with the porter today","Carers' lead arranges a carer's assessment through the local authority (Care Act 2014)","PALS and complaints details given in writing; help offered to write the complaint if she wants it"],"safety":["Incident reports: the 9-hour wait after decision to admit, and the missed evening medicines","Ward manager told today, including the remark on arrival, for feedback to the nurse","His suicide risk reviewed today; she is given the ward number to ring any time with concerns"]},"facts":[{"h":"Complaints: 12 months, 3 days","x":"Complaints can be oral or written, usually within 12 months. The trust must acknowledge within 3 working days, investigate and reply in writing."},{"h":"PALS first, formal any time","x":"PALS resolves concerns informally and quickly. It does not replace the formal route; she can use both."},{"h":"The Ombudsman is the last step","x":"If she is unhappy with the trust's final response, she can ask the Parliamentary and Health Service Ombudsman to review it."},{"h":"Apologise for what happened","x":"'I'm sorry he waited nine hours on a chair' works. 'I'm sorry you feel upset' makes people angrier."},{"h":"Consent: his care, her experience","x":"A complaint about his care needs his consent while he has capacity. Her own experience, such as no updates, she can complain about herself."},{"h":"Carers are entitled to assessment","x":"Under the Care Act 2014 any adult caring for another can ask the local authority for a carer's assessment, whatever the level of care."}],"type":"Handling a complaint","do":"Hear her complaint in full, apologise, fix what you can today, and explain PALS and formal complaints."},"c-m-04":{"obj":["Briefed the interpreter on first-person, word-for-word interpreting, confidentiality and distressing topics","Declined the son as interpreter with a clear, kind reason and seen her without family","Assessed core depressive symptoms, the meaning of her bodily distress, and suicidal thoughts directly","Asked about safety at home while she was alone","Checked by teach-back that she understood the diagnosis, sertraline and talking therapy with an interpreter"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"My son can translate. He's outside. Why do we need a stranger?\"","r":"Your son sounds very caring. A trained interpreter means you can speak freely, and he doesn't have to carry your worries. He can join us at the end if you'd like."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"My heart is squeezed all the time. Like a stone.\"","r":"That sounds very heavy to carry. Tell me more about that feeling, and when it started?"},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I left my mother there. I couldn't even go to her funeral.\"","r":"I'm so sorry. Not being able to say goodbye is one of the hardest things. It makes sense that you're grieving so deeply."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"In my family we don't have this. People will say I'm mad.\"","r":"I understand that worry. Depression isn't madness, and it's very common. What we talk about stays private."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Sometimes I ask God to take me in my sleep.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me; that's important. Have you ever thought of ending your life yourself, or made any plans?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will I have to take tablets forever?\"","r":"No. Usually for at least six months after you feel well, then we reduce slowly together. Please don't stop suddenly."}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline 50 mg once daily in the morning; range 50 to 200 mg; increase in 50 mg steps no sooner than weekly if needed","Warn: nausea, loose stools, headache, more anxiety in the first 1 to 2 weeks; benefit after 2 to 4 weeks; do not stop suddenly","GP bloods: FBC, U&E (sodium), TFTs, B12, folate, ferritin, vitamin D, HbA1c; weight recorded today","Review in 2 weeks, earlier if suicidal thoughts increase"],"psych":["NHS Talking Therapies referral: high-intensity CBT for depression with an Arabic interpreter at every session","Screen for PTSD at review if nightmares or flashbacks persist; trauma-focused CBT or EMDR if confirmed","Bereavement work about her mother's death as part of therapy"],"social":["Interpreter booked now for every future appointment; letters and crisis information in Arabic","With her consent, signpost a local Arabic-speaking women's or refugee community group","Son told only what she agrees to share"],"safety":["Passive death wishes, no plan: crisis line via NHS 111 option 2, asking for an Arabic interpreter","Safety at home asked privately; domestic abuse pathway and IDVA if ever disclosed","GP letter today with the plan, risk summary and the need for an interpreter"]},"facts":[{"h":"Never a child, rarely family","x":"Professional interpreters only, except in a genuine emergency. Children must not interpret: they filter, and it harms them. Record the interpreter's name or ID."},{"h":"Brief before, debrief after","x":"Two minutes before: purpose, first person, everything interpreted, cultural terms explained openly, confidentiality, distressing topics. Afterwards ask how they are."},{"h":"Speak to the patient, not the interpreter","x":"Look at her and say 'you', not 'ask her'. Short sentences, one question, no idioms. Pause for each chunk."},{"h":"Check dialect and connection","x":"Arabic varies widely by region. Confirm the interpreter is comfortable with Syrian Arabic and does not know the family, especially in small communities."},{"h":"Sertraline start and review","x":"50 mg once daily; usual range 50 to 200 mg. Review within 2 weeks for adults (1 week if under 25 or higher risk). Check sodium if she becomes confused or unwell."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"Brief the interpreter, assess her mood and suicide risk speaking directly to her, and confirm understanding by teach-back."},"c-m-05":{"obj":["Explained Mum's illness in simple, concrete words a 10-year-old repeated back correctly","Told Lily clearly, more than once, that Mum's illness is not her fault","Answered her questions about the police, catching it and when Mum comes home honestly","Agreed a phone call and a planned visit in the family room","Arranged a Young Carers referral and school support with Dad's consent and Lily's say"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"It's my fault. I shouted at her and said I hated her.\"","r":"I'm really glad you told me. It is not your fault. Lots of children say that when they're cross. Nothing you said made Mum ill."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is Mum going to die?\"","r":"No. This illness doesn't make people die. Doctors know a lot about it, and most people get better with medicine and rest."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why did the police come? Is she in prison?\"","r":"She's not in trouble, and this is a hospital, not a prison. The police helped get her here safely because she was very poorly."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Can I catch it?\"","r":"You can't catch it, like you can't catch a broken leg. Being near Mum or hugging her is completely safe."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"When is she coming home?\"","r":"I don't know yet, and I don't want to make a promise I can't keep. Usually it's a few weeks. You can talk to her and see her before then."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Will she be weird when I see her?\"","r":"She might talk fast or seem sleepy from her medicine. That's the illness. If it feels too much, you can stop the visit, and that's OK."}],"bps":{"bio":["Mum: antipsychotic at night to help sleep and slow her thoughts (for Lily: 'medicine to help her sleep and slow her thoughts down')","Visiting timed with nurses for when Mum is calmer, not straight after a PRN dose"],"psych":["Repeat the three messages in a few days; children need to hear them more than once","Lily makes a card or drawing to take to Mum; Dad to keep a short daily routine","If Lily has persistent sleep problems, school refusal or distress beyond a few weeks, GP to consider CAMHS"],"social":["Young Carers: refer with Dad's consent; young carer's needs assessment from children's services if needed","Dad tells Lily's class teacher on Monday (only the teacher, as Lily asked)","Carers' lead offers Mr Bennett a carer's assessment through the local authority","Named nurse phones Dad daily at an agreed time"],"safety":["Phone call Sunday with a nurse beside Mum, only if she is settled","First visit in the family room off the ward, Dad present, about 20 to 30 minutes, staff nearby; agreed by the nurse in charge","Think Family section of Mum's record updated: Lily lives with Dad, cared for safely; reviewed at ward round"]},"facts":[{"h":"Three messages, simple words","x":"Mum is ill in her brain and being looked after. It is not your fault. You can still see and talk to her. Repeat them, and ask her to say them back."},{"h":"Name the guilt and correct it","x":"Children often link a parent's illness to something they did. Say 'It is not your fault' plainly, link it to the argument she mentioned, and say it again."},{"h":"Honest about uncertainty","x":"'I don't know yet' is safer than a date. A broken promise about coming home damages trust more than honest uncertainty."},{"h":"Children visit off the ward","x":"The MHA Code of Practice expects hospitals to support children's visits in a family room away from the ward, planned with staff and in the child's interests."},{"h":"Young carers have a right to assessment","x":"Under the Children and Families Act 2014, the local authority must assess a young carer's needs. Ask what she does at home for Mum."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Family meeting","do":"Explain Mum's illness to Lily in child-friendly words, address her guilt, and agree visiting and Young Carers support."},"c-m-06":{"obj":["Heard Daniel's own view first and confirmed his consent to share with his mother","Explored the family's spiritual explanation without dismissing it, and named one shared goal","Agreed that Daniel continues aripiprazole alongside prayer and the service, with akathisia addressed","Checked what the service involves and agreed he keeps drinking fluids and takes every dose","Made a chaplaincy referral and shared three relapse signs, the crisis line and a review date"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Doctors don't believe in God. You think we're backward.\"","r":"I'm sorry if it has felt like that. Your faith is important, and I want to understand it. Can you tell me what you believe is happening to Daniel?"},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"He's not mad. This is a spiritual attack on our family.\"","r":"I can hear how strongly you feel this, and I'm not here to argue with your faith. Whatever the cause, we've both seen him suffer. Can we agree that keeping him well comes first?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"If we pray hard enough, why does he need tablets?\"","r":"That's a fair question. Many families see medicine as one of the ways God helps. Prayer and tablets can go together; the tablets keep the illness quiet while you pray."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"He walks up and down all night. These tablets are poisoning him.\"","r":"That must be hard to watch. Thank you for telling me; that restlessness is a known side effect and we can usually fix it by changing the dose, without stopping altogether."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"The healer is booked. We are going, whatever you say.\"","r":"I respect that you'll go, and I'm not asking you to cancel. I'm asking that he keeps taking his tablet and keeps drinking water during it. Can we agree that?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"The pastor says he must fast three days. No food, no water.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me. Three days without water could make him seriously ill. Could he fast from food but keep drinking? Our chaplain can talk with the pastor if that helps."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Maybe we brought this on him. Maybe we didn't pray enough.\"","r":"That sounds painful to carry. This is not your fault, and nothing you did caused it. You came today because you love him, and that is what will help him most."}],"bps":{"bio":["Aripiprazole: reduce from 15 mg to 10 mg each morning for akathisia (range 10-30 mg); review restlessness in 2 weeks.","Aripiprazole can be taken with or without food, so fasting from food needs no change; fluids and daily dose must continue.","NICE physical monitoring: weight weekly for 6 weeks, then at 12 weeks and 1 year; pulse, BP, HbA1c and lipids at 12 weeks."],"psych":["CBT for psychosis, at least 16 sessions, EIP psychologist.","Family intervention, at least 10 sessions over 3 months to 1 year, with Daniel and both parents, EIP family worker.","Relapse signature written with Daniel and his mother: poor sleep, suspiciousness, withdrawing to his room."],"social":["Trust chaplaincy team meets the family within 2 weeks and, with consent, speaks with their pastor.","Care coordinator offers both parents a Carer's assessment by the local authority.","EIP employment worker (Individual Placement and Support) plans his return to college or work."],"safety":["Crisis plan: EIP duty line in hours; NHS 111, option 2 for mental health, out of hours.","Care coordinator visits within 3 days after the service.","Safeguarding adults concern if restraint, fasting without fluids or travel abroad against his will is planned."]},"facts":[{"h":"Shared belief is not delusion","x":"A belief held by the person's cultural or faith group is not a delusion. If unsure, ask the family and, with consent, a faith leader from their community."},{"h":"Relapse risk if stopped early","x":"After a first episode, stopping an antipsychotic within 1 to 2 years makes relapse much more likely. NICE advises discussing this risk before any planned stop."},{"h":"Aripiprazole: watch for restlessness","x":"Start 10-15 mg once daily, usual 15 mg, maximum 30 mg. Less weight gain and prolactin rise than many. Akathisia is common early and drives stopping; ask about it."},{"h":"Chaplaincy serves every faith","x":"NHS chaplaincy teams include multi-faith and non-religious staff. They can pray with families and talk with their own faith leaders, which often eases conflict over treatment."},{"h":"Adults decide for themselves","x":"Parents cannot consent or refuse for a 22-year-old. If he has capacity, his choice stands; your job is to inform him well and keep the door open."}],"type":"Family meeting","do":"Agree with Daniel and his mother a plan that combines faith practice with continued aripiprazole and a chaplaincy link."},"c-m-07":{"obj":["Checked the new information first-hand before raising it, with times and who said what","Raised the concern privately with the consultant, stating the facts, your worry and a specific request","Stepped up from a question to a clear challenge when first reassured, without accusation","Agreed a joint review before any discharge, interim observations and collateral from his wife and brother","Documented the concern, the discussion and the outcome, and named who you would escalate to"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"He told me he's fine. Are you saying I got it wrong?\"","r":"Not at all. You saw him this morning and he did seem better. Something new came up afterwards that I think changes the picture. Can I tell you what it is?"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"We've got three people waiting for a bed. He goes.\"","r":"I know the bed pressure is real. I'm concerned this is a safety issue, and I'm not comfortable with him going today until one of us has seen him again."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What exactly makes you think he's high risk?\"","r":"Three things: he gave his dog away yesterday, he told staff he'd sorted everything, and he lives alone with painkillers at home. With last week's overdose, that worries me."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Fine. If you want to keep him, it's on you.\"","r":"I don't want to take over, and I'd rather we decide this together. Could we see him jointly at two? I'll record what we both decide."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I just want to get home. I've made my peace with things.\"","r":"I'm glad you feel more settled. When you say you've made your peace, what do you mean? I want to understand so we can plan the next few days properly."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You said I could go. Now you're keeping me here?\"","r":"I understand why that's frustrating, and I'm sorry the plan has changed. We had some new information and want to be sure you're safe. The consultant will see you at two."}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue sertraline 50 mg each morning (started on admission); on discharge, 7 days' supply at a time via weekly pharmacy dispensing.","Ask the GP to stop repeat co-codamol and review analgesia; no stockpile of paracetamol-containing medicines at home."],"psych":["Joint consultant and CT2 reassessment at 14:00: intent, plans, reasons for living, what 'sorted everything' means.","If discharged later: written safety plan with him before he leaves (warning signs, coping, people, crisis numbers, means).","For you: debrief with your educational supervisor this week."],"social":["CT2 or named nurse takes collateral from wife and brother before 13:30.","Brother keeps the dog for now and removes medicines from the flat if discharge goes ahead.","Home treatment team alerted today; daily visits for the first week if discharged."],"safety":["Nurse in charge pauses discharge; intermittent observations every 15 minutes until review.","If he tries to leave before review: nurse Section 5(4) (6 hours) or doctor Section 5(2) (72 hours) while an MHA assessment is arranged.","If still unresolved: escalate the same day to the clinical director, telling the consultant first.","Any discharge: follow-up within 72 hours."]},"facts":[{"h":"Use graded assertiveness","x":"Probe ('Can I check something?'), Alert ('I'm concerned about...'), Challenge ('I'm not comfortable...'), Emergency ('Stop, this is unsafe'). Step up each time you are not heard."},{"h":"The two-challenge rule","x":"If you voice a safety concern clearly twice and it is not addressed, you are entitled to go to the next senior person. Say it plainly both times."},{"h":"Calm can mean a decision","x":"Sudden improvement, giving away pets or possessions and 'sorting affairs' can mean a decision to die has been made. Ask directly what has changed."},{"h":"The first days home are riskiest","x":"Suicide risk is highest in the first weeks after discharge from inpatient care, peaking in the first few days. Follow-up within 72 hours is the national standard."},{"h":"Write it down the same day","x":"Record the facts, who you told, what was agreed and when. Keep it factual and neutral; no comment on the consultant's judgement."},{"h":"Speak Up Guardians are there for you","x":"Every NHS trust in England has a Freedom to Speak Up Guardian. Contact them in confidence if the usual routes have not worked or feel unsafe."}],"type":"Professional and ethical dilemma + Handover and escalation","do":"Raise the discharge concern with the consultant using graded assertiveness, agree a safer plan, and document it."},"c-m-08":{"obj":["Acknowledged his distress before giving one clear, kind no to diazepam with the reason","Found out his street benzodiazepine use, alcohol use, withdrawal symptoms and suicide risk","Offered sertraline with dose and side effects, NHS Talking Therapies and a drug service referral","Stayed calm when he stood up, set a limit on behaviour and kept safety in mind","Gave PALS details without defensiveness and confirmed his care would not change"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You lot never listen. Diazepam is the only thing that works!\"","r":"I can hear how desperate you are, and I am listening. Diazepam works fast, which is why it feels like the only thing. Let me explain why I won't prescribe it, and what I can offer."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I'll put in a complaint. I'll go to the papers.\"","r":"That's your right, and I'll give you the PALS details. It won't change how I treat you. Right now, I'd like us to agree something that helps you."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Just give me a week's worth. Then I'll do your therapy.\"","r":"I understand wanting something to tide you over. With what you're already taking, even a week adds to the dependence. Let's start sertraline today and get the drug service involved this week."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"My heart pounds all day. I can't live like this.\"","r":"That sounds exhausting and frightening, and you've coped alone for months. When you say you can't live like this, have you had thoughts about ending your life?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What's the difference? Blues are the same as diazepam.\"","r":"Good question. Many tablets sold as diazepam are something stronger, and there's no way to know the dose. People have died from them, especially with alcohol. That's why I want the drug service involved."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not taking antidepressants. They're for depressed people.\"","r":"Lots of people think that. Sertraline is one of the main treatments for anxiety too. It takes a few weeks and doesn't cause dependence. Would you be willing to try it?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"If you won't give me any, I'll just buy more.\"","r":"Thank you for being honest; I'd rather know. If you do, don't take more than now, don't mix them with alcohol and don't use alone. The drug service can help you cut down safely."}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline 25 mg each morning for 7 days, then 50 mg (usual 50-200 mg; off-label for GAD, as NICE recommends). Warn: nausea, jitteriness for 1-2 weeks, sexual side effects.","No benzodiazepine prescription; avoid pregabalin (Schedule 3 controlled drug with misuse risk).","Drug service assesses dependence; if indicated, they lead a supervised, gradual diazepam reduction.","AUDIT-C at this visit; alcohol plus benzodiazepines raises overdose risk."],"psych":["NHS Talking Therapies: self-refer online today for CBT for GAD or applied relaxation.","Brief explanation of the anxiety cycle and avoidance; written NHS self-help information."],"social":["Drug and alcohol service key worker: harm reduction and support to reduce.","GP letter today: declined request, plan, recommendation of no benzodiazepine prescribing; copy to patient."],"safety":["Sit nearest the door, alarm on; clinic nurse aware; end the appointment if threats escalate.","Harm reduction: no increase, no alcohol or opioids with them, never use alone; 999 for a fit.","Crisis: NHS 111 option 2; Samaritans 116 123.","Tell consultant today; incident report if aggression; review in 2 weeks."]},"facts":[{"h":"NICE: no benzos for GAD","x":"Do not offer benzodiazepines for generalised anxiety disorder except short term during a crisis. Offer CBT or applied relaxation, or an SSRI: sertraline first (off-label for GAD)."},{"h":"Street blues are not diazepam","x":"Many 'diazepam' tablets bought online or on the street contain stronger benzodiazepines such as bromazolam or etizolam. Strength is unpredictable; deaths are linked to mixing with opioids or alcohol."},{"h":"Dependence within weeks","x":"The BNF limits benzodiazepines to 2-4 weeks for severe anxiety. Longer daily use causes dependence; stopping suddenly can cause rebound anxiety, insomnia and seizures."},{"h":"Validate, boundary, offer","x":"Name the feeling, give one clear no with one reason, then move to what you can do. If pushed, repeat the same calm no; don't add new reasons or argue."},{"h":"A complaint does not change care","x":"Give PALS details readily. GMC guidance says a complaint must not affect the care you provide. Tell your consultant and keep a factual record."},{"h":"SSRIs can worsen anxiety early","x":"Warn that jitteriness can rise in the first 1-2 weeks. Start low (sertraline 25 mg), review at 2 weeks, and increase gradually."}],"type":"Professional and ethical dilemma + Explain a treatment","do":"Decline diazepam with a clear reason, offer two concrete alternatives, and keep the consultation calm and safe."},"c-m-09":{"obj":["Told him the decision early, after a warning shot, in a private room with a nurse present","Allowed silence and named his anger and disappointment before giving information","Explained three routes accurately: Managers' hearing now, next tribunal on renewal, and IMHA support","Asked directly about suicide, harm to others and leaving the ward, and agreed a weekend plan","Documented the conversation and handed over risk to the weekend on-call doctor"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"It's a fix. You lot told them what to say!\"","r":"I can see how angry you are, and how unfair this feels. The tribunal is independent and makes its own decision. I know that doesn't make it easier. Can I tell you what you can do next?"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"There's no point. I'm never getting out of here.\"","r":"It sounds like you feel trapped and hopeless right now. That's a heavy feeling. When you say there's no point, are you having thoughts about ending your life?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"When can I appeal again?\"","r":"You can ask the Hospital Managers to review your section at any time, starting today. You can apply to the tribunal again once your section is renewed. An advocate can help with both."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"My solicitor said I'd win.\"","r":"That must make this even harder to take in. She'll call you on Monday to go through the written reasons and whether there are grounds to challenge the decision."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"If I take the tablets, will you let me go next week?\"","r":"I can't promise a date, and I don't want to give you false hope. I'll ask your consultant to set out clearly what needs to happen for more leave and discharge, and go through it with you on Monday."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not staying in this room. I'm going out.\"","r":"You need some space, and that's fair. I need you to stay on the ward tonight. Could you take some time in the garden with Sam, and we talk again in half an hour?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Last night I thought about tying something round my neck.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me; I know that wasn't easy to say. I want to keep you safe tonight. Did you do anything, or get anything ready? How strong is that thought now?"}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue olanzapine 15 mg at night; ask about sedation and weight gain, common reasons for wanting off the section.","If very distressed tonight, offer oral PRN as charted (for example promethazine 25 mg), with his agreement."],"psych":["Named nurse spends 1:1 time this evening and checks in again before the night shift.","RC reviews him on Monday: explains the tribunal's reasons and sets clear steps to leave and discharge.","Ward psychologist offered to work on hopelessness and recovery goals next week."],"social":["IMHA referral today; visit Monday.","Help him write a request for a Hospital Managers' hearing; MHA office logs it.","Phone call to his mother tonight if he wants; IMHA explains her nearest relative rights."],"safety":["Intermittent observations every 15 minutes overnight, agreed with the nurse in charge.","Ground leave paused this weekend until the nurse in charge and on-call doctor review it with him tomorrow; reasons recorded.","Remove obvious ligature items from his room per ward policy, explaining why.","RC told today; handover to weekend on-call doctor."]},"facts":[{"h":"When he can apply again","x":"Section 3: one tribunal application in the first 6 months, one in the next 6 months, then one a year. Losing this one uses up the chance for the current period."},{"h":"Managers' hearing: any time","x":"He can ask the Hospital Managers to review his detention at any time, in writing or by telling staff. A panel can discharge him, and asking does not use up his tribunal right."},{"h":"Appeal only on a point of law","x":"To challenge the decision itself, his solicitor asks the First-tier Tribunal for permission to appeal to the Upper Tribunal, usually within 28 days of the written reasons. Disagreement alone is not enough."},{"h":"The RC can still discharge him","x":"The tribunal decision does not tie the RC's hands. The RC can grant Section 17 leave or discharge him whenever the criteria are no longer met."},{"h":"The nearest relative has a power","x":"She can order discharge with 72 hours' written notice. The RC can bar it if he would be likely to act dangerously; she can then apply to the tribunal within 28 days."},{"h":"Bad news raises risk","x":"Hopelessness after a lost appeal can raise suicide and absconding risk. Ask directly, review observations and leave, and hand over before the weekend."}],"type":"Breaking bad news + Mental Health Act process","do":"Tell him the tribunal outcome clearly, explain three routes to challenge detention, and assess suicide and absconding risk."},"c-m-10":{"obj":["Gave an SBAR referral that stated possible lithium toxicity in the first sentence","Made one clear request: a GP visit by 4pm with an urgent lithium level and U&E","Acknowledged the GP's workload and offered what your team will do in return","Agreed who withholds lithium, stops ibuprofen, checks results and acts on red flags tonight","Read back the plan, documented it and emailed a summary to the practice"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"She's your patient. Can't your team deal with it?\"","r":"I understand; you're stretched and this landed late. We'll do our part: the CPN stays, we stop the lithium and follow up. But she needs a medical assessment and bloods today, and you're best placed."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"We've no visits left. Send her to A&E.\"","r":"That would be my first choice too. She's refused, and she has capacity for that. A home visit is the only way she'll get bloods today. Could you or your paramedic practitioner go?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How sure are you it's lithium toxicity?\"","r":"I'm not sure, which is why I need the level. But three days of vomiting and diarrhoea, a coarse tremor, unsteadiness and ibuprofen on lithium is a classic picture. I'd rather we ruled it out today."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can it wait till tomorrow morning?\"","r":"I see why you'd want that. My worry is that her level keeps rising overnight while she's still being sick, and at 72 she can become toxic quickly. Could she be the last visit of the day?"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I'm not going to hospital. Last time I was on a trolley for fourteen hours.\"","r":"That sounds awful, and I understand not wanting that again. We'll get a doctor to you at home. If you become confused or can't keep fluids down, hospital may become the safest place."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Should I take my lithium tonight?\"","r":"Good question. Please don't take it tonight. We'll check your blood level first and tell you when to restart. Keep sipping water, and stop the ibuprofen."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Your lot started her on lithium; monitoring is on you.\"","r":"Fair point that it's shared. Our local agreement puts levels with the practice and dosing with us. Today I'm giving you our advice, and we'll pick up the dose decision from here."}],"bps":{"bio":["Withhold lithium 600 mg from tonight until the level is back and psychiatry has reviewed it.","Stop ibuprofen; paracetamol 1 g up to four times a day for hip pain (lower dose if under 50 kg).","GP bloods today: lithium level (write time of last dose), U&E with creatinine and eGFR, calcium; marked urgent.","Lithium 1.5 mmol/L or above, falling eGFR, worsening tremor, ataxia or confusion: hospital admission.","If restarted: consultant sets dose; level one week after restart, 12 hours after the dose."],"psych":["CPN asks about sleep and mood at each contact while lithium is held, to catch early relapse.","Consultant reviews whether lithium is still right and her target range (often 0.4-0.8 mmol/L in older adults)."],"social":["CPN stays until the visit is confirmed and leaves fluids within reach.","Daughter (next of kin) told with Mrs Doyle's consent; checks on her this evening.","Ask her regular pharmacy to note lithium and advise against over-the-counter NSAIDs."],"safety":["Red flags to her, daughter and GP: confusion, slurred speech, unable to stand, vomiting everything, fits: call 999.","Duty GP receives results until 18:30; out of hours flagged after that.","You phone at 09:00 tomorrow; note and email summary to practice today."]},"facts":[{"h":"Toxicity can occur 'in range'","x":"Toxicity is usually above 1.5 mmol/L, but older people and those who are dehydrated or have kidney impairment can be toxic at lower levels. Treat the symptoms, not just the number."},{"h":"NSAIDs push lithium up","x":"Ibuprofen and other NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, angiotensin receptor blockers and thiazide diuretics raise lithium levels. Over-the-counter ibuprofen is a common trigger; ask what they buy."},{"h":"Know the toxic signs","x":"Coarse tremor, vomiting, diarrhoea, unsteadiness, slurred speech, confusion. Severe: seizures, reduced consciousness, arrhythmias. Vomiting and diarrhoea also dehydrate, raising the level further."},{"h":"Timing of the level","x":"Routine levels are taken 12 hours after the last dose. When toxicity is suspected, take it straight away and write the time of the last dose on the form."},{"h":"SBAR in one breath","x":"Situation: who and your worry. Background: drug, dose, last level. Assessment: findings and what you think. Recommendation: one specific request with a time. Then read back who does what."},{"h":"Shared care sets who monitors","x":"In many areas the GP does lithium levels and kidney and thyroid tests under a shared care agreement, and psychiatry advises on dose. Know your local agreement before you ring."}],"type":"Handover and escalation","do":"Use SBAR to secure a same-day GP visit and lithium level, agreeing owners, red flags and documentation."},"c-s-02":{"obj":["Confirmed the daughter's consent and explained what can and cannot be shared","Established onset, trigger, course and the full depressive picture, including sleep, appetite, weight, slowing and negative thoughts","Distinguished depression from an eating disorder and screened for psychotic, manic and substance causes","Assessed risk: death wishes, plans, access to means, self-neglect, risk to others and the father's suicide","Agreed urgent next steps, safety advice and crisis numbers, and offered carer support"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I'm so sorry she didn't come. I feel like I've wasted your time.\"","r":"\"You haven't at all. You know her better than anyone, and what you tell me today will help us help her faster. Thank you for coming.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will you tell Ella what I've said?\"","r":"\"We'll be open with her that we met you and talked about how worried you are. If there's something you'd rather we didn't pass on, tell me and we'll think about it together.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is she anorexic? She's so thin now.\"","r":"\"I can see why you'd worry. From what you say she isn't trying to lose weight; she's lost her appetite. That fits depression more than an eating disorder, but we'll weigh her and check properly.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Her dad was depressed too. He took his own life. What if it's happening again?\"","r":"\"I'm so sorry. That's a terrible loss, and of course this frightens you. It's exactly why we'll act quickly. You did the right thing coming today.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Should I make her start the tablets the GP gave her?\"","r":"\"Please don't force them. It's her decision, and we'll talk it through with her when we see her this week. What would help most now is keeping all medicines locked away.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why can't you just admit her and fix this?\"","r":"\"I understand wanting her somewhere safe. The first step is to see her ourselves, at home, within two days. If we're worried about her safety, there are options, including the home treatment team or a hospital bed.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Team to weigh her and check BMI at the home visit; GP bloods (FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, glucose) were normal 3 weeks ago","Medication discussion at assessment: sertraline 50 mg once daily is a reasonable first choice if she agrees, with review at 2 weeks"],"psych":["Direct assessment within 48 hours by two team members, including mental state and risk","If confirmed: CBT or behavioural activation alongside medication, per NICE NG222"],"social":["Mother offered a carer's assessment (local authority) and local carers' support group details","Ella's university student support and GP to be informed once she consents"],"safety":["Mother to lock away paracetamol and the unstarted sertraline today","Crisis line, NHS 111 mental health option and 999 given; duty worker phones Ella today"]},"facts":[{"h":"Consent first, then talk freely","x":"With the patient's consent, you can discuss her care with family. Without it you can still listen. Document what was agreed and its limits."},{"h":"Appetite loss is not anorexia","x":"In depression food loses its taste and appeal. In anorexia nervosa intake is restricted on purpose, driven by fear of weight gain and a distorted view of the body."},{"h":"Ask about slowing directly","x":"Relatives notice slow speech, long pauses and slow movement but rarely volunteer it. It marks a more severe, biological depression."},{"h":"Family suicide raises the risk","x":"A parent's suicide is a strong risk factor. With hopelessness and a death wish, it moves the plan from routine to urgent."},{"h":"Carers have their own rights","x":"Under the Care Act 2014, anyone caring for an adult can ask the council for a carer's assessment of their own needs."}],"type":"Collateral history","do":"Take a structured collateral history of depression from the mother, rule out an eating disorder, assess risk, and plan."},"c-s-03":{"obj":["Identified her main concern and the timeline linking the restlessness to the haloperidol increase","Elicited inner and visible restlessness and excluded parkinsonism, dystonia, restless legs, anxiety, relapse and substances","Asked directly about suicidal thoughts and linked them to the akathisia","Explained akathisia in plain words as a real, treatable side effect","Agreed a plan she accepts (lower dose plus propranolol, switch if needed) with monitoring and safety-netting"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Everyone just keeps telling me to take it. Nobody listens.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry it's felt like that. I'm not here to push the tablet. I want to hear exactly what it's doing to you, because it sounds awful.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Am I going mad again? Is the illness coming back?\"","r":"\"From what you're telling me, no. The voices have gone and your thinking is clear. This sounds like a side effect of the tablet, called akathisia, and it can be treated.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"If this never stops, I'd rather be dead.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me that; it shows how unbearable it is. Do you have any plans to act on it? This is exactly why we'll change things today.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not taking any more haloperidol. Full stop.\"","r":"\"That's your right, and I understand why. Can we look at options together? A lower dose plus a tablet for the restlessness often works, or we can switch to something else.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Propranolol? Isn't that a heart tablet?\"","r":"\"It is, and at a low dose it calms akathisia well. I'll check your pulse and blood pressure first, and the nurses will check them before doses.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why didn't anyone tell me this could happen?\"","r":"\"That's a fair point, and I'm sorry. It's a common side effect, especially when a dose goes up. Let me give you a leaflet so you know what to look out for in future.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Haloperidol: reduce from 10 mg to 5 mg once daily today, with her consent; watch for return of voices","Propranolol 10 mg three times daily (off-label); increase to 20 mg three times daily after 3 to 5 days if needed; avoid in asthma, heart block, bradycardia or low BP","Check pulse and BP before starting and before each dose for the first days; hold if HR under 60 or systolic under 100","If no improvement in about a week: switch to an antipsychotic with lower akathisia risk (for example olanzapine), with weight and bloods monitored; mirtazapine 15 mg at night is an alternative add-on"],"psych":["Barnes Akathisia Rating Scale today and at each review","Give a written leaflet on akathisia so she can recognise and report it early"],"social":["Named nurse to check in each shift about restlessness and sleep","Let her stand or walk in ward round and the medication queue while symptoms persist"],"safety":["Tell the nurse in charge about her suicidal thoughts; review observation level today","Discuss the plan with the consultant the same day; review in 3 days or sooner if worse"]},"facts":[{"h":"Inside and outside","x":"Akathisia has two parts: a felt inner tension with an urge to move, and visible restlessness such as rocking, shifting feet and pacing. Ask about one and watch for the other."},{"h":"Dose rises trigger it","x":"It usually starts within days to weeks of starting an antipsychotic or raising the dose. High-potency drugs like haloperidol carry the highest risk."},{"h":"Anticholinergics rarely help","x":"Procyclidine treats parkinsonism and dystonia, not akathisia. First steps: lower the dose, switch, or add propranolol."},{"h":"Propranolol checks","x":"Before starting, ask about asthma and check pulse and blood pressure. Low doses work; many need only 10 to 20 mg two or three times daily."},{"h":"Akathisia raises risk","x":"It is linked with suicidal thoughts, impulsive self-harm and aggression. Always ask, and link what you find to the restlessness."},{"h":"Restless legs is different","x":"Restless legs syndrome comes on in the evening at rest, mainly in the legs, and is eased by moving. Akathisia is daytime, whole-body and drug-linked."}],"type":"Medication review","do":"Recognise haloperidol-induced akathisia, check suicidal thoughts, explain it, and agree a treatment change she accepts."},"c-s-04":{"obj":["Explored his understanding and beliefs about OCD, including that it is 'just who he is'","Explained obsessions, compulsions and the cycle that keeps them going, in plain words","Explained what his severity score means and the outlook, with honest hope","Explained CBT with ERP and an SSRI: how each works, time course, side effects and the addiction worry","Recommended combined treatment, addressed his ideas about washing properly and antibiotics, and agreed next steps"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is this just who I am now? I was an anxious kid.\"","r":"\"I hear that worry a lot. Being an anxious child can make OCD more likely, but OCD isn't your personality. It's a condition, and it responds well to treatment.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"If I could just wash properly once, really properly, would the thoughts stop?\"","r":"\"I can see why that feels like the answer. But every wash teaches your brain the danger was real, so the thought returns stronger. Treatment works the opposite way.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Could I just take antibiotics, in case?\"","r":"\"I understand the pull of 'just in case'. That's the OCD asking for certainty. Antibiotics wouldn't help, could cause side effects, and would feed the cycle.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want an antidepressant. I'm not depressed, and they're addictive.\"","r":"\"Fair questions. SSRIs treat OCD directly, depressed or not. They're not addictive: no craving, no needing more. Stopping suddenly can cause unpleasant symptoms, so we'd always reduce slowly.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I'm not lying on a couch talking about my childhood. My childhood was fine.\"","r":"\"Good news: ERP isn't like that. It's practical and focused on now. You and the therapist plan small steps to face the fear without washing, and practise between sessions.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will I get back to my unit?\"","r":"\"Many people return to full duties once the OCD is under control. With your consent, I can write to your medical officer so they can plan a phased return with you.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline 50 mg each morning with food; increase by 50 mg steps no sooner than weekly, up to 200 mg if needed","Allow up to 12 weeks at a good dose before judging response; continue at least 12 months after remission, then taper","Warn: nausea, loose stools, headache and a short-lived rise in anxiety early on; sexual side effects; report any suicidal thoughts"],"psych":["CBT with ERP: weekly sessions of about an hour with a trained therapist, more than 10 therapist hours, plus daily homework","Build a written exposure ladder with the therapist, starting with moderately difficult steps"],"social":["With consent, letter to his unit medical officer: diagnosis, plan, and phased return to duty","Invite his partner to a session to learn not to give reassurance or join in the rituals"],"safety":["Review at 2 weeks after starting sertraline, then every 4 to 6 weeks","If mood drops or suicidal thoughts appear, contact the clinic, NHS 111 option 2 or 999"]},"facts":[{"h":"Y-BOCS bands","x":"0 to 7 subclinical, 8 to 15 mild, 16 to 23 moderate, 24 to 31 severe, 32 to 40 extreme. Track it to show change over time."},{"h":"Severe OCD: combine","x":"NICE advises combined SSRI and CBT including ERP for adults with severe functional impairment. Either alone is weaker for severe illness."},{"h":"OCD doses run higher","x":"SSRIs often need higher doses and longer for OCD than for depression: allow up to 12 weeks at a good dose before calling it a failure."},{"h":"Not addictive, but taper","x":"SSRIs cause no craving or tolerance. Stopping suddenly can bring dizziness, irritability and flu-like symptoms, so always reduce gradually."},{"h":"Reassurance is a compulsion","x":"Answering 'Am I clean?' or prescribing 'just in case' gives brief relief and feeds the cycle. Name it kindly and do not join in."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Explain a treatment","do":"Explain OCD and its cycle, then CBT with ERP, SSRIs and combined treatment, and agree his next step."},"c-s-05":{"obj":["Described a typical attack: onset, peak, duration, frequency, physical symptoms and catastrophic thoughts","Established that the attacks are unexpected and assessed worry between attacks and avoidance, including agoraphobia","Excluded generalised anxiety, social anxiety, OCD, PTSD and health anxiety, and screened mood, suicidal thoughts and psychosis","Excluded physical, medicine and substance causes, including caffeine and alcohol","Named panic disorder in plain words, linked it to the body's alarm system, and outlined the next step"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I'm worried you'll just say it's in my head.\"","r":"\"These attacks are real, and they're clearly awful. Your body is genuinely going through all of that. I want to understand exactly what happens so we can treat it properly.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"The tests must have missed something. It feels like a heart attack.\"","r":"\"It really does feel like one. Your ECG, 24-hour monitor and bloods all came back normal, which is reassuring. A racing heart, chest tightness and dizziness are also exactly what the body's alarm does.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"At the worst point the world isn't real. Am I going mad?\"","r":"\"That's a frightening feeling, and very common at the peak of a panic attack. It's the brain's response to extreme fear. It isn't madness, and it passes as the attack settles.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I turned down a promotion because of these.\"","r":"\"That's a big loss, and I'm sorry the attacks have cost you that. It tells me how much they're affecting your life, which matters for the plan.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can't you just give me something to take when it starts?\"","r":"\"I understand wanting something quick. Tablets like diazepam make panic worse in the long run and can be habit-forming. The treatment that works best is a short course of CBT for panic.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it the coffee?\"","r":"\"It may well be adding fuel. Four coffees and an energy drink is a lot of caffeine, and caffeine can trigger the same sensations. Cutting down gradually is a good first step.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Reduce caffeine gradually to 1 to 2 cups a day; stop energy drinks","No further heart tests needed; no benzodiazepines (NICE)","If medication is wanted later: an SSRI licensed for panic disorder, started low because of early jitteriness"],"psych":["CBT for panic via NHS Talking Therapies: weekly sessions, 7 to 14 hours in total, completed within about 4 months","Meanwhile: NHS self-help guide on panic, slow breathing, and staying in the situation until the attack passes"],"social":["Return to the gym gradually once therapy starts; avoidance of a raised heart rate keeps the fear going"],"safety":["Seek urgent help if chest pain is different, persists, or comes with collapse; otherwise ride the attack out","Review in 8 weeks; contact the GP sooner if mood drops"]},"facts":[{"h":"Panic disorder needs two things","x":"Recurrent unexpected panic attacks, plus ongoing worry about more attacks or changes in behaviour to avoid them."},{"h":"Tingling means overbreathing","x":"Rapid breathing lowers carbon dioxide, causing tingling lips and fingers, dizziness and chest tightness. Explaining this defuses the fear of a heart attack."},{"h":"Unreal feelings are not psychosis","x":"Derealisation or depersonalisation at the peak of an attack is common and passes. Psychosis means fixed false beliefs or hearing voices outside panic."},{"h":"Screen agoraphobia every time","x":"Ask about crowds, queues, public transport, open spaces and being out alone. Agoraphobia changes the treatment plan and the outlook."},{"h":"No benzodiazepines for panic","x":"NICE advises against them: they impair recovery and risk dependence. CBT is first line; an SSRI is the drug option."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Characterise the attacks, exclude physical, substance and other anxiety causes, screen agoraphobia and mood, and name panic disorder."},"c-s-06":{"obj":["Explained why lithium is added to amitriptyline in recurrent depression, and that it does not mean bipolar disorder","Listed the baseline tests and the level schedule: 12 hours after a dose, weekly until steady, then every 3 months","Named early and long-term side effects, four toxicity signs, and the interactions she must avoid","Addressed her father's kidney failure and her pregnancy plans, including contraception and breastfeeding","Confirmed understanding by teach-back and agreed her decision and next steps"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Isn't lithium for manic depression? Do you think I'm bipolar now?\"","r":"That's a really common worry, and I'm glad you asked. Your diagnosis hasn't changed. Lithium is also used to boost an antidepressant when depression keeps coming back."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"My dad died of kidney failure. I watched him on dialysis. I can't go through that.\"","r":"I'm so sorry; that must have been very hard to watch. Let's check your kidneys before anything starts, and keep checking. If they show any change, we review straight away."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"That's a lot of blood tests. I hate needles.\"","r":"You're right, it is a lot at first. It settles down to every three months. The tests are what keep lithium safe, so I'd rather be honest about them now."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I read online it can poison you.\"","r":"You're right that too much is dangerous, which is why we check levels. I'll show you the warning signs, so you'll know exactly what to look for and what to do."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"I want a baby in a couple of years. Will this stop me?\"","r":"Thank you for telling me; it really matters. It won't stop you, but we'd plan it together first. Until then, reliable contraception is important. Can I explain why?"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Nothing has worked. Maybe I'm just someone who'll always be like this.\"","r":"It sounds exhausting to keep trying things. Many people whose depression only partly lifts do improve when lithium is added. I'm hopeful for you."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want to decide today.\"","r":"That's completely fine. Take the booklet, talk to your partner, and we'll do the baseline tests so the option is ready. We can talk again on Thursday."}],"bps":{"bio":["Baseline: U&Es with eGFR, TFTs, calcium, FBC, weight and BMI, ECG, urine pregnancy test.","Priadel 400 mg at night; level 12 hours after dose on day 7, then weekly after each change until two levels are stable.","Adjust in 200 mg steps; target 0.4 to 1.0 mmol/L (aim 0.6 to 0.8 first).","Then levels every 3 months for the first year; U&Es, eGFR, TFTs, calcium and weight every 6 months.","Continue amitriptyline 150 mg at night; stop ibuprofen; paracetamol for pain."],"psych":["Ward psychology: offer CBT for depression alongside medication.","Named nurse goes through the lithium booklet and toxicity signs again before her first dose."],"social":["With consent, invite her partner to hear the toxicity and sick-day advice.","Ward social worker: fit note and liaison with her school about a phased return."],"safety":["Ward pharmacist checks interactions and gives counselling before discharge.","On discharge, weekly supplies of lithium and amitriptyline while risk is reviewed.","Sick-day rule: with vomiting or diarrhoea, stop lithium and get a level the same day."]},"facts":[{"h":"Level 12 hours after the dose","x":"Take the night dose about 10 pm and the blood test at 9 to 10 am. A level taken too soon after a dose reads falsely high."},{"h":"Four signs of toxicity","x":"Vomiting or diarrhoea, coarse tremor, unsteadiness or slurred speech, drowsiness or confusion. Stop lithium and get a same-day level; severe signs go to the emergency department."},{"h":"What pushes levels up","x":"NSAIDs such as ibuprofen, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, thiazide diuretics, dehydration, hot weather, a low-salt diet and vomiting or diarrhoea illness."},{"h":"Stop slowly","x":"Unless toxic, reduce lithium gradually over at least four weeks. Stopping suddenly raises the risk of relapse."},{"h":"Pregnancy and breastfeeding","x":"Lithium carries a small extra risk of heart defects in the baby. Plan pregnancy with the team. Levels are checked every 4 weeks, then weekly from 36 weeks. Breastfeeding is not advised."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explain lithium augmentation, tests, levels, toxicity signs and pregnancy issues, then agree Rebecca's decision."},"c-s-07":{"obj":["Heard Joanne's fears about ECT and explained why it is recommended now, including the risk of not eating or drinking","Described the procedure, the twice-weekly course of usually 6 to 12 treatments, and the likely benefit","Gave an honest account of side effects, including memory, and how they are reduced","Explained the alternatives, the SOAD safeguard and her role, and asked about Peter's past wishes","Agreed time to reflect, a visit to the ECT suite, written information and a follow-up meeting"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Isn't that what they did in asylums? Strapping people down and shocking them?\"","r":"That picture frightens a lot of people. Modern ECT is very different: he'd be fully asleep, with a muscle relaxant, so there's no pain and his body barely moves."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"If I let you do this, he'll never forgive me.\"","r":"That's a heavy thing to carry. You aren't deciding this or doing it to him. What you can do is tell us what he'd want, and that really helps."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You've tried everything else and nothing worked. Why would this?\"","r":"It's fair to be sceptical after five months. ECT works differently from tablets, and for depression this severe, nothing we have works better. Most people in his state improve."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will he still know who I am? Will he lose his memories?\"","r":"He won't forget who you are. Memory for the weeks around treatment is often patchy, and most improves. Some people report lasting gaps, so we check his memory and adjust the treatment."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can't you just wait for the tablets to work?\"","r":"I wish we could. He's barely drinking and his kidneys are starting to struggle. Another medicine could take weeks, and he may not have weeks."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I didn't even know he'd been this low. Mum never said.\"","r":"That must be such a shock. Depression often hides, especially in men of his generation. You're here now, and that matters to him."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it his childhood? Is that why he's like this?\"","r":"Hard early experiences can make depression more likely, alongside genes and stress. Right now, though, the priority is getting him well. Talking therapy can explore the rest later."}],"bps":{"bio":["ECT twice weekly (Monday and Thursday), usually 6 to 12 treatments; start right unilateral unless speed is critical, then bilateral.","Nil by mouth from midnight; anaesthetic review, ECG, U&Es, FBC before the first treatment.","Daily fluid balance and U&Es while intake is poor; VTE prophylaxis per medical review.","Cognitive checks (orientation and a brief memory test) at baseline and after every 2 to 3 treatments.","After response: continue antidepressant and lithium to prevent relapse."],"psych":["Once he can engage: CBT for depression with ward psychology.","Named nurse spends time with him daily, even while he cannot speak."],"social":["Ward social worker offers Joanne a Carer's assessment.","ECT nurse lead shows Joanne the suite and answers questions before the first treatment."],"safety":["Request SOAD under Section 58A; if intake worsens, consultant considers Section 62.","Check for any advance decision refusing ECT or a health and welfare LPA before treatment.","Medical review if he stops drinking altogether or creatinine keeps rising."]},"facts":[{"h":"A course, not a single treatment","x":"Usually twice a week, often 6 to 12 treatments, reviewed after each. Many people start improving within two to three weeks."},{"h":"The day itself","x":"Nothing to eat or drink from midnight. Anaesthetic and muscle relaxant through a cannula, oxygen, a mouth guard, a seizure lasting under a minute, then waking in recovery within minutes."},{"h":"Memory: be honest","x":"Confusion for an hour or so is common. Gaps for events around the course are common and mostly improve. Some people report lasting gaps for past events. Unilateral placement reduces this."},{"h":"Who can stop ECT","x":"For a detained patient lacking capacity, a valid advance decision refusing ECT, a health and welfare attorney or the Court of Protection can block it; a relative alone cannot."},{"h":"Section 62 is narrow","x":"Urgent ECT without a certificate is lawful only to save life or prevent serious deterioration, not merely to relieve suffering."}],"type":"Explain a treatment + Family meeting","do":"Explain ECT to Joanne: reasons, procedure, memory effects, alternatives and safeguards, then agree her next steps."},"c-s-08":{"obj":["Confirmed his current mood, the absence of suicidal thoughts, and what helped or troubled him in past treatments","Outlined the options: medication change or augmentation, ECT, rTMS and CBT","Explained how rTMS works, its effectiveness, the course, screening checks and side effects","Answered his questions on medication, driving and top-up sessions","Gave a reasoned recommendation, checked understanding and agreed a referral and review"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"I read it's like ECT but without the anaesthetic. Is that right?\"","r":"That's a good way to put it. Both stimulate the brain, but rTMS uses magnetic pulses while you're awake, and it doesn't cause a seizure. It's gentler, but also not quite as strong."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I'm sick of trying things. Every tablet's been a let-down.\"","r":"That sounds exhausting, and I can hear how fed up you feel. That's exactly why we're looking at something different today."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why didn't anyone offer this before?\"","r":"That's a fair question. It hasn't been widely available here until recently. It's on the table now, and I think it's worth considering for you."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will it mess with my memory like ECT did?\"","r":"No, rTMS doesn't affect memory. The common side effects are headache and some scalp discomfort during sessions, and they usually ease after the first week."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Could it give me a fit?\"","r":"It's very rare. That's why we check for things like past seizures, metal in the head and some medicines beforehand, and the team is trained to manage it."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"If it works, will I need it forever?\"","r":"Most people don't. If your mood dips later, top-up sessions are possible. We'd also keep your tablet going to help protect against relapse."}],"bps":{"bio":["rTMS: 5 days a week for 4 to 6 weeks (20 to 30 sessions); first session measures motor threshold.","Continue trazodone 300 mg at night unchanged; report any medicine changes to the rTMS team.","Fallback: lithium augmentation (start 400 mg at night, level at 1 week) or aripiprazole 2 to 5 mg each morning.","Paracetamol before sessions for headache if needed."],"psych":["CBT for depression, 16 to 20 sessions, NHS Talking Therapies or CMHT psychologist.","Behavioural activation: one planned activity daily while waiting."],"social":["Plan work hours around daily sessions; fit note for reduced hours if needed.","With his consent, include his wife in the plan and warning signs."],"safety":["Re-check suicidal thoughts at each review; crisis line and NHS 111 option 2 given.","Review in clinic at 4 weeks; rTMS team to rate mood weekly."]},"facts":[{"h":"Awake, seated, no anaesthetic","x":"A coil on the scalp delivers rapid magnetic pulses to the left front of the brain. Sessions last a few minutes to about 40, depending on the protocol."},{"h":"How well it works","x":"About 4 in 10 respond and about 3 in 10 remit; less effective than ECT. Benefit builds over 2 to 4 weeks."},{"h":"Screen before starting","x":"Metal in the head (not dental fillings), cochlear implants, pacemakers, a seizure history, recent head injury, heavy alcohol use and seizure-lowering medicines."},{"h":"Side effects","x":"Headache, scalp discomfort, facial twitching and tiredness are common and mild. Seizure is very rare. No memory effects. Earplugs protect against the clicking."},{"h":"Other uses","x":"Also used for OCD and, as single-pulse devices, for migraine; research continues in other conditions."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explain rTMS to Graham, compare it with ECT and medicines, and agree a referral plan."},"c-s-09":{"obj":["Let Callum describe his experiences in his own words before asking specific questions","Asked precisely about thought echo, third-person voices discussing him and running commentary","Distinguished thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcast from his own unwanted thoughts or ordinary forgetting","Asked about made feelings, impulses and acts, somatic passivity and delusional perception","Summarised the symptoms found and absent, checked accuracy and explained the next steps"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You think I'm mad, don't you? Everyone does.\"","r":"I don't think that. I can see these experiences are real and frightening for you. I want to understand them properly, so we can help."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why are you asking such strange questions?\"","r":"Fair question. Some of them sound unusual, but I ask everyone, because people often have experiences they've never been asked about. Your answers help us understand what's happening."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"The network puts thoughts in. They're not mine.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me. That sounds unsettling. Can you give me an example of a thought like that, and how you know it isn't yours?"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I tell you everything, will you lock me up?\"","r":"I can hear you're worried. We see many people with experiences like these, and most are helped at home. Telling me helps us find the right support."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't even think straight any more. It's exhausting.\"","r":"That sounds exhausting. Let's take it slowly; we can pause whenever you need."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"It's not an illness. It's real. They're doing it to me.\"","r":"I can see it feels completely real to you. I'm not here to argue. I'd like to understand it fully, then we can think together about what might help."}],"bps":{"bio":["Baseline physical screen before any antipsychotic: weight, BMI, BP, pulse, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin, ECG.","Urine drug screen with consent."],"psych":["EIP offers CBT for psychosis (at least 16 sessions) once assessment is complete.","Family intervention offered to Callum and his mother (at least 10 sessions)."],"social":["Care coordinator completes risk, substance use and social history today.","With consent, his mother is updated and offered a Carer's assessment."],"safety":["Crisis plan: EIP duty number and NHS 111 option 2 out of hours.","EIP consultant review within the week."]},"facts":[{"h":"Third person is the key","x":"Commentary and discussion are first-rank only in the third person ('he's going out now'). Voices saying 'you' are psychotic but not first-rank."},{"h":"Insertion vs obsession","x":"In insertion the thought belongs to someone else. In OCD the thought is unwanted but recognised as one's own. Ask: 'Whose thought is it?'"},{"h":"Delusional perception: two steps","x":"A real, ordinary perception (a red van) followed by a sudden, unrelated conviction ('I've been chosen'). The meaning arrives at once and fully formed."},{"h":"Passivity has three forms","x":"Made feelings, made impulses, made acts: the experience comes from outside. Somatic passivity is a bodily sensation imposed by an outside agency."},{"h":"Persuasive, not diagnostic","x":"First-rank symptoms also occur in mania, delirium and drug-induced psychosis. ICD-11 no longer gives them special weight; duration and the whole picture matter."}],"type":"Mental state examination","do":"Elicit and record each first-rank symptom with precise questions, then summarise findings back to Callum."},"c-s-10":{"obj":["Assured confidentiality and heard what Hannah has read and fears most","Explained that vulnerability, not the illness, is inherited, with risk in numbers both ways (1 in 100 vs about 13 in 100)","Stated that no test can predict schizophrenia and explained other causes, including ones she can influence","Supported her decision about the pregnancy without steering it, and gave counselling routes","Checked her mental state, medication and safety, and agreed a perinatal referral and follow-up"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I can't do this to a child. I can't give them what I've got.\"","r":"I can hear how much you already love this baby. Let me give you the real numbers, because they're often much less frightening than what people read online."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is there a test? Can you check the baby?\"","r":"I wish I could give you that certainty. There isn't a test, before or after birth, that can tell whether a child will develop schizophrenia."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"It's selfish of me to even have a baby, isn't it?\"","r":"No, it isn't selfish. Most children of parents with schizophrenia never develop it, and many parents with the illness raise happy, healthy children."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"It's against my faith. If my family find out I even thought about this, they'll disown me.\"","r":"That's a painful place to be. What you tell me stays confidential. This is your decision, and we can support you to think it through, at your own pace."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What else causes it, if it's not just genes?\"","r":"Good question. Things like heavy cannabis use as a teenager, serious stress or trauma in childhood, and problems around birth can add to the risk. Some of those you can influence."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I stopped my tablets six months ago. I didn't tell anyone.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me. I'm not cross. It's important we know, because pregnancy and the months after birth can bring a higher risk of becoming unwell again."}],"bps":{"bio":["Perinatal psychiatrist reviews restarting aripiprazole (previously 10 mg each morning), weighing relapse risk against pregnancy risks.","Folic acid 400 micrograms daily to 12 weeks; routine antenatal bloods and scans with the midwife.","If an antipsychotic is restarted: weight and glucose monitoring; oral glucose tolerance test as per maternity guidance."],"psych":["Relapse signature agreed: poor sleep, suspiciousness, withdrawing.","Perinatal team offers psychological support for anxiety about the baby."],"social":["With consent, involve Imran in the plan; offer a joint appointment.","Care coordinator links with midwife and, later, health visitor."],"safety":["Urgent referral to the perinatal community mental health team.","Pre-birth planning meeting at about 32 weeks; care plan in maternity notes.","Crisis plan: CMHT duty line and NHS 111 option 2; review in 1 week."]},"facts":[{"h":"Risk numbers to quote","x":"General population about 1 in 100. One affected parent about 13 in 100, so 87 in 100 unaffected. Both parents about 4 to 5 in 10. Identical twin about 1 in 2."},{"h":"No test exists","x":"No genetic, blood, scan or prenatal test predicts schizophrenia. Many genes each add a tiny effect; none decides it alone."},{"h":"Risks a parent can influence","x":"Avoiding cannabis in adolescence, a stable, warm home, good antenatal care and early help if a child struggles all reduce risk."},{"h":"Relapse risk after birth","x":"Women with schizophrenia have a raised risk of relapse in pregnancy and particularly in the first weeks after birth, higher if medication has stopped."},{"h":"Refer to perinatal early","x":"NICE advises referral to a specialist perinatal mental health service for women with a current or past severe mental illness in pregnancy."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Management plan","do":"Explain schizophrenia's causes and her baby's risk in numbers, support her decision, and refer to the perinatal team."},"c-s-13":{"obj":["Stated the limits of Tom's consent and kept to them, including when new information was offered","Explained why Tom has adult ADHD, using current inattention, overactivity and impulsivity","Advised against stopping methylphenidate and explained why other medicines and herbal remedies are unsuitable","Explained that methylphenidate is banned in competition but can be allowed through a TUE, and what the application needs","Agreed reasonable workplace adjustments and the next steps with Tom"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"He's been picked for the nationals and now this. Just take him off it.\"","r":"\"I can hear how much this means to you both, and how unfair the timing feels. Stopping would bring his symptoms back within a day. There's a better way: let me explain the exemption.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Does he even have ADHD? Loads of lads are restless.\"","r":"\"Fair question. It's more than restless: he's had it since he was small, and you've described it yourself. Careless mistakes, missed instructions, interrupting, the temper. That's the pattern.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Isn't it just a performance drug, then? That's why it's banned.\"","r":"\"I see why it looks that way. In ADHD it brings focus up to an ordinary level; it doesn't give an edge. It's banned because it could be misused by others, which is why exemptions exist.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"I've found a herbal calming remedy online. Could he use that?\"","r":"\"You're trying to find a way through, and I respect that. There's no good evidence it works, and supplements sometimes contain banned substances. If he tested positive, he'd be responsible.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Did you know he takes ecstasy at parties?\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; I can see you're worried. I can't discuss anything Tom hasn't agreed to share. I'd encourage you to talk to him, and I'll make sure we cover drug use with him too.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Can you just give me a certificate saying he's fine to compete?\"","r":"\"I wish it were that simple. Only UK Anti-Doping can grant the exemption. What we can do is write the specialist report his application needs, this week.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue methylphenidate XL 36 mg each morning; no change before the championships","Monitoring: BP, pulse and weight every 6 months; appetite and sleep at each review","Atomoxetine not to be retried (worse symptoms, raised liver enzymes at 16); other stimulants also prohibited in competition"],"psych":["Psychoeducation for Tom and, with consent, his manager: written NHS and ADHD UK information","Offer CBT-based ADHD skills group for organisation and impulse control"],"social":["Workplace adjustments: written task lists, one task at a time, short daily check-in, movement breaks, quiet space for admin","Access to Work application for ADHD coaching; occupational health referral if the employer has one","Tom applies for a TUE through UK Anti-Doping now; consultant report sent within a week"],"safety":["Advise no supplements without checking a batch-tested source","Drug use: private conversation with Tom about MDMA risks with stimulants and testing","Review in 4 weeks; no information beyond consent shared with the employer"]},"facts":[{"h":"Banned in competition, not banned for life","x":"Methylphenidate is a stimulant on the WADA list, prohibited in competition. An athlete with documented ADHD can apply for a TUE with specialist evidence. Apply well before the event."},{"h":"What a TUE application needs","x":"A specialist's report: how ADHD was diagnosed, symptoms and impact, treatments tried and why, and current dose. Missing history is the commonest reason for delay."},{"h":"Stopping is safe, but symptoms return","x":"Methylphenidate has no dangerous withdrawal, but it works day by day: stop it and focus, impulse control and safety drop within a day."},{"h":"Supplements and strict liability","x":"Athletes are responsible for any banned substance found in them, whatever the source. 'Natural' remedies can be contaminated. Use batch-tested products only."},{"h":"Consent has edges","x":"Written consent to discuss ADHD does not cover drug use, family or past events. You can listen to a third party; you cannot confirm or deny."},{"h":"Reasonable adjustments","x":"Under the Equality Act 2010, employers must make reasonable adjustments if ADHD has a substantial, long-term effect on daily life. Access to Work can fund coaching."}],"type":"Explain a treatment + Professional and ethical dilemma","do":"Within Tom's consent, explain adult ADHD and its treatment, advise against stopping, and outline the exemption and adjustments."},"c-s-15":{"obj":["Confirmed Chloe's consent and heard her father's understanding and worries","Explained functional seizures as real and involuntary, how they were diagnosed and the mind-body link","Asked about Chloe's mood, safety and possible stressors, including the bereavement","Explained that anti-epileptic medicines do not help, the treatment that does, and the outlook","Gave first-aid advice for attacks and agreed how to invite Chloe back"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"So the neurologists think she's putting it on?\"","r":"\"I can see why it sounded like that, and I'm sorry it did. No: these attacks are real and she isn't doing them on purpose. Let me explain what's actually happening.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"If the tests are normal, how can there be anything wrong?\"","r":"\"Good question. The tests show the brain's structure and wiring are healthy. The problem is in how it's working, like software crashing on a good computer.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will the epilepsy tablets stop them?\"","r":"\"Sadly not; they work on electrical seizures, and these aren't. The neurologist will reduce them slowly. Please don't stop them suddenly.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Is this because of her mum? Should I have done something?\"","r":"\"You've both been through so much, and it's clear how much you care. Losing her mum may be part of it, but nobody caused this. What matters is what we do now.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will she get better?\"","r":"\"Many people do, and some stop having attacks altogether. The best sign is understanding the diagnosis and starting the right therapy early, and she's been diagnosed within a year.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"What do I do when she has one? Should I call an ambulance?\"","r":"\"That's frightening to watch. Stay calm, keep her safe from knocks, don't hold her down or put anything in her mouth, and talk to her quietly. Call 999 if she's injured or it's different from usual.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Neurology to taper levetiracetam 500 mg twice daily slowly; do not stop suddenly","If assessment confirms depression or anxiety: sertraline 50 mg once daily, review at 4 weeks; offered alongside therapy, not instead of it","No rescue medicines (such as buccal midazolam) for typical attacks"],"psych":["Psychology referral: CBT-based therapy for functional seizures, about 12 sessions, neuropsychiatry psychologist","Grounding techniques for warning signs: slow breathing, naming five things she can see, feeling her feet on the floor","Full psychiatric assessment of mood, anxiety, trauma and grief when she attends"],"social":["Cruse Bereavement Support offered to Chloe and her father","Work or study plan with occupational therapy if attacks are stopping her working","Father given neurosymptoms.org and the NHS FND page to share"],"safety":["Attack first aid: keep her safe, no restraint, nothing in the mouth, calm voice, time it; recovery position afterwards","999 if injured, breathing difficulty, or an attack very different from usual","Not to drive or bathe alone until her neurologist advises; showers instead of baths","Mood and suicide risk screened at first appointment; GP copied; review 4 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Words that help and harm","x":"Say 'functional' or 'dissociative' seizures. Never 'pseudo', 'psychogenic' or 'hysterical'. Patients often hear these as 'fake' and drop out of care."},{"h":"Video-EEG makes the diagnosis","x":"A typical attack recorded on video with normal EEG during the event is the gold standard. It is a positive diagnosis based on what the attacks look like, not just normal tests."},{"h":"Common, not rare","x":"Around one in five people referred to specialist seizure clinics with apparent epilepsy have functional seizures. Some people have both."},{"h":"Anti-epileptics: taper, don't stop","x":"They do not help functional seizures and cause side effects. Neurology tapers them once the diagnosis is secure."},{"h":"First aid is low-key","x":"Protect from injury, do not restrain, speak calmly, time it. Avoid crowds and rushed ambulance calls for typical attacks; agree a written plan for when to call 999."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis","do":"Explain functional seizures to her father without blame, and outline treatment, outlook and first aid."},"c-s-16":{"obj":["Elicited the symptoms of high prolactin: periods, breasts, galactorrhoea and libido","Excluded pregnancy, other drugs, thyroid and kidney causes, and pituitary red flags","Explained the result and the risperidone link in plain words, with long-term risks","Agreed one of four options with her, plus bloods and a review date","Covered fertility return, contraception and a perinatal preconception referral"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Is something wrong with me? Is it cancer?\"","r":"That's a frightening thought, and I'm glad you said it. A level like yours on risperidone, with no headaches or sight changes, is very unlikely to be a tumour. Let me explain what it does mean."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not changing my tablets. Risperidone's the only thing that's worked.\"","r":"You've worked hard to stay well, and I don't want to risk that. One option keeps risperidone exactly as it is and adds a small tablet. Can I explain it?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've been avoiding sex with Tom. It's embarrassing.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me. It's a common effect of high prolactin, not something you're doing wrong. It usually improves once the level comes down."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"We want a baby. Will this stop me getting pregnant?\"","r":"It can make it harder, so it's good we found it now. Once the level falls, fertility often returns quickly. Let's plan contraception until you're ready, and advice from the perinatal team before you try."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why didn't anyone warn me about this?\"","r":"You should have been told, and I'm sorry you weren't. It's a known side effect of risperidone. We'll check it regularly from now on, and I'll make sure it's in your care plan."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I'm not putting on weight again like on olanzapine.\"","r":"That's completely understandable after 18 kilos. Aripiprazole is one of the least likely to cause weight gain. We'll weigh you at each visit so you can see for yourself."}],"bps":{"bio":["Aripiprazole 5 mg once daily in the morning added to risperidone 6 mg at night; warn about restlessness and nausea in the first 2 weeks","Today: urine pregnancy test; repeat prolactin after resting 20 minutes (lab to screen for macroprolactin); TFTs; U&E","Prolactin at 3 months; if not falling, consider a switch or endocrinology referral; weight and BP at each review"],"psych":["Relapse signature and early warning signs reviewed with her and her care coordinator"],"social":["Care coordinator to see her at home in 1 week to check side effects and mood","Perinatal mental health team preconception referral by you, with consultant agreement"],"safety":["Same-day duty line if headache, sight change, restlessness or return of voices","Barrier contraception until she chooses to conceive; folic acid 400 micrograms daily from trying","GP letter today with the result, the change and the monitoring plan"]},"facts":[{"h":"Ranking prolactin risk","x":"Highest: risperidone, paliperidone, amisulpride, sulpiride, first-generation drugs. Low: olanzapine. Minimal: aripiprazole, quetiapine, clozapine, lurasidone."},{"h":"Exclude pregnancy first","x":"Pregnancy raises prolactin and stops periods. Do a urine test even when she says it's impossible, before blaming the medicine."},{"h":"Add-on aripiprazole works","x":"Aripiprazole 5 mg daily added to risperidone often normalises prolactin within weeks to months. No fall by 3 months makes success unlikely."},{"h":"Fertility comes back fast","x":"Once prolactin falls, ovulation can return within weeks. Advise contraception until she is ready, and folic acid 400 micrograms from trying to conceive."},{"h":"Red flags mean endocrinology","x":"Headache, visual field loss, a level in the thousands, or no fall after a drug change: refer to endocrinology for pituitary MRI."},{"h":"Dopamine agonists are second line","x":"Cabergoline or bromocriptine can worsen psychosis. Leave them to endocrinology, after antipsychotic changes have failed."}],"type":"Medication review + Explain a treatment","do":"Take a prolactin symptom history, exclude other causes, explain the result and agree a management option with her."},"c-s-17":{"obj":["Established the adherence pattern and past risperidone response","Explained what a depot is, the choices and the schedule, tied to his past response","Explained test doses, side effects and monitoring honestly","Explained consent, Section 3, the three-month rule and a CTO accurately","Checked his understanding and offered a leaflet, carer's assessment and family meeting"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I don't want to hurt him. It sounds brutal.\"","r":"Of course you don't; you're protecting him. Most people find it's a short sting, and the sites are rotated. Can I explain how it works so you can judge for yourself?"},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I feel like I'm going behind his back.\"","r":"That's a very natural feeling. You're not. You're trying to stop a fourth relapse, and the decision is the team's. He's agreed we can talk to you."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What if he just refuses when he's home?\"","r":"A fair worry, and I'll be straight with you. Nobody can inject him at home against his will. A Community Treatment Order can make it a condition, with recall to hospital if needed."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why didn't anyone suggest this after the first relapse?\"","r":"I understand the frustration. I can't speak for earlier decisions, but it's the right question now, and I'll make sure your view is heard at ward round."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it a stronger drug?\"","r":"No. It's the same kind of medicine as his tablets, just released slowly from the muscle. The dose is matched to what worked for him."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"He thinks I'm working with the police. My own son.\"","r":"That must be heartbreaking. It's the illness talking, not Daniel. When he was well before, did those beliefs fade?"}],"bps":{"bio":["Paliperidone palmitate 150 mg deltoid day 1, 100 mg deltoid day 8, then 75 mg monthly (range 25–150 mg), deltoid or gluteal","Consider 3-monthly paliperidone after at least 4 months stable on monthly doses","Baseline and 3-month weight, BP, HbA1c, lipids and prolactin, then yearly; ECG if cardiac risk"],"psych":["Family intervention offered to Daniel and his father (NICE: at least 10 sessions over 3–12 months)","Psychoeducation on early warning signs once Daniel is willing"],"social":["Care coordinator to give the depot at home after discharge","Carer's assessment for Mr Price from the local authority; carers' group details"],"safety":["RC and AMHP to consider a CTO before discharge","Missed dose: care coordinator alerts RC within 7 days; monthly window is plus or minus 7 days"]},"facts":[{"h":"Match the depot to past response","x":"If risperidone worked and was tolerated, paliperidone palmitate is the natural choice: it is risperidone's active metabolite, so oral tolerance is already shown."},{"h":"Paliperidone start doses","x":"150 mg day 1 and 100 mg day 8, both into the deltoid, then monthly. Missing day 8 matters: steady levels depend on both starting doses."},{"h":"Test doses for older depots","x":"Flupentixol, zuclopenthixol and haloperidol depots need a small test dose first, then the full dose after about 5–10 days if tolerated."},{"h":"Three-month rule","x":"Under Section 58, consent or a SOAD certificate is needed once three months have passed since the first dose of medication in this detention."},{"h":"CTOs allow recall, not force","x":"A CTO patient who refuses cannot be injected at home. The RC can recall him to hospital for up to 72 hours to treat."}],"type":"Explain a treatment + Mental Health Act process","do":"Explain depot injections, schedule, side effects and the Mental Health Act to his father; check his understanding."},"c-s-18":{"obj":["Uncovered the precipitating event and elicited the panic attack","Mapped the feared situations and the fear of being trapped without help","Separated agoraphobia from panic disorder, depression, psychosis and other anxiety disorders","Assessed self-neglect, substances and suicidal thoughts","Explained the provisional diagnosis in plain words"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm fine. I just prefer being at home.\"","r":"Lots of people do, and I'm glad home feels good. Your daughter noticed a change, though. Has anything happened in the last couple of months?"},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"A grown man who can't buy his own bread. It's shameful.\"","r":"I can hear how hard that is. It doesn't make you less of a father. Fear like this can happen to anyone after a frightening event."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I thought it was a heart attack. Maybe it was.\"","r":"That's how a panic attack feels, and it's terrifying. We'll make sure your GP checks your heart and bloods, so you can be confident."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Am I going mad?\"","r":"No. This is a common anxiety problem with a clear cause. Your mind is trying to protect you, and it's overdoing it. It's very treatable."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I got on the bus to church and had to get off and come straight home.\"","r":"That took courage, and it tells me how strong the fear is. What did you feel just before you got off?"},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can't I just stay in? I can work from here.\"","r":"You can, for now, and you're doing well at work. But you miss church and your independence. Treatment can give those back, at a pace you choose."}],"bps":{"bio":["GP: FBC, TFTs, glucose and ECG to exclude physical causes of panic"],"psych":["NHS Talking Therapies (self-referral): CBT with graded exposure for agoraphobia"],"social":["Daughter continues online shopping meanwhile; launderette trip as an early exposure goal"],"safety":["Review self-neglect at follow-up; GP to re-refer if mood falls or he stops eating"]},"facts":[{"h":"Agoraphobia is about escape","x":"Fear in several places where getting out or getting help is hard: transport, shops, crowds, queues, being out alone. The feared outcome is panic or collapse."},{"h":"Ask about attacks at home","x":"Panic disorder needs recurrent unexpected attacks. Attacks only in feared places point to agoraphobia; both can coexist."},{"h":"Realistic fear is not paranoia","x":"Fear after a real threat is anxiety. Ask whether he thinks they target him personally; if not, it isn't a persecutory belief."},{"h":"Anxiety disorders travel together","x":"Finding one prompts a screen for PTSD, social anxiety, OCD, depression and alcohol used to cope."},{"h":"Duration matters","x":"ICD-11 expects symptoms for several months. At two months, say 'provisional', and treat anyway."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"Take a history that diagnoses agoraphobia, rules out its look-alikes, and assesses self-neglect and suicide risk."},"c-s-19":{"obj":["Gave the news after a warning, in plain words, and stayed with her shock","Linked the finding to his symptoms and answered prognosis honestly","Explained the MRI, urgent neurosurgical referral, driving, work and Samuel's right to decide","Gave clear red-flag safety-netting","Agreed how Samuel will be told and checked her understanding"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Brain tumours kill people. He's my best friend.\"","r":"I'm so sorry. That's a terrifying thought. Most meningiomas are not cancer and grow slowly, but we'll know more after the MRI. Take your time."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You said the scan would be normal. Why didn't anyone check sooner?\"","r":"You're right to be upset; this wasn't what you were told to expect. The scan is exactly why we check, and now we can act quickly."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How long has he got?\"","r":"I understand you need to know. I can't answer that honestly until the MRI and the specialists. For most people with a meningioma, it's very treatable."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can he still drive? It's his job.\"","r":"I know how much depends on it. He must stop driving now and tell the DVLA. The specialists will advise when it might be safe again."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will I have to make all his decisions now?\"","r":"That's a lot to carry. No: Samuel still makes his own decisions. You can support him, and he can choose to involve you."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can you not tell him yet? Let him have a normal week.\"","r":"I understand wanting to protect him. He asked to know everything, and the next steps are urgent. Let's tell him together tomorrow, gently."}],"bps":{"bio":["MRI head with contrast Friday; urgent neurosurgical referral sent today by the consultant","Antipsychotic start on hold pending neurosurgical advice; any steroid (e.g. dexamethasone) decided by neurosurgery"],"psych":["Ongoing CMHT review; consultant liaison with the neurosurgical team"],"social":["GP fit note; CMHT support worker to help with employer and statutory sick pay","The Brain Tumour Charity information for Esi and their mother"],"safety":["No driving; Samuel to inform DVLA; advice documented","Red flags: worsening headache, vomiting, drowsiness, confusion, seizure, new weakness → 999/A&E","Joint home visit tomorrow to tell Samuel; GP informed today"]},"facts":[{"h":"Explain the word","x":"'Meningioma: a growth from the lining that covers the brain, pressing on it from outside.' Check she has understood before going on."},{"h":"Mostly benign, but unconfirmed","x":"Most meningiomas are non-cancerous and slow-growing. Grade and treatment come from MRI and the specialist team, so do not promise outcomes."},{"h":"Frontal lesions change behaviour","x":"Frontal growths can cause personality change, apathy, disinhibition, suspiciousness and headache, mimicking a first episode of psychosis."},{"h":"Driving stops today","x":"Advise no driving and that he must tell the DVLA. Document the advice. The specialist team decides when he may return."},{"h":"Silence is part of it","x":"After the news, stop. Let her lead with her questions. Information given into shock is lost."}],"type":"Breaking bad news","do":"Tell his sister about the meningioma plainly, answer her questions honestly, and plan the urgent next steps."},"c-s-20":{"obj":["Asked about all four EPSE groups, falls and NMS features, linked to the dose change","Examined face, mouth, hands, tone, finger tapping, standing and gait with consent and a chaperone","Explained the finding of drug-induced parkinsonism in plain words","Offered dose reduction, switch or short-term procyclidine, and planned falls safety"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Have I got Parkinson's disease?\"","r":"That's a frightening thought. It isn't Parkinson's disease. It's a side effect of the haloperidol, and it usually gets better once we change the dose."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I feel like I'm made of wood.\"","r":"That sounds really uncomfortable, and I'm glad your mum spoke up. Let me ask a few questions and examine you so we can fix it."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why did they put it up if it does this?\"","r":"Fair question. The higher dose was to help your mood and thinking, and side effects aren't always predictable. Now we know, we can adjust it."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not taking any more tablets on top.\"","r":"I understand. The first choice is to lower or change the haloperidol, not add more. Procyclidine is only a short-term option if you want it."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will I go back to how I was?\"","r":"Most people do, over days to weeks after the change. We'll check you again tomorrow and see how you're walking."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I'm scared of falling again.\"","r":"After last week, that makes sense. Use the handrails, and the physio will check your walking. We'll also check your blood pressure when you stand."}],"bps":{"bio":["Discuss with consultant today: reduce haloperidol to 5 mg once daily, or switch to an antipsychotic with lower EPSE risk","If needed short term: procyclidine 2.5 mg three times daily (BNF usual max 30 mg daily); warn dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision, confusion, urinary retention","Lying and standing BP; daily obs including temperature while changes are made; Simpson-Angus and AIMS baseline"],"social":["Update his mother with his consent"],"safety":["Physio falls assessment; handrails; falls risk care plan","Nurses to escalate fever, sweating, confusion or rising rigidity (NMS) immediately","Review tomorrow"]},"facts":[{"h":"Four EPSEs, four timelines","x":"Dystonia: hours to days. Akathisia: days to weeks. Parkinsonism: weeks. Tardive dyskinesia: months to years. Ask about all four every time."},{"h":"Watch before you touch","x":"Seated, hands on knees, observe the whole body; look at the tongue at rest and protruded before testing tone."},{"h":"Bring out rigidity","x":"Move the wrist slowly; if subtle, ask him to open and close the other hand to bring out cogwheeling."},{"h":"Procyclidine is a bridge","x":"Use short term for parkinsonism or dystonia. It worsens tardive dyskinesia, memory and constipation. Review the antipsychotic dose first."},{"h":"Rigidity plus fever is NMS","x":"Rigidity with fever, sweating, fluctuating BP or confusion: stop the antipsychotic, check CK, transfer to medical care."}],"type":"Physical examination","do":"Ask about all four EPSE groups, examine in order with a chaperone, then explain findings and options."},"c-s-21":{"obj":["Gained consent with a chaperone offered, said a rectal and genital check would be omitted, and positioned him flat","Checked the peripheral signs of chronic liver disease from the hands to the chest","Tested for encephalopathy: a 15-second flap test, orientation, star copy and fetor, and told it apart from withdrawal and Wernicke's","Palpated, percussed (with shifting dullness) and auscultated the abdomen and checked legs, reflexes and gait","Told him the findings honestly, arranged liver bloods, an ultrasound and a FibroScan, and gave four warning signs"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"My dad died of his liver at sixty. I'm going the same way, aren't I?\"","r":"I'm sorry about your dad; no wonder this scares you. Today's check shows strain but no signs of failure, and you've stopped drinking, which is the biggest thing anyone can do. Let's find out exactly where you are."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How am I looking so far?\"","r":"Good question. So far your colour, eyes and pulse are all normal. I'll keep telling you as I go, and sum it all up at the end."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why are you looking at my hands? It's my liver I'm worried about.\"","r":"Fair point. The liver leaves clues all over the body, and the hands show some of the earliest. I'll get to your tummy in a minute."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I'm embarrassed. I let myself get like this.\"","r":"Thank you for saying that. Alcohol dependence is an illness, not a failing, and you're here doing something about it. There's nothing to be embarrassed about in this room."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"If the scan's all right, I could have the odd pint, couldn't I?\"","r":"I can see why you'd hope so. With the drinking you've described, most people do best stopping completely. Let's get the results, then talk it through with the alcohol team before you go home."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why aren't you doing the back passage? Isn't that part of it?\"","r":"It is part of a complete check, but nothing you've told me needs it today. If anything changes, like black stools, we'd think again."}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods today: LFTs, GGT, albumin, bilirubin, INR, FBC (low platelets suggest portal hypertension), U&E, glucose, magnesium.","Liver ultrasound plus transient elastography (FibroScan); NICE NG50 offers it to people drinking harmfully to test for cirrhosis.","Chlordiazepoxide: review with the consultant if albumin is low or INR is raised; oxazepam is the usual switch in significant liver impairment.","Oral thiamine (often 100 mg three times daily under local protocol) after the Pabrinex course, continued while drinking risk remains."],"psych":["Relapse prevention with the community alcohol team; acamprosate or naltrexone choice depends on liver results (both have hepatic cautions)."],"social":["Alcohol liaison nurse meets him before discharge and books community alcohol team follow-up."],"safety":["Nurses report drowsiness, new confusion, a flap, jaundice, vomiting blood or black stools to the on-call doctor at once.","Ward doctor reviews results within 24 hours and documents the plan."]},"facts":[{"h":"Hold the flap for fifteen seconds","x":"Asterixis is a brief loss of posture, so a quick look misses it. Arms out, wrists back, fingers spread, watch for at least 15 seconds."},{"h":"Fine tremor is not a flap","x":"A fast, fine tremor of the fingers suggests withdrawal, anxiety or thyrotoxicosis. A slow, irregular, coarse flap of the wrists suggests encephalopathy."},{"h":"Copy a star to catch it early","x":"Constructional difficulty appears before obvious confusion. Ask him to copy a five-pointed star or draw a clock; poor copying plus slowed thinking suggests early encephalopathy."},{"h":"Benzodiazepines can tip the liver","x":"Sedatives, infection, constipation, bleeding and low potassium all precipitate hepatic encephalopathy. A drowsy detox patient needs a liver check, not just a lower dose."},{"h":"Normal check, silent cirrhosis","x":"Many people with cirrhosis have no signs. A normal examination never replaces liver tests, platelets, INR and elastography in someone drinking heavily for years."}],"type":"Physical examination","do":"Examine the abdomen, liver stigmata and encephalopathy signs, telling him findings, then order liver tests and a FibroScan."},"c-s-35":{"obj":["Elicited everyday examples of inattention, hyperactivity and impulsivity","Established onset in early childhood and presence at home, school and at least one other setting","Covered pregnancy, prematurity, milestones and family history","Screened for learning difficulty, autism, oppositional behaviour, low mood, sleep and hearing problems and substance use","Assessed impact and risk, explored her concerns about exclusion and medicine, and agreed next steps"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I'm terrified he'll get excluded. Then what?\"","r":"\"That's a real worry, and you're doing the right thing coming now. Whatever we find, I'll write to the school about support straight away; that often helps prevent exclusion.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"He can play the Xbox for three hours. So he can concentrate when he wants to, can't he?\"","r":"\"Lots of parents notice that. Games give a reward every few seconds, so they hold attention even in ADHD. What tells us more is something dull, like homework.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"We shout at him all the time. Have we caused this?\"","r":"\"Thank you for being honest; most parents shout when they're worn out. Parenting doesn't cause ADHD, but support for you can make home calmer. Have things ever gone further than shouting?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"His dad was exactly the same. Is it genetic?\"","r":"\"It can run in families, and a parent with similar traits is common. It's useful for me to know, and it doesn't mean Callum's path will be the same.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want him on tablets. I've read they turn kids into zombies.\"","r":"\"That's a fair worry. Medicine is never automatic; it's your and Callum's choice. If it's ever used, we start low and check closely. It shouldn't change who he is.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"He says he's stupid. He's a lovely boy and everyone just sees the bad bits.\"","r":"\"I can hear how much you love him and how hard it is to watch. Has he ever talked about hurting himself or wishing you weren't here?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Before any medicine (only after diagnosis): height, weight, BP, pulse, cardiac history; methylphenidate is first line for children aged 5 and over when impairment persists (NICE NG87)"],"psych":["ADHD-focused parent information and group support (NICE NG87); sleep routine: screens off an hour before bed"],"social":["School: SENCo to complete teacher rating scale and report; reasonable adjustments now (front seat, chunked instructions, homework planner)"],"safety":["Safety net: if he talks of self-harm, contact CAMHS or NHS 111 (or 999 in an emergency)"]},"facts":[{"h":"Examples beat labels","x":"Ask 'How long can he do homework before getting up?' rather than 'Can he concentrate?'. Specific examples across home, school and clubs make the history useful."},{"h":"Gaming isn't a test of attention","x":"Fast, rewarding screen activities hold attention even in ADHD. Ask about dull tasks: homework, reading, getting dressed, listening to instructions."},{"h":"More than one setting","x":"ADHD shows across settings. Problems only at school suggest a learning or school issue; only at home suggest family stress. Ask about clubs and relatives too."},{"h":"Prematurity and family history","x":"Being born early or small and having a parent with ADHD both raise the likelihood. Ask about both in every collateral history."},{"h":"Rating scales from two sources","x":"UK clinics usually gather parent and teacher questionnaires plus a school report before deciding. NICE says scales support, but do not replace, clinical assessment."}],"type":"Collateral history","do":"Take a collateral history covering ADHD's three domains across settings, development, differentials, risk and her concerns."},"c-s-36":{"obj":["Found out what he knows and fears, and explained ADHD in plain words as a brain-development condition with three core features","Explained the causes and strong family link honestly, and said clearly that parenting did not cause it","Agreed an ADHD parent-training group and a school support plan through the SENCO","Explained methylphenidate: how it helps, common side effects, the baseline heart checks and the monitoring schedule","Checked understanding with teach-back and gave a safety net and a follow-up date"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Half my family say ADHD is just a fancy name for a naughty kid.\"","r":"\"A lot of people hear that, so I'm glad you asked. Naughtiness is a choice. Jake's difficulties show up everywhere, even when he's trying hard and gets told off for it. That's what we measured.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I work long hours. Is this because I'm not around enough?\"","r":"\"It's clear how much you care. No: ADHD comes from how the brain develops, mostly through genes. Your time with him helps him cope, but your hours didn't cause it.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Honestly, I was exactly like him. Teachers said I'd never sit still.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. That fits: ADHD runs strongly in families. It also means you know how he feels. If it still affects you, your GP can refer you for an adult assessment.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I don't want him turned into a zombie.\"","r":"\"That's a fair worry. At the right dose children are still themselves, just more able to focus. If he seems flat or tearful, that tells us the dose is too high, and we lower it.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Isn't it addictive? It's a stimulant.\"","r":"\"Good question. Taken as prescribed by mouth, children don't become addicted, and treatment does not raise the later risk of drug problems. It is a controlled drug, so keep it locked away and never share it.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will he grow out of it?\"","r":"\"Many children improve, especially the restlessness. Concentration problems more often carry on into adult life, but adults learn to manage. We'll review every year whether he still needs medicine.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Methylphenidate MR (e.g. Concerta XL) 18 mg once each morning with breakfast; increase by 18 mg weekly if needed; licensed maximum 54 mg/day","Baseline and at each dose change: pulse and BP on age centiles; height every 6 months, weight every 3 months (aged 10 or under), on growth charts","Sustained resting HR over 120, arrhythmia or BP above the 95th centile on two occasions: reduce dose and refer to paediatrics","If methylphenidate fails after 6 weeks at an adequate dose: switch to lisdexamfetamine (NICE NG87)"],"psych":["ADHD-focused group parent-training programme, weekly for about 10 to 12 weeks, run by CAMHS or local family services","Psychoeducation for Jake at his level; consider CBT later if impairment persists despite medication"],"social":["With consent, letter to the SENCO: SEN support plan, seat near the teacher, short instructions, movement breaks, teacher rating scale","Ask the school to request an EHC needs assessment from the local authority if SEN support is not enough","Signpost ADHD Foundation and local parent support groups"],"safety":["Road safety: hold hands near roads and supervise crossings until impulsivity improves","Store tablets locked away; one adult gives the dose","Stop and call CAMHS or 111 if chest pain, fainting, palpitations, marked low mood or unusual thoughts"]},"facts":[{"h":"Not a diet problem","x":"NICE advises against routinely cutting out additives or colourings. If a food or drink seems to make him worse, keep a diary and ask for dietitian advice."},{"h":"ECG is not routine","x":"Before a stimulant, take the cardiac history and family history and check pulse and BP. ECG only if there is a cardiac concern or family history of sudden death."},{"h":"Appetite is the big side effect","x":"Give the dose with or after breakfast, allow a bigger evening meal and snacks. Weight falling across centile lines needs a dose review or planned breaks."},{"h":"Sleep onset can slip","x":"Morning dosing and a calm evening routine help. Keep a two-week sleep diary before and after starting."},{"h":"Heritability is high","x":"Twin studies put heritability around 70 to 80%. Brothers and sisters have a higher chance; a parent often recognises themselves."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Explain a treatment","do":"Explain ADHD, its causes and the home, school and methylphenidate plan, with cardiac checks and monitoring."},"c-s-37":{"obj":["Built a timeline from pregnancy and birth to now, with motor and language milestones and their ages","Elicited specific examples of social communication difference: response to name, pointing to share, eye contact, pretend play and peer play","Elicited restricted, repetitive and sensory behaviours, each with an example","Asked about loss of skills, hearing, co-occurring conditions and self-injury to weigh the differentials","Summarised back to her and explained the multidisciplinary assessment that comes next"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I went back to work when he was one. Did I do this to him?\"","r":"\"I can hear how much that's been weighing on you. No. These differences come from how a child's brain develops, not from childcare. Let's understand Noah properly so we can help him.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"He bangs his head on the floor when he's upset. I can't stop him.\"","r":"\"That sounds frightening to watch. Thank you for telling me. Has he ever hurt himself badly, a cut or a bump that needed checking? We'll include this in the plan.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it autism? Just tell me.\"","r":"\"I understand you want an answer. Some things you've described do fit autism, but others could too, like hearing or language problems. A full assessment will give you a clear answer.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"The paediatrician said he's just a late talker.\"","r":"\"That's often how it first looks. What you've told me today is about more than talking: how he plays, shares and copes with change. That's why it's worth assessing now.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will he ever have friends?\"","r":"\"That's such an important worry. Many children like Noah make good friendships, often through shared interests. The assessment will help us and the school know how to support him.\""},{"tag":"Comparison","p":"\"Alfie was nothing like this.\"","r":"\"It's natural to compare. Alfie gives us a useful picture of what you'd expect. Tell me what Alfie did at two that Noah didn't.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Confirm hearing: audiology report from last spring; repeat if any doubt","Sleep: behavioural sleep plan first; consider melatonin only if problems persist after that (NICE CG170)","Physical check by the team: head circumference, skin for neurocutaneous signs, dysmorphic features; genetic tests only if features or learning disability suggest them"],"psych":["Multidisciplinary autism assessment: ADOS-2 observation, developmental interview, cognitive and language assessment","Speech and language therapy assessment now, without waiting for a diagnosis"],"social":["With consent, school report from class teacher and SENCO; ask for SEN support now","Health visitor or early help for sleep and meltdown strategies","Signpost National Autistic Society parent line and local parent groups"],"safety":["Head-banging: soft area, stay close, reduce triggers; seek urgent care for any head injury with vomiting or drowsiness","Ask about road safety and wandering; review at next contact"]},"facts":[{"h":"Regression is a red flag","x":"Loss of words or social skills at any age needs prompt paediatric review. Most autistic children show delay, not loss, so always ask directly."},{"h":"Hearing first","x":"Poor response to name can be deafness. Check a recent formal hearing test; if none, request audiology before or alongside the assessment."},{"h":"Pointing to share matters","x":"Pointing to request ('I want that') is common; pointing to share interest ('look, a plane') is often missing in autism. Ask which one he does."},{"h":"Echolalia is communication","x":"Repeating TV lines or your words can be his way of joining in or asking. Record examples; it is common in autistic children's language."},{"h":"Normal motor, odd language","x":"Walking on time with late, unusual language points away from global delay. Late walking too suggests a wider developmental or learning disability."}],"type":"Collateral history","do":"Take a chronological developmental history covering social communication, repetitive behaviour, sensory issues, regression, differentials and impact."},"c-s-38":{"obj":["Explained the diagnosis in plain words as a lifelong difference in social communication and flexibility, including Isla's strengths","Explained why it was recognised at 14, what causes autism, and that Asperger's now sits within autism","Screened for co-occurring anxiety, low mood, self-harm, sleep and eating difficulties and agreed follow-up for each","Explained that no medicine treats autism itself and agreed school adjustments through the SENCO and family support","Agreed how Isla will hear about her diagnosis and checked understanding with teach-back"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"How did everyone miss this for ten years?\"","r":"\"It's understandable to feel angry. Many girls copy others and hide their difficulties, and it often only shows when secondary school gets harder. You noticed, and that's why she's been assessed now.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Was it something I did? The vaccines? Her diet?\"","r":"\"I can see you've been searching for a reason. Autism is mostly genetic, from many genes together. Vaccines, diet and parenting don't cause it. Nothing you did caused this.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Isn't this Asperger's? That's what my sister said.\"","r":"\"Good question. Asperger's was the old name for autism without language delay. Doctors now use one term, autism, for the whole range. Isla might hear both.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is there a tablet that helps?\"","r":"\"I understand wanting something concrete. There's no medicine for autism itself. Medicine can help things alongside it, like anxiety or sleep. What helps most is understanding and the right support.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will she be able to live on her own, have a job, a partner?\"","r":"\"That's a big worry. Many autistic adults live independently, work and have relationships. Getting support now gives her the best chance. Let's focus on what helps her this year.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"She's been refusing school on Mondays. She says she's exhausted.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that matters. Keeping up all week can be draining for autistic girls. Let's look at school adjustments and help for her anxiety.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No medication for autism itself (NICE CG170)","Sleep: written sleep plan first; consider prolonged-release melatonin 2 mg, 30 to 60 minutes before bed, only if problems persist; review at 3 months","If moderate to severe depression develops despite psychological therapy, specialist may add fluoxetine 10 mg once daily (NICE NG134)"],"psych":["Feedback session for Isla next week, at her pace, with written summary","CBT adapted for autism for anxiety, about 12 to 16 weekly sessions, CAMHS psychology","Post-diagnostic parent group, 6 sessions, run by the neurodevelopmental team"],"social":["With consent, report to the SENCO: time-out card, quiet lunch space, warning of changes, seating, uniform flexibility","If needs exceed SEN support, ask the school or parent to request an EHC needs assessment from the local authority","Carer's needs assessment from the local authority; National Autistic Society information"],"safety":["Screen mood, self-harm and eating at every review","Mother to contact CAMHS or GP if self-harm, weight loss or worsening school refusal; 111 or A&E in an emergency"]},"facts":[{"h":"Masking delays diagnosis in girls","x":"Copying peers and hiding difficulties can hide autism until social demands rise. Exhaustion after school and Monday refusal can be signs of the strain."},{"h":"Asperger's is now autism","x":"ICD-11 and DSM-5 use autism spectrum disorder for the whole range, with specifiers for intellectual and language ability."},{"h":"No medicine for core features","x":"NICE advises against medication for core autism features. Treat co-occurring anxiety, ADHD, depression or sleep problems in their own right."},{"h":"Eating can be a clue","x":"Autistic girls have higher rates of ARFID and anorexia nervosa. Ask about range of foods, weight and body image at each review."},{"h":"Isla owns the diagnosis","x":"Agree with her how she hears it, who else is told, and which words she likes. Telling school needs consent."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis","do":"Explain the autism diagnosis, why it came late, co-occurring conditions and support, and agree how Isla is told."},"c-s-39":{"obj":["Acknowledged the bed pressure and explained, with her specific risks, why medically fit does not mean safe to go home tonight","Agreed overnight admission to a paediatric bed, explaining why a psychiatric ward is not right tonight and when CAMHS inpatient care is considered","Agreed a written observation level, a safe room and a plan if she tries to leave, including Gillick competence","Agreed safeguarding checks and what her mother will be told","Explained how the morning assessment decides between discharge and admission, and the follow-up offered either way"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"She's medically fit. Mum's nearly here. Why can't she just go?\"","r":"\"I get it, you've six children waiting and that's hard. Medically she's fine. But she still feels she'd be better off dead, and we've no information from home or school yet. Tonight isn't safe.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can't she go to your psych ward then? We need the cubicle.\"","r":"\"I'd want her out of a cubicle too. An adult ward isn't safe or legal for a 15-year-old, and she doesn't need a mental health bed tonight. A paediatric bed with her mum is right.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What if she tries to walk out at 4am?\"","r":"\"Good thing to plan for. Please keep her safe and call me straight away. I'll check her understanding and involve the on-call CAMHS consultant. We don't just rely on mum's say-so if she refuses.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How do you decide tomorrow whether she goes home?\"","r":"\"Our liaison team talks to Chloe and her mum, school and the GP. If risk settles and home is safe, she goes with a safety plan. If not, we look at more intensive support.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Every time it's the same. You lot leave them with us all night.\"","r":"\"You're right that it lands on your team, and I'm sorry. I'll write a clear plan, give you my bleep, and make sure the liaison team come first thing.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"She told my nurse there's stuff at home she won't talk about.\"","r":"\"Thank you, that's important. I'll ask her gently tonight, and we'll check with children's social care and the safeguarding team. Please write down exactly what she said.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Confirm ED clearance: ibuprofen dose about 58 mg/kg (under 100 mg/kg); paracetamol and salicylate negative at 4 hours","No routine psychotropic tonight; if she cannot sleep, simple sleep measures rather than sedation"],"psych":["Brief, supportive contact tonight; full psychosocial assessment by CAMHS liaison in the morning with Chloe alone and with her mother","Joint safety plan with Chloe: warning signs, coping steps, people to contact, crisis line"],"social":["With consent, collateral from school (head of year, designated safeguarding lead) and GP","School to address the online exclusion and bullying; if threats or images, consider police","Mother advised on means restriction: lock away all medicines at home, including paracetamol and ibuprofen"],"safety":["Paediatric observation bed; ligature-reduced room; belongings checked with consent; no medicines left in room","Observation: within eyesight when alone; at least every 15 minutes when settled with mother present","If she tries to leave: staff call on-call psychiatry; on-call CAMHS consultant informed; consider MHA assessment","CP-IS checked; hospital safeguarding team told in the morning; community CAMHS follow-up within 7 days if discharged"]},"facts":[{"h":"Medically fit is not safe","x":"Medical clearance answers 'will the tablets harm her?' It doesn't answer 'is she safe tonight?' Ongoing intent, first presentation and no collateral argue for staying."},{"h":"Paediatric bed, not psych ward","x":"Young people who self-harm usually stay on a paediatric ward while CAMHS assesses. MHA s131A requires age-appropriate care for under-18s."},{"h":"Gillick, not MCA, under 16","x":"Assess whether she understands the decision. If competent and refusing, don't rely on parental consent for mental health care; get senior input."},{"h":"Ibuprofen toxicity is dose-related","x":"Under 100 mg/kg rarely causes serious harm; over 400 mg/kg can cause seizures, acidosis and kidney injury. Always check a paracetamol level too."},{"h":"Follow-up within days","x":"If discharged after self-harm, a community CAMHS appointment within 7 days, a written safety plan and a named contact."}],"type":"Management plan + Handover and escalation","do":"Agree overnight paediatric admission, observation level, consent and safeguarding steps, and the morning CAMHS plan with the nurse."},"c-s-40":{"obj":["Elicited the delusional belief, how it began and its evidence, and tested its conviction without colluding or arguing","Elicited hallucinations, thought interference and the other first-rank symptoms, and recorded a neologism and responding to voices","Asked the headache red-flag, head injury, seizure, fever and medication questions needed to exclude an organic cause","Took the substance, mood, prodrome, developmental and family history needed for the differential","Screened risk to self and others and explained the next steps in a way he agreed to"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Not here. They can hear what I think.\"","r":"\"That sounds really frightening. This is a private room, and I'm here to help, not to judge. Can you talk me through what's been going on?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You don't believe me, do you?\"","r":"\"I can see how real this is for you, and I won't pretend otherwise. I'm trying to understand it properly. Can I ask how sure you are, out of 100?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"It's zintered. The signal. Why don't you get it?\" (frustrated)","r":"\"I'm sorry, I don't know that word, and I want to get it right. Can you tell me what 'zintered' means?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"The voice behind me says don't trust you.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me that; I know it's hard with that voice there. Does it tell you to do anything? Is it saying anything now?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Am I going mad?\"","r":"\"No, you're not mad. I think your brain may be under a lot of strain, and that can make experiences feel very real. It's treatable, and the sooner we start, the better.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"Don't tell my mum. She'll freak.\"","r":"\"I understand you're worried about her reaction. She's here because she's worried about you. Can we decide together what I tell her, and you can be there?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, glucose, calcium, CRP; urine drug screen; consider HIV and syphilis tests; MRI if focal signs or atypical features; EEG if seizures suspected","No antipsychotic in A&E unless acutely disturbed; EIP specialist to start an oral antipsychotic with him and his family if psychosis persists (NICE CG155), with baseline weight, waist, pulse, BP, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin and ECG if indicated"],"psych":["Early intervention in psychosis referral today; CBT for psychosis and family intervention offered (NICE CG155)","Psychoeducation for Jayden and his mother about psychosis and recovery"],"social":["With consent, contact college about absence and a return plan","EIP care coordinator to support education and social links"],"safety":["Risk to others: no intent, no weapons; reassess if he names a target","Leaves ED with his mother only after the plan is agreed; crisis line and when to return","Discuss with on-call CAMHS consultant tonight; MHA assessment if he refuses help and risk rises"]},"facts":[{"h":"Test conviction, don't argue","x":"Ask how sure he is and whether anything could change his mind. A belief held at 100% despite contrary evidence, out of keeping with his culture, is a delusion."},{"h":"Neologisms: clarify and record","x":"Ask what the word means and write it down exactly. New words with private meaning suggest formal thought disorder."},{"h":"Headache red flags first","x":"Worse lying down or on waking, vomiting, focal signs, seizures, fever, confusion or papilloedema need imaging and medical review before a psychiatric label."},{"h":"Ask about synthetic cannabinoids","x":"Spice and other synthetic cannabinoids cause psychosis and are missed by standard urine screens. Ask about vapes and what was in them."},{"h":"EIP within two weeks","x":"Early intervention in psychosis teams take referrals from age 14; the standard is treatment started within two weeks of referral."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Establish psychotic symptoms and conviction, screen organic and substance causes, and assess risk in a 16-year-old."},"c-s-41":{"obj":["Found out what Mr Whitfield had noticed, what he knew and his main fears before explaining","Explained psychosis in plain words using Josh's symptoms, said the physical tests were normal and answered 'mad' and 'dangerous'","Described the treatment plan: low-dose antipsychotic, family intervention, CBT for psychosis and early intervention follow-up","Explained Section 2, its 28-day limit, the right to appeal, the IMHA and his rights as nearest relative","Checked understanding by teach-back and left leaflets, ward contacts and a family meeting date"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why am I talking to a psychiatrist? I thought he'd hit his head or something.\"","r":"That's a fair question. The medical team checked him carefully: bloods, a heart tracing and a brain scan are all normal. That's why we think this is coming from his mental health."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you saying he's mad?\"","r":"No. He's unwell, the way someone with a bad infection is unwell. His mind is struggling to tell what's real right now. It's an illness, and it's treatable."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is he dangerous? Should we be scared of him?\"","r":"I can see why you'd worry. Most young people with psychosis are frightened, not violent, and Josh is mainly a danger to himself tonight, like stepping into traffic. That's why we want him safe in hospital."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not having him locked up. I'll take him home and watch him.\"","r":"I know you want him home, and that shows how much you care. Tonight he ran into traffic and won't accept help. Hospital is the safest place for now, and it's for weeks, not for ever."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I don't want him zombified on drugs.\"","r":"Nor do we. We start with a low dose, increase slowly and check side effects every week. The aim is for Josh to feel like himself again, not sedated."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I thought he was just being a moody teenager. I should have seen it.\"","r":"That's what almost every parent thinks. It came on slowly and looked like normal teenage stuff. You've noticed things now, and that's helping him."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will he ever be normal again?\"","r":"Many young people recover well from a first episode, and starting treatment early helps a lot. Some have only one episode. The early intervention team will stay with him for up to three years."}],"bps":{"bio":["Organic screen complete: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, calcium, glucose, urine drug screen, ECG, CT head all normal.","On the ward, after assessment: aripiprazole (licensed from 15 for schizophrenia) 2 mg daily for 2 days, 5 mg for 2 days, then 10 mg daily; review at 4 to 6 weeks.","Baseline before the antipsychotic: weight, height, BMI, waist, pulse, BP, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin and a movement check; weight weekly for 6 weeks."],"psych":["Family intervention with Josh and both parents, at least 10 sessions, EIP family worker.","CBT for psychosis, at least 16 sessions, EIP or ward psychologist, once he can engage."],"social":["Ward liaises with school (education on the unit, then a return-to-school plan).","Parents offered a carer's assessment and Rethink or Mind carer support."],"safety":["1:1 nursing in A&E until the adolescent bed is confirmed.","AMHP consults the father as nearest relative; rights read to Josh under section 132 and IMHA offered.","Family meeting with the ward consultant within 7 days; CAMHS crisis line number given."]},"facts":[{"h":"Tests first, then psychiatry","x":"Every first episode needs bloods, a drug screen and, where indicated, brain imaging. Tell the family these are done; it answers 'why a psychiatrist?' and builds trust."},{"h":"Parents cannot consent at 16","x":"For a 16 or 17-year-old refusing, or lacking capacity where admission deprives liberty, parental consent is not enough. Use the Mental Health Act (Code of Practice, chapter 19)."},{"h":"Section 2 in one breath","x":"Up to 28 days for assessment and treatment. AMHP plus two doctors. Appeal to the Tribunal within 14 days. Nearest relative can ask for discharge with 72 hours' notice."},{"h":"Early intervention for three years","x":"EIP teams take people from 14 and offer up to three years of care: medicine, CBT, family work, education and work support. Treatment should start within two weeks of referral."},{"h":"Aripiprazole in teenagers","x":"Licensed for schizophrenia from 15: 2 mg, then 5 mg, then 10 mg daily over 4 days. Less weight gain and prolactin rise than many; watch for restlessness."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Mental Health Act process","do":"Explain psychosis, the treatment and early intervention plan and Section 2 to his father, checking understanding by teach-back."},"c-s-42":{"obj":["Seen Maya alone with confidentiality explained, and described her appearance, behaviour and speech as you observed them","Elicited elated mood, reduced sleep, grandiosity and flight of ideas, and checked hallucinations, passivity and past low mood","Assessed risk to herself, to other people and from other people, including suicidal thoughts and online contact","Managed overfamiliarity and irritability calmly while keeping the interview on track","Presented a concise MSE with insight, working diagnosis, differentials and risk to the consultant"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Are you married, doc? You've got kind eyes.\"","r":"Thanks, but I keep things about you, not me. That's how I work with everyone. You were telling me about your week?"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"You wouldn't understand my theory. It's way above your level.\"","r":"You might be right; physics isn't my field. I'm more interested in how it's been for you. When did the ideas start coming?"},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Why do you keep asking? You're just like my parents!\"","r":"I can hear I've annoyed you, and that's not my aim. Let's leave the theory. Tell me about sleep: how much have you had?"},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not ill. I've never felt better in my life.\"","r":"I believe you feel brilliant, and I'm glad you're not suffering. Your parents are worried because of the sleep and not eating. Can we look at that together?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"There's this guy online, he's 19, he wants to meet me tomorrow.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me; that's important. I'm worried, because you're 15 and he wants to meet. I'll need to share that to keep you safe, and I'll tell you what happens."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Am I going to be locked up?\"","r":"Nobody's decided anything yet. I'm going to talk to my consultant and your parents, and we'll explain the plan to you before anything happens."}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, glucose; pulse, BP, temperature; urine drug screen (negative).","No antidepressants. If treatment is needed, the consultant usually chooses an antipsychotic such as aripiprazole (licensed from 13 for mania, up to 12 weeks)."],"psych":["Collateral from parents on onset, prior low moods and family history, by the ward team."],"social":["School told of absence via parents; education on the unit if admitted."],"safety":["1:1 nursing in A&E; safeguarding discussion about the 19-year-old online contact tonight.","Parents asked to secure bank cards; consultant decides on admission tonight."]},"facts":[{"h":"Present in a set order","x":"Appearance and behaviour, speech, mood and affect, thought form and content, perception, cognition, insight, then risk and impression. Under 90 seconds."},{"h":"Rhyming is a sign, so name it","x":"Clang associations and puns show flight of ideas. Quote an example in your presentation: it proves you observed rather than guessed."},{"h":"Three-way risk","x":"To self (sleep, food, spending, suicide), to others (irritability, aggression), from others (sexual and online exploitation). Teenagers with mania are especially exposed online."},{"h":"Mania can hide despair","x":"Ask about low mood and suicidal thoughts every time. Mixed states and the crash after mania carry high suicide risk."},{"h":"No valproate for her","x":"MHRA (2024): do not start valproate in anyone under 55 unless two specialists document there is no other suitable option; in girls, avoid it."}],"type":"Mental state examination + Risk assessment","do":"Complete every mental state domain and a three-way risk check, then present it in under 90 seconds."},"c-s-43":{"obj":["Found out what Bethany remembered about ADHD and what she found hard at her class, in her own words","Explained what methylphenidate does, the start-low, increase-slowly plan and the move to a once-daily long-acting tablet","Explained why pulse, blood pressure and weight are checked before starting and every 6 months","Explained the ECG concretely, responded kindly to her fear and gained her agreement with a support person","Confirmed understanding by teach-back and gave an easy-read leaflet, side-effect advice and a follow-up date"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why have I come back so soon? I only came last month.\"","r":"Good question. Last time we found out you have ADHD. Today is about something that might help you at your cooking class. Is that okay?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What's stimulant? Is it drugs?\"","r":"It's a medicine, made for ADHD and given by doctors. It helps your brain focus. We start with a small amount and check you're okay."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I don't want wires on me. Will it hurt my heart?\"","r":"I can see that worries you. The stickers just listen to your heart, like a stethoscope. Nothing goes into you, and it doesn't hurt. Dawn can come with you."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why do you need to weigh me?\"","r":"This medicine can make you less hungry. Weighing you helps us check you're eating enough. If you lose weight, we'll change something."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How long do I have to take it?\"","r":"As long as it helps you. We'll check with you every few months. If it isn't helping, we stop it or try something else."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I'm stupid. I can't remember all this.\"","r":"You're not stupid at all. It's a lot of new information. I'll give you a leaflet with pictures, and we can go over it again next time."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What if it doesn't work?\"","r":"Then we try a different amount, or a different medicine. There are other choices. You won't be stuck with something that doesn't help."}],"bps":{"bio":["Before starting: pulse, BP, weight, height; ask about fainting, chest pain, breathlessness or palpitations; ECG because of family history of arrhythmia.","Methylphenidate immediate-release 5 mg at breakfast and lunchtime; increase by 5 to 10 mg a day each week by response; BNF adult maximum 100 mg a day. Last dose no later than early afternoon.","Once effective, switch to a once-daily modified-release tablet each morning to cover her 9-to-5 class.","Monitoring: pulse and BP at every dose change and every 6 months; weight at 3 and 6 months, then every 6 months. Warn: less appetite, poor sleep, headache, stomach ache, feeling jittery."],"psych":["ADHD psychoeducation in easy-read format by the CLDT nurse; course tutor to allow movement breaks."],"social":["Support worker helps with breakfast before the tablet and keeps tablets locked (controlled drug).","GP shared care once the dose is stable for at least 4 weeks."],"safety":["If the ECG is abnormal: cardiology opinion before any stimulant.","Phone review at 1 week; clinic review at 4 weeks; Bethany or Dawn to ring sooner for chest pain, fainting or a racing heart."]},"facts":[{"h":"ECG when family history says so","x":"NICE NG87: ECG before a stimulant if there is a personal or family history of serious heart disease, young sudden death, or abnormal heart findings. Otherwise not routine."},{"h":"Methylphenidate: start low","x":"Adult immediate-release: 5 mg two or three times a day, increase weekly by response. Switch to once-daily modified-release for all-day cover. Last dose early afternoon to protect sleep."},{"h":"Three checks, every six months","x":"Pulse, blood pressure and weight before starting, after each dose change and every 6 months. A sustained rise or weight loss means review the dose."},{"h":"Teach-back beats 'Do you understand?'","x":"Ask her to explain it back in her own words, in small chunks. If she can't, rephrase; the failure is in the explanation."},{"h":"Schedule 2 controlled drug","x":"Prescriptions are usually for up to 30 days. Keep tablets locked away, never share them, and carry a letter if travelling abroad."}],"type":"Explain a treatment + ECG and investigations","do":"Explain methylphenidate, the ECG and physical checks in plain words; confirm three key points by teach-back."},"c-s-44":{"obj":["Confirmed consent and learned what Hannah knew, what she had noticed, and whether there were threats, a real affair or children","Explained morbid jealousy in plain words, including its cause, treatment and the honest outlook","Stated clearly the risk to Hannah and to the colleague, using the past assault, the knife and the stalking","Explained why admission is needed now, under the Mental Health Act if he refuses, and why tablets at home are not enough","Discussed separation honestly and agreed a safety plan with somewhere to stay, a helpline, an IDVA referral and follow-up"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"He's not ill. He's just a bit jealous. Every man is.\"","r":"A bit of jealousy is normal. This is different: he's certain you're unfaithful because of one photo, and nothing you say changes it. That certainty is the illness."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"The knife thing was years ago. He was stressed.\"","r":"I understand you want to see the best in him. That attack happened when he held this same belief. That's exactly why I'm so concerned today."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can't you just give him his tablets back and let me take him home?\"","r":"I wish it were that simple. The tablets take weeks to work, he says he won't take them, and he's carrying a knife. Home isn't safe yet, for you or him."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Should I leave him? I love him.\"","r":"That's your decision, and I can hear how much you love him. Some couples do well once treatment works. If it doesn't, living apart is sometimes the only safe option. For now, please don't be alone with him."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"He reads my messages every night. He says it's because he loves me.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me. I know it feels like love, but checking and tracking is part of the illness, and it's also controlling. You deserve support with that."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will he get better?\"","r":"Many people improve with treatment, and he did well before. But this can come back, so he'll need long-term follow-up, and we'll keep an eye out for warning signs together."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"So you're going to section him? That's going to destroy him.\"","r":"I can hear how upsetting that is. He's refused every other option, and the risk is serious. It's to keep him and others safe and treat him, and he can appeal."}],"bps":{"bio":["Restart risperidone (previous good response): 2 mg on day 1, 4 mg on day 2, usual 4 to 6 mg a day; check prolactin, weight, glucose, lipids and ECG.","Ask about sexual side effects: raised prolactin can lower sex drive, which may feed the jealousy.","Screen for alcohol and drug use and treat if present."],"psych":["Structured violence risk assessment (HCR-20) on the ward, with forensic advice.","CBT for the jealous beliefs once he is less fixed; couple work only when the risk is low and Hannah wants it."],"social":["Hannah offered a carer's assessment and an IDVA referral.","Ward social worker plans a gradual return to the community, with where he lives agreed with Hannah."],"safety":["Police informed of the knife and the risk to the named colleague.","Hannah stays with her sister tonight; DASH checklist completed; MARAC referral if high risk.","National Domestic Abuse Helpline (Refuge) 0808 2000 247, 24 hours; 999 if in danger.","Ward phones Hannah within 48 hours; she is told before any leave or discharge."]},"facts":[{"h":"It's a delusion, not a feeling","x":"Morbid (delusional) jealousy is an unshakeable false belief of infidelity built on trivial evidence. Checking phones, underwear and times are typical. A real affair would not rule it out."},{"h":"Past violence predicts the future","x":"A previous attack driven by the same belief, plus a weapon and stalking the rival, makes this high risk. Say it plainly; this is what partners most need to hear."},{"h":"Two people at risk","x":"The partner and the suspected rival. Share with the police to protect the rival (GMC public interest); you need no consent where there is a risk of serious harm."},{"h":"Separation is sometimes the treatment","x":"If treatment fails, living apart may be the only safe option. The belief often fades when the relationship ends, but can return with a new partner."},{"h":"Risperidone and the jealousy loop","x":"Raised prolactin can lower libido; reduced intimacy can feed his suspicion. Ask about sexual side effects and check prolactin."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Safety plan","do":"Explain morbid jealousy, its risk and the admission plan to his partner, and agree her safety plan for tonight."},"c-s-45":{"obj":["Presented a structured summary with a one-line opener, history, MSE and risk in under 2 minutes","Named delusional disorder, erotomanic type, with five supporting features and four differentials","Listed additional sources of information and how you would get them","Proposed an immediate plan covering medical clearance, safe setting, consent and the Mental Health Act","Proposed a long-term biopsychosocial plan with risk assessment, police liaison and protection of both people at risk"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You've been talking for two minutes. What's your impression?\"","r":"Sorry, I'll cut to it. I think this is erotomania, a delusional disorder, with a high risk to her employer and his wife."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What type of delusional disorder?\"","r":"Erotomanic: she's certain a higher-status man secretly loves her, despite rejection, and she blames his wife for blocking it."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why not schizophrenia?\"","r":"Her belief is circumscribed, her mood and thinking are otherwise normal, and there are no hallucinations or negative symptoms. Delusional disorder fits better, but we'll watch for evolving symptoms."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What else do you need to know?\"","r":"Police records and the warning, her GP summary, her sister's account and her work record. I'd also want thyroid tests and a drug screen tonight."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"She's calm and polite. Why not discharge to the community team?\"","r":"I understand she seems settled. But she took a knife to their home, she blames his wife, and she has no insight. Calm doesn't mean safe here."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Her sister's phoned twice, in tears, asking if Rachel will lose her job.\"","r":"That's hard for the family. With Rachel's consent I'll ring her sister after this, and once she's settled, occupational health and her union can advise on work."}],"bps":{"bio":["Medical clearance: TFT (on levothyroxine), FBC, U&E, LFT, glucose, urine drug screen, alcohol level if indicated.","Antipsychotic for delusional disorder, e.g. risperidone 1 to 2 mg at night, increasing to 4 mg over 1 to 2 weeks; check prolactin, weight, glucose, lipids, ECG.","Advise stopping alcohol; offer support if dependent."],"psych":["Stalking risk assessment (Stalking Risk Profile) and HCR-20 on the ward, with forensic psychology.","CBT for delusions once engaged (limited evidence); psychoeducation."],"social":["No contact with Mr Hale or his family; consider a Stalking Protection Order (police).","Occupational health and her union on work; ward social worker for finances and benefits while suspended.","CMHT follow-up after discharge, with a named care coordinator and police liaison."],"safety":["Tonight: 1:1 nursing with security; knife retained by police; informal admission offered; MHA assessment if refused.","Police told of the risk to Mr Hale and his wife; reasons recorded.","Forensic on-call advice by morning; Mr Hale's family told via police before any leave or discharge."]},"facts":[{"h":"Present in a set structure","x":"One-line opener with the risk, then history, MSE, impression, differentials, risk and plan. Under 2 minutes. Consultants stop rambling presenters."},{"h":"Five features of erotomania","x":"Fixed belief of being loved, object of higher status or out of reach, rejection reinterpreted, pursuit or stalking, no insight. Name them; that is what earns the diagnosis."},{"h":"The rival is often the target","x":"Violence in erotomania is often aimed at people seen as blocking the romance, here his wife. Include them in the risk plan."},{"h":"Delusional disorder vs schizophrenia","x":"Delusional disorder: a single, circumscribed delusion for at least 3 months, otherwise normal functioning, no prominent hallucinations or thought disorder."},{"h":"FTAC is for public figures","x":"The Fixated Threat Assessment Centre handles threats to public figures. For a private victim, work with local police and consider a Stalking Protection Order."}],"type":"Handover and escalation + Management plan","do":"Present the case in under 2 minutes, justify erotomania with differentials, and propose an immediate and long-term risk plan."},"c-s-46":{"obj":["Elicited the delusional perception, the grandiose mission and referential ideas, and screened for other delusions","Characterised his voices (person, content, commands obeyed) and thought insertion and withdrawal, and linked them to the fire","Screened mood for elation and assessed insight","Established the fire's motive, planning, ignition source, location choice and intent to repeat, plus absconding, self-harm and medication risks","Agreed eyesight observations, a search for ignition sources and same-day RC review with the nurse in charge"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I've told the nurses already. Why do you need to hear it again?\"","r":"\"Fair enough, it's tiring repeating yourself. I'd rather hear it from you in your words than from the notes. I'll keep it as short as I can.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"It's the device in my tooth. It tells me when to move.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me that. Tell me more about the device: when did you first notice it, and what does it tell you?\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"There's nothing wrong with me. You're the ones keeping me from my work.\"","r":"\"I can hear how real and urgent this feels to you. I see it differently: I think an illness is giving you these experiences. Can we agree to disagree for now and keep talking?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I won't take tablets. They'll block the signal.\"","r":"\"I understand why you'd want to protect it. The medicine is meant to ease the pressure and the voices. I'll talk with your consultant today, and you'll hear the plan before anything changes.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Are the police going to get involved?\"","r":"\"It's a fair question and I won't pretend I know yet. The team will decide that with your consultant. Right now my job is keeping you and everyone here safe.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If you watch me all the time, I'll never finish the mission.\"","r":"\"I can see that feels like a big setback. Staff staying close is to keep you safe, not to punish you. We'll review it every day as things settle.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Physical check now: burns, airway, sats; repeat obs in 1 hour","RC to agree antipsychotic today, e.g. olanzapine 10 mg at night (range 5–20 mg), under Section 63 if he declines after a proper attempt at consent","Baseline bloods and ECG if not done on admission (FBC, U&E, LFT, glucose, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin); urine drug screen"],"psych":["Daily one-to-one with named nurse to monitor command voices and intent to repeat"],"social":["RC or named nurse to update his parents with his consent; ward manager to review how the lighter got in"],"safety":["Eyesight observations, reviewed at least daily by the MDT","Search of person and room for ignition sources, with explanation and record; escorted smoking only","Incident report today; RC and nurse in charge to decide police reporting and PICU referral"]},"facts":[{"h":"Ask the fire question last","x":"Understand the beliefs first; then motive, planning, ignition source, location, aftermath and repeat intent. Asked early, 'why did you do it?' makes people defensive."},{"h":"Commands he obeys raise risk","x":"Ask what the voices say word for word, whether he resists, and whether he has acted on them before. Obeyed commands plus no insight is a high-risk mix."},{"h":"Grandiosity means screen mania","x":"A world-changing mission can be mania with psychotic features. Ask about mood, sleep, energy, speed of thoughts and spending before settling on schizophrenia."},{"h":"Where did the lighter come from?","x":"Most ward fires use a lighter brought in through smoking areas, visitors or leave. The answer drives the search plan and the incident review."}],"type":"Mental state examination + Risk assessment","do":"Elicit the psychosis behind the fire, rate repeat-fire and absconding risk, and start ward safety measures today."},"c-s-47":{"obj":["Checked post-restraint safety and the victim's care before starting","Completed an MSE that described the persecutory delusions (content, conviction, plans), perceptions, mood and insight","Explored the assault: trigger, intent, weapon, remorse and intent to repeat","Gathered historical factors: past violence and prison, illness course, PICU, adherence, substances, social situation","Agreed enhanced observations, separation from the victim, an oral medicine offer and same-day RC review"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You lot dragged me here. Now you want me to talk?\"","r":"\"I can see you're angry, and being brought in at night would shake anyone. I'm not police. I just want to hear what happened from you.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"He was an assassin. You could hear it in his voice.\"","r":"\"That sounds terrifying, believing someone came to kill you. Tell me what you heard in his voice that told you that.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Are you saying I'm imagining it?\"","r":"\"No, I'm saying I want to understand it. It's clearly very real to you. I see things differently, but you can tell me anything.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not having your memory serum again.\"","r":"\"I hear you; being held and injected before sounds awful. I'd like to offer you a tablet to ease the fear, and you can ask me anything about it first.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"If another one comes near me, I'll finish the job.\"","r":"\"Thank you for being honest; that helps me keep everyone safe, you included. Staff will stay close so nobody can get near you without us.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So what now, am I getting locked up somewhere worse?\"","r":"\"Fair question. I'll talk to your consultant today about the safest place for you. I'll tell you what we decide, and why.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Offer oral olanzapine 10 mg now (max 20 mg/day) or oral lorazepam 1–2 mg; RT plan per NICE NG10 if de-escalation and oral fail","ECG before any IM haloperidol; after any IM, obs with NEWS2 at least hourly until settled, every 15 min if he is sedated or was harmed in restraint (NICE NG10)","Urine drug screen; FBC, U&E, LFT, CK after restraint"],"psych":["Debrief him after the restraint; nurse-led one-to-one time twice a shift"],"social":["Victim supported by nurse in charge, offered police contact and a separate debrief"],"safety":["Enhanced obs within eyesight; nursed away from the main corridor","RC informed today; PICU referral if risk persists","Incident report; risk assessment and formulation updated before the end of the shift"]},"facts":[{"h":"Past violence predicts future violence","x":"Previous violence, especially with convictions, is the single strongest historical predictor. Always ask about police, courts and prison."},{"h":"Calm is not safe","x":"If the belief that drove the attack is unchanged and he would act again, the risk is still high, however settled he looks now."},{"h":"Ask about intent, not just the act","x":"'What did you want to happen?' separates a push to escape from an attempt to kill. Intent, weapon use and remorse shape the risk level."},{"h":"Check yourself and the room","x":"Sit nearer the exit with a nurse present, know where your alarm is, and stop the interview if he escalates."}],"type":"Mental state examination + Risk assessment","do":"Complete his MSE and a historical-clinical-situational violence risk assessment, then agree immediate ward safety measures."},"c-s-48":{"obj":["Checked her understanding of schizophrenia and the role of cannabis, and filled the gaps","Explained the discharge plan: accommodation, clozapine monitoring, care coordinator, drug service","Built Callum's relapse signature with her (early, middle, late signs) and named his risk factors","Asked about her health, support, visits, weapons at home and her own feelings towards him","Agreed a written three-step crisis plan with named numbers, and checked she could use it"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"It's schizophrenia, I know. But will it ever go away?\"","r":"\"That's the question most parents ask. For most people it's long term, like diabetes, but it can be kept well controlled. The key is staying on clozapine even when he feels completely well.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I knew he was smoking that stuff. I should have stopped him.\"","r":"\"It's natural to look back and wish you'd done something. You couldn't have controlled it. What you can do now is help us spot changes early.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"What if he decides I'm poisoning him again?\"","r":"\"That's a very real fear after what happened. If he refuses food you've made or talks about poison, that's a late warning sign. Leave, and ring us or 999 straight away.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I still jump when a door slams. I can't sleep.\"","r":"\"I'm so sorry. That sounds like the shock of the assault is still with you. Can we talk about getting you some support too? Your GP can refer you for talking therapy.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can he come home for Sunday dinner? Just once?\"","r":"\"I can hear how much you want normal life back. Let's start with visits where he lives, with staff nearby, and review home visits at his first CPA meeting.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"How do I know your team will actually answer the phone?\"","r":"\"Fair, and I'd rather you tell me now. I'll give you the care coordinator's direct line, the duty number and the crisis line, so there's always a second number.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Clozapine 400 mg a day continued; FBC monitoring (monthly after the first year) through the clozapine clinic","Clozapine level if he starts or stops smoking tobacco, or misses doses; more than 48 hours missed means re-titration, never restart at full dose","Constipation check at every review (clozapine-induced gastrointestinal hypomotility)"],"psych":["Relapse prevention work with care coordinator, using his written relapse signature","Family intervention (NICE CG178), 10 or more sessions over 3 months to 1 year, with Callum and his mother","Mother: GP referral to NHS Talking Therapies for trauma symptoms"],"social":["24-hour supported accommodation; staff given his relapse signature and crisis plan","Care coordinator (CMHT) visits weekly for the first month; community drug and alcohol service with urine testing by agreement","Carer's assessment for his mother through the local authority (Care Act 2014)"],"safety":["Visits at the accommodation, staff told beforehand, short and daytime, for the first 3 months","Crisis plan: early signs, care coordinator; worse, crisis line or 111 mental health option; danger, leave and call 999","Home visits reviewed at the first CPA meeting; MDT reviews the plan if any warning sign appears"]},"facts":[{"h":"Smoking changes clozapine levels","x":"Tobacco smoke speeds clozapine breakdown. Stopping smoking can push levels up into toxicity within days; restarting can drop them. Tell the team about any change."},{"h":"48 hours missed means restart slowly","x":"If clozapine is missed for more than 48 hours, it must be re-titrated from a low dose. Taking his usual dose after a gap can cause collapse or seizures."},{"h":"Families see the first signs","x":"Ask what she noticed first last time. Those early, personal changes are often the most useful part of a relapse signature."},{"h":"Ask the carer about her own feelings","x":"After a serious assault, ask gently whether she has ever thought of harming him. It's part of a full risk picture, and most parents are relieved to be asked."},{"h":"Carers have their own rights","x":"Under the Care Act 2014 any carer can ask the local authority for a carer's assessment. Give her the form or the number."}],"type":"Family meeting + Safety plan","do":"With consent, agree his relapse signature, risk factors and a written three-step crisis plan with his mother."},"c-s-49":{"obj":["Explained what delirium tremens is, why it happens and why it is a medical emergency","Justified the diagnosis from the collateral history, timing of the last drink and his signs, and named the differentials","Set out the plan: oral lorazepam, Pabrinex IV before glucose, IV fluids and electrolytes","Agreed monitoring, nursing care, an interpreter and clear escalation triggers with the nurse","Checked her understanding and outlined follow-up with the alcohol care team"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Sorry, I've never heard of DTs. Is it just bad withdrawal?\"","r":"\"Good question, and lots of people haven't. It's the most severe form of withdrawal: withdrawal plus delirium. That's why it's an emergency, not just a rough hangover.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Could you say that bit about timing again? I lost you.\"","r":"\"Of course. Withdrawal starts within hours of the last drink. DTs usually comes later, two to four days after. He'd been on the floor about two days, so the timing fits.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Shouldn't we just give him haloperidol? He's seeing things.\"","r":"\"It's a fair thought, because it works in other delirium. Here the hallucinations come from withdrawal, so lorazepam treats the cause. Haloperidol can lower the fit threshold, so we'd only add it if lorazepam isn't enough.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"What if he fits on my shift?\"","r":"\"That's a sensible worry. Put him in the recovery position, protect his head, time it, give oxygen and put out the emergency call. A fit over five minutes needs IV lorazepam and the medical team at once.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I use the CIWA score on him?\"","r":"\"Good thinking, but CIWA relies on him answering questions, which he can't while delirious. Use obs, NEWS2 and a sedation score instead, and tell us if anything changes.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"His neighbour speaks Gujarati. Can she translate?\"","r":"\"She's been really helpful, and she can help reassure him. For medical questions and consent we need a professional interpreter, so nothing gets lost and his privacy is kept.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Oral lorazepam per trust alcohol withdrawal protocol (often 1–2 mg, repeated hourly as needed), titrated to calm and rousable; parenteral lorazepam if oral refused (NICE CG100)","Pabrinex IV 2 pairs three times daily for 3–5 days (suspected Wernicke's), given before or with any glucose; then oral thiamine 100 mg three times a day","IV fluids for dehydration and raised CK; daily U&E, CK, Mg, PO4, K, glucose; replace Mg and PO4 if low","Antipsychotic (e.g. haloperidol) only as an add-on if lorazepam fails, with ECG; never alone"],"psych":["Once delirium clears: brief intervention and motivational interviewing by the alcohol care team; discuss acamprosate or naltrexone after detox"],"social":["Professional Gujarati interpreter for assessment and every key decision","Alcohol care team before discharge; referral to community alcohol service; ask about living alone and falls"],"safety":["Side room close to the nurses' desk, lit, clock visible, same nurses where possible, one-to-one observation","Obs and NEWS2 at least hourly, sedation score, glucose 4-hourly, fluid balance; falls and seizure precautions","Escalate to the medical team and consider HDU for a seizure, NEWS2 5 or more, oversedation or failure to settle"]},"facts":[{"h":"DTs usually starts day 2 to 4","x":"Withdrawal begins within hours of the last drink; delirium tremens typically appears 48 to 96 hours later. A long lie or admission is a common hidden stop date."},{"h":"Lorazepam first for DTs","x":"NICE CG100: oral lorazepam is first line for delirium tremens. If symptoms persist or he refuses oral, give parenteral lorazepam, haloperidol or olanzapine."},{"h":"Thiamine before glucose","x":"In a confused drinker, give Pabrinex IV before or with glucose, never after a delay: glucose without thiamine can tip him into Wernicke's. Don't withhold glucose if he is hypoglycaemic."},{"h":"CIWA-Ar needs an answering patient","x":"Withdrawal scales rely on self-report. In delirium, use obs, NEWS2 and a sedation score to guide benzodiazepine doses."},{"h":"Long lie means check CK","x":"Two days on the floor risks rhabdomyolysis and kidney injury. Daily CK and U&E, and IV fluids to keep urine output up."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Management plan","do":"Explain to the nurse how DTs was diagnosed and agree treatment, monitoring and escalation triggers she can follow."},"c-s-50":{"obj":["Took a GBL history covering dose, frequency, dosing pattern, tolerance and previous collapses","Identified dependence and current early withdrawal, and escalated for urgent treatment","Covered other drugs, alcohol and injecting, and asked directly about intent and suicidal thoughts","Assessed chemsex risks: condom use, consent, BBV testing, PrEP and the PEP window","Agreed a plan: withdrawal treatment, same-day sexual health review, chemsex support referral and harm reduction"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I'm so sorry. I'm taking up a bed someone actually needs.\"","r":"\"You don't need to apologise. You were seriously unwell and this is exactly what the bed is for. I'd like to understand what happened, so it doesn't happen again.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"It was a party. I don't really want to go into it.\"","r":"\"That's understandable; it feels private. I ask everyone the same questions, nothing will shock me, and it stays confidential. It really helps me keep you safe.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will this get back to my firm?\"","r":"\"I can see that's a big worry. This stays between you and the health team. I'd only share more widely if someone was at serious risk, and I'd talk to you first.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not addicted. I just use a bit at night to sleep.\"","r":"\"Thanks for being honest about that. Needing it to sleep, and feeling shaky without it, are signs your body has come to rely on it. That's important to know, because stopping suddenly can be dangerous.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I can't remember the end of the night. I don't know what happened.\"","r":"\"That must be frightening. You don't have to decide anything now. If something happened you didn't agree to, there's a specialist centre that can help, and you choose whether police are involved.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"I can't enjoy sex without G. Is there actually help for that?\"","r":"\"Yes, and asking is a big step. Chemsex support services and psychosexual therapy help people rebuild sex without drugs. I can refer you today.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["GBL withdrawal: medical team to start diazepam per trust protocol, often higher doses than for alcohol, titrated to symptoms with hourly obs; addiction specialist advice today; baclofen only on specialist advice","Same-day sexual health review: HIV PEP (within 72 h of exposure), HIV, hepatitis B and C and syphilis tests, hepatitis B vaccination, discuss PrEP","Bloods: U&E, LFT, CK; ECG"],"psych":["Chemsex support service (sexual health clinic or LGBT drug service): structured psychosocial intervention","Psychosexual therapy to rebuild sex without drugs","Brief motivational work by liaison before discharge"],"social":["GP letter with his consent","Needle and syringe programme details; harm-reduction leaflet on G and methamphetamine","Discuss fitness to work and sick note if withdrawing; disclosure to employer is his choice"],"safety":["Hourly obs during withdrawal; escalate for agitation, confusion, hallucinations or seizure","Harm reduction: never with alcohol or other depressants, measure with an oral syringe, at least 2 hours between doses, never use alone, put people in recovery position and call 999","SARC information given; liaison review tomorrow before discharge"]},"facts":[{"h":"G plus alcohol stops breathing","x":"GBL and GHB have a tiny margin between high and coma. Alcohol, benzodiazepines or opioids make that margin even smaller. Most G deaths involve a second depressant."},{"h":"Round-the-clock use means dependence","x":"Dosing every few hours, dosing to sleep or on waking, and shakes without it mean physical dependence. Withdrawal can begin within hours and become severe."},{"h":"GBL withdrawal can kill","x":"Tremor, sweating, anxiety and tachycardia can progress to delirium, psychosis and seizures within a day or two. Treat early with benzodiazepines and expert advice."},{"h":"PEP is time-limited","x":"HIV post-exposure prophylaxis should start as soon as possible and within 72 hours of condomless sex. Get him to sexual health or the ED the same day."},{"h":"Ask about consent sensitively","x":"Unconsciousness at chemsex parties carries a real risk of non-consensual sex. Ask gently and offer SARC; he decides about police."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"Take a GBL-focused history, identify dependence and withdrawal, assess suicide and chemsex risks, and agree a plan."},"c-s-51":{"obj":["Established the core depressive symptoms and their six-week duration","Quantified his drinking in units and confirmed the features of dependence, including complicated withdrawal","Set out which came first: heavy drinking before the low mood, with no sober period since","Assessed suicide and other alcohol-related risks, including his river plan and the kitchen fire","Told him the likely diagnosis in plain words and why mood is reassessed after 3 to 4 weeks sober"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I came here for depression, not to be lectured about beer.\"","r":"That's fair, and I'm not here to lecture. Your low mood is real. I ask about drink because it can cause low mood too, and I want to get your treatment right."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"Everyone has a few pints. I'm not an alcoholic.\"","r":"I'm not putting a label on you. Let's just look at the numbers together, and what happens when you stop. Then we can both see where things are."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why won't you just give me antidepressants?\"","r":"I understand you want something that works. While you drink this much, they tend not to help, and the low mood often lifts once the drink is out of the picture. We'll check again then."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Last time I stopped I saw things crawling on the wall.\"","r":"That sounds terrifying, and I'm glad you told me. It means your body reacts badly to stopping suddenly, so we'll plan it safely with medical support, not on your own."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Some nights I think I'll get really drunk and go in the river.\"","r":"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. It tells me how much pain you're in. Can we talk about it properly now, so we can keep you safe?"},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"My wife left because of me. I shout at her over nothing.\"","r":"You clearly care about her, and that guilt is heavy. Drink and low mood both shorten tempers. Getting well gives you the best chance of putting things right."}],"bps":{"bio":["No antidepressant today; reassess depressive symptoms 3 to 4 weeks after abstinence (NICE CG115).","Inpatient medically assisted withdrawal: past probable delirium tremens and suicide risk; chlordiazepoxide by symptom-triggered or fixed regimen on the unit.","Oral thiamine before and during withdrawal; IV Pabrinex on the unit if any sign of Wernicke's encephalopathy.","Bloods today: FBC, LFTs with GGT, U&Es, glucose, clotting; check BP (hypertension)."],"psych":["Motivational interviewing at each contact; relapse prevention with the community alcohol team after withdrawal.","Offer mutual aid: Alcoholics Anonymous or SMART Recovery."],"social":["With consent, involve his wife; offer her support and signpost Al-Anon.","GP fit note; occupational health contact with his consent to protect his job."],"safety":["Same-day crisis team assessment; consultant informed before he leaves clinic.","Written safety plan: crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, 999 if he goes to the river.","Do not cook after drinking; fire service Safe and Well visit for smoke alarms."]},"facts":[{"h":"Treat the drink first","x":"NICE CG115: in alcohol misuse with depression or anxiety, treat the drinking first. If symptoms persist after 3 to 4 weeks of abstinence, assess and treat them in their own right."},{"h":"Count units every time","x":"Units = volume (ml) x ABV% / 1000. A pint of 4% lager is about 2.3 units. Five pints a day is around 80 units a week; the low-risk limit is 14."},{"h":"ICD-11 dependence: two of three","x":"Impaired control, drinking taking priority over other things, and physiological signs (tolerance, withdrawal). Two or more, usually over 12 months, or 3 months if near-daily."},{"h":"Past DTs means supervised withdrawal","x":"NICE: consider inpatient assisted withdrawal if there were past withdrawal fits or delirium tremens, high suicide risk, or over 30 units a day."},{"h":"AUDIT 20 or more","x":"The 10-question AUDIT scores out of 40. A score of 20 or more suggests possible dependence; follow with a severity tool such as SADQ."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"Take a timeline history separating alcohol dependence from depression, and assess his suicide risk before he leaves."},"c-s-52":{"obj":["Confirmed identity, gained consent in simple words, offered a chaperone and checked for pain","Assessed posture, gait, heel-to-toe walking and Romberg's test while keeping him safe from falls","Tested speech, eye movements, tone, rapid alternating movements, finger-nose, reflexes and heel-shin on both sides","Adapted to his memory loss by demonstrating and repeating each instruction","Interpreted the findings, recognised possible Wernicke's encephalopathy and arranged same-day IV thiamine"],"snip":[{"tag":"Confabulation","p":"\"I'm only here to drop off this letter for the manager.\"","r":"Thanks for bringing it. While you're here, I'd like to check your balance and coordination. Is that alright with you?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What's all this for? I'm fine.\"","r":"You may well feel fine. Your keyworker mentioned some falls, and these tests show me how your balance is working, so we can keep you safe."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I don't want to close my eyes. I'll fall.\"","r":"That's a sensible worry. I'm right next to you and Sam is on the other side. We'll catch you. Just for a few seconds."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Did I do it wrong? I can't remember what you said.\"","r":"You haven't done anything wrong. That's my fault for rushing. Let me show you again, nice and slowly."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going to any hospital. I've got things to do.\"","r":"I hear you, and I know hospitals aren't welcome. Your eyes and balance tell me your body is short of a vitamin, and a drip today can stop your memory getting worse. Can we talk it through?"}],"bps":{"bio":["Suspected Wernicke's: IV Pabrinex, usually 2 pairs of ampoules three times a day for 3 to 5 days (BNF and local protocol), given where anaphylaxis can be treated.","Then oral thiamine long term; check magnesium and correct it, as thiamine works poorly when magnesium is low.","Bloods: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, glucose, magnesium, phosphate, B12, folate; MRI brain via the medical team."],"psych":["Cognitive assessment (for example ACE-III) once treated and abstinent, by the alcohol service or memory team."],"social":["Keyworker to arrange physiotherapy and occupational therapy falls assessment and check home safety.","Local authority care needs assessment under the Care Act 2014 if memory loss persists."],"safety":["Same-day transfer to the acute medical team, accompanied; consultant informed.","Falls risk flagged to keyworker and GP."]},"facts":[{"h":"Wernicke's: treat on suspicion","x":"Any one of confusion, eye signs or ataxia in a heavy drinker warrants IV thiamine. All three together appear only in a minority, so do not wait for the full triad."},{"h":"Romberg tests position sense","x":"Steady with eyes open but falling when they close is a positive Romberg: a position-sense (proprioceptive) problem, often neuropathy. Cerebellar patients are unsteady even with eyes open."},{"h":"Alcohol hits the midline","x":"Alcohol-related cerebellar degeneration mainly damages the vermis: wide-based gait and heel-shin worse than arms; speech and eyes often less affected."},{"h":"DANISH: the cerebellar signs","x":"Dysdiadochokinesis, Ataxia, Nystagmus, Intention tremor, Slurred or scanning speech, Heel-shin and hypotonia. Use it to make sure nothing is left out."},{"h":"Pabrinex and anaphylaxis","x":"Serious allergic reactions to IV Pabrinex are rare; give it where resuscitation equipment is available. Risk of untreated Wernicke's is far greater."}],"type":"Physical examination","do":"Complete a cerebellar examination on both sides, adapting for memory loss, and act on signs of Wernicke's."},"c-s-53":{"obj":["Linked the return of symptoms to stopping olanzapine, and found out why he stopped","Quantified his cannabis use and its part in past episodes","Checked risk to himself and others, and who supports him at home","Agreed an antipsychotic plan that addresses his erection problems, with side effects explained","Agreed cannabis advice, a drug service referral, crisis team input and a review within a week"],"snip":[{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"It's embarrassing. I couldn't... you know, in bed.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me; that's really common and it matters. It's a side effect we can do something about, so it's important we plan around it."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can I keep smoking a bit, just in the evenings?\"","r":"I understand it feels like it helps. Honestly, even a little is likely to make the voice worse. Our drug service can help with sleep and irritability while you stop."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going into hospital.\"","r":"I hear you, and I don't think you need to. You're here, you want help, and you'll take medicine. We can treat this at home with extra support."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I keep thinking they're trying to get me sacked.\"","r":"That must be frightening to sit with at work. This is the illness coming back, the same as before. The medicine usually eases that fear within days."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Do I have to take it forever?\"","r":"Not this one, if it doesn't suit you. Let's get you well first, then we switch to one that's kinder, and talk about long-term plans when you're settled."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Weed helps me sleep. Your tablets ruined my sex life.\"","r":"Both of those are fair points, and I'm not dismissing them. Let's find a plan that gets your sleep and your sex life back, without the voice."}],"bps":{"bio":["Olanzapine 10 mg at night (his effective dose; range 5-20 mg); warn about drowsiness and increased appetite.","Planned switch to aripiprazole 10 mg each morning once settled (range 10-30 mg): less sexual dysfunction and prolactin rise; warn about restlessness and impulse-control problems (MHRA).","Baseline: weight, BMI, waist, BP, pulse, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin; repeat weight weekly for 6 weeks, bloods at 12 weeks.","If fear is severe: lorazepam 1 mg up to twice daily for no more than 5 days."],"psych":["Brief motivational work on cannabis at each contact; drug service when he is ready.","CBT for psychosis (at least 16 sessions) once settled, psychology team."],"social":["Care coordinator weekly; crisis team home visits, daily if needed, this week.","Fit note for a week; with consent, his mother stays with him in the evenings."],"safety":["Crisis line and NHS 111 option 2; 999 or A&E if he feels unsafe.","Review within a week; earlier if beliefs extend to harming anyone."]},"facts":[{"h":"Ask why they stopped","x":"Sexual dysfunction, weight gain and sedation are leading hidden reasons for stopping antipsychotics. Name them: patients rarely volunteer erection problems."},{"h":"Daily strong cannabis and psychosis","x":"Daily use of high-potency cannabis is linked with around five times the odds of psychosis. Continued use after a first episode predicts more relapses and admissions."},{"h":"Smoke speeds up olanzapine","x":"Tobacco smoke, including joints with tobacco, induces CYP1A2 and lowers olanzapine levels. If he stops smoking, levels rise: watch for drowsiness."},{"h":"Aripiprazole spares prolactin","x":"Aripiprazole rarely raises prolactin and causes less sexual dysfunction. MHRA warns of impulse-control problems such as gambling; ask about them."},{"h":"Benzodiazepines: days, not weeks","x":"A short course can ease fear in relapse. Limit to a few days and say so at the start, because dependence develops quickly."}],"type":"Management plan + History to a diagnosis","do":"Link his relapse to stopping olanzapine and cannabis, then agree a short-term plan he accepts."},"c-s-54":{"obj":["Heard his key thoughts about the match, his career and his family in his own words","Named at least five thinking errors and explained each in plain language, checking he understood","Tested how firmly he holds the beliefs, confirming they are distortions, not delusions","Checked for suicidal thoughts and psychotic symptoms","Offered hope and agreed a first CBT step: a thought record before review"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I let everyone down. It was all on me.\"","r":"That sounds a crushing weight to carry. Can we look at the match together? You scored 22, and nobody else reached 10. How do you make sense of that?"},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"My average doesn't count. Anyone can score against weak sides.\"","r":"I notice you're quick to brush off the good bits. Low mood does that; it shrinks the positives. Would your coach agree those runs don't count?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So you're saying I'm making it up?\"","r":"Not at all. The feelings are completely real. I'm saying low mood bends how we see the facts. Spotting that is the first step to feeling better."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I'm a useless dad. They'd be better off without me moping around.\"","r":"I can hear how much you love them, which makes this so painful. Has your wife or anyone said you're failing them? And when you say better off without you, have you thought of ending your life?"},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Positive thinking won't win me matches.\"","r":"You're right, and I'm not asking you to pretend. I'm asking for balanced thinking: weigh all the evidence, good and bad, like a coach reviewing the whole innings."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I go back and fail again, that's it, I'm finished.\"","r":"That fear makes sense after a painful day. Notice the 'that's it': one innings deciding everything. What would you tell a teammate who said that?"}],"bps":{"psych":["CBT for depression through NHS Talking Therapies, weekly, high-intensity therapist.","Homework: daily thought record; behavioural activation, starting with a net session with one teammate.","PHQ-9 at each review to track change."],"social":["With consent, involve his wife; consider his club's welfare officer or player support scheme."],"safety":["No suicidal thoughts today; crisis line and NHS 111 option 2 given; review in 4 weeks."]},"facts":[{"h":"Distortion or delusion?","x":"Test with evidence. A cognitive distortion loosens when contradictory evidence is shown ('I might be wrong'). A delusion stays fixed despite clear evidence and is out of keeping with culture."},{"h":"Rate the belief, twice","x":"Ask 'How much do you believe it, 0 to 100?' before and after weighing evidence. A drop shows him, and you, that the belief can shift."},{"h":"Name it after he sees it","x":"Describe the pattern in his words first, then give the label: 'Doctors call that catastrophising.' Labels help him spot it again in homework."},{"h":"Chunk and check","x":"Give one idea, then check: 'Does that fit for you?' Low mood slows processing; long explanations are lost."},{"h":"CBT in NG222","x":"NICE NG222 lists individual CBT among first-line options for depression, alongside behavioural activation and guided self-help for less severe depression."}],"type":"Therapy assessment + Explain a diagnosis","do":"Identify at least five thinking errors in his words, test them gently, and explain each in plain language."},"c-s-55":{"obj":["Reviewed what she has already tried and why self-help and sertraline have not been enough","Asked directly about suicidal thoughts, past attempts and alcohol","Explained graded exposure and systematic desensitisation in plain words, including why anxiety falls if she stays","Built a first hierarchy with her, from imagining going out to the supermarket, rated 0 to 100","Agreed a plan: weekly home-based CBT, daily practice, her husband's role, a sertraline review and a plan if anxiety overwhelms her"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I've tried everything. Nothing works for me.\"","r":"After two years of trying, I can see why you feel that. The online course asked a lot of you on your own. This time a therapist does it with you, one small step at a time."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you going to make me walk to the shops today?\"","r":"No, definitely not. We start with something that feels only a little uncomfortable, maybe just thinking about the door. You set the pace."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Does desensitisation mean throwing me in at the deep end?\"","r":"Good question, and no. That's a different approach. This is gradual: small steps, each paired with relaxation, only moving on when one feels manageable."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I lost my job because of this. I'm useless.\"","r":"That's a real loss, and I'm sorry. It shows how strong this anxiety is, not that you're useless. Getting you out of the house is how we get you back to work."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Some mornings I think everyone would be better off without me.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me. That matters. Can I ask more: have you thought of ending your life, or made any plans?"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I can't come to the clinic. I just can't.\"","r":"I understand, and you don't have to yet. The therapist can start here at home. Getting to clinic can be one of the later steps on your list."}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline: increase from 50 mg to 100 mg each morning; review in 4 weeks; maximum 200 mg. Warn about a brief rise in anxiety or nausea after increase.","If no improvement after 12 weeks at an adequate dose, consider imipramine or clomipramine (NICE CG113). No benzodiazepines."],"psych":["Therapist-led CBT with graded exposure and relaxation: weekly, about 60 minutes, 7 to 14 hours in total over up to 4 months (NICE CG113), psychological therapies team.","First sessions at home, then on the doorstep and outside; daily practice of 30-60 minutes between sessions with a diary.","Relaxation training first: slow breathing and progressive muscle relaxation."],"social":["Husband as coach: accompanies early steps, then steps back; avoids reassurance and escape.","Later: employment adviser in the psychological therapies service to plan a return to work."],"safety":["Written safety plan: crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, duty number for an urgent call-back; 999 if at risk.","Plan for overwhelming panic during practice: slow breathing, stay if possible, phone her husband, note it for the next session.","Review in 4 weeks; care coordinator informed."]},"facts":[{"h":"The anxiety curve","x":"Stay in a feared situation and anxiety rises, peaks and falls by itself, usually within an hour. Repeating it lowers the peak each time (habituation). Leaving early resets it."},{"h":"Desensitisation adds relaxation","x":"Systematic desensitisation pairs each step with relaxation, often first in imagination. Graded exposure climbs the same ladder in real life, relying on habituation alone."},{"h":"Hierarchy: 0 to 100","x":"List 8 to 10 situations, rate each 0 to 100 for anxiety, and start around 30 to 40. Move up only when the current step falls to about half its starting rating."},{"h":"NICE: 7 to 14 hours of CBT","x":"CBT for panic disorder: weekly sessions of 1 to 2 hours, 7 to 14 hours in total, completed within 4 months."},{"h":"No benzodiazepines for panic","x":"NICE CG113 advises against benzodiazepines for panic disorder: poorer long-term outcomes, dependence, and they blunt the learning exposure relies on."}],"type":"Explain a treatment + Management plan","do":"Explain graded exposure and systematic desensitisation, build a rated hierarchy, and agree a home-based plan with her."},"c-s-56":{"obj":["Heard her reasons for stopping and ruled out practical barriers and problems with the therapist's conduct","Linked her feeling about the therapist to her father and her partner, using tentative language","Explained transference in plain words as a normal, useful part of psychodynamic therapy","Agreed she raises her wish to stop at tomorrow's session, with consent to email the therapist","Closed with a brief safety check, a safety net and a teach-back"],"snip":[{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"This is embarrassing. I'm a grown woman crying in A&E about my therapist.\"","r":"It takes courage to come and say it. Lots of people have strong feelings about therapy; that is not silly. What's been going on?"},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"She just sits there. Five minutes of silence. She's clearly bored of me.\"","r":"That sounds really uncomfortable to sit through. Silence can feel very loud. What goes through your mind about her in those moments?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can you just tell her I'm stopping? I can't face her.\"","r":"I can hear how hard facing her feels. I'm not going to take sides, but I'd like to understand first. Can we look at what makes it so hard?"},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"My dad leaving has nothing to do with this. I'm over that.\"","r":"You may be right, and you know your life best. I only wondered because the feeling sounds similar. What do you make of that idea?"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I tell her I think she doesn't care, she'll drop me.\"","r":"That fear makes complete sense given what you've lived through. Therapists expect to hear exactly this, and it often moves the work on. Would you test it with her tomorrow?"},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"So it's all in my head? Great.\"","r":"I'm sorry, that's not what I meant. Your feelings are real. I'm also checking whether she has done anything wrong, because sometimes she will have. Has she?"}],"bps":{"psych":["Continue weekly 50-minute psychodynamic psychotherapy (month 3 of 12) with her named therapist.","Therapist to work with the transference in session; if the fit is still wrong, discuss with the therapist's supervisor before ending."],"social":["Partner back from work trip tomorrow; she agrees to tell him she came to A&E."],"safety":["Brief check only: no self-harm or suicidal thoughts tonight.","Safety net: therapy service duty line in hours; GP; NHS 111, option 2, out of hours."]},"facts":[{"h":"Transference is the work","x":"In psychodynamic therapy, feelings carried over from earlier relationships onto the therapist are expected and are used to understand old patterns. A wish to leave is often the moment to talk, not to quit."},{"h":"Check the therapist first","x":"Before naming transference, ask about lateness, cancellations, boundary crossings, the therapist leaving and trust. A real problem is not transference and may need PALS or the service lead."},{"h":"Silence is a technique","x":"Dynamic therapists often stay quiet so the patient's own thoughts surface. Patients can read it as boredom or rejection; that reading is itself useful material."},{"h":"Suggest, never pronounce","x":"Offer links tentatively: 'I wonder whether…', 'What do you make of…?'. Definite interpretations from a stranger in A&E feel intrusive and can push her out of therapy."},{"h":"Return it to the therapy","x":"Liaison does not replace the therapist. Help her take the feeling to the next session, and with consent let the therapist know she attended."}],"type":"Therapy assessment + Explain a treatment","do":"Explore why she wants to stop, explain transference, and agree she raises it with her therapist tomorrow."},"c-s-57":{"obj":["Heard the father's view of Maya and her treatment and named that no one is to blame","Explored the parental conflict and separation, including a screen for domestic abuse","Explained what family therapy involves, how often, who attends and why it may help after CBT","Agreed a referral with flexible attendance, and that Maya's own view will be sought","Checked his understanding and answered all three of his questions"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"She's thirteen. She's not depressed, she's a teenager. She needs to get on with it.\"","r":"I can see you want her to be okay, and pushing on has worked for you. Her low mood has lasted months and is affecting school and friends. Can I tell you what we've seen?"},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"So this is our fault now? We've done everything for her.\"","r":"I'm sorry if it sounded that way; that isn't what I think. You've clearly done a lot. Family therapy is about using what you do well, not finding someone to blame."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"CBT was meant to fix her. Why would this be any different?\"","r":"That's a fair question after all those sessions. CBT has helped a bit. Maya says the progress gets undone at home. Family therapy works on that part directly."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Her mum and I are splitting up. We haven't told her.\"","r":"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. It sounds a hard time for you all. Children usually pick up tension even when nothing is said. Could that be part of what she's feeling?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"I work twelve-hour shifts. I can't come every week.\"","r":"That's a real problem, and the team will work with it. Sessions are usually a few weeks apart, and some can be early evening. Not everyone has to come every time."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will strangers be watching us through a mirror?\"","r":"It's a good question. Some teams have colleagues watching on video to share ideas, but only with your agreement. You can meet them, and you can say no."}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue fluoxetine 20 mg each morning; at each review ask about suicidal thoughts, agitation and self-harm (higher risk in under-18s on SSRIs)."],"psych":["Refer to CAMHS family therapy team (systemic family therapy); first session to agree who attends and how often.","Maya continues individual CBT until the family therapist and CBT therapist agree a joint plan."],"social":["Parents to consider telling Maya about the separation together, with family therapist support.","Mother encouraged to continue GP treatment for her own depression; offered a separate meeting."],"safety":["Screen for domestic abuse; if present, safeguarding children discussion the same day.","Maya and parents given the CAMHS duty line and NHS 111, option 2; review in 4 weeks."]},"facts":[{"h":"A NICE option for teen depression","x":"For moderate or severe depression in 12 to 18-year-olds, NICE lists family therapy (systemic or family-based IPT) among the psychological choices, alongside individual CBT, IPT-A and psychodynamic therapy."},{"h":"The family is the patient","x":"Systemic therapy sees each person's responses as part of a loop. Circular questions ask how others notice and react, so no one is singled out as the problem."},{"h":"Flexible format","x":"Sessions are often every two to four weeks, about an hour or more, over a few months. The therapist agrees with the family who attends; separated parents can come separately."},{"h":"Screens and reflecting teams","x":"Some teams watch by video or behind a screen, then share ideas with the family. This happens only with consent; explain it beforehand."},{"h":"Children notice tension","x":"Keeping a separation secret rarely protects a child; they sense it and may blame themselves. Helping parents tell her together, simply and honestly, often reduces distress."}],"type":"Explain a treatment + Therapy assessment","do":"Explain family therapy to Maya's father without blame, explore the parental conflict, and agree whether to refer."},"c-s-58":{"obj":["Established the timeline of his first episode and that he is currently well","Elicited predisposing, precipitating, perpetuating and protective influences, including cannabis and medication doubts","Shared a tentative formulation linking his past mistrust to his paranoid experiences, and checked it fits","Explained how CBT for psychosis works, using his own experience, and its format","Judged him suitable and motivated and agreed a referral to the EIP psychologist"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not sure it was psychosis. I was just stressed and smoking too much.\"","r":"That makes sense; stress and cannabis were big parts of it. You don't have to agree on the label to do CBT. It's about making sense of what happened your way."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"The tablets... they're causing problems in bed. It's embarrassing.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me. That's a common side effect and nothing to be embarrassed about. We can check a blood test and look at options, so you don't have to stop on your own."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How is talking supposed to stop me getting ill again?\"","r":"Fair question. CBT helps you spot early warning signs, test worrying thoughts before they grow, and cut back what raises the risk, like cannabis. It works alongside the tablets."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Everyone smokes at uni. You're going to tell me to stop.\"","r":"I'm not here to lecture you. It's your choice. I'd just like you to have the facts: strong cannabis raises the risk of this coming back. We can help if you want to cut down."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"After what happened at school I don't trust anyone. Why would I open up to a therapist?\"","r":"That sounds like it really hurt, and it makes sense you're careful. The psychologist will go at your pace, and building trust can be part of the work itself."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I'm scared it'll happen again in third year.\"","r":"That fear is understandable. It also shows how much you want to stay well, which is a strength. A relapse plan from CBT is one of the best protections."}],"bps":{"bio":["Risperidone 3 mg at night continues; check prolactin now and discuss sexual side effects with the consultant (switch, for instance to aripiprazole, if confirmed)."],"psych":["CBT for psychosis, one-to-one, at least 16 planned sessions, EIP psychologist.","Family intervention offered to Callum and his mother."],"social":["EIP substance use worker for cannabis, if he wants it.","University disability and wellbeing service for coursework adjustments, with his consent."],"safety":["Relapse signs written with him: poor sleep, feeling watched, withdrawing.","Care coordinator in hours; NHS 111, option 2, out of hours."]},"facts":[{"h":"CBTp for everyone with psychosis","x":"NICE: offer CBT for psychosis to all people with psychosis, including a first episode, one-to-one, at least 16 planned sessions, following a manual. It runs alongside medication."},{"h":"Insight is not a prerequisite","x":"CBT for psychosis works with doubt about the diagnosis. Curiosity, some willingness to look at experiences and wanting to stay well matter more than accepting a label."},{"h":"Remember the obstetric history","x":"Prematurity, low birth weight and birth complications slightly raise later psychosis risk. Ask about pregnancy, birth and milestones in every vulnerability history."},{"h":"Cannabis and relapse","x":"Daily or high-strength cannabis raises the risk of a first episode, and continued use after it raises relapse risk. Offer help to cut down rather than a lecture."},{"h":"Ask about sexual side effects","x":"Risperidone often raises prolactin, causing erectile or ejaculatory problems or low libido. Young men rarely volunteer this. Ask directly and check prolactin."}],"type":"Therapy assessment","do":"Take a 5P history, share a formulation he accepts, and decide with him whether CBT for psychosis fits."},"c-s-59":{"obj":["Explained what anorexia is and why Chloe meets the diagnosis, in plain words","Explained the causes as a mix of influences and lifted the mother's guilt","Explained the physical risks and monitoring, linking them to Chloe's results","Explained the treatment plan and a balanced outlook","Agreed how the mother can help and gave written information and carer support"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I read that people die from anorexia. Is she going to die?\"","r":"That's a terrifying thing to read about your daughter. It is serious, and that's why we check her body closely. Most people recover, especially when help starts early, as it has."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I had problems with food at sixteen. Did I pass this on to her?\"","r":"Thank you for telling me; that can't be easy. Genes play some part, but anorexia comes from many things together. You didn't cause this, and your support now matters a lot."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why can't she just eat? She knows she's too thin.\"","r":"It's so hard to watch. The illness makes eating feel frightening, almost like a phobia. That's why she needs a structured plan and therapy, not just willpower."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is there a tablet that will fix this?\"","r":"I wish there were. There's no medicine that treats anorexia itself. The main treatments are regular eating with a dietitian and a specialist talking therapy."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Every meal ends in tears. I don't know what to say to her.\"","r":"That sounds exhausting. Many parents feel exactly this. Try to stay calm, keep mealtimes regular, and talk about other things. Our carers' group teaches exactly these skills."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will she have to go into hospital?\"","r":"Not at the moment; most people are treated as outpatients. If her weight or heart checks get worse, a hospital stay may be needed to keep her safe."}],"bps":{"bio":["Weekly weight, pulse, lying and standing BP, temperature; bloods (FBC, U&E, phosphate, magnesium, glucose) with the GP; repeat ECG if pulse falls.","Dietitian meal plan: three meals and snacks, gradual increase; watch phosphate early (refeeding).","No medication for anorexia itself; DEXA scan if underweight for a year or more."],"psych":["Eating-disorder-focused CBT (CBT-ED), up to 40 sessions over 40 weeks, eating disorders therapist.","Family involved in sessions with Chloe's consent."],"social":["University: interruption of studies with academic tutor and student support.","Mother: carers' skills group, Beat helpline, Carer's assessment by the local authority."],"safety":["Red flags for same-day review: fainting, chest pain, breathlessness, confusion, weakness standing or climbing stairs, pulse under 40.","Admission considered if MEED risk rises; ask about self-harm and suicidal thoughts at each review."]},"facts":[{"h":"Highest death rate in psychiatry","x":"Anorexia has the highest mortality of any mental illness, from heart rhythm problems, starvation and suicide. Say it gently, then explain the monitoring that keeps her safe."},{"h":"Low white count is common","x":"Starvation suppresses the bone marrow, so a mildly low white count, slow pulse and low BP fit a BMI of 15. They usually improve with weight gain but need regular checks."},{"h":"No pill for anorexia","x":"NICE: do not offer medication as the only treatment for anorexia. Medicines may treat other conditions, such as depression, but the core is food plus therapy."},{"h":"Outlook: half recover fully","x":"Roughly half recover fully, about a third improve but keep some symptoms, and around a fifth stay unwell long term. Early treatment and family support improve the odds."},{"h":"Carers need their own help","x":"Offer Beat's helpline, a carers' skills group and a Carer's assessment by the local authority. Calm, consistent parents improve meal support at home."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis","do":"Explain anorexia, its physical risks, treatment and outlook to Chloe's mother, and agree how she can help."},"c-s-60":{"obj":["Mapped the course of her anorexia: onset, behaviours present and absent, delay to treatment, treatment and recovery","Identified the poor prognostic signs, including depression, overdoses, self-harm and EUPD","Identified the favourable signs, including completed treatment, years of stable weight and current support","Explored her personality traits, family relationships and history of bullying","Checked current mood and risk, summarised the balance and agreed next steps"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't really know where to start.\"","r":"That's completely fine; there's no right place. Could we start with when eating first became difficult, and go from there at your pace?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Kids at school called me names because I was bigger.\"","r":"That sounds really painful, especially at that age. Thank you for telling me. How did that change the way you thought about food?"},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Three years and I didn't tell anyone. That was stupid.\"","r":"It's very common to hide it; the illness often makes people feel they have to. You did get help in the end, and you finished treatment. That took strength."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"When I thought people would leave me, I'd cut. It was the only thing that worked.\"","r":"It sounds as if the feelings were overwhelming and cutting was how you survived them. When did it stop, and what helped?"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you going to discharge me? What if it comes back?\"","r":"That's an understandable worry, especially given what you've been through. Nothing will be decided without you, and any plan would include how to get help back quickly."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why do you need to know about my parents?\"","r":"Fair question. Family relationships can affect how people recover. You don't have to go into detail; just tell me what feels okay."}],"bps":{"psych":["Continue monthly sessions with her CMHT psychologist until discharge is agreed.","Relapse prevention plan written with Leah: early signs (skipping meals, more exercise, body checking, low mood)."],"social":["GP letter with relapse plan and direct re-referral route to the eating disorders service."],"safety":["Current risk low: no self-harm or suicidal thoughts; protective friends and work.","Crisis plan: GP, CMHT duty line, NHS 111 option 2; review with consultant before any discharge."]},"facts":[{"h":"Signs of a good outlook","x":"Onset in adolescence, restricting only, short illness before treatment, completing treatment, full weight restoration kept for years, good relationships, work and no substance misuse."},{"h":"Signs of a poor outlook","x":"Long illness before treatment, very low lowest weight, bingeing or purging, comorbid depression or personality disorder, self-harm, substance misuse and difficult family relationships."},{"h":"Relapse clusters early","x":"Relapse is most likely in the first one to two years after weight restoration. Several years well is a strong favourable sign, but write a relapse plan anyway."},{"h":"Ask what she did not do","x":"Absence of bingeing, vomiting, laxatives, diet pills and drugs is prognostic. A strict marker looks for the negatives as well as the positives."},{"h":"Quiet patients need space","x":"Use open questions, reflections and short silences. Saying 'take your time' and naming what is hard often opens a closed patient more than more questions."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"Take a time-ordered history sorting her anorexia prognosis into poor and favourable signs, and explore personality briefly."},"c-s-61":{"obj":["Established her relapse history and what happened when she was off lithium","Explained the risk to the baby from lithium and the risk of relapse and psychosis after birth, in plain numbers","Set out the three options (continue, switch, slow taper) with the main risk of each, and answered fertility and breastfeeding","Explained level monitoring in pregnancy and around the birth, and the signs of toxicity","Agreed a written plan: folic acid, perinatal referral, what to do if pregnant, and a review date"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I read lithium damages the baby's heart. I can't take that risk.\"","r":"\"That's a frightening thing to read, and I'm glad you asked before stopping anything. The heart risk is real but small, and most babies exposed have normal hearts. Let me put it next to your own risks.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Could I just come off it for the first three months and go back on?\"","r":"\"That's a fair idea and some women do it. The catch is that relapse is likely without it for you, and coming off quickly makes relapse more likely still. If you chose it, we'd taper slowly with a clear plan.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will lithium stop me getting pregnant?\"","r":"\"No, it isn't known to affect fertility. The thing to plan for is what happens once you are pregnant.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't go back to being sectioned. Not with a baby.\"","r":"\"Those admissions sound awful, and wanting to protect yourself and a baby from that makes complete sense. That's exactly why we plan now, while you're well.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I breastfeed?\"","r":"\"I know that matters to many mums. Lithium gets into breast milk, and levels can build up in a baby, so we usually advise against breastfeeding on it. Planning feeding early means it isn't a shock later.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Isn't it selfish to take medicine when I'm pregnant?\"","r":"\"Not at all. Staying well is part of looking after a baby. A mum who relapses can't eat, sleep or attend scans, and that carries risks too.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Lithium (Priadel) 800 mg at night continued; aim for the lowest effective level, usually 0.6 to 0.8 mmol/L, set by the perinatal psychiatrist","Folic acid 400 micrograms daily now until 12 weeks of pregnancy (5 mg only if another risk factor applies)","Before conception: lithium level, U&Es, eGFR, calcium and TSH; repeat in pregnancy with each level as the team advises","Once pregnant: levels every 4 weeks, weekly from 36 weeks, within 24 hours of birth; detailed heart scan of the baby in mid-pregnancy via obstetrics","Avoid NSAIDs (including ibuprofen after the birth), ACE inhibitors and diuretics; drink regularly, more in heat or vomiting"],"psych":["Written relapse signature agreed: sleeping less, spending, racing thoughts, irritability","Psychoeducation for her and her partner on early signs and postpartum psychosis"],"social":["Partner invited to the next review; plan for protected night sleep after birth (partner does a night feed with formula)","GP, midwife, obstetrician and health visitor receive the perinatal care plan once pregnant"],"safety":["If pregnant: do not stop lithium, ring the team the same day; level and review within a week","Toxicity signs (vomiting, diarrhoea, coarse tremor, unsteadiness, confusion): stop the dose and attend A&E for an urgent level","Perinatal care plan written by 32 weeks, including daily contact in the first weeks after birth and the mother and baby unit route; crisis line and NHS 111 option 2"]},"facts":[{"h":"Heart risk in perspective","x":"First-trimester lithium is linked with a small rise in heart defects, higher with higher levels. Ebstein's anomaly is rare. Most exposed babies have normal hearts; a detailed heart scan checks."},{"h":"Levels fall, then spike","x":"Levels drift down through pregnancy as kidneys clear more lithium, then rise quickly after delivery. Check every 4 weeks, weekly from 36 weeks and within 24 hours of birth."},{"h":"After the birth is the danger","x":"Bipolar disorder carries a high risk of severe illness in the first weeks after birth; stopping medicine raises it sharply. Postpartum psychosis can start within days."},{"h":"Taper, never stop","x":"If she chooses to stop, reduce gradually over at least 4 weeks. Abrupt stopping makes relapse more likely and does not undo early exposure."},{"h":"Lithium and breastfeeding","x":"Lithium passes into milk and can reach high levels in the baby. Breastfeeding is usually not advised; decide before the birth."}],"type":"Explain a treatment + Management plan","do":"Explain lithium and relapse risks in pregnancy, give three options with monitoring, and agree a written perinatal plan."},"c-s-62":{"obj":["Elicited her thoughts and worries about pregnancy, relapse and her medicines","Explained valproate's risks in numbers, folic acid, and the Pregnancy Prevention Programme rules","Explained her risk of relapse and postpartum psychosis, and that sertraline can continue","Agreed a gradual switch to olanzapine, with weight monitoring, keeping contraception until she is stable","Answered whether she can have a family and what social services would do"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Would social services take my baby because I've been sectioned?\"","r":"\"That's a really common fear and I'm glad you asked. Having bipolar disorder isn't a reason to remove a baby. If you ever became unwell, any social care role would be about support.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Should I just stop the valproate now and get the implant out?\"","r":"\"I understand wanting to get going. Stopping suddenly could bring on mania, and you'd have no protection. We'll switch step by step, and the implant comes out last.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going back on olanzapine. I put on two stone.\"","r":"\"That weight gain was really upsetting, and it's fair to be wary. This time we'd start a dietitian and monitoring first, and use the lowest dose that works. Can we look at it together?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Is it selfish to want a baby with my illness?\"","r":"\"Not at all. Lots of women with bipolar disorder have children and do well. Planning like this is what a careful parent does.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I keep my sertraline?\"","r":"\"Yes. It's one of the best-studied antidepressants in pregnancy and breastfeeding. The baby may be a bit jittery for a few days after birth, and the midwives watch for that.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I've been well three years. What if changing breaks it?\"","r":"\"That fear makes sense; you've worked hard for this. We go slowly, see you every two weeks, and stop if warning signs appear. Olanzapine has held you before.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Olanzapine 5 mg at night for 1 week, then 10 mg (range 5 to 20 mg); warn about sleepiness and appetite","Once settled on olanzapine, reduce sodium valproate by 250 mg every 2 weeks from 1 g to stop (about 8 weeks)","Folic acid 5 mg daily while on valproate; then 400 micrograms (5 mg if BMI 30 or more)","Weight, BMI, waist and BP at baseline and weekly for 6 weeks; HbA1c and lipids at baseline and 12 weeks, then yearly","Sertraline 100 mg each morning continues"],"psych":["Relapse signature written with her and her husband: less sleep, overspending, grand plans, irritability","Psychoeducation on postpartum psychosis for both of them"],"social":["Dietitian and local healthy weight support referral before olanzapine starts","Husband invited to the next review; perinatal care plan by 32 weeks once pregnant, shared with midwife, GP and health visitor"],"safety":["Implant stays until she has been stable on olanzapine alone for 3 to 6 months","If pregnant on valproate: do not stop suddenly; ring the perinatal team the same day for urgent review","Review every 2 weeks during the switch; crisis line and NHS 111 mental health option if warning signs appear"]},"facts":[{"h":"Valproate in numbers","x":"About 11 in 100 babies exposed in pregnancy have a major birth defect (2 to 3 in 100 normally), and 30 to 40 in 100 children have learning or developmental problems."},{"h":"The Pregnancy Prevention Programme","x":"Highly effective contraception, pregnancy testing as needed, and an annual specialist review with a signed Annual Risk Acknowledgement Form. Since 2024, a second specialist must agree continuation."},{"h":"Contraception comes off last","x":"Switch first, then wait until she is stable on the new medicine alone. Removing the implant early risks conceiving mid-switch."},{"h":"Never stop it suddenly","x":"If pregnant on valproate, she keeps taking it and rings the team that day. Abrupt stopping risks relapse; the specialist decides how to change it."},{"h":"Bipolar I and the newborn weeks","x":"Bipolar I with past psychosis carries a high risk of severe illness after birth, including postpartum psychosis, which can start within days. Plan by 32 weeks."}],"type":"Explain a treatment + Management plan","do":"Explain valproate's pregnancy risks and MHRA rules, address her worries, and agree a gradual switch before conception."},"c-s-63":{"obj":["Summarised her course and confirmed the treatments tried, at what dose and for how long","Explored and named the perpetuating factors: negative self-beliefs, low activity and feelings about stopping breastfeeding","Checked risk to the mother and baby and agreed what the health visitor should escalate","Recommended specific biological, psychological and social interventions with who delivers each","Set out a plan B if the first changes fail"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"She's done everything you asked. Why isn't it working?\"","r":"\"That's a fair challenge, and it's frustrating for everyone, her most of all. It doesn't mean she's failed; it means this medicine isn't the right one. Let's look at what's keeping it going.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Shouldn't we just give the sertraline longer?\"","r":"\"I understand the instinct. Eight weeks at the top dose with little change is a fair trial, so another month is unlikely to help. Switching gives her a better chance sooner.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"She told me she's a rubbish mum, but she's lovely with Isla.\"","r":"\"That's really important. That gap between how she sees herself and what you see is probably keeping the depression going. It's something therapy can target directly.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I worry I missed something. I've known her since the birth.\"","r":"\"You haven't missed it; you've spotted it. Ringing us now is exactly the right thing, and your observations are shaping the plan.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What if the new tablet doesn't work either?\"","r":"\"Good question to ask now. There's a clear next step: adding a second medicine, rethinking the diagnosis, ECT or magnetic stimulation, or a mother and baby unit. We won't run out of options.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is there anything I can do on my visits?\"","r":"\"Yes, a lot. Notice and tell her what she does well with Isla, help her plan one outing a day, and ring us the same day if anything about risk changes.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Switch antidepressant after review this week: e.g. mirtazapine 15 mg at night, up to 30 mg after 1 to 2 weeks, while sertraline is tapered (e.g. 200 to 100 to 50 mg over 2 to 3 weeks)","Alternatives: venlafaxine MR 75 mg, rising to 150 mg (check blood pressure), or another SSRI; not breastfeeding, so choice is wider","Bloods via GP: TFTs (postpartum thyroiditis), FBC, ferritin, B12, folate, vitamin D","Weekly review for 4 weeks after the switch; assess response at 4 to 6 weeks on the new dose"],"psych":["Perinatal psychology assessment for interpersonal therapy (about 16 sessions) or parent-infant work such as video interaction guidance","Therapy to target beliefs about being a bad mother and partner, and any guilt about stopping breastfeeding","Behavioural activation within OT: one planned activity a day, building up"],"social":["Perinatal occupational therapist and nursery nurse: graded activity, baby groups, family hub sessions","Peer support: perinatal mental health charity groups (e.g. PANDAS, Home-Start volunteer visits)","Carers' assessment for her husband via the local authority; Early Help referral if the family wants extra support","Psychoeducation session for her husband and mother"],"safety":["Health visitor to ring the perinatal duty worker the same day if thoughts of self-harm, harm to Isla, neglect, or unusual beliefs appear","Crisis plan copied to the patient: team duty line, NHS 111 mental health option, 999","Plan B: augmentation (e.g. quetiapine, aripiprazole or lithium), diagnostic review for bipolar disorder, ECT or rTMS, mother and baby unit referral"]},"facts":[{"h":"What counts as a fair trial","x":"About 4 to 6 weeks at a therapeutic dose with little change is a failed trial. Eight weeks at 200 mg sertraline qualifies; check adherence before calling it."},{"h":"Switch before you add","x":"After one failed antidepressant, switching is usually the next step. Augmentation (e.g. quetiapine, aripiprazole, lithium) comes after two failed drugs or partial response."},{"h":"Check the thyroid","x":"Postpartum thyroiditis can mimic or worsen depression in the first year after birth. Add TFTs, FBC and ferritin whenever postnatal depression resists treatment."},{"h":"Rethink the diagnosis","x":"Resistant postnatal depression may be bipolar depression. Ask about past highs, family history and mixed features before more antidepressants."},{"h":"Mother and baby units","x":"Specialist wards that admit mothers with their babies, usually up to 12 months old, for severe or resistant illness. Referral is through the perinatal team."}],"type":"Management plan + Collateral history","do":"Identify her perpetuating factors with the health visitor and agree specific biopsychosocial next steps and a plan B."},"c-s-64":{"obj":["Explored her first pregnancy and her current worries about quetiapine and diabetes","Explained quetiapine's risks in pregnancy and breastfeeding, and that the diabetes link is uncertain while her previous diabetes is the main risk","Compared the alternatives with their risks and explained her postpartum relapse risk","Gave a clear recommendation and agreed it with her","Agreed early glucose testing, diabetes prevention and a shared plan with obstetrics and the perinatal team"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is the quetiapine going to give me diabetes again?\"","r":"\"That's a really reasonable worry after last time. The honest answer is it might add a little, but having had it before is the bigger factor. So we test early and act fast, whatever you take.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"The caesarean was awful. I wanted a normal birth.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry it went that way; it sounds like it still hurts. Let's ask the midwives about a birth choices appointment, so this time you're part of the plan from the start.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What would you do if you were me?\"","r":"\"I'll give you a straight answer. Quetiapine has kept you well for four years and through a pregnancy, so I'd stay on it and tackle the diabetes risk head-on. It's still your call.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I breastfeed on 400 milligrams?\"","r":"\"Yes, many women do. Only small amounts get into milk. We'd watch that the baby isn't too sleepy, and feeding before your night dose helps.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"My partner's gone and my parents are miles away. I'm on my own with Lily.\"","r":"\"That's a lot to carry while pregnant. Thank you for telling me; it changes how we plan. Let's think now about who could be with you after the birth.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Couldn't I come off it till the baby's born?\"","r":"\"I can see why that's tempting. With your history, the risk of becoming unwell, especially after birth, is high without it. Stopping now would trade an uncertain risk for a much bigger one.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue quetiapine XL 400 mg at night; do not stop suddenly; review dose each trimester (lowest effective dose)","Early 75 g OGTT (or self-monitoring) now via obstetrics; repeat OGTT at 24 to 28 weeks if normal; antenatal diabetes clinic if positive (diet first, then metformin or insulin)","Weight and BMI each review; HbA1c is not used to diagnose in pregnancy","After birth: fasting glucose at 6 to 13 weeks (or HbA1c after 13 weeks), then yearly HbA1c via GP","Neonatal team told: observe the baby for sleepiness, poor feeding or jitteriness for the first days"],"psych":["Relapse signature agreed: poor sleep, racing thoughts, overspending, irritability","Birth choices or birth reflections midwife to discuss the previous caesarean and her wishes"],"social":["Dietitian and healthy pregnancy activity advice: regular meals, fewer sugary foods, about 150 minutes of moderate activity a week","Plan who will stay with her for the first weeks after birth (parents to stay; childcare for Lily if admission is needed)","Health visitor and family hub informed; Home-Start or similar volunteer support offered"],"safety":["Perinatal care plan by 32 weeks, shared with obstetrics, midwife, GP and health visitor: protected sleep, early postnatal review, mother and baby unit route","Crisis contacts: team duty line, NHS 111 mental health option, 999","Review in 4 weeks, sooner if mood or glucose results change"]},"facts":[{"h":"Previous GDM is the big risk","x":"Women who had gestational diabetes before have about a 1 in 3 or greater chance of it again. Test early whatever the medicine."},{"h":"NICE timing for testing","x":"After previous gestational diabetes: early self-monitoring or 75 g OGTT soon after booking, then OGTT at 24 to 28 weeks if the first is normal."},{"h":"Quetiapine and birth defects","x":"Not linked with a clear rise in major malformations. Late-pregnancy exposure can leave babies unsettled or sleepy for a few days; the neonatal team observes."},{"h":"Not every mood stabiliser works","x":"Lamotrigine mainly prevents depression, not mania or postpartum psychosis. Valproate is not used in pregnancy. Untried drugs mid-pregnancy risk relapse."},{"h":"Breastfeeding on quetiapine","x":"Small amounts pass into milk; generally considered compatible. Watch for infant sleepiness, especially at higher doses or in premature babies."}],"type":"Medication review + Management plan","do":"Review quetiapine in pregnancy, explain gestational diabetes risk and screening, recommend a plan, and share it with obstetrics."},"c-s-65":{"obj":["Established when it started, how it has progressed, and its link to the birth and her past OCD","Described the obsessions' content and form, including intrusive harm thoughts, and her resistance","Listed every compulsion and avoidance, with the time they take and the anxiety behind them","Assessed impact on her functioning and the baby's care, and risks of self-neglect, suicide and harm","Screened for depression, psychosis, anxiety and substance use, and summarised back to her"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I know it sounds mad. A proper mum wouldn't do this.\"","r":"\"It doesn't sound mad at all. It sounds exhausting. These worries are common after a birth, and they show how much you care. Can you tell me what happens?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Sometimes I get a picture of dropping him down the stairs. Am I a danger?\"","r":"\"I'm grateful you trusted me with that; it takes courage. Thoughts like that are common, and the fact they horrify you is typical. Having the thought is not the same as wanting it.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you going to tell social services?\"","r":"\"I can hear how frightening that is. No. Unwanted thoughts like these aren't a reason to involve them. My job is to help you feel better.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is this my fault for having a baby when I had OCD?\"","r":"\"Not at all. Having a baby is a common time for OCD to flare, because the stakes feel so high. It's treatable, and you've beaten it before.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I'm so tired. It takes hours every day.\"","r":"\"That sounds exhausting on top of a newborn. Let's work out exactly how much time it takes, so we can plan the right help.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"I didn't breastfeed because of the sertraline. Did I do the wrong thing?\"","r":"\"You made the best choice with what you were told. Sertraline is actually one of the antidepressants we prefer in breastfeeding, so it needn't stop you next time.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Review sertraline 100 mg with her: OCD often needs higher doses (up to 200 mg), increased in 50 mg steps every few weeks if tolerated","Simple emollient for cracked hands"],"psych":["CBT with exposure and response prevention through the perinatal psychology team, starting within weeks; partner to join one session","Y-BOCS at baseline to measure change"],"social":["Health visitor informed with consent, to support feeding and reduce reassurance-giving","Partner to share bottle preparation as part of the ERP plan"],"safety":["If harm thoughts change, she feels she might act, mood drops or she cannot care for Leo: ring the team the same day","Crisis: team duty line, NHS 111 mental health option, 999; review in 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Harm thoughts are common","x":"Most new mothers have occasional unwanted thoughts that the baby could come to harm. In OCD they are distressing, resisted and avoided, and not linked with acting on them."},{"h":"OCD or psychosis?","x":"In OCD she knows the thoughts are her own and senseless. In psychosis, beliefs are fixed, may feel imposed, and there may be confusion or hallucinations: urgent review."},{"h":"Measure the cost","x":"Ask how many hours a day rituals take. More than an hour a day, or delayed feeds, means significant impairment and faster treatment."},{"h":"Reassurance feeds OCD","x":"Repeated reassurance gives brief relief and strengthens the cycle. Validate her feelings, but do not keep confirming the bottles are clean."},{"h":"Sertraline and breastfeeding","x":"Sertraline passes into milk in very small amounts and is a preferred choice in breastfeeding. Many women are wrongly told to stop feeding."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"Take a postnatal OCD history covering obsessions, compulsions, avoidance, impact, comorbidity and risk to her and the baby."},"c-s-66":{"obj":["Elicited the content of the thoughts, that they intrude, are his own, and are resisted and ruminated on","Traced onset and course, and how his insight moved from 'silly' at first to 'maybe true' now","Found all his checking and avoidance, including body checking, sick leave and isolation","Excluded paedophilic interest, psychosis, a depressive episode and risk to self, and found a past episode","Named severe OCD with poor insight, explained why from his own history, and checked his understanding"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I tell you what the thoughts are, you'll have to report me.\"","r":"\"I understand why you'd worry about that. Thoughts you don't want and don't act on are not something I report. Telling me helps me work out what this is.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Am I going mad?\"","r":"\"That's a really common fear with thoughts like these. Let me ask a few questions I ask everyone, and then I'll tell you honestly what I think.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Having the thought means part of me must want it.\"","r":"\"I can see why it feels that way. But your horror at the thought tells me the opposite. In OCD the mind fixes on exactly what a person fears most.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can you just tell me I'm not one? Then I'll be fine.\"","r":"\"I can hear how badly you want that. Reassurance helps for a minute and then the doubt returns stronger. I'll explain what this is instead, because that lasts longer.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't go near my nephews any more. My sister thinks I've fallen out with her.\"","r":"\"That sounds lonely and painful. Avoiding them is a way of coping with the fear, and it's something treatment can help you undo, step by step.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Fifty-fifty they're right. How can you be sure it's OCD?\"","r":"\"Fair question. At the start you knew they were absurd, you've always fought them, and nothing else suggests psychosis. That pattern points clearly to OCD.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline 50 mg each morning; increase in 50 mg steps every few weeks if needed, up to 200 mg. OCD often needs higher doses and up to 12 weeks for full effect","Warn about early nausea, jitteriness, sleep change and sexual side effects; review at 2 weeks, ask about suicidal thoughts"],"psych":["CBT with exposure and response prevention, more than 10 hours of therapist time, delivered by the psychology team (severe impairment)","Family and friends to stop giving reassurance on request; explain why at a joint session"],"social":["Fit note for 4 weeks; plan a graded return to work with his manager once ERP starts","Signpost OCD-UK for peer support and information"],"safety":["No safeguarding concern: record the reasoning (no urges, arousal, images or contact)","Crisis plan: GP, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123; clinic review in 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Horror is the clue","x":"In sexual-theme OCD the thought is repugnant to the person and drives avoidance and checking. Paedophilic interest is wanted and acted towards. Ask both ways, plainly."},{"h":"Poor insight is not psychosis","x":"ICD-11 OCD can be 'with poor to absent insight'. Ask how the thoughts began: if he once called them absurd, insight was lost through severity."},{"h":"Unwanted thoughts are not risk","x":"People with sexual or harm obsessions are not at higher risk of acting on them. Thoughts alone do not trigger a safeguarding referral."},{"h":"Reassurance is a compulsion","x":"Each 'you're not one' eases anxiety briefly and keeps the cycle going. Explain the diagnosis once, clearly, then stop reassuring."},{"h":"Severe OCD gets both","x":"With marked impairment, NICE CG31 advises combining an SSRI with CBT including ERP. SSRIs for OCD may need high doses and 12 weeks to judge."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Show his thoughts began as resisted obsessions, exclude paedophilic interest, psychosis and depression, and name severe OCD."},"c-s-67":{"obj":["Described her low spells fully, how she is between them, and how they have changed over six years","Shown the spells start about a week before her period and lift once it starts, in most cycles","Excluded bipolar disorder, psychosis, emotionally unstable traits, physical causes and substances","Assessed her suicidal thoughts and agreed a safety plan for the premenstrual week","Named PMDD, explained it in plain words, and agreed a two-cycle symptom diary"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I read about bipolar online. Will I be on lithium for life?\"","r":"\"That sounds like a frightening thing to read about yourself. Let's look at your pattern carefully first. From what you've told me so far, I'm not thinking of bipolar.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"That week I'm horrible to my boyfriend. I don't recognise myself.\"","r":"\"That must feel awful, especially when you're kind the rest of the month. It tells me this comes and goes with something. Let's work out what.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Sometimes that week I think everyone would be better off without me.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that's important. Those thoughts can be really frightening. Can I ask a bit more: have you ever made a plan or done anything?\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"Everyone gets moody before their period. Maybe I'm just weak.\"","r":"\"Lots of women notice some change, but what you're describing stops you working and brings dark thoughts. That's a recognised condition, not weakness.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So is it my hormones being wrong?\"","r":"\"Good question. Usually the hormone levels are normal. It's that some brains are extra sensitive to the normal monthly changes. That's why it responds to treatment.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why do I need a diary? I've told you what happens.\"","r":"\"You have, and it's very clear. Daily ratings over two cycles confirm the pattern and show which treatment is working. It's the standard way to be sure.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["GP to check TFT and FBC; pelvic ultrasound only if cycles become irregular or other PCOS signs appear","GP may offer sertraline 50 mg daily, either all month or from mid-cycle until the period starts; SSRIs work within days in PMDD","Or a combined pill containing drospirenone, taken continuously or with fewer breaks, if she wants contraception"],"psych":["CBT for premenstrual symptoms via NHS Talking Therapies, around 6 to 12 sessions","Plan the premenstrual week: lighter diary at work, regular sleep, exercise, less alcohol and caffeine"],"social":["With her consent, help her explain the pattern to her boyfriend so he can spot the week early","Signpost the National Association for Premenstrual Syndrome (NAPS) for information and peer support"],"safety":["Written safety plan for the premenstrual week with two named contacts, Samaritans 116 123, NHS 111 option 2","If an SSRI starts, review within 1 week for agitation or suicidal thoughts","Clinic review in 8 weeks with diaries"]},"facts":[{"h":"Ask how spells start and end","x":"Patients rarely link mood to periods unprompted. 'How does it start and stop?' and 'where in your cycle?' find PMDD in two questions."},{"h":"Two cycles of daily ratings","x":"Recall overstates the link. Daily ratings over at least two cycles confirm PMDD and separate it from premenstrual worsening of an all-month disorder."},{"h":"No highs, no bipolar","x":"Bipolar disorder needs an elevated or energised phase with less need for sleep. Cycle-locked lows with normal mood between are not bipolar."},{"h":"SSRIs work fast here","x":"In PMDD, SSRIs help within days, so they can be taken only in the second half of the cycle. Continuous dosing is an option if luteal dosing fails."},{"h":"Common mild, rare severe","x":"Many women notice premenstrual change; severe PMDD affects roughly 3 to 8%. Passive suicidal thoughts are common in it and need a safety plan."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Explain a diagnosis","do":"Link her low spells to the premenstrual week, exclude bipolar and other mimics, assess risk, and explain PMDD."},"c-s-68":{"obj":["Established the time course, the character of her symptoms and any life stresses","Shown her mood, enjoyment and outlook are intact and unlike past depressive episodes","Found night sweats, flushes, vaginal symptoms and periods that became irregular and stopped","Checked for lithium toxicity, interacting medicines and thyroid symptoms, and assessed risk including driving","Told her the likely cause is perimenopause, with reasons, and agreed bloods and next steps"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I'm terrified it's coming back. Last time I ended up in hospital.\"","r":"\"That makes complete sense after what you went through. Let's look carefully at what's happening now, because it may be something different.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I snapped at my daughter on the phone and cried for an hour after.\"","r":"\"That sounds upsetting, especially when you're close. It tells me something is pushing your emotions around. Let's find out what.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Things with my husband aren't great. Sex hurts now, so I avoid it.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that's not easy to say. Discomfort like that is often a physical change around this time of life, and it's very treatable.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Isn't 49 too young for the menopause?\"","r":"\"It's a good question. The average age in the UK is 51, and the changes often start a few years before. At 49 it's very typical.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I take HRT with lithium?\"","r":"\"Yes, there's no important interaction with lithium or citalopram. Your GP will go through the benefits and risks for you.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"Maybe I should just stop the lithium. Perhaps that's causing it.\"","r":"\"I understand wanting a quick fix. Stopping lithium suddenly raises the risk of relapse. Let's check your level first and decide together with the result.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Lithium level 12 hours after the 22:00 dose, U&E, eGFR, TFT and calcium today; target her usual level (usually 0.6 to 0.8 mmol/L)","Continue lithium 800 mg nocte and citalopram 20 mg mane; stop ibuprofen, use paracetamol","GP to discuss HRT for vasomotor and mood symptoms, and vaginal oestrogen for dryness and painful sex","Contraception until 2 years after the last period, as she is under 50"],"psych":["CBT for menopausal symptoms is an option if she prefers not to take HRT, via NHS Talking Therapies","Agree her relapse signature in writing: early waking, guilt, withdrawal, unusual beliefs"],"social":["Discuss reasonable adjustments with her line manager or occupational health","Offer a joint appointment with her husband if she wishes"],"safety":["Lithium toxicity advice: tremor, diarrhoea, vomiting or unsteadiness means stop and get a same-day level","Drink to thirst, more in hot weather and with night sweats","No motorway driving while concentration is poor; review in 6 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"No FSH test over 45","x":"NICE NG23: diagnose perimenopause from vasomotor symptoms and irregular periods in women over 45. FSH testing is for women aged 40 to 45 with symptoms, or under 40."},{"h":"Sweats can raise lithium","x":"Heavy sweating and low fluid intake concentrate lithium; NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors and thiazides cut its clearance. Check a 12-hour level when anything changes."},{"h":"Lithium hits thyroid and calcium","x":"Hypothyroidism is common on lithium, especially in middle-aged women; hyperparathyroidism raises calcium and causes irritability and fatigue. Check TFT and calcium every 6 months."},{"h":"Ask what wakes her","x":"'What wakes you at night?' finds night sweats; then ask about daytime flushes, vaginal dryness and the last period. Women rarely volunteer these."},{"h":"Contraception still matters","x":"Under 50, contraception is advised until 2 years after the last period; over 50, until 1 year after. HRT is not contraception."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Explain a diagnosis","do":"Find the cause of her new symptoms, separating perimenopause from relapse, lithium or thyroid problems, and explain it."},"c-s-69":{"obj":["Clarified the collapse, any seizure features, her state since, and all tests and results","Found the recent family shock, how Sophie reacted, and her past and family history","Explained dissociative stupor plainly, what normal tests mean, and that she is not choosing this","Answered whether her uncle harmed her honestly, without assuming either way","Set out the plan, likely recovery, and how her mother can help, and checked her understanding"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is she going to die? She hasn't had a drink since yesterday.\"","r":"\"I can see how frightened you are. She's getting fluids through the drip, and the nurses are checking her closely. Her brain and body tests are healthy, which is reassuring.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"So you're saying she's putting it on?\"","r":"\"No, and I'm sorry if it sounded that way. She isn't choosing this. Her mind has shut down after a shock, and it's as real as any illness.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"If it's psychological, can't she just snap out of it?\"","r":"\"I understand why you'd hope so. She can't switch it off, and pushing her can make it last longer. Calm, ordinary company helps most.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Did Paul do something to her? Is that why?\"","r":"\"That's a terrible thing to be wondering. I don't know, and I won't guess. It's possible; it's also possible the shock of the news alone did this. We'll let her talk when she's ready.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have seen how upset she was this week.\"","r":"\"You noticed she was withdrawn, and you've been here all night. Nobody could have predicted this. What matters now is what you're already doing: being with her.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will she end up in a mental hospital?\"","r":"\"Not for now. She needs to stay here while she can't eat or drink. Most people recover over days to weeks. If she needs more help later, we'll plan it with you.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Stay on AMU: IV fluids with fluid balance chart; U&E daily; NEWS2 4-hourly","NG feeding in her best interests if no oral intake by day 3, with dietitian; check for refeeding risk","Pressure area care, VTE risk assessment, bladder scan if no urine output","Bush-Francis score; consultant to consider lorazepam challenge (e.g. 1 to 2 mg IV, observe response)"],"psych":["Liaison psychiatry review daily; once she responds, a gentle assessment of mood, trauma and the stressor at her pace","Psychological therapy for the underlying stress after recovery, via liaison or community services"],"social":["Mother and close family to visit calmly, talk normally, and not quiz her about the uncle","Offer her mother support for herself (GP, carers' services); college informed only with Sophie's consent later"],"safety":["Any disclosure of abuse: adult safeguarding referral and inform police, who already have an investigation open","Re-review if anything changes: fever, fits, new neurological signs, or falling consciousness","Update her mother daily; named liaison nurse as contact"]},"facts":[{"h":"Normal tests are good news","x":"Normal scans, EEG and bloods mean no damage. Say that, then say the condition is still real: it is a problem of how the brain is working, not its structure."},{"h":"Rule out catatonia","x":"Catatonia can look identical. Score the Bush-Francis scale; a lorazepam challenge that brings rapid improvement points to catatonia, which needs a different treatment."},{"h":"Keep her on the medical ward","x":"While she cannot eat or drink she needs IV fluids, possibly NG feeding and nursing care; psychiatric wards cannot usually give IV treatment."},{"h":"Answer the abuse question","x":"Say you don't know; it is possible but not assumed, and it will be explored only when she is well. Avoiding the question destroys trust."},{"h":"Calm normality, no pushing","x":"Visitors should talk normally, keep things calm and not fuss over the symptoms. Most dissociative stupors resolve over days to weeks."}],"type":"Collateral history + Explain a diagnosis","do":"Take a brief collateral history, explain dissociative stupor, answer the abuse question honestly, and agree the plan."},"c-t-01":{"obj":["Presented Mr Whitfield's case in a clear, concise summary with key positives and the absence of suicidal thoughts","Justified a moderate depressive episode and gave at least three reasoned differentials","Explained why depression after a heart attack matters for recovery","Set out a short- and long-term biopsychosocial plan naming sertraline, the antiplatelet bleeding risk, a named therapy, and alcohol and smoking support","Checked Priya's understanding and answered her questions"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Isn't it normal to feel low after a heart attack?\"","r":"\"Good point, and some sadness is normal. What tips it into depression is how long, how many symptoms and the effect: three weeks of no enjoyment, early waking, weight loss and guilt, and he's stopped rehab.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Could the bisoprolol be making him depressed?\"","r":"\"Sensible to ask. The link between beta-blockers and depression is weak, and stopping it would put his heart at risk. I'd note it, but not change it without cardiology.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why not citalopram? That's what my GP placement used.\"","r":"\"Fair, it's widely used. It can lengthen the QT interval, which matters more after a heart attack. Sertraline has the best safety data in heart disease, so it's my first choice here.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Would you want to speak to anyone else?\"","r":"\"Yes: his wife, with his permission, about how he was before and since; the nurses about sleep and eating; and his GP records for any past depression.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What sort of talking therapy would you offer him?\"","r":"\"Good question. Start with behavioural activation on the ward, linked to rehab goals. Then CBT through Talking Therapies' long-term conditions pathway, which can tackle his guilt and self-blame.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"It's quite sad, isn't it? He sounds like a lovely man.\"","r":"\"It is, and it's fine to feel that. He's also very treatable, which is the encouraging part. Shall we go through how we'll help him?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods: TFTs, B12, folate, LFTs, GGT, U&E (sodium), HbA1c; ECG with QTc before starting","Sertraline 50 mg each morning; review at 2 weeks; consider 100 mg after 4-6 weeks if partial response","With aspirin and clopidogrel: add lansoprazole 15-30 mg daily (not omeprazole, which weakens clopidogrel); check sodium at 2-4 weeks","Avoid tricyclics; avoid citalopram above standard doses because of QT prolongation","Nicotine patch plus a short-acting product, agreed with cardiology; dietitian review for poor intake"],"psych":["Behavioural activation now, with small daily goals tied to rehab and music","Talking Therapies long-term conditions pathway: CBT, around 16-20 sessions, addressing guilt and self-blame","Brief alcohol intervention now; motivational interviewing about drinking after discharge"],"social":["Cardiac rehab nurses: restart a paced programme with clear, small targets","Wife to bring in his guitar or music; side room if available to improve sleep","Fit note and occupational health for a phased return to work","Alcohol liaison nurse and stop smoking service before discharge; GP letter with plan"],"safety":["Repeat suicide enquiry at each review: older man, new illness, alcohol","On discharge: he and his wife have NHS 111 option 2 for urgent mental health help"]},"facts":[{"h":"Sertraline is first choice after MI","x":"Sertraline has the strongest safety evidence in coronary heart disease. Start 50 mg each morning. Avoid tricyclics; citalopram can prolong QT."},{"h":"SSRIs plus antiplatelets bleed","x":"SSRIs reduce platelet serotonin. With aspirin and clopidogrel, consider a PPI. Choose lansoprazole: omeprazole and esomeprazole reduce clopidogrel's effect (MHRA)."},{"h":"Check sodium in older adults","x":"SSRIs can cause hyponatraemia, mostly in the first month and in older people. Check U&E at baseline and 2-4 weeks; ask about confusion or drowsiness."},{"h":"Depression slows cardiac recovery","x":"Depression after a heart attack is common and is linked with lower rehab uptake, poorer adherence and worse cardiac outcomes. Treating it is part of cardiac care."},{"h":"Severity sets the first step","x":"NICE CG91: mild depression with a long-term condition starts with guided self-help, group activity or peer support. Moderate or worse: antidepressant or high-intensity therapy."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Management plan","do":"Present the case concisely, justify a moderate depressive episode, and give a specific biopsychosocial plan for the student."},"c-t-02":{"obj":["Acknowledged her frustration and established her understanding and main worries","Explained treatment-resistant schizophrenia and why clozapine is the right next step","Explained the cross-titration, the blood test schedule and the heart and physical checks","Described common and serious side effects with their warning signs, including constipation, infection and smoking","Agreed her role as a carer, answered her questions and checked her understanding"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Nobody tells me anything. I find out what's happening from Daniel shouting at me.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry, that must be really upsetting. Daniel has said I can talk to you, so let's put that right today. What would you like to know first?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I read online that clozapine can kill your blood. Is that true?\"","r":"\"I can see why that frightened you. In a small number of people it lowers the infection-fighting white cells. That's why every person has regular blood tests, so we catch it early and stop the drug.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"If it's so good, why wasn't it tried before?\"","r":"\"Fair question. Because it needs blood tests and close checks, guidelines say to try two other medicines first. Daniel has now had three, so clozapine is the right next step.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will it turn him into a zombie?\"","r":"\"That's a common worry. It can make him sleepy at first, especially in the mornings, but we increase it slowly so his body adjusts. Many people say they think more clearly once it works.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Couldn't he just have it as an injection, so he doesn't forget?\"","r":"\"I understand why you'd want that. There's no long-acting injection of clozapine, so it has to be tablets. We'll work with him on routines, and the blood tests help us see he's taking it.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"He's on so many tablets already. Is this just one more?\"","r":"\"Good point. As clozapine goes up, haloperidol comes down and stops. If it works, we'll aim to cut back the clonazepam and promethazine too.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Won't all these side effects mean he dies young?\"","r":"\"That's a very natural fear. Large studies show people on clozapine live longer on average than those on other antipsychotics, partly because it lowers suicide. We'll watch his weight, sugar and heart closely.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Register with the clozapine monitoring service; baseline FBC with neutrophils, U&E, LFTs, lipids, HbA1c, weight, BP, ECG, troponin and CRP","Clozapine 12.5 mg on day 1, rising by 25-50 mg a day as tolerated to about 300 mg a day over 2-3 weeks; haloperidol tapered as clozapine rises","FBC weekly for 18 weeks, fortnightly to week 52, then monthly; troponin and CRP weekly for the first 4 weeks; pulse, BP and temperature daily in titration","Laxative from day one (senna plus docusate); clonazepam reduced slowly; plasma level aiming for at least 350 micrograms/L","Hyoscine hydrobromide 300 micrograms at night if drooling; if doses are missed for more than 48 hours, restart at a low dose"],"psych":["Family intervention for Daniel and his mother: about 10 sessions over 3-12 months","CBT for psychosis once more settled: at least 16 sessions","Psychoeducation on clozapine, side effects and early relapse signs"],"social":["Carer's assessment by the local authority and a carers' support group (e.g. Rethink Mental Illness)","Named nurse and ward pharmacist to meet her with Daniel next week","Care coordinator to arrange clozapine clinic follow-up near home before discharge","Tell the team if Daniel starts or stops smoking: it changes clozapine levels sharply"],"safety":["Urgent review for fever, sore throat, flu-like illness, chest pain, breathlessness, fits, or no bowel opening for 3 days","Ward phone number now; NHS 111 option 2 after discharge"]},"facts":[{"h":"Blood tests: weekly, fortnightly, monthly","x":"FBC weekly for the first 18 weeks, fortnightly to one year, then monthly for as long as he takes it. No valid result, no supply."},{"h":"Constipation can kill","x":"Clozapine slows the gut. Severe constipation can cause obstruction and death (MHRA). Ask about bowels daily, give laxatives early, and act if no movement in 3 days."},{"h":"Myocarditis peaks early","x":"Most cases occur in the first two months. Watch for fever, fast pulse, chest pain or breathlessness; check troponin and CRP weekly for 4 weeks."},{"h":"Smoking changes the level","x":"Tobacco smoke speeds clozapine breakdown. Stopping smoking, even on a smoke-free ward, can raise levels sharply and cause toxicity. Recheck the level and reduce the dose."},{"h":"No depot exists","x":"There is no long-acting injection of clozapine. A short-acting injection exists only for special short-term use. Adherence to tablets is essential."},{"h":"Missed 48 hours means restart","x":"If more than 48 hours of doses are missed, restart from 12.5 mg and retitrate, because of the risk of low blood pressure and collapse."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explain why clozapine, how it starts, its monitoring and side effects, and her warning-sign role to Daniel's mother."},"c-t-03":{"obj":["Outlined the trauma sensitively, without forcing detail, and gave him control","Elicited re-experiencing, avoidance, numbing and hyperarousal with examples","Screened for depression, alcohol use, guilt and blast head injury","Assessed suicide risk, firearms access, anger at home and effect on work and family","Explained PTSD in plain words and that trauma-focused therapy works"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not a head case. My GP just made me come.\"","r":"\"I hear that, and plenty of people feel the same at first. Coming here doesn't make you a head case. Let's just see what's been happening, and you decide what to share.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is this going to end up with my commanding officer?\"","r":"\"That's a really reasonable worry. What you tell me stays confidential unless you agree, or there's a serious risk to you or someone else. I'll tell you before anything is shared.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"They were my lads. I chose that route.\"","r":"\"That's a heavy thing to carry. It makes sense you feel responsible for them. We don't need to go further today. Can I ask how that guilt shows up for you now?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Honestly, the whisky's the only thing that gets me to sleep.\"","r":"\"Thank you for being straight with me. A lot of people use drink to quieten things down. How much would you have on a typical night?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So is there anything that actually works, or do I just live with it?\"","r":"\"Yes, it works. A specific talking therapy called trauma-focused CBT helps most people, usually over 8 to 12 weekly sessions. Many get their sleep and their family life back.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want tablets turning me into a zombie.\"","r":"\"That's fine; tablets aren't the first choice for this. Therapy is. If you ever wanted medication alongside, there are options that don't sedate you, and it would be your decision.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No medication first-line: trauma-focused psychological therapy comes first","If he prefers medication or cannot engage in therapy: sertraline 50 mg once daily, up to 200 mg, or venlafaxine; review at 2-4 weeks","No benzodiazepines; prazosin not recommended by NICE","AUDIT, LFTs, GGT, FBC; alcohol reduction plan with GP; thiamine 100 mg three times daily if drinking stays heavy"],"psych":["Trauma-focused CBT: 8-12 weekly sessions, often 60-90 minutes, with a trained therapist (more if needed)","EMDR is an NICE option for non-combat trauma; for combat-related trauma, trauma-focused CBT is first choice","Brief alcohol intervention now; motivational interviewing to cut down before trauma work","Psychoeducation about PTSD for him and, with consent, his wife"],"social":["With consent, liaise with his military GP and Department of Community Mental Health (DCMH)","If he leaves the forces: Op COURAGE, the NHS veterans' mental health service","SSAFA and Combat Stress for peer and family support; carer support for his wife","Work adjustments through his chain of command only with his consent; GP letter"],"safety":["Crisis plan: Combat Stress 24-hour helpline, NHS 111 option 2, 999 in an emergency","Recheck firearms access if circumstances change; avoid drinking when distressed"]},"facts":[{"h":"Flashbacks happen in the present","x":"In PTSD, reliving feels as if it is happening now, with fear and body reactions. Simply remembering a bad event, even often, is not a flashback."},{"h":"Combat trauma: trauma-focused CBT first","x":"NICE NG116 recommends trauma-focused CBT for PTSD. EMDR is recommended after non-combat trauma; evidence in combat-related PTSD was weaker."},{"h":"No benzodiazepines in PTSD","x":"NICE advises against benzodiazepines; they may worsen outcomes and are risky with alcohol. If medication is wanted: sertraline or venlafaxine."},{"h":"Ask about weapons directly","x":"Serving personnel and veterans may have service or sporting firearms. Ask about access at home and at work, and any firearm certificate."},{"h":"Ask about blast injury","x":"Blast exposure can cause mild traumatic brain injury. Ask about loss of consciousness, headaches and memory problems; it overlaps with PTSD symptoms."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"Elicit all PTSD clusters, alcohol use and firearms risk without forcing trauma detail, then explain the diagnosis."},"c-t-04":{"obj":["Established the attempt in sequence: trigger, planning, note, alcohol, method, expected outcome and how it stopped","Assessed current intent, feelings about surviving, plans and access to means","Explored mood, hopelessness, alcohol, past self-harm, impulsivity and a psychosis screen","Identified protective factors and formulated the risk clearly","Agreed a safe plan with him, including admission, his sister and crisis contacts"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm fine now. Can I just go home?\"","r":"\"I can hear you want to get out of here, and it's been a long night. Last night was very serious, though, so give me twenty minutes to understand it. Then we'll plan together.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you going to lock me up?\"","r":"\"That's a fair thing to worry about. You're not in trouble. My aim is to keep you safe, and I'd much rather we agree a plan together than anything being forced.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I lost my job, then she left. There's nothing left.\"","r":"\"That's two huge losses in a few weeks, and it makes sense you feel like that. I want to understand how bad it's got. Are you still thinking about ending your life?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I wrote a note on my phone. To my sister.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that helps me understand how serious things were. Can I ask what the note said, and when you wrote it?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I feel stupid. That poor woman on the platform.\"","r":"\"You're not stupid; you were in a terrible place. She did the right thing, and you let her help. That matters. Let's think about what would help now.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"If I promise not to do anything, can I go?\"","r":"\"I appreciate you offering that. But a promise isn't enough when you're unsure you're glad to be alive and you'd be on your own. Let's find a plan that actually keeps you safe.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Breath alcohol now, bloods including LFTs, GGT, FBC; reassess mood once sober for several days","CIWA-Ar monitoring and thiamine (oral 100 mg three times daily, or Pabrinex if at risk) as he drinks daily","No new antidepressant tonight; if a depressive episode is confirmed, sertraline 50 mg once daily with small supplies","Review any tablets at home; dispense small quantities only"],"psych":["Collaborative written safety plan before he leaves the ward: triggers, warning signs, coping, people, numbers","CBT for depression (around 16-20 sessions) once the crisis settles","Brief alcohol intervention now; referral to the local alcohol service"],"social":["Informal admission tonight; home treatment team at discharge, ideally staying with his sister","With consent, phone his sister Elena in Leeds tonight","Ward social worker: Jobcentre, benefits, housing and debt advice (Citizens Advice)","GP letter today; follow-up within 72 hours of any discharge"],"safety":["Eyesight observation in ED until the bed is ready; security aware; MHA assessment if he insists on leaving and risk remains high","Crisis numbers: NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123, 999 if in immediate danger"]},"facts":[{"h":"Ask about final acts","x":"A note, saying goodbye, giving things away or settling affairs show planning and intent. Ask directly: 'Did you write anything, or do anything to prepare?'"},{"h":"Survival feelings predict risk","x":"Regret at surviving, or ambivalence ('not sure'), signals ongoing risk. Relief, and clear reasons to live, are more reassuring, though not a guarantee."},{"h":"No risk scores for decisions","x":"NICE NG225: risk scales and tools should not be used to predict suicide or to decide on treatment or discharge. Base care on a full psychosocial assessment."},{"h":"Section 5(2) is for inpatients","x":"Section 5(2) cannot be used in A&E. If an informal patient lacks capacity to leave, the MCA may apply; otherwise arrange an MHA assessment."},{"h":"Alcohol raises suicide risk","x":"Alcohol increases impulsivity and lethality and deepens low mood. Ask about intake last night and daily, and plan for withdrawal."}],"type":"Risk assessment + Management plan","do":"Assess the attempt, current intent, lead-up, background and protection, then formulate risk and agree a safe plan."},"c-t-05":{"obj":["Assessed current depressive symptoms and severity, and screened for bipolar and psychotic features","Established each treatment's dose, duration, adherence, side effects and response, and the therapy history","Explored contributing factors and assessed risk, including passive death wishes","Explained treatment-resistant depression with hope and agreed a specific next step with monitoring and follow-up"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I'm starting to think nothing works for me.\"","r":"\"After eight months of trying, that's completely understandable. I want to be honest: there are still several good options, and most people do find something that helps. Let's work out what's happened so far.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I gave up on the therapy. Maybe it's my fault.\"","r":"\"It isn't your fault. Exhaustion is part of depression, and it makes therapy hard. We can try it again in a way that fits your energy, perhaps once things lift a little.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Some nights I hope I just won't wake up.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; I know that's hard to say. It tells me how heavy this is. Have those thoughts ever gone further, to thinking about ending your life?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Antipsychotic? I'm not mad.\"","r":"\"You're not, and I'm sorry if it sounded that way. At a small dose, aripiprazole boosts antidepressants. It's used this way all the time for depression that hasn't lifted.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will I need blood tests for all this?\"","r":"\"Good question. For aripiprazole, a few at the start and after three months: weight, sugar and cholesterol. Lithium needs regular level checks, which is why some people prefer other options.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What about ECT? I've heard it's for people like me.\"","r":"\"It's a fair question. ECT works well for very severe depression or when other options haven't. You've still got several good options first, so I'd keep it in reserve.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods already normal; repeat only if new symptoms; baseline weight, BP, HbA1c and lipids before an antipsychotic","Agreed: keep mirtazapine 30 mg at night; add aripiprazole 2.5 mg each morning, 5 mg after 1 week, up to 10 mg; warn about restlessness (akathisia), nausea and insomnia","Alternatives: mirtazapine to 45 mg; lithium aiming 0.4-0.8 mmol/L (U&E, eGFR, TFTs, calcium, ECG if cardiac risk; level 12 hours post dose, weekly until stable); quetiapine 150-300 mg at night","Or switch to venlafaxine 75 mg rising to 150-225 mg with BP checks; or combine an SSRI with mirtazapine","If severe and unresponsive to these: discuss ECT"],"psych":["High-intensity CBT or behavioural activation through CMHT psychology, 16-20 sessions, shorter sessions at first","Combined medication and therapy, as advised for more severe depression","Relapse prevention plan once well, given this is her third episode"],"social":["Outpatient review: phone at 2 weeks, clinic at 4 weeks; care coordinator if risk or needs rise","Occupational health and fit note; phased return to work when ready","With consent, involve her husband in the safety plan and review","GP shared care letter with doses and monitoring"],"safety":["Safety plan: warning signs, her husband, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123","Small supplies if risk rises; review sooner if passive thoughts become active"]},"facts":[{"h":"Two adequate trials define resistance","x":"Treatment-resistant depression: no adequate response to two antidepressants at an effective dose for at least 4-6 weeks each, taken as prescribed."},{"h":"Check pseudo-resistance first","x":"Missed doses, low doses, short trials, alcohol, thyroid disease or a missed bipolar diagnosis cause apparent non-response. Ask about each before labelling it resistant."},{"h":"NICE next steps","x":"NICE NG222: increase the dose, switch, combine with another antidepressant, or augment with lithium or an antipsychotic such as aripiprazole or quetiapine."},{"h":"Aripiprazole: watch for akathisia","x":"Low-dose aripiprazole augmentation is usually well tolerated. Restlessness and an urge to move are the commonest problems: reduce the dose if it occurs."},{"h":"Lithium levels for augmentation","x":"Aim for 0.4-0.8 mmol/L, 12 hours after the dose. Check weekly after each change, then every 3 months in year one; U&E and TFTs every 6 months."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Management plan","do":"Confirm treatment-resistant depression from dose, duration, adherence and response, assess risk, and agree a next step with her."},"c-t-06":{"obj":["Established his understanding of the collapse, his diabetes and why insulin is advised, and filled the gaps in plain words","Tested retention later in the conversation and explored how he uses and weighs the risks against his reasons","Elicited the belief behind the refusal, rated its conviction and origin, and screened for delirium, low mood and suicidal thinking","Reached a decision- and time-specific conclusion using the MCA test and explained it to him without blame","Agreed less restrictive next steps: oral treatment to discuss with the diabetes team, family involvement and daily reassessment"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"So they've sent the psychiatrist. You all think I'm mad.\"","r":"\"I can see why it feels like that, and I'm sorry you were told you couldn't go out until I came. I don't start from the idea that you can't decide. I want to hear your reasons.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"It's my body. I can say no if I want.\"","r":"\"You're right, and many people refuse treatment and that's their choice. My job is only to check you've got what you need to make that choice. Can I ask what you've been told?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"It's not the insulin. The nurses put chips in the bottles.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me that. That must feel frightening. What do the chips do, and when did you first notice them?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are they going to hold me down and inject me?\"","r":"\"No one's going to rush you or hold you down. First we'll look for a treatment you can accept, and anything beyond that would need careful legal steps and your family's views.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"I'll take tablets. Tablets can't carry chips.\"","r":"\"That's really helpful to know. I can't promise tablets are enough for your sugar, but I'll ask the diabetes team today whether they're a safe option for now.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"So you've decided I'm mad. That's it then.\"","r":"\"That's a hard thing to hear, and I understand why you're angry. It isn't about you as a person. It's only this one decision, today, and I'll come back to see if it changes.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why do I need injections? I've managed with diet before.\"","r":"\"That's a fair question. Your sugar got so high you collapsed and became very dried out. Insulin brings it down most reliably now. The diabetes team can explain whether that changes later.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Best-interests decision under the MCA led by the endocrinology consultant; least restrictive option first; no restraint for insulin without senior and legal advice","Ask endocrinology about oral treatment he would accept, e.g. gliclazide started at 40 mg with breakfast, with capillary glucose four times daily and hypo checks","Antipsychotic review by the ward or community consultant: olanzapine raises glucose and weight; consider switching to aripiprazole 10 mg once daily if clinically appropriate","HbA1c, U&E, osmolality as directed by the medical team; 4AT daily while on the ward"],"psych":["Consistent staff and calm, honest explanations; avoid arguing with the belief","Diabetes specialist nurse education with simple written information once more settled","Reassess capacity daily and record each review; the conclusion may change as the psychosis settles"],"social":["Consult his sister Abena (consent given) about his wishes and values; IMCA if nobody appropriate is available for serious medical treatment","Inform the CMHT care coordinator today; plan district nurse or supported medicine help after discharge","Consultant to consider a Mental Health Act assessment if his psychosis needs treatment in its own right"],"safety":["Medical team monitor glucose and fluids closely while insulin is refused; escalate early if osmolality or glucose rises","Escorted smoking breaks agreed with the nurse in charge; if he tries to leave, assess capacity for that decision and seek senior advice","Full capacity note in the record today, with his exact words for each part of the test"]},"facts":[{"h":"Unwise is not incapable","x":"MCA section 1(4): a person is not treated as lacking capacity merely because the decision is unwise. Name the failing part of the test and quote his words."},{"h":"Functional test first, then cause","x":"Since the 2021 Supreme Court JB judgment: can he understand, retain, use or weigh, and communicate? If not, is that caused by an impairment of mind or brain?"},{"h":"MHA does not cover his diabetes","x":"The Mental Health Act authorises treatment for mental disorder. Insulin for diabetes not caused by the mental disorder falls under the MCA best-interests framework."},{"h":"Retention only needs to last","x":"He must hold the facts long enough to make the decision. Ask him to repeat the key risks near the end of the conversation, not straight after you explain."},{"h":"Olanzapine worsens glucose control","x":"Olanzapine and clozapine carry the highest risk of weight gain and raised glucose. Flag it to the consultant when diabetes is poorly controlled."},{"h":"IMCA for serious treatment","x":"If a person lacking capacity has nobody appropriate to consult, the NHS body must instruct an Independent Mental Capacity Advocate before serious medical treatment."}],"type":"Capacity assessment","do":"Test all four parts of capacity for refusing insulin, find the cause of any failure, and record a decision-specific conclusion."},"c-t-07":{"obj":["Let her voice her anger and fear, validated it, and took her account of the six months before admission","Explained first-episode psychosis using Owen's own experiences, why depression is not the main problem, and how psychosis differs from schizophrenia","Explained the likely causes without blame, the ward checks, and olanzapine: benefits, side effects, monitoring and duration","Agreed removal of knives and sharps before home leave and gave crisis contacts","Described Early Intervention care and a realistic outlook, checked her understanding, and offered written information and a family meeting"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Nobody's told me anything. I had to hear 'schizophrenia' from a nurse at the desk.\"","r":"\"I'm really sorry. You shouldn't have heard it like that. Owen's happy for me to talk openly, so let's put that right now. Tell me everything that's worried you first.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"He's depressed. He's been depressed since uni. That's all it is.\"","r":"\"It made sense to think that: pulling away and not washing can be depression. But the voice and the microchip belief point somewhere else. Can I explain what we found?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Schizophrenics are violent. Is my son dangerous?\"","r":"\"That's a common fear, and the media feed it. Most people with psychosis are never violent; they're more often hurt by others. His risk right now is mostly to himself, and we're managing that.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have seen it. I thought he was being a lazy teenager.\"","r":"\"So many parents say that. Early on it looks just like depression or pulling away. You noticed, and when it mattered you got him help.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will he have to take these tablets forever?\"","r":"\"Good question. We usually advise at least one to two years once he's well, because stopping early is the main cause of relapse. Any reduction would be planned slowly, with him.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I keep seeing him with that knife. I thought I'd lost him.\"","r":"\"That sounds terrifying, and it's no wonder it's still with you. He's safe here. Before any leave we'll plan together, including putting the knives away.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will he ever go back to university?\"","r":"\"Many young people do. The early intervention team helps with exactly that, alongside treatment. Staying off cannabis gives him the best chance.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Olanzapine 5 mg at night, increased to 10 mg if needed after review (usual range 5-20 mg); lowest effective dose in a first episode","Baseline weight, waist, pulse, BP, HbA1c or fasting glucose, lipids, prolactin and ECG (inpatient); weight weekly for 6 weeks, then all again at 12 weeks and yearly","Warn: sleepiness and hunger; dietitian and activity support from the start to limit weight gain","FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, urine drug screen; MRI brain only if neurological signs or atypical features","Continue antipsychotic for 1-2 years after recovery before any planned, gradual reduction"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis, at least 16 planned sessions, delivered by the EIP psychologist","Family intervention, at least 10 sessions over 3-12 months, with Owen and both parents","Psychoeducation and a written relapse-signature plan built with Owen and his parents","Cannabis: brief intervention on the ward and support to stop, as continued use strongly predicts relapse"],"social":["Early Intervention in Psychosis team for up to 3 years, with a named care coordinator","Carer's assessment for Bethan and her husband by the local authority; carers' group and Rethink Mental Illness information","Individual Placement and Support and university student support when ready to return to study","Named nurse to phone Bethan after each ward round"],"safety":["Remove or lock away knives, sharps and stored medicines at home before any leave; risk reviewed by the team before each leave","Crisis contacts: ward number, local crisis line or NHS 111 option 2, 999 if immediate danger","Ward observations as set by the team; family meeting with the consultant booked this week"]},"facts":[{"h":"Psychosis is not a diagnosis","x":"Psychosis describes hallucinations and delusions. Schizophrenia, bipolar disorder, severe depression and drugs can all cause it. After one week, call it first-episode psychosis."},{"h":"The prodrome mimics depression","x":"Months of withdrawal, poor self-care, falling grades and lost interest often come before voices or delusions. Families and GPs commonly read it as depression."},{"h":"Cannabis raises the risk","x":"Frequent use of high-potency cannabis is linked with a several-fold higher risk of psychosis, and carrying on after a first episode increases relapse."},{"h":"EIP within two weeks","x":"The national standard: people with first-episode psychosis start treatment with an Early Intervention team within two weeks of referral, with CBTp, family intervention and physical health checks."},{"h":"Violence myth: correct it gently","x":"Most people with psychosis are never violent and are more likely to be victims of crime. Say so plainly; relatives often fear it most."},{"h":"Olanzapine: hunger before weight","x":"Appetite rises within weeks and weight gain is common in young people. Weigh weekly for six weeks and offer diet and activity support from the start."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Collateral history","do":"Hear her out, explain first-episode psychosis and its treatment plainly, agree home safety steps and check her understanding."},"c-t-08":{"obj":["Explored what he read and how it has affected him, and confirmed honestly that the gap is real and why","Elicited his personal risk factors: smoking, weight, activity, diet, BP, glucose, lipids, constipation and family history","Agreed a smoking-cessation plan and explained the clozapine level rise and the level checks before and after quitting","Agreed one or two practical lifestyle goals and the monitoring that the clinic and his GP will do","Checked his mood and understanding, safety-netted for clozapine toxicity and bowel problems, and arranged review"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"I've done everything I was told. Why did nobody ever tell me this?\"","r":"\"That's a fair question, and I'm sorry it wasn't discussed. You've clearly looked after yourself. Let's use today to make up for it and plan what will make a real difference.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"So I'm going to die at sixty?\"","r":"\"No. Those figures are averages, not your fate. Much of the gap comes from smoking, weight and missed checks, and you can change those. That's what we're here for.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it the clozapine killing me? Should I come off it?\"","r":"\"I understand why you'd wonder. Clozapine does cause hunger and weight gain, but it keeps you well, and people on it actually live longer overall than those not treated. Let's manage the side effects instead.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I'm stopping smoking this weekend. Cold turkey.\"","r":"\"That's brilliant, and it's the best thing you can do. We just need to plan it, because stopping can make your clozapine level jump. Can we set a date and check your level first?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't need blood pressure tablets. I feel fine.\"","r":"\"Most people with high blood pressure feel completely fine; that's why it's missed. With your dad's stroke and smoking, it matters more. Would you be willing to talk it through with your GP?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I can't afford a gym, and there's nothing to do at the house.\"","r":"\"That's a real barrier. A gym isn't needed. Brisk walking counts, and there's a free walking group. Would your key worker come with you the first few times?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Keep clozapine; never stop suddenly. Plasma level before quitting and about 1 week after; reduce dose if level rises, guided by the level and symptoms","Combination NRT (24-hour patch 21 mg plus lozenges or inhalator) or varenicline 0.5 mg once daily, titrated to 1 mg twice daily, for 12 weeks","Constipation: senna 15 mg at night plus docusate 100 mg twice daily; avoid bulk-forming laxatives; review bowel habit at every visit","Yearly: weight, BMI, waist, BP, HbA1c, fasting lipids, LFT, ECG, plus QRISK3; FBC every 4 weeks via clozapine monitoring","GP to confirm BP with home or ambulatory readings and treat; offer atorvastatin 20 mg if QRISK3 is 10% or more; consider metformin with GP if weight and HbA1c keep rising"],"psych":["Motivational approach: two goals he chose (stop smoking, walk three times a week), reviewed monthly","Stop-smoking adviser weekly for 12 weeks, with carbon monoxide readings","Mood check today and at review; reassure about continuing clozapine"],"social":["Care coordinator or key worker to go with him to a free walking group, 3 times a week, for the first month","Accommodation staff to help with shopping lists and simple healthy cooking; dietitian referral","GP: annual SMI physical health check, NHS Diabetes Prevention Programme for HbA1c 42-47, dental check every 6-12 months"],"safety":["After quitting, report drowsiness, drooling, confusion, muscle jerks or fits urgently (clozapine toxicity)","No bowel opening for 3 days, abdominal pain, swelling or vomiting: same-day medical help","Fever, sore throat or chest pain: urgent FBC and review; follow-up clinic in 4 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Smoke, not nicotine, lowers clozapine levels","x":"Chemicals in tobacco smoke induce CYP1A2, which clears clozapine. NRT and vapes do not. Stopping smoking can raise levels sharply within days."},{"h":"Check level before and after quitting","x":"Take a clozapine level before the quit date and about one week after. Reduce the dose if it rises; warn about drowsiness, drooling, confusion and seizures."},{"h":"Ask about bowels every visit","x":"Clozapine slows gut movement; obstruction can be fatal. Use a stimulant plus softener, avoid bulk-forming laxatives, and act if no opening for three days."},{"h":"QRISK3 counts his illness","x":"QRISK3 includes severe mental illness and atypical antipsychotic use as risk factors. A score of 10% or more means offering a statin."},{"h":"HbA1c 42-47 is a warning","x":"This range means raised diabetes risk. Refer to the NHS Diabetes Prevention Programme; 48 mmol/mol or more on repeat means type 2 diabetes."},{"h":"The gap is mainly preventable","x":"Heart disease, stroke, lung disease and diabetes account for most of the excess deaths. Smoking is the biggest single changeable cause."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Management plan","do":"Explain the mortality gap, map his cardiac and metabolic risks, and plan safe smoking cessation and monitoring."},"c-t-09":{"obj":["Established the injury: mechanism, loss of consciousness, length of the memory gap, scans, ICU stay and surgery","Described the change in behaviour with examples and timing, against his previous personality, work and mental health","Took a detailed alcohol and drug history including last drink and withdrawal signs, and asked whether the crash could have been deliberate","Assessed current risks: aggression, leaving the ward, self-harm and driving","Explained the likely causes and next steps in plain words, addressed her worries honestly, and gave support and a review date"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"He's not himself at all. He was always so gentle.\"","r":"\"That must be really frightening to see. You know him best, which is exactly why I wanted to talk to you. Can you tell me what he was like before?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I'm so embarrassed. The things he's saying to those young nurses.\"","r":"\"I understand, and the staff do too. Comments like that are very often part of the illness or the injury, not who he is. It helps us to know it's out of character.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is this brain damage? Will I get my son back?\"","r":"\"That's the big question, and it's too early to say for sure. Some of this looks like confusion that usually clears with treatment. Brain injury changes often improve over months. We'll know much more in a few weeks.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"He only has a few drinks after work. Everyone does.\"","r":"\"That's really common, and I'm not judging him. I ask because stopping suddenly after regular drinking can cause exactly this kind of confusion, and it's very treatable. Roughly how much is a few?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why don't they just give him something to calm him down?\"","r":"\"I can see why you'd want that. If it's partly alcohol withdrawal, the right medicine is a specific sedative plus vitamins, not a general tranquilliser. We'd rather treat the cause.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"He's been drinking more since Jess left. He was in a bad way.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me that, it's important. Did he ever say anything about wishing you weren't here, or about hurting himself?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Delirium screen: FBC, CRP, U&E, sodium, calcium, magnesium, LFT, glucose, cultures, urinalysis, chest X-ray; check wounds and chest drain site","CIWA-Ar 1- to 4-hourly; chlordiazepoxide by symptom-triggered or fixed reducing regime per local protocol; IV Pabrinex (2 pairs three times daily for 3-5 days), then oral thiamine 100 mg three times daily","Review morphine PCA and any anticholinergic or sedating drugs with the surgical team; good analgesia, treat constipation and urinary retention","Discuss repeat CT or MRI brain with the surgical team or neurosurgery given persistent confusion and change","Avoid antipsychotics where possible; if severe distress or risk despite non-drug measures, haloperidol 0.5 mg orally, ECG first, shortest course"],"psych":["Non-drug delirium care: orientation, clock, glasses, family visits, same nurses, quiet and lit appropriately at night","Neuropsychology assessment of attention, memory and executive function once delirium has cleared","Behaviour plan with ward staff: calm, short instructions; do not argue; note triggers","Alcohol: brief intervention once clear-headed; referral to the community alcohol service"],"social":["Headway information and helpline for Maureen; carer's assessment if he needs ongoing support","Fit note; business partner informed with Liam's consent","Community brain injury or neurorehabilitation team referral before discharge"],"safety":["Enhanced nursing observation at night; falls precautions; capacity assessment if he tries to leave","No driving or riding until cleared; tell the DVLA if notifiable","Liaison review again on Monday, sooner if worse; mother has the ward number"]},"facts":[{"h":"Memory gap length grades severity","x":"Post-traumatic amnesia over 24 hours points to at least a moderate brain injury; over a week, severe. Ask the family for the last and first things he remembers."},{"h":"Day 3 to 4: think withdrawal","x":"Delirium tremens usually starts 48 to 72 hours after the last drink. Trauma patients often arrive unable to give an alcohol history; relatives can."},{"h":"Pabrinex before glucose","x":"Give parenteral thiamine to heavy drinkers at risk of Wernicke's encephalopathy before or with any glucose, then continue oral thiamine."},{"h":"Normal first CT is not the end","x":"Diffuse axonal injury and delayed bleeds can be missed on the first scan. New or persisting confusion or change needs repeat imaging, usually MRI."},{"h":"Frontal injury looks like rudeness","x":"Disinhibition, crude remarks, poor judgement, irritability and apathy are typical frontal-lobe effects. Judge them only once delirium has cleared."}],"type":"Collateral history","do":"Take a collateral covering injury severity, change, baseline, alcohol and risks; explain likely causes and next steps."},"c-t-10":{"obj":["Took a clear timeline of the recent deterioration, with specific symptoms, behaviours and self-care","Established past psychiatric history, relapse triggers, medication adherence, reasons for stopping and previous response","Quantified substance use and covered physical health, forensic history and social circumstances","Assessed risk to others, to self, self-neglect, children in contact and driving","Agreed an initial ward plan, summarised back to Paul and set how ward and community team will stay in touch"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I keep thinking I should have pushed for this a week earlier.\"","r":"\"That's understandable, and it shows you care. You noticed the change, you escalated it, and he's safe now. What you tell me today matters more than anything.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"It's all in the notes. Do you really need me to go through it?\"","r":"\"I know you're busy, and thanks for coming. The notes don't have the last two weeks; you do. Ten minutes in a set order and I'll have what I need.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Are you going to put him on a depot this time?\"","r":"\"It's a fair question, given the pattern. We'll restart what worked first, then discuss a long-acting injection with him once he's settled enough to choose. I'd value you being part of that.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"He did say once, 'Might as well end it if they keep watching.'\"","r":"\"Thank you, that's important and I'll make sure the ward knows. Did he say anything about how, or do anything to prepare?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"He's been sleeping in his car in this cold. It's grim.\"","r":"\"That sounds really hard to see. It tells us how frightened he's been of the flat. We'll start housing support early so he isn't going back to the same door.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What about his little girl? She's meant to stay this Saturday.\"","r":"\"Thanks for raising it. That contact needs to pause while he's this unwell. I'll speak with the ward social worker today about informing her mum and children's services, following policy.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Restart olanzapine 10 mg at night (his previous effective dose; range 5-20 mg), under Section 63 if he declines after a proper attempt at consent","Lorazepam 1 mg orally as needed for agitation, up to 4 mg in 24 hours; rapid tranquillisation only per NICE NG10 with post-dose monitoring","Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin; urine drug screen; ECG; weight and BMI on admission","CIWA-Ar for 72 hours given 4 cans a day; NRT on admission for 20 cigarettes a day","Once settled, discuss a long-acting injection and weight management (dietitian, consider metformin) to tackle the reasons he stopped"],"psych":["Named nurse one-to-one daily; engagement before challenge of beliefs","Cannabis brief intervention on the ward and referral to the substance misuse service","CBT for psychosis and family work with his mother when settled"],"social":["Care coordinator Paul to attend weekly ward review; CPA review before any discharge","Ward social worker: housing officer contact about a move away from the neighbour; benefits check","Ward social worker to discuss safeguarding of his daughter (7) and inform her mother via policy"],"safety":["Intermittent observations every 15 minutes, reviewed daily; search of belongings on admission","No Section 17 leave until RC review; rights explained, IMHA offered","Advise not to drive; record DVLA advice; police and neighbour information-sharing per trust policy"]},"facts":[{"h":"Ask why, not just whether","x":"Weight gain, sexual side effects, sedation and feeling flat are common reasons for stopping. Treat the reason, or the next relapse is already set up."},{"h":"Quantify cannabis by cost and type","x":"Ask type (skunk or resin), amount, frequency and spend per day. High-potency daily use after a first episode sharply increases relapse."},{"h":"Section 63 for three months","x":"Under Section 3, treatment for mental disorder can be given without consent for the first three months; then a T2 (consent) or T3 (SOAD) certificate is required."},{"h":"Children are part of risk","x":"Always ask who lives with or visits the patient. A child in his delusional system or care while he is unwell needs a safeguarding discussion."},{"h":"Psychosis and driving","x":"Patients must not drive during an acute psychotic episode and must tell the DVLA. Record the advice; GMC guidance permits disclosure if they continue."}],"type":"Collateral history + Handover and escalation","do":"Take a structured collateral from the CPN covering change, background, medication, substances and risks, then agree a plan."},"c-t-11":{"obj":["Reconstructed the attempt in time order: planning, research, choice of a distant hotel, precautions, letters, will, method, alcohol and how he was found","Explored his bright mood: regret at surviving, relief of a decision made and ongoing hopelessness","Screened for a depressive episode with biological symptoms, psychotic features and alcohol misuse, and identified the hidden debt","Weighed risk against protective factors, including family history and means at home, and judged the risk high","Explained clearly why he should stay, agreed informal admission, and set out the legal route if he refused"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"Honestly, doctor, it was a moment of madness. I'm fine now.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you feel steadier this morning. From what the notes say, though, it wasn't quick; you travelled a long way. Help me understand how it got to that point.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I've waited three hours. I just want to go home.\"","r":"\"That's completely understandable, and thank you for waiting. Last night nearly ended your life, so I'd like twenty minutes to understand it properly. Then we'll talk about what happens next, together.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"The business went under. I owe ninety grand and Denise doesn't know.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; I can hear how much you've been carrying on your own. Debt like that feels endless, but there is real help with it. Has anything about it changed since last night?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I've let them all down. They'd be better off with the insurance.\"","r":"\"It sounds as if shame is speaking very loudly right now. Many people in your position feel exactly that, and it's often part of depression. Your sons would lose their dad, not a debt.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You can't keep me here. I haven't done anything wrong.\"","r":"\"You haven't done anything wrong, and this isn't a punishment. I'm worried you're still at risk. I'd much rather you stay by choice; if you can't, I'd need to ask colleagues to assess you under the Mental Health Act.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If Denise finds out about the money, that's it. She'll leave.\"","r":"\"That fear makes sense. You don't have to face that conversation alone or today. We can help you plan how and when to tell her. For now, can I let her know you're safe in hospital?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Informal admission today; if he refuses and the risk stays high, MHA assessment for Section 2","Bloods: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, GGT, TFTs, B12, folate, calcium, glucose; record breath alcohol","CIWA-Ar every 4 hours; chlordiazepoxide by symptom-triggered protocol if withdrawal appears; oral thiamine 100 mg three times daily (Pabrinex if Wernicke's suspected)","Once depression confirmed: sertraline 50 mg in the morning, review at 1 to 2 weeks for agitation and suicidal thoughts; mirtazapine 15 mg at night if sleep and appetite dominate"],"psych":["Daily one-to-one time with his named nurse; build a safety plan before any leave","CBT for depression once settled; problem-solving sessions on the debt and the business loss","Alcohol: brief intervention on the ward, then the community alcohol service"],"social":["With consent, tell his wife he is safe in hospital; he chooses how and when to tell her about the debt, with the ward social worker","Debt advice through StepChange or Citizens Advice; AMHP can certify Mental Health Crisis Breathing Space to pause creditor action","Bed manager and his local crisis team: transfer to a bed near home","GP informed today"],"safety":["One-to-one observation within arm's length in the ED; remove cords, belts and bag straps","Security aware; if he tries to leave, the doctor and senior are called at once","Ask family to remove rope and tools from the garage before any home leave; review risk daily"]},"facts":[{"h":"Calm after a decision is a warning","x":"Relief that comes from having a plan can look like recovery. Ask what has changed since the attempt. If nothing has, the calm is not reassuring."},{"h":"No holding powers in the ED","x":"Section 5(2) and 5(4) apply only to inpatients on a ward. In the ED use an urgent MHA assessment, and meanwhile the MCA or common law, with senior advice."},{"h":"Signs of high intent","x":"Planning, research, isolation, steps to avoid discovery, final acts such as letters or a will, a violent method, and being found by chance. Ask about each one."},{"h":"Hanging is the commonest method","x":"Hanging and strangulation are the most common suicide method in England and Wales, and among the most lethal. Surviving one usually reflects chance, not low intent."},{"h":"Risk scales do not decide discharge","x":"NICE NG225: risk scales should not be used to forecast suicide or to decide who goes home. Base the decision on a full psychosocial assessment."},{"h":"Breathing Space pauses creditors","x":"Mental Health Crisis Breathing Space protects people receiving crisis care from most creditor action for the length of treatment plus 30 days. An AMHP certifies it."}],"type":"Risk assessment + History to a diagnosis","do":"Reconstruct the hanging attempt, explain his bright mood, screen for depression, weigh risk and agree admission."},"c-t-12":{"obj":["Asked her name and pronouns at the outset and used them throughout","Traced her gender history from childhood, through puberty, to two years of living as Nadia, with her support network","Explored her goals and expectations for hormones and surgery, including voice, facial hair and fertility","Screened mood, anxiety, psychosis, OCD and body dysmorphia, and asked about suicidal thoughts, self-surgery, online hormones, assault and sexual health","Explained the next steps: bloods, fertility referral, voice therapy, peer support and the second assessment"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why are you asking if I hear voices? Do you think I'm mad because I'm trans?\"","r":"\"No, not at all, and I'm sorry it came across that way. I ask everyone the same questions so nothing gets missed. Being trans isn't a mental illness.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So when can I start hormones? I've waited years for this.\"","r":"\"You have waited a long time, and I want to keep things moving. First come baseline bloods and a second assessment; then hormones can be recommended, often prescribed by your GP. I'll be clear about each step.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"A friend gets oestrogen online. I've been tempted, honestly.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; lots of people feel that pull while they wait. Unmonitored hormones can cause clots and liver problems. Let's get your bloods done now so the safe route moves faster.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I was beaten up on a bus last year. I still get scared going out.\"","r":"\"That's awful, and it makes sense you feel scared. You didn't deserve that. Are you having nightmares or flashbacks? And would it help to talk to someone about feeling safe again?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will hormones make my voice higher?\"","r":"\"That's a really common hope. Oestrogen doesn't change the voice, because the vocal cords have already changed. Voice therapy with a speech and language therapist works well, and I can refer you.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't stand my body down there. It disgusts me.\"","r":"\"That sounds really painful to live with every day. Have you ever thought about harming that part of your body, or tried to? There are safe options, and we'll support you through them.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Baseline bloods now: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, lipids, HbA1c, testosterone, oestradiol, prolactin; BP and BMI","After hormones are recommended: transdermal oestradiol (gel or patches), safer for clots than oral, with a GnRH analogue (e.g. triptorelin 11.25 mg every 12 weeks) if testosterone stays high","Monitor oestradiol, testosterone, prolactin, LFTs and lipids every 3 to 6 months in year one, then yearly","Smoking cessation now: oestrogen plus smoking raises clot risk; NHS stop-smoking service and nicotine replacement"],"psych":["Optional psychology sessions on coping with stigma and anxiety after the assault","Voice and communication therapy with a speech and language therapist, usually several sessions","Time to reflect on surgical expectations before any surgical referral"],"social":["NHS fertility clinic referral for sperm storage before hormones","GP to update her name and gender on the NHS record","Peer support: local trans support group, LGBT Foundation; Gender Recognition Certificate information if she wants it","Equality Act protection at work; advice from her union or ACAS if problems arise"],"safety":["Advise against online hormones; if she starts them, tell the clinic so bloods can be checked","If distressed or suicidal: GP, NHS 111 option 2, or Samaritans 116 123","Sexual health clinic offered for a routine check"]},"facts":[{"h":"Ask pronouns, then never slip","x":"Ask her name and pronouns in your first line and use them every time, in speech, notes and letters. A single slip can undo trust; correct yourself briefly."},{"h":"Not a mental disorder in ICD-11","x":"ICD-11 moved gender incongruence out of mental disorders into a chapter on sexual health. You assess needs and readiness, and screen for other problems alongside."},{"h":"Oestrogen won't raise the voice","x":"Voice pitch is fixed after puberty; oestrogen does not change it. Refer to a speech and language therapist. Facial hair thins only partly; laser or electrolysis is usually needed."},{"h":"Fertility before hormones","x":"Oestrogen and testosterone suppression reduce sperm production, possibly permanently. Offer sperm storage before starting, through an NHS fertility referral."},{"h":"Transdermal oestradiol and no smoking","x":"Transdermal oestradiol carries a lower clot risk than oral. Smoking adds to that risk; support her to stop before hormones start."},{"h":"Online hormones are a real risk","x":"Long waits push some people to buy hormones online without monitoring. Ask directly, without judgement, and offer bloods so it can be made safer."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Explain a treatment","do":"Assess her gender history, goals and risks respectfully, then explain the next steps towards hormones."},"c-t-19":{"obj":["Acknowledged the key worker's worry and the assault, and established Josh's baseline","Elicited the six-week change with biological features: sleep, appetite, weight and enjoyment","Screened physical causes, including constipation, pain and medication, and psychological and social triggers","Assessed risk to Josh, to others and from others, asking directly about abuse","Explained probable depression and urgent constipation review, and gave staff clear steps for now"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I've been really worried about him. He's not the Josh we know.\"","r":"\"That comes across, and it's clear how much you care about him. You know him best, so tell me what you've noticed, and I'll ask more as we go.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"What if he does it again? Sarah was really shaken.\"","r":"\"That's a real worry, and I'm sorry your colleague was hurt. Let's look at what was happening just before, so we can plan how to keep everyone safe.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Can't you just give him something to calm him down?\"","r":"\"I understand why you'd want that. Sedating medicine would hide what's wrong, though. Let's find out why he's distressed first; that's what will actually help him.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Oh, the GP did say he's constipated. He's not been yet.\"","r":"\"Thank you, that's really important. Constipation can make someone feel awful and can become dangerous. I'll ask the GP to see him again today.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it just his autism getting worse?\"","r":"\"It's a good question. When someone changes this much, we look for a new cause rather than putting it down to autism. From what you say, it sounds more like depression.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Maybe we pushed him too hard to come out of his room.\"","r":"\"You were trying to help him. Now we know more, we can plan gentler ways to encourage him, and write them down so everyone does the same thing.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["GP review today for constipation: feel the abdomen; if laxatives have failed, consider suppositories or an enema; refer urgently if vomiting, abdominal swelling or severe pain","Bloods: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, TFTs, glucose, calcium, B12, folate; weight weekly; dental and ear check","If depression persists once physical causes are treated: sertraline 25 mg each morning for 1-2 weeks, then 50 mg; review at 2 and 4 weeks for agitation, sleep change and GI upset","No antipsychotic for behaviour (STOMP)"],"psych":["Adapted behavioural activation with the team psychologist or nurse, weekly, using pictures and short sessions","Functional assessment and a positive behaviour support plan for head-banging and aggression","Easy-read information about feeling sad, shared by Declan"],"social":["Gradual return to the day centre with a familiar person; restore valued routines","Contact Josh's mother about visit frequency or video calls","Care home manager: record incidents (ABC chart), debrief staff, agree a consistent gentle approach","Speech and language therapy review of how Josh communicates pain and distress"],"safety":["Same day call to the team, or 111 out of hours, if he injures his head, stops eating or drinking, or staff cannot keep him safe; 999 in an emergency","Watch for bruising and record any concerns; safeguarding referral if abuse is suspected","Review in 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Diagnostic overshadowing kills","x":"Attributing new behaviour to a learning disability can miss treatable illness. LeDeR reviews repeatedly find missed constipation, aspiration and sepsis among causes of avoidable death."},{"h":"Depression shows in behaviour","x":"In people with limited speech, depression often appears as withdrawal, crying, poor sleep, weight loss, aggression or self-injury, rather than as reported low mood."},{"h":"Pain looks like aggression","x":"Constipation, toothache, ear infection, reflux and period pain often present as head-banging, hitting out or refusing food. Check the body before diagnosing."},{"h":"Start low, go slow","x":"People with learning disabilities are more sensitive to side effects. Start antidepressants at a low dose, increase slowly, and use a mood and sleep chart to judge effect."},{"h":"Always ask about abuse","x":"Ask directly about bruises, fear of particular people or places, and changes after visits. Raise a safeguarding concern if anything suggests harm."}],"type":"Collateral history + Risk assessment","do":"Take a collateral from Josh's key worker, screen physical, mental and social causes, assess risks, and answer his concerns."},"c-t-20":{"obj":["Validated Bernadette's anxiety and established Owen's baseline, including any earlier cognitive assessment","Described the onset, speed and pattern of memory, functional and mood change","Screened thyroid, hearing, vision, sleep apnoea, infection, medication, seizures, psychosis and life events","Assessed wandering, road and home safety, abuse, and her own coping","Explained the likely differential of depression versus early dementia and agreed the tests and next steps"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I'm sorry, I'm a bit on edge.\"","r":"\"That's completely understandable; it's been a worrying month. Owen's happy for us to talk. You know him better than anyone, so let's start with what you've noticed.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it dementia? I've read people with Down's get it early.\"","r":"\"You're right that the risk is higher, and it's sensible to ask. But a change this quick, with his mood so low, means we check for treatable causes before deciding.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I've been locking the door. I feel like I'm imprisoning him.\"","r":"\"You did that to keep him safe after he got lost, which was a caring thing to do. Let's look at other ways too, like a door alarm, so he has more freedom.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I'm sixty-eight. What happens to him when I can't cope?\"","r":"\"That's a big worry to carry alone. You don't have to. We'll arrange a carer's assessment and talk about help at home and planning for the future.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"He snores terribly. Sometimes he seems to stop breathing.\"","r":"\"Thank you, that's really useful. Poor sleep from breathing problems can affect memory and mood. We'll arrange a sleep assessment.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why do you need bloods? Just tell me what it is.\"","r":"\"I wish I could today. Thyroid problems are very common in Down's syndrome and can cause exactly this, and they're easy to treat. The bloods tell us quickly.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods: TFTs, FBC, U&Es, LFTs, calcium, glucose, B12, folate; urine dip for infection","Hearing test and ear check for wax; eye test for cataracts; sleep study referral for snoring and pauses","MRI brain (or CT if MRI not tolerated) if decline persists after treatable causes are addressed","If depression is confirmed: sertraline 25 mg once daily, increase to 50 mg after 1-2 weeks if tolerated","If Alzheimer's is later diagnosed: donepezil 5 mg at night, increase to 10 mg after 4 weeks; check pulse before starting"],"psych":["Team psychologist: DSQIID with Bernadette and direct assessment, compared with his baseline and repeated over time","Behavioural activation: planned enjoyable activities each week, including football","Visual timetable and consistent routine at home"],"social":["Carer's assessment for Bernadette by adult social care, with respite options","Telecare door sensor instead of locking the door; Herbert Protocol form with the police","Occupational therapy home visit for dressing aids and kitchen safety","Future planning: who would support decisions if his capacity changes"],"safety":["If he goes missing, call 999 and share the Herbert Protocol form","Supervise cooking and crossing roads until reviewed","Review in 4 weeks with results"]},"facts":[{"h":"Very high Alzheimer's risk","x":"Extra copies of chromosome 21 include the amyloid precursor protein gene. Most adults with Down's syndrome develop Alzheimer's changes by 40, and many develop dementia in their 50s."},{"h":"Check the thyroid first","x":"Hypothyroidism is common in Down's syndrome and causes slowing, low mood and poor memory. TFTs are the first test in any decline."},{"h":"Baseline at 30","x":"UK guidance recommends a baseline assessment of skills and memory by about age 30, so later decline can be measured against it."},{"h":"New fits can signal dementia","x":"Late-onset seizures or myoclonic jerks in adults with Down's syndrome are strongly linked to Alzheimer's disease. Ask every time."},{"h":"Depression mimics dementia","x":"Low mood, withdrawal, poor sleep and slowing can look like decline. Treating depression often restores function; serial assessment tells the two apart."}],"type":"Collateral history + History to a diagnosis","do":"Take a collateral from Owen's mother, weigh depression against dementia, screen treatable causes, and assess risk."},"c-t-21":{"obj":["Explained your role and the limits of confidentiality and got him talking about the fortnight in the garden","Elicited the sexual motivation: arousal at being seen, erections, masturbation and fantasies of approaching the neighbour","Mapped the past pattern, psychosexual history, other paraphilias and any offences","Tested his belief about the neighbour's interest and screened for mood, psychosis, cognition, drink and drugs","Rated the risk to the neighbour, children, the public and himself and told custody your view"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"It's my own garden. Since when is that a crime? I don't need a shrink.\"","r":"\"I can see you're angry, and being arrested is a shock. I'm not police and I'm not here to judge. I want to understand what's been happening so you get fair treatment.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"She's a prude. If she doesn't like it, she can shut her blinds.\"","r":"\"I hear that you feel she overreacted. Help me understand it from your side: when you were out there, were you thinking about her seeing you?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"This is humiliating. You're making me sound like some sort of pervert.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry, I know these questions are uncomfortable. I ask them of everyone in this situation, and your honest answers are what let me get you the right help.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"All right. Knowing she could see me... it got me going.\"","r":"\"Thank you for being honest; that took courage. Did that lead to an erection or touching yourself while you were out there?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Are you going to tell the police all this?\"","r":"\"Fair question. Most of it stays in your health record. But if I think someone could be at real risk, I have to share that part. I'll tell you what I'm passing on.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"So what happens to me now? Will I go to prison?\"","r":"\"I understand that's frightening. That's for the police and courts, not me. What I can do is make sure they know your health picture and that you get help to manage these urges.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No routine medication; if urges stay strong despite therapy, forensic psychiatry may consider fluoxetine 20 mg each morning, rising to 40–60 mg, for sexual preoccupation","Antilibidinal treatment only with specialist input and full informed consent","GP to screen mood and alcohol (AUDIT-C) after the separation"],"psych":["Forensic psychology assessment, then CBT-based work on urges, triggers and victim empathy","Self-referral to Stop It Now for confidential support while the case proceeds"],"social":["L&D report to custody: no mental illness, fit for interview, appropriate adult not needed","Police to consider bail conditions: no contact with the neighbour","GP letter today with risk summary and referral request"],"safety":["Police to inform the neighbour of the bail conditions and safety advice","Gareth told: GP, NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans 116 123 if he feels hopeless after release"]},"facts":[{"h":"Arousal is the deciding question","x":"Naturism is not sexual. If being seen by an unwilling person causes arousal, erection or masturbation, the behaviour is exhibitionistic, and the risk assessment changes."},{"h":"A named target raises concern","x":"Most people who expose never commit a contact offence. A specific woman, knowing when she is alone, and fantasies of approaching her are warning signs to report."},{"h":"Test the belief, then move on","x":"Ask: 'Could she simply be upset by it?' Accepting that she may be means hopeful thinking. Fixed certainty she secretly loves him suggests erotomania and psychosis."},{"h":"Custody needs four answers","x":"Is there a mental disorder? Is he fit for interview? Does he need an appropriate adult? What is the risk to others and to himself after release?"},{"h":"Release is a suicide risk point","x":"Arrest for a sexual offence carries shame and loss of reputation. Ask about suicidal thoughts and give crisis contacts before he leaves custody."}],"type":"Risk assessment + History to a diagnosis","do":"Elicit the sexual motivation and past pattern, exclude illness, and rate the risk to the neighbour, children and himself."},"c-t-22":{"obj":["Moved from the sleep request to the story of the alleged affairs and how it began","Tested the belief's conviction and alternative explanations and screened for other psychotic and mood symptoms","Mapped the behaviours from checking to planned force, including the 'truth serum' research","Established past partner violence, alcohol, weapons, children and risk to rivals and self","Explained your concern honestly and agreed same-day action to protect his partner"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"I just need to sleep. Can't you give me something?\"","r":"\"Weeks without proper sleep is exhausting, and I want to help with it. Sleep usually improves when the stress behind it is sorted, so tell me what's been on your mind.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Don't tell me I'm imagining it. I know what I smelt.\"","r":"\"I'm not saying that. I can hear how real and painful this is for you. I'd just like to understand exactly what you noticed and how you've made sense of it.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"She's lying. Wouldn't you want the truth if it was your partner?\"","r":"\"Anyone would struggle if they believed that. What worries me is what the search for the truth is doing to you, and where it might lead.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I'll get the truth out of her one way or another.\"","r":"\"Thank you for being honest. I need to understand that properly. What exactly do you mean by 'one way or another'?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not staying in hospital. I've got work.\"","r":"\"I understand; that's a big thing to ask. I'm worried about Shona's safety and about you. Let's talk about what would keep you both safe tonight, and my consultant will see you today.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"You're going to tell her what I said, aren't you?\"","r":"\"I won't hide it from you: yes, she needs to know, because I'm worried she could be hurt. I'll tell you what we share and why.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Once assessed, antipsychotic for delusional jealousy, e.g. risperidone 1 mg at night, rising to 2–4 mg over 1–2 weeks; baseline weight, BP, glucose, lipids, prolactin, ECG","No hypnotics; bloods (FBC, LFT, GGT) and urine drug screen to look for organic and substance causes","Alcohol: reduce safely; assess for dependence before any detox plan"],"psych":["Psychoeducation about morbid jealousy once safe; later CBT for delusions with a clinical psychologist, around 16 sessions"],"social":["Consultant review today; Mental Health Act assessment if he declines admission","Recommend separate living: Shona offered an IDVA and refuge options","Care coordinator allocated; fit note for work while unwell"],"safety":["Shona told of the risk today by the team; DASH checklist and MARAC referral via the trust domestic abuse lead","Police informed after consultant discussion, given planned force and method","Shona to call 999 if threatened"]},"facts":[{"h":"Delusion or overvalued idea?","x":"Ask: 'Could there be another explanation?' and 'On a scale of nought to a hundred, how certain are you?' Any flexibility suggests an overvalued idea; total certainty on trivial evidence is a delusion."},{"h":"Jealousy kills: ask about force","x":"Morbid jealousy carries a real risk of serious violence and homicide towards the partner. Ask plainly about threats, force, weapons and plans to extract a confession."},{"h":"Past partner violence predicts most","x":"A previous conviction for violence against a partner is the single strongest pointer. Always ask about police, convictions and restraining orders."},{"h":"Separation often matters most","x":"Medication helps the belief slowly. Living apart, at least during treatment, is often the most effective immediate safety step."},{"h":"Warning her is lawful and right","x":"GMC guidance supports disclosure without consent to prevent serious harm. Tell him if safe, involve your consultant, and record the reasons."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"Elicit and test the jealous delusion, map escalation to force, and act today to protect his partner."},"c-t-23":{"obj":["Settled and engaged her with reassurance and redirection, and described her appearance, behaviour and speech","Assessed mood, sense of threat, thought content and risk to self","Elicited visual and tactile hallucinations without colluding, and tested orientation and attention","Took the alcohol history and asked about the physical causes","Named delirium tremens and handed over an urgent medical plan"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Can't you see them? They're everywhere!\"","r":"\"I can see you're very frightened, Margaret. I can't see them myself, but I believe you're seeing them. You're safe, and I'm staying with you.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"They're on my legs! Get them off me!\"","r":"\"That sounds horrible. Let's look together: I'm holding your hand, and the nurse is here. Take a breath with me. Now look at my face for a moment.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why aren't you doing anything? You think I'm mad!\"","r":"\"No, I don't think you're mad. I think something is making your brain see and feel things, and I want to find out what so we can treat it quickly.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I only have a glass or two, like everyone.\"","r":"\"Thank you. Lots of people find it hard to say. It really helps me treat you safely if I know: how many bottles would you get through on a usual day?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Husband: \"What on earth is happening to her? Is it the anaesthetic?\"","r":"\"I understand how alarming this is. It's not the anaesthetic. It's a serious form of alcohol withdrawal, and it's treatable. The team are starting treatment now.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"Husband: \"I should have told them how much she drinks.\"","r":"\"You couldn't have known this would happen, and many families don't mention it. What matters is that we know now, and you've helped by telling us.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Benzodiazepine per NICE CG100 and local protocol: oral lorazepam first-line, titrated to CIWA-Ar; parenteral lorazepam if she cannot take oral; caution with co-prescribed opioids","IV Pabrinex 2 pairs three times daily for 3–5 days, given before any glucose; then oral thiamine 100 mg three times daily","Bloods: FBC, U&E, Mg, PO4, glucose, LFT, clotting, CK, CRP; urine dip; correct magnesium and potassium; IV fluids","NEWS2 hourly at first; HDU if seizures or unstable obs"],"psych":["Reorientation: clock, glasses, calm repeated explanations, same staff where possible","Once recovered, brief intervention and explanation of what happened"],"social":["Husband updated and involved in reassurance","Alcohol liaison nurse referral before discharge; community alcohol service after"],"safety":["Side room near the nurses' desk, low bed, falls precautions, avoid bed rails (climbing risk)","MCA best-interests record; least restrictive care; consider DoLS if supervision continues"]},"facts":[{"h":"Last drink is the key question","x":"Delirium tremens usually starts 48–96 hours after the last drink. Ask the time of the last drink in every acutely confused adult, especially after surgery."},{"h":"Thiamine before glucose","x":"Glucose without thiamine can tip a depleted patient into Wernicke's encephalopathy. Give IV Pabrinex first, then correct glucose if needed."},{"h":"Benzodiazepines first, not haloperidol","x":"NICE CG100: oral lorazepam first-line for delirium tremens. Antipsychotics only if symptoms persist; they lower the seizure threshold and do not treat withdrawal."},{"h":"Don't argue, don't collude","x":"'I can't see them, but I believe you can' validates the fear without confirming the insects. Arguing raises agitation; agreeing deepens the false belief."},{"h":"4AT in two minutes","x":"Alertness, four orientation questions, months of the year backwards, and acute change or fluctuation. A score of 4 or more suggests delirium."}],"type":"Mental state examination + History to a diagnosis","do":"Settle her, elicit the hallucinations and cognitive change, get the alcohol history, and name delirium tremens."},"c-t-24":{"obj":["Explained why she was referred and got her account of the singing at night","Characterised the voice as a true hallucination and screened for other psychotic and mood symptoms","Took the alcohol history with amounts, duration, last drink and withdrawal symptoms","Built the timeline from stopping to the voices and excluded delirium tremens, primary psychosis and medical causes","Checked the risk to the neighbour and herself and explained the likely diagnosis and plan"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"I don't know why the doctor sent me to a psychiatrist. I'm not crazy.\"","r":"\"That's a fair question, and no, I don't think you're crazy. Your GP was worried about your sleep and the singing at night. I'd like to understand it, and then we can make sense of it together.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"It's definitely him. I know his voice.\"","r":"\"I can hear how real and clear it is for you. When you open the door and nobody's there, what do you make of that?\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I never get drunk, so the wine isn't a problem.\"","r":"\"Lots of people who drink every day don't feel drunk; the body gets used to it. That's partly why stopping can affect it so much. How much would you have on a typical day?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Since my husband died, the evenings are so empty.\"","r":"\"I'm so sorry. Losing him must have changed everything, and the evenings sound like the hardest part. Is that when the wine became more of a habit?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"So am I going mad? Will it last forever?\"","r":"\"No, you're not going mad. This is a known effect of stopping after years of heavy drinking. It usually fades over days to weeks if you stay off alcohol.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What should I do about my neighbour?\"","r":"\"I understand you want it to stop. Please don't confront him: I think the voice is coming from your brain, not from him. If it gets worse or frightening, ring us or 111 option 2.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Oral thiamine 100 mg three times daily via GP; IV Pabrinex if any confusion, unsteadiness or eye movement problems","Bloods: FBC (MCV), LFT with GGT, U&E, clotting, magnesium; GP to arrange liver ultrasound if LFTs stay abnormal","If voices persist or distress is high, short course of antipsychotic, e.g. olanzapine 5 mg at night, reviewed within weeks","Once stable, relapse prevention: acamprosate 666 mg three times daily if 60 kg or over, for up to 12 months"],"psych":["Community alcohol service: motivational work and relapse prevention, weekly at first","Bereavement support (Cruse) for the loss behind the drinking","Mutual aid, e.g. AA or SMART Recovery, if she wishes"],"social":["Occupational health discussion before full return to the school kitchen","GP to monitor LFTs and mood; CMHT review in 2 weeks"],"safety":["Do not confront the neighbour; call the team or 111 option 2 if voices become threatening","New shakes, confusion or a fit: 999 or the emergency department"]},"facts":[{"h":"Clear mind separates hallucinosis from DTs","x":"Alcoholic hallucinosis: usually voices, in clear consciousness, oriented and attentive. Delirium tremens: confused, disoriented, visual and tactile hallucinations, tremor and sweating. The second is a medical emergency."},{"h":"The timeline makes the diagnosis","x":"Heavy drinking for years, abrupt stop, voices within days, nothing before: that chronology, plus no other psychotic features, points to alcohol rather than schizophrenia."},{"h":"Two bottles is about 20 units","x":"A 750 ml bottle of 13% wine is roughly 10 units. Two bottles daily is around 140 units a week, ten times the UK low-risk guideline of 14."},{"h":"Usually self-limiting with abstinence","x":"Hallucinosis normally settles within days to weeks if she stays off alcohol. Persistent or recurrent voices without drinking need reassessment for another psychotic illness."},{"h":"Thiamine for every heavy drinker","x":"Give oral thiamine to anyone with harmful or dependent drinking. Switch to IV Pabrinex if confusion, ataxia or eye signs suggest Wernicke's encephalopathy."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Characterise the voice, link it in time to stopping drink, exclude delirium tremens and primary psychosis."},"c-t-25":{"obj":["Reviewed his obsessions, compulsions, insight, daily impact and suicide risk before planning","Explained OCD and the anxiety-ritual cycle in plain words, checked by teach-back","Explained ERP with a personal fear ladder and how anxiety falls without the ritual","Explained the CBT work on responsibility and HIV facts, with homework and experiments","Agreed medication, skin care, work support and a referral that suits him, with review"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"It's embarrassing, to be honest.\"","r":"\"That's completely understandable, and thank you for telling me. Lots of people with these fears feel the same. What you're describing is a recognised problem, and it's very treatable.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I don't wipe it twice, someone could get HIV. I can't take the chance.\"","r":"\"I can hear how real and heavy that responsibility feels. Part of you also knows it's unlikely. Therapy helps you test which part is right, a step at a time.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So what's this therapy? Will it actually stop it?\"","r":"\"Good question. It's the treatment with the best evidence for exactly this. You practise facing the worry without cleaning, in small steps, and the fear fades. Most people improve a great deal.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I can't come here every week. This place makes my skin crawl.\"","r":"\"That makes sense, and thank you for being honest. Sessions can be by video, by phone, or some in your kitchen, which actually suits ERP well. Shall I ask for that?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"So you want me to touch dirty things on purpose? That sounds mad.\"","r":"\"It does sound odd, I agree. You'd never start with the hardest thing. You choose the first step, and you stay with the anxiety until it drops by itself. That's how the brain relearns.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I keep taking the fluoxetine?\"","r":"\"Yes, I'd like you to. It's only been at 40 mg for a few weeks, and medicine plus therapy works better than either alone. We can raise it to 60 mg later if needed.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue fluoxetine 40 mg each morning; review at 6 weeks; can increase to 60 mg if partial response","Continue for at least 12 months after improvement; reduce slowly when stopping","GP: emollient and soap substitute, short course of topical steroid for dermatitis; dermatology if not settling","If no response to two SSRIs with CBT, consider clomipramine with baseline ECG"],"psych":["CBT with ERP, more than 10 therapist hours, weekly 60–90 minute sessions, with clinical psychology","Fear ladder: touch the counter and wait 5 minutes before washing; wipe once; leave colleagues' areas; finish on time","Cognitive work: inflated responsibility, HIV transmission facts, behavioural experiments, daily homework"],"social":["Occupational health, with consent: agreed food-hygiene cleaning standard and glove use while hands heal","OCD-UK information and peer support; family or partner involved if he wishes"],"safety":["Review in 6 weeks; contact the team or 111 option 2 if mood drops or thoughts of self-harm start"]},"facts":[{"h":"Rituals keep the fear alive","x":"Cleaning brings brief relief, so the brain learns cleaning is needed. Resisting the ritual lets anxiety fall on its own, and the trigger loses its power."},{"h":"ERP: climb a ladder he builds","x":"List feared situations from 1 to 10. Start around 3, stay until anxiety halves without the ritual, repeat daily, then climb. Never start at the top."},{"h":"NICE: more than 10 hours","x":"For moderate OCD, NICE CG31 recommends CBT including ERP with more than 10 therapist hours, or an SSRI. If response is partial, combine both."},{"h":"HIV does not spread via surfaces","x":"HIV is not passed on through food, worktops or touch. It survives poorly outside the body. Sharing this once helps; repeating it on request becomes a ritual."},{"h":"Reassurance is a hidden compulsion","x":"Asking others 'Is it safe?' brings relief like washing does. Agree with him and his family to answer once, then gently decline."}],"type":"Explain a treatment + Management plan","do":"Check his OCD and risk, explain ERP and CBT with his own fear ladder, and agree a plan."},"c-t-26":{"obj":["Confirmed the pattern of binges, loss of control, vomiting, other purging and weekend eating","Explained bulimia nervosa in plain words and why anorexia and binge eating disorder do not fit","Explained the restrict-binge-purge cycle and its physical effects, and arranged potassium checks and dental advice","Agreed guided self-help with regular eating as the first step, with CBT-ED next if needed and no tablets first-line","Checked her understanding and closed with a referral, Beat details and a review date"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I'm so ashamed. I'm an adult, I should be able to control what I eat.\"","r":"\"It makes sense you feel that way, and shame is part of this illness, not a sign of weakness. Starving all day would make anyone binge. Let's look at what's driving it.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I stop being sick, I'll just put on loads of weight.\"","r":"\"That's the most common worry, and it's understandable. Being sick doesn't get rid of most of what you've eaten. When people eat regularly, binges usually fall and weight tends to stay fairly steady.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is there a tablet that can stop the binges?\"","r":"\"Good question. Talking treatment works best and comes first. There is a medicine, fluoxetine, that can be added later if therapy alone isn't enough, but not on its own.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"My BMI is normal, so it can't be that serious.\"","r":"\"I'm glad your weight is stable, but bulimia can be serious at any weight. Being sick every day can drop your potassium, which affects your heart, so I'd like a blood test today.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I'm terrified work will find out.\"","r":"\"That's a really understandable fear. This stays between you, us and your GP unless you choose otherwise. If you ever want support at work, we can help you decide what to say.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I don't have time for weekly therapy with my job.\"","r":"\"Your job is demanding, I hear that. The first step is guided self-help: a book plus short sessions, some by phone. We can fit it around work.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No medication as the sole or first treatment (NICE NG69)","U&Es today for potassium; repeat in 4 weeks while vomiting continues; ECG if potassium below 3.5 mmol/L or palpitations","Dental: rinse with water after vomiting, no brushing for an hour, avoid acidic drinks, register with a dentist","If therapy alone is not enough: fluoxetine 60 mg once daily as an add-on (BNF licensed dose); warn about nausea and early agitation"],"psych":["Bulimia-focused guided self-help: CBT-based book with 4-9 short supportive sessions over about 16 weeks","Not improving or not acceptable after 4 weeks: individual CBT-ED, up to 20 sessions over 20 weeks","Regular eating: three meals and two to three snacks, no gap over 3-4 hours; food and mood diary"],"social":["Community eating disorder service key worker; GP to monitor bloods","Occupational health or regular break adjustments only if she wishes","Beat helpline and online support; tell one trusted person to support regular meals"],"safety":["Seek urgent help (111 or 999) for palpitations, fainting, muscle cramps, weakness or vomiting blood","Review at 4 weeks to check progress and bloods"]},"facts":[{"h":"Normal BMI does not mean safe","x":"Daily vomiting loses potassium and acid, causing low potassium and alkalosis. Low potassium can trigger arrhythmias. Check U&Es; ECG if potassium is low."},{"h":"Vomiting does not empty the stomach","x":"A large share of calories is absorbed before vomiting, and laxatives act too late in the gut to affect calories. Purging is a poor weight control method."},{"h":"Two differentials to name","x":"Anorexia nervosa needs significantly low weight; binge eating disorder has binges without compensation. Bulimia: binges plus compensation, at normal or high weight."},{"h":"NICE order of treatment","x":"Bulimia-focused guided self-help first; if not working after 4 weeks or unacceptable, CBT-ED (up to 20 sessions over 20 weeks)."},{"h":"Fluoxetine dose is higher","x":"For bulimia the BNF dose is 60 mg daily, higher than for depression. Used alongside therapy, not instead of it."},{"h":"Signs that give it away","x":"Swollen parotid glands, eroded teeth, and calluses on the back of the hand (Russell's sign) all point to repeated self-induced vomiting."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Explain a treatment","do":"Confirm the bulimia pattern, explain the diagnosis and its cycle, cover physical risks, and agree a NICE treatment plan."},"c-t-27":{"obj":["Explained section 136 in plain words and established what happened tonight","Elicited persecutory and referential delusions, their conviction and the delusional perception that started them","Characterised the voices, including their person, content and presence in the suite, and screened other senses","Quantified his cannabis use and checked cognition, intoxication and insight","Assessed risk to self, to others and of accidental harm, and explained the MHA assessment"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why am I here? I haven't done anything.\"","r":"\"You haven't done anything wrong. The police were worried you'd get hurt in the road, so they used a law that lets a doctor and a social worker check you're OK. I'm here to listen.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"They followed me here. I can hear them right now.\"","r":"\"That must be frightening. You're safe in this room and staff are right outside. What are they saying at the moment?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You think I'm mad, don't you?\"","r":"\"No. I think you've been really scared for weeks and haven't slept. I'm not here to judge, I'm here to work out how to help.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"It's not the weed. Weed calms me down.\"","r":"\"I can see why it feels that way when you're this frightened. Strong cannabis can also make these experiences worse over time. Can we look at how much you've been using?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've got a hammer by the door in case they come in.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that helps me keep everyone safe, you included. Have you ever thought of using it, or taking it out with you?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going in a hospital. I'll go to my sister's.\"","r":"\"I understand hospital feels a big step. Your safety tonight matters most. A social worker and another doctor will see you this morning, and your sister's offer is something we'll consider with them.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Urine drug screen; FBC, U&E, LFT, glucose, TFT; consider HIV and syphilis with consent; physical observations","Before any antipsychotic: weight, BMI, waist, BP, pulse, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin, ECG","If antipsychotic agreed: aripiprazole 10 mg each morning or olanzapine 5 mg at night, low dose in first episode; review at 4-6 weeks","If very distressed: oral lorazepam 1 mg, offered first; avoid high doses"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis, at least 16 one-to-one sessions, through the early intervention in psychosis team","Family intervention, around 10 sessions, if he agrees to involve family","Motivational interviewing to reduce or stop cannabis, with the drug and alcohol service"],"social":["AMHP coordinates MHA assessment with a section 12 doctor within the 136 time limit; admission likely","Early intervention in psychosis referral and a care coordinator","Benefits advice (Universal Credit) after job loss; with consent, contact his sister"],"safety":["Stays in place of safety; observation level agreed with nurse in charge","Weapon at home: discuss removal with him and, with consent, his sister; record in risk assessment","Advise not to drive and to notify the DVLA"]},"facts":[{"h":"The 136 clock","x":"Up to 24 hours from arrival at the place of safety, extendable by 12 hours by the doctor for clinical reasons. A doctor and an AMHP must both assess."},{"h":"Delusional perception hides at onset","x":"Ask 'What was the very first thing that felt wrong?' A normal sight given a sudden, unrelated personal meaning is a first-rank symptom."},{"h":"Ask about voices now","x":"Hearing them in the suite, far from where he believes they come from, shows an extracampine quality and confirms ongoing psychosis."},{"h":"Skunk and psychosis","x":"Daily high-potency cannabis is linked to a several-fold higher risk of psychosis. Symptoms lasting weeks, beyond intoxication, suggest a primary psychotic illness."},{"h":"Frightened people arm themselves","x":"Persecutory beliefs plus fear raise the chance of defensive violence. Always ask about weapons at home or carried."}],"type":"Mental state examination + Risk assessment","do":"Elicit and characterise psychotic symptoms, cannabis use, cognition, insight and risk, then explain the next steps."},"c-t-28":{"obj":["Established events since discharge, his routine and what stresses him in the hostel","Found out he stopped olanzapine and why, and screened for substances","Compared the current changes with his last relapse and checked insight","Elicited persecutory beliefs and a commenting voice, and separated them from low mood and negative symptoms","Assessed suicide, harm to others, self-neglect and fire risk and agreed a plan he accepts"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"What's this about? Have the staff been talking about me?\"","r":"\"They mentioned you've been in your room a lot and they care how you are. I'd rather hear it from you. How have things been?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I stopped the olanzapine. I put on two stone.\"","r":"\"Thank you for being honest. Lots of people stop for that reason, and it matters. Let's look at a medicine that's kinder to your weight.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going back to hospital.\"","r":"\"I hear you, and that's not my plan. You've worked hard to get here. Let's look at what would help you feel settled here.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"They open my letters before I get them. I've seen the envelopes.\"","r":"\"That must feel horrible, like you've no privacy. What makes you sure it's the staff? And what have you done about it?\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not ill. I'm just stressed with the noise here.\"","r":"\"The noise sounds really stressful. Last time, did stress and poor sleep come before things got harder? I'd like us to catch it early this time.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"If I take something else, will I still put weight on?\"","r":"\"Aripiprazole causes much less weight gain than olanzapine for most people. We'll weigh you regularly and add diet and exercise support.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Aripiprazole 10 mg each morning, staff-prompted; less weight gain than olanzapine; warn about restlessness (akathisia) and nausea","Bloods at restart: FBC, U&E, LFT, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin; weight, waist and BP now and at 12 weeks; ECG if cardiac risk factors","If adherence slips again: offer aripiprazole long-acting injection 400 mg monthly after oral tolerability is shown","Urine drug screen only with his agreement"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis, at least 16 sessions with the psychology team, focusing on the voice and suspicion","Relapse prevention with care coordinator: written early warning signs card shared with the hostel","Healthy living support: diet and exercise sessions for weight"],"social":["Care coordinator visits twice weekly; joint plan with hostel key worker","Home treatment team for daily contact if symptoms worsen","Structured day: day centre, IPS employment specialist; check benefits and tenancy"],"safety":["If the voice gives orders or he feels unsafe: tell staff, call the crisis line or NHS 111 option 2","Hostel: fire safety check of his room, no smoking indoors; daily check on eating and self-care; review in 1 week"]},"facts":[{"h":"Non-adherence drives relapse","x":"Stopping antipsychotics is the single biggest cause of relapse in schizophrenia. Ask why, without blame: side effects are the usual reason."},{"h":"Use his relapse signature","x":"Each person's early signs tend to repeat. Compare now with last time: sleep, withdrawal, suspicion. Write it on a card he and the hostel keep."},{"h":"Weight: switch, don't lecture","x":"Olanzapine and clozapine cause the most weight gain; aripiprazole is among the lowest. Switching is a reasonable shared decision."},{"h":"Running commentary voice","x":"A voice describing his actions in the third person is a first-rank symptom. Ask about commands and whether he obeys."},{"h":"Three reasons to withdraw","x":"Fear (avoiding a threat), low mood (no pleasure, hopeless) or negative symptoms (no drive). Each needs a different response."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Mental state examination","do":"Establish events since discharge and adherence, elicit relapse symptoms, insight and risk, and agree a community plan."},"c-t-30":{"obj":["Checked current intent, plans, means and reasons for living before planning","Identified his triggers and early warning signs, including alcohol","Agreed his own coping steps and people and places that help, in his own words","Listed professional and crisis contacts with numbers he can use day or night","Agreed a specific means reduction step for the stored tablets and closed with a copy and review date"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"It's the same old thing. I've done all this before.\"","r":"\"I hear you, and ten years is a long time to carry this. This time it's your plan, in your words, built round what's actually helped you. Shall we try?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I don't want to burden my mates. They've got their own problems.\"","r":"\"That's a kind instinct. Most friends would far rather get a text than find out later. Would one message, like 'rough night, can you call?', feel possible?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've still got a drawer of old tablets at home.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that took honesty. That drawer is the riskiest thing in the house on a bad night. Could you take them to the pharmacy today on your way home?\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"The drinking helps me sleep. It's not a problem.\"","r":"\"I get that it takes the edge off. The trouble is it also lowers the brakes on these thoughts. Could we agree no drinking alone after a row?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Sometimes I think my lad would be better off without me.\"","r":"\"That sounds like a really painful thought, and I'm glad you said it. You've also told me he's why you keep going. What would he want for you?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"This is all useful, but is there anything else that would actually help long term?\"","r":"\"Yes, Rhys, there are a few things alongside the plan that we can try, and some are different from before, like a skills group and help with drinking.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Return stockpiled tablets to the pharmacy today; GP to prescribe in weekly quantities and avoid tricyclics","If an antidepressant is continued: a single agent of lower overdose toxicity, such as sertraline 50-200 mg daily","Alcohol: brief intervention; check LFTs; offer referral to the alcohol service"],"psych":["Safety plan (Stanley-Brown style) reviewed at every contact and kept on his phone","DBT skills group or psychological therapy targeting suicidal thinking, weekly","Distress tolerance tools: delay rule, grounding, Stay Alive app, hope box"],"social":["Care coordinator with planned reviews, not only in crisis","Family mediation or legal advice on contact with his son","Peer support, Recovery College courses; local Mind groups"],"safety":["Crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123, SHOUT text 85258; 999 or A&E if he cannot keep safe","Review in 2 weeks with care coordinator; GP informed today"]},"facts":[{"h":"Six parts of a safety plan","x":"Warning signs, own coping, people and places to distract, people to ask for help, professionals and crisis lines, making the environment safer. Add reasons for living."},{"h":"Means reduction works","x":"Suicidal crises are often brief. Making the chosen method harder to reach saves lives: return stockpiles, limit prescriptions, and agree who holds medicines."},{"h":"Numbers to write in","x":"Samaritans 116 123 (free, 24/7); SHOUT text 85258; NHS 111 option 2 for the local crisis line in England; 999 in an emergency."},{"h":"Alcohol is an accelerator","x":"Alcohol is involved in a large share of suicides and self-harm. It deepens low mood and loosens restraint. Name it and plan around it."},{"h":"No risk scores","x":"NICE NG225 advises against using risk tools to predict suicide. A good plan and a good relationship matter more than a number."}],"type":"Safety plan","do":"Build a written safety plan with him: warning signs, coping, people, contacts, means reduction, and a copy to keep."},"c-t-32":{"obj":["Built enough trust with a hostile, unsettled patient to discuss the test calmly","Uncovered the fears behind his refusal: leave, police, discharge home, and voices about the staff","Explained why the test matters, that it needs his consent, how leave will be reviewed and who sees the result","Found out what happened on leave, including drug used, debt and exploitation","Agreed physical checks, observation and the next steps, whether or not he gave the sample"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"I'm fine. Leave me alone. Why is everyone staring at me?\"","r":"\"Sorry, it must feel like everyone's on your back the minute you walk in. I'm here because the nurses are worried about you. Can we sit for five minutes?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not pissing in a pot. No way.\"","r":"\"OK, I hear you. It's your choice, and nobody will force you. Can you tell me what worries you about it? There might be something I can sort out.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If it's positive you'll call the police.\"","r":"\"That's a real worry, and I'm glad you said it. The result stays with your care team. We don't report a positive test to the police. We use it to keep you safe.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"I'll do it if you promise my leave stays the same.\"","r":"\"I'd love to promise that, but I won't lie to you. Your consultant will review your leave either way. Being open and doing the test will count for you, not against you.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"The voices said you'd use my piss to make copies of me.\"","r":"\"That sounds frightening. Thank you for telling me. The sample goes in a sealed pot to the lab, only to check for drugs. You can watch the nurse seal it.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Dean says I owe him forty quid now. He wants my tablets next time.\"","r":"\"That sounds like he's putting you under pressure, and that isn't OK. You've done the right thing telling me. Let's think together about keeping you safe from him.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So am I in trouble now?\"","r":"\"You're not in trouble with me, Tyrone. You've been honest, and that helps. Now we focus on getting you well and planning safer leave.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Urine drug screen with consent; ask the lab specifically for synthetic cannabinoid testing, as standard screens often miss it","NEWS2 hourly for 4 hours then 4-hourly for 24 hours; ECG, U&E, CK and glucose tonight","Abnormal movements: sustained twisting, eye rolling or neck spasm suggests acute dystonia, so give procyclidine 5 mg orally or IM; brief jerks with normal tone suggest intoxication","Avoid extra sedation on top of intoxication; if PRN needed for agitation, lorazepam 1 mg orally, max 4 mg in 24 h, with observations"],"psych":["Dual diagnosis worker: motivational interviewing about spice and what leave is for","Psychoeducation: synthetic cannabinoids are strong, unpredictable and worsen psychosis","Relapse prevention plan for future leave: where he goes, who to avoid, what to do if approached"],"social":["Section 17 leave suspended pending responsible clinician review on Monday; explained and documented, not removed as punishment","Safeguarding concern to the ward manager about financial exploitation and pressure to bring medication","Care coordinator to sort the electricity at his flat before discharge planning","Community drug and alcohol service named in his discharge plan"],"safety":["Intermittent observation every 15 minutes tonight, reviewed by the nurse in charge in the morning","Escalate to the on-call medical team or 999 if seizure, chest pain, temperature above 38, rigidity or reduced consciousness"]},"facts":[{"h":"Standard screens often miss spice","x":"Synthetic cannabinoids are not detected by most ward urine dip tests. Ask the lab for specific testing; a negative dip test does not rule them out."},{"h":"Consent, not force","x":"A urine drug screen needs consent. Detention does not authorise taking a sample by force. Record the refusal and the reasons; it informs risk and leave decisions."},{"h":"Leave belongs to the RC","x":"Under section 17 only the responsible clinician grants, changes or revokes leave. Ward staff can delay leave on safety grounds pending review."},{"h":"Dystonia or intoxication?","x":"Sustained twisting, eyes rolling up or a fixed neck suggests acute dystonia: give procyclidine. Brief jerks, fast pulse and sweating after leave point to intoxication."},{"h":"Debt to a dealer is safeguarding","x":"A detained patient pressured for money or medication is at risk of exploitation. Raise a safeguarding concern and plan leave around it."}],"type":"Professional and ethical dilemma + Risk assessment","do":"Explore why he refuses, make an honest case for the urine screen, and agree safety checks either way."},"c-t-33":{"obj":["Used short, concrete questions and breaks so Callum could give his own account","Clarified the voice and the belief about the man, with his conviction and what he has done because of it","Screened mood, anxiety, sleep and eating against his usual autistic baseline","Looked for causes: pain and physical illness, routine changes, a new neighbour, cannabis and exploitation","Assessed risk to himself and others and his vulnerability, summarised, and agreed to collateral from his support worker"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"The man is listening now. Is he in your phone too?\"","r":"\"That sounds really scary, Callum. My phone is switched off and in the drawer. You're safe in this room. Can you tell me what the man says?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can you make the man stop talking?\"","r":"\"I believe the voice is really upsetting you. There are things that can help it get quieter. First I need to understand it properly. Is that OK?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Too many questions. My head is busy.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. Let's have a break. We can stop for two minutes, then I'll ask just a few more, one at a time.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want Gemma to know about Kai.\"","r":"\"OK, I hear that. I'm worried Kai might be taking your money, and that isn't fair on you. Can we talk about who could help keep you safe?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Kai gives me smoke. It makes the man quieter.\"","r":"\"Thank you for being honest. Lots of people feel that at first. Sometimes smoke can make voices louder later on. How many times a week do you have it?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"It's not in my head. He's real.\"","r":"\"I can see you're sure, and I'm not saying you're making it up. I hear how real it is for you. I want to work out why it's happening and how to help.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods (FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, calcium, glucose, HbA1c, lipids) and urine drug screen to look for physical and substance causes","Physical review: teeth, abdomen and bowels, ears, skin; ask staff about seizures or absences","If psychosis is confirmed by the consultant: start low, such as risperidone 0.5 mg at night, increase slowly; baseline weight, BP, ECG and prolactin, repeat at 12 weeks","STOMP: antipsychotic only for a diagnosed psychosis, with a written review date"],"psych":["Easy-read explanation of voices and psychosis, given with Gemma present","Adapted CBT for psychosis from the team psychologist, short weekly sessions with pictures","Positive behaviour support plan to restore predictable daily routines"],"social":["Safeguarding concern to the local authority today about Kai: money taken and cannabis supplied (possible cuckooing)","Community learning disability nurse as key worker; staff keep a sleep, food and behaviour diary","Day opportunities officer to find a winter placement to replace the garden centre routine"],"safety":["Crisis plan shared with supported living staff; crisis team or 999 if he puts himself or others at risk","Consultant review within one week; staff to call the team sooner if he stops eating or drinking"]},"facts":[{"h":"Diagnostic overshadowing misses illness","x":"Blaming new behaviour on learning disability or autism is a common cause of missed psychosis, depression and physical illness. Ask what is new compared with his usual self."},{"h":"Pain drives behaviour change","x":"Dental pain, constipation, reflux, ear infection and seizures often present as agitation, shouting or withdrawal in people with learning disability. Ask and examine every time."},{"h":"One question at a time","x":"Short, single, concrete questions, the person's own words and pauses work best. Avoid idioms and double questions; check understanding by asking them to say it back."},{"h":"Start low, go slow","x":"People with learning disability are more sensitive to side effects. Use low starting doses, slow increases and planned reviews, in line with STOMP."},{"h":"Cuckooing is safeguarding","x":"When someone uses a vulnerable adult's home or money for drugs, raise a safeguarding concern with the local authority under the Care Act 2014."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"Take an adapted history of the behaviour change covering psychosis, mood, body, triggers, substances and risk."},"c-t-35":{"obj":["Elicited her obsessions with their key features: own thoughts, unwanted, resisted, distressing and seen as excessive","Described her compulsions, reassurance-seeking and avoidance, with time spent each day","Established onset, course, recent triggers, past treatment and impact on work, family, sleep and skin","Screened for depression, tics, psychosis and substance use, and assessed suicidal and harm thoughts","Summarised, named OCD and outlined that effective treatment exists"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"If I'd just stop being so silly, it'd all be fine.\"","r":"\"It isn't silliness, and you've tried hard to stop. That's the nature of OCD: the harder you push the thoughts away, the louder they get. It's treatable.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Sometimes I get a picture of giving her the wrong medicine. Does that make me dangerous?\"","r":"\"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. No, it doesn't. These thoughts horrify you, so you avoid giving her medicine: that's OCD, not danger.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will you have to tell social services?\"","r":"\"I understand why you'd worry. Unwanted thoughts that upset you aren't a reason to involve social services. Anaya is well cared for. This stays between us, your GP and the team.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I'm so tired. I'm up till one bleaching handles.\"","r":"\"That sounds exhausting, night after night. Let's look at how much of your day this takes, because that will help me understand how hard it's been.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can this actually get better, or is it just who I am?\"","r":"\"It can get much better. This is OCD, not your personality. A therapy called exposure and response prevention, often with a medicine, helps most people get their lives back.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I work in a pharmacy. I know about tablets. I don't want to be zonked.\"","r":"\"Fair point, and you'll know more than most. The usual first choice is an SSRI like sertraline, which doesn't sedate. We'd also start therapy. It's your choice.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline 50 mg each morning; increase in 50 mg steps every 2 to 4 weeks up to 200 mg if needed; judge after 12 weeks at a good dose","Warn: nausea, jitteriness and sexual side effects early; do not stop suddenly; review at 2 weeks for agitation or suicidal thoughts","Emollient for her hands after each wash; GP to check cracked skin for infection"],"psych":["CBT with exposure and response prevention, more than 10 therapist hours, weekly, through NHS Talking Therapies or the local OCD service","Shared hierarchy starting low, such as touching her work bag without washing","Dev to join a session on reducing reassurance and family accommodation"],"social":["Occupational health referral and reasonable adjustments at work if rituals cause lateness","OCD-UK information and peer support groups","Psychiatry review in 4 weeks; letter to GP today"],"safety":["If mood drops or she feels hopeless, contact GP urgently or NHS 111 option 2"]},"facts":[{"h":"Harm thoughts in OCD are unwanted","x":"Intrusive thoughts of harming a child are common in OCD. They horrify the person and drive avoidance. Without intent or neglect, they are not a safeguarding concern."},{"h":"Own thoughts, not inserted","x":"In OCD the person knows the thoughts come from their own mind and are excessive. Thoughts felt as put in from outside suggest psychosis."},{"h":"Measure severity with Y-BOCS","x":"Score time, interference, distress, resistance and control for obsessions and compulsions. Over 3 hours a day or major interference points to severe OCD."},{"h":"ERP is the core therapy","x":"NICE: CBT with exposure and response prevention. For moderate impairment, intensive CBT or an SSRI; for severe, offer both."},{"h":"OCD needs higher SSRI doses","x":"OCD often needs higher SSRI doses than depression and up to 12 weeks to work. Keep going for at least a year after improvement."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Elicit obsessions, compulsions, course, impact, comorbidity and risk, then explain the likely diagnosis of OCD."},"c-t-36":{"obj":["Established how the OCD affects him now, including time, skin, work, mood and risk","Explained the OCD cycle and how exposure and response prevention breaks it, in words he can repeat back","Described how therapy runs: who delivers it, how often, how many sessions, the fear ladder and homework","Answered his worries honestly, including fear of the anxiety, effectiveness and the medication option","Checked understanding by teach-back, gave written information and agreed next steps"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I don't wash after touching a pallet, I'll lose it. The anxiety would be too much.\"","r":"\"That fear makes complete sense; almost everyone has it before starting. You won't begin with pallets. We start with something that scores about three out of ten, and you choose each step.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"So you want me to touch dirty things and not wash? How does that help?\"","r":"\"It sounds backwards, I know. Every wash tells your brain the danger was real. Holding off lets it learn the anxiety falls on its own and nothing bad happens. That's what weakens OCD.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"What if I actually get ill because I didn't wash?\"","r":"\"A fair worry. The steps are ones most people do every day without getting ill, like touching a door handle. Normal hygiene stays: washing before eating and after the toilet is fine.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will it actually work for me?\"","r":"\"I can't promise, but it's the most effective treatment we have. Most people who complete it improve a lot, especially with homework. Your good insight is a strong sign.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Do I have to go into hospital for it?\"","r":"\"No. It's outpatient, about an hour a week with a therapist, plus practice at home. Hospital is only for very severe OCD that hasn't responded.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"My girlfriend checks my hands for me and tells me they're clean.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; she's clearly trying to help. That reassurance works like washing, though: it brings quick relief and keeps OCD going. The therapist can invite her to a session to plan how she helps.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will my work have to know?\"","r":"\"Only what you choose to tell them. Occupational health can arrange time off for appointments without sharing the diagnosis.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline optional alongside therapy: 50 mg once daily, increase by 50 mg steps at least a week apart to a maximum 200 mg if needed","Allow up to 12 weeks at a good dose before judging; warn about early nausea, sweating, sexual side effects and a brief rise in anxiety","If OCD is severe or one treatment alone fails, combine an SSRI with CBT including ERP","Stop bleach on skin; emollient after each wash and gloves for cleaning at work"],"psych":["CBT with ERP from the psychology team, weekly sessions of about 60 minutes, typically 12-20","Build a fear ladder together, starting with a step he rates about 3/10, such as touching a door handle","Homework most days: stay with the anxiety without washing until it falls; rate it before, during and after","Later sessions: beliefs about germs and responsibility, then a written relapse prevention plan"],"social":["Psychology or NHS Talking Therapies waiting time checked; OCD-UK self-help materials meanwhile","Occupational health for time off for sessions; he decides what his employer is told","Partner invited to one session to stop checking his hands and reassuring","OCD-UK and OCD Action for information and peer support groups"],"safety":["If mood falls or he feels hopeless: GP, NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans 116 123","Review in clinic in 6 weeks; GP letter today"]},"facts":[{"h":"Response prevention is the active ingredient","x":"Exposure followed by washing teaches nothing. The learning happens when he stays with the anxiety and does not wash, so it falls on its own."},{"h":"Sertraline in OCD: higher and longer","x":"Start 50 mg daily, up to 200 mg. Response in OCD is slower than in depression: give up to 12 weeks at a good dose, and continue at least 12 months after improvement."},{"h":"Reassurance is a compulsion too","x":"A partner checking his hands or saying 'they're clean' brings relief and keeps OCD going. Involve family in therapy so they can step back kindly."},{"h":"Calming skills must not become rituals","x":"Slow breathing can help him start a step. Used to switch off anxiety during exposure, it becomes a new safety behaviour and slows progress."},{"h":"Intensity matches severity","x":"NICE CG31: mild impairment, low-intensity CBT up to 10 hours; moderate, more intensive CBT or an SSRI; severe, both together."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explain the OCD cycle, ERP and how sessions run, answer his fears, and confirm understanding by teach-back."},"c-t-37":{"obj":["Found the traumatic event behind the 'work stress' and explored it with care","Elicited re-experiencing, avoidance and numbing, and hyperarousal, with their impact","Screened for depression, panic, psychosis and head injury, and quantified alcohol and drugs","Reviewed the paroxetine trial and assessed suicide risk and fitness to drive trains","Summarised and named PTSD in plain words with a clear next step"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"The tablets haven't done a thing. What's the point of another doctor?\"","r":"\"Four weeks with no change is really frustrating. I'd like to understand what's driving this, because the right treatment depends on it. Can we start from when things changed?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I saw his face. Right before. I can't get it out of my head.\"","r":"\"That sounds horrifying, and I'm sorry you went through it. Take your time; you don't need to give me every detail. Does that picture come back when you don't want it to?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have stopped in time. I keep thinking I could've.\"","r":"\"Lots of drivers feel that, even though a train can't stop in that distance. That guilt is part of what keeps this going, and therapy works on it directly.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I only have a few cans to get to sleep. It's not a problem.\"","r":"\"It makes sense you'd want something to switch off. Let's count it together, though: how many cans, what strength, and how late before a shift?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you going to stop me working? Driving's all I've done.\"","r":"\"I can hear how much the job matters. Right now your sleep, startle and drinking make driving unsafe for you and others. Occupational health can plan a safe way back, and most drivers do return.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What's wrong with me? Am I going mad?\"","r":"\"You're not going mad. This is post-traumatic stress disorder: your brain is stuck on alert after something awful. It's common after what you saw, and it's very treatable.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Trauma-focused therapy first; if he prefers medication, sertraline 50 mg daily rising to 100-200 mg, or venlafaxine (NICE)","If switching from paroxetine, cross-taper with the GP over 1-2 weeks to avoid discontinuation symptoms","Alcohol: AUDIT today; LFT and GGT via GP; cut down gradually by about one can every few days; no drinking within 24 hours of any driving duty","No benzodiazepines or hypnotics; sleep routine advice"],"psych":["Trauma-focused CBT, usually 8-12 weekly sessions (often 60-90 minutes), from a trained therapist via NHS Talking Therapies or the trauma service","EMDR as an alternative, 8-12 sessions, if he prefers","Psychoeducation for Bayo and his wife about PTSD and how she can help"],"social":["Occupational health referral; not to drive trains until assessed fit; phased return on a different route","GP fit note; rail operator trauma support scheme and union representative","Plan around the inquest date with his therapist"],"safety":["Crisis plan: GP, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123, or 999","Review in clinic in 4 weeks; GP letter today"]},"facts":[{"h":"Ask what happened around then","x":"A GP letter saying 'work stress' often hides a trauma. One question about what happened when it started can change the diagnosis and the whole plan."},{"h":"Three clusters make PTSD","x":"ICD-11 needs reliving the event now, keeping away from reminders, and feeling under current threat (startle, hypervigilance), for several weeks or more, with clear impairment."},{"h":"Therapy beats tablets for PTSD","x":"NICE NG116: trauma-focused CBT or EMDR first. Sertraline or venlafaxine only if he prefers medication or therapy is not possible."},{"h":"Paroxetine stops badly","x":"Short half-life: stopping suddenly causes dizziness, electric-shock feelings, irritability and flu-like symptoms. Taper or cross-taper, never stop abruptly."},{"h":"Alcohol worsens nightmares","x":"Drink helps him fall asleep but fragments sleep later and deepens low mood. Quantify it and link it to shift times."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Find the trauma behind the 'stress', elicit all PTSD clusters, screen comorbidity and risk, and name PTSD."},"c-t-39":{"obj":["Took a drug-by-drug history of current use: amount, frequency, cost, route, sharing and last use","Traced the story from first use, including treatment, abstinence and relapse","Established each feature of the dependence syndrome with his own examples","Covered complications, background and risk, including overdose and the unborn baby","Summarised dependence plainly, outlined treatment and gave naloxone"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You lot just want to get me on methadone and forget about me.\"","r":"\"Fair enough, that's how it can look. Today I want to understand what's going on for you first. Treatment comes after, and you'll have a say in it.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I tell you about the baby, social services will take it.\"","r":"\"That's a really common fear, and I'm glad you said it. Their job is to support families. Parents who are in treatment and honest usually keep their babies. We'd do it together.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"The blues are just to help me sleep. They're not a problem.\"","r":"\"Lots of people use them that way. Mixed with heroin, though, they're the main reason people stop breathing. How many do you take, and do you ever use them with gear?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I don't want another blood test. I'd rather not know.\"","r":"\"That makes sense; it's frightening. Hepatitis C can now be cured with tablets in about 8 to 12 weeks. Knowing protects you, Chloe and the baby.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"If I wanted to get off it, what would you actually do?\"","r":"\"It's great that you're asking. We'd check a urine test, then start methadone or buprenorphine at a safe dose, with a keyworker. First, take this naloxone kit today.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"What kind of dad am I going to be?\"","r":"\"That question tells me how much you care. Lots of people get stable on treatment and are good parents. You coming here today is a real first step.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Urine drug screen before any prescription; BBV tests (hepatitis B, C, HIV) and hepatitis B vaccination","Opioid substitution: methadone starting 10-30 mg daily, increased by 5-10 mg every few days, usual 60-120 mg, supervised consumption at first","Or buprenorphine started only once in clear withdrawal (COWS about 12 or more), titrated to around 12-16 mg daily","Naloxone kit today with training for him and Chloe; ECG if methadone dose is high or other QT risks","Street diazepam not prescribed long-term; if dependent, a slow supervised diazepam reduction"],"psych":["Keyworker sessions using motivational interviewing, weekly at first","Psychosocial groups or contingency management at the drug service","Mutual aid: Narcotics Anonymous or SMART Recovery"],"social":["Community drug service prescriber and named keyworker","Needle exchange and safer injecting advice while he still injects","Midwife and children's social care referral for pre-birth assessment, with his knowledge; specialist substance misuse midwife for Chloe","Housing, benefits and debt advice; ask about driving and DVLA"],"safety":["Overdose risk highest after a break or when mixed with benzodiazepines or alcohol; never use alone; test a small amount first","If someone is unresponsive: naloxone, call 999, recovery position; take-home doses in a locked box away from children"]},"facts":[{"h":"Six features of dependence","x":"Craving, impaired control, tolerance, withdrawal (or using to relieve it), drug taking priority over other things, and persisting despite harm. ICD-11 groups these into three."},{"h":"Tolerance drops fast","x":"After prison, detox, hospital or a few days off, a usual dose can kill. Ask about recent breaks and warn him directly."},{"h":"Benzodiazepines plus opioids kill","x":"Both suppress breathing. Street 'diazepam' often contains stronger benzodiazepines such as bromazolam; potent synthetic opioids may also be mixed into heroin."},{"h":"Buprenorphine needs real withdrawal","x":"Started too soon after heroin, it can cause precipitated withdrawal. Wait for clear signs, often 8-12 hours after last heroin, COWS about 12 or more."},{"h":"Hepatitis C is curable","x":"Direct-acting antivirals cure most people in 8-12 weeks. Sharing spoons, filters and water spreads it, not just needles."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Take a full drug history, establish each dependence feature with examples, assess overdose and safeguarding risk, and summarise."},"c-t-41":{"obj":["Characterised her memory complaints with examples, course and fluctuation, and confirmed daily function is intact","Elicited the features of generalised anxiety disorder and screened mood, psychosis, alcohol, medicines and physical causes","Explored her mother's illness, her caring role, retirement and current risk","Explained that this is generalised anxiety disorder, not dementia, and what the CT, bloods and ACE-III scores mean"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Just tell me. Is it dementia? I watched what it did to Mum.\"","r":"\"I can hear how scared you are, and after caring for your mum that makes complete sense. I will give you a straight answer today. First let me hear what's been happening, so the answer fits you.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"My husband says I don't listen any more. That's how it started with Mum.\"","r":"\"That must have been frightening to hear. Can I ask what you're thinking about when he's talking? Often it's that the mind is elsewhere, worrying, rather than that memory is failing.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I do those brain games every night. If I'm slow I lie awake panicking.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that sounds exhausting. Testing yourself every night keeps the fear switched on and makes sleep worse. That pattern is a big clue to what's going on.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not anxious. I've always been a worrier, that's just me.\"","r":"\"Lots of people say that, and I believe it's been part of you a long time. What's changed is how much it takes over now. When worry gets that big, it has a name and it's very treatable.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"But the scan could have missed something, couldn't it?\"","r":"\"It's natural to wonder. No test is perfect, but your scan, bloods, rising score and the way you manage everything at home all point the same way. Together they're far stronger than any single test.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will I get Alzheimer's because Mum had it?\"","r":"\"That's an understandable worry. When a parent develops it in their eighties, your own risk is only slightly higher than average. Most Alzheimer's isn't passed down directly. Looking after your sleep, blood pressure and activity helps everyone.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No dementia medication; no routine repeat ACE-III or scan unless function declines","If she wants medication: GP to offer sertraline 50 mg once daily, review at 2-4 weeks for early jitteriness and nausea","GP to review regular codeine and advise keeping alcohol within 14 units a week; avoid benzodiazepines"],"psych":["NHS Talking Therapies self-referral: guided self-help, then CBT for GAD if needed","Stop nightly memory testing and online symptom searching as a first step"],"social":["Offer a joint talk with her husband, with her consent, to explain the results","Bereavement support (for example Cruse) for the loss of her mother"],"safety":["GP to re-refer if new difficulties with daily tasks, getting lost or recognising people","If mood drops or hopelessness appears, contact GP or NHS 111 option 2"]},"facts":[{"h":"Attention failures mimic memory loss","x":"Information that never goes in cannot be recalled. Anxiety steals attention, so forgetting why you entered a room is common; losing well-established memories is not."},{"h":"A rising score argues against dementia","x":"Neurodegenerative disease declines over time. An ACE-III that improves from 82 to 88, with losses mostly on attention, points to a reversible cause such as anxiety."},{"h":"ACE-III: a screen, not a diagnosis","x":"Scored out of 100; 88 and 82 are the usual cut-offs. Mood, education, language and anxiety all affect the score, so read it with the history."},{"h":"Function is the key question","x":"Dementia requires decline that affects daily life. Ask about money, cooking, appliances, driving, getting lost and recognising people. Intact function is strong reassurance."},{"h":"Late-onset family history: small extra risk","x":"Alzheimer's starting in a parent's eighties raises a child's risk only modestly. Strongly inherited forms usually begin before 65 and affect several relatives."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Explain a diagnosis","do":"Distinguish anxiety-driven attention problems from dementia by history, then explain the diagnosis and each test result plainly."},"c-t-42":{"obj":["Assessed her mood, anxiety, bonding and past depression, and excluded psychosis and mania","Assessed risk to Leah and to Alfie from her, including suicidal thoughts and thoughts of harming the baby","Established the shaking incident in detail and the pattern of domestic abuse, including coercion and isolation","Explained the safeguarding referral honestly and agreed a written plan: paediatric check today, safe place tonight, IDVA, follow-up within a week"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I tell you everything, are you going to take him off me?\"","r":"\"I can see how frightened you are of that. What I've seen today is a mum who protects her baby. Social care's aim is to keep children safe with parents like you, and I'll tell them that.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have stopped Dean. It's my fault.\"","r":"\"You took Alfie off him straight away, and you've told me now. That's protecting him. The responsibility for shaking a baby sits with the person who did it, not with you.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"Dean's not a bad person. He was drunk and tired, that's all.\"","r":"\"I hear that you care about him, and that's real. Drinking and tiredness explain it, but don't make it safe. A baby can be badly hurt in seconds, so we need a plan that protects Alfie.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going to A&E. He's fine, look at him.\"","r":"\"He does look well, and I'm glad. Shaking can cause injury you can't see from the outside, so a children's doctor needs to check him today. I'll ring ahead so you're expected.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"He takes my bank card and checks my phone. He doesn't like me seeing Mum.\"","r":"\"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. Controlling money, your phone and who you see is a form of abuse too. You deserve support, and there are people whose whole job is helping with this.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"If I took tablets, would they get into my breast milk?\"","r":"\"Good question. A small amount gets through, but sertraline, which helped you before, is one of the preferred choices while breastfeeding. Take a leaflet home; we can decide together next week.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Alfie: same-day paediatric assessment via the children's emergency department; clinic rings ahead","Leah: no medication today, as she prefers; leaflet on sertraline (50 mg once daily, compatible with breastfeeding) to decide at the one-week review","EPDS today and at each review; GP to check full blood count and thyroid function"],"psych":["Perinatal community nurse visits weekly; psychiatric review in four weeks","NHS Talking Therapies with perinatal priority: CBT, about 8 weekly sessions","Parent-infant work (for example video interaction guidance) once settled"],"social":["Children's social care referral today, explained to Leah; strategy discussion with police and health may follow","DASH risk checklist today; referral to an IDVA; MARAC if the score is high","Leah and Alfie stay with her mother tonight; housing options officer about separate accommodation","Health visitor, midwife and GP told today; Dean signposted to local drug and alcohol service"],"safety":["Alfie not to be left alone with Dean until social care have assessed","Safe contact by her mother's phone; 999 if Dean becomes violent; National Domestic Abuse Helpline 0808 2000 247","If her mood worsens or thoughts of harm appear: perinatal team duty line or NHS 111 option 2"]},"facts":[{"h":"Shaken infant: medical check the same day","x":"Head injury after shaking may show only as drowsiness, vomiting, poor feeding, irritability or fits. Any concern of physical harm to a non-mobile baby needs paediatric assessment today."},{"h":"Crying peaks at six to eight weeks","x":"Normal infant crying rises from about two weeks and peaks around six to eight weeks. This is the highest-risk time for shaking by a frustrated carer."},{"h":"Sertraline is preferred in breastfeeding","x":"Breast-milk levels are low and the baby's blood levels usually undetectable. Start 50 mg once daily; monitor the baby for poor feeding or drowsiness."},{"h":"Abuse often starts or worsens around birth","x":"Pregnancy and the first months after birth are times when domestic abuse commonly begins or escalates. Ask every new mother, alone, every time."},{"h":"You can share without consent","x":"To protect a child from serious harm, share information with social care without consent. Tell the parent what you are sharing unless that would put the child at greater risk."}],"type":"Risk assessment + Safety plan","do":"Assess every direction of risk to mother and baby, then agree a safeguarding plan she understands and accepts."},"c-t-44":{"obj":["Elicited his picture of ECT, his main fear and what he hopes for","Explained why ECT is suggested now, how likely it is to help, and what happens on a treatment day and over a course","Gave honest, specific information about memory and other side effects and how they are reduced","Addressed his blood pressure and kidneys, stressed his right to choose or stop, and offered alternatives and written information"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I've seen it in films. They held the man down and he screamed.\"","r":"\"That's a frightening image, and it's no wonder you're worried. Modern ECT is completely different: you're asleep under an anaesthetic, with a muscle relaxant, so you feel nothing and barely move.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I've done the crossword every morning for forty years. I can't lose that.\"","r":"\"That clearly matters a great deal to you. Most memory effects are gaps around the time of treatment, and they usually come back. We check your memory as we go and can adjust the treatment.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why should this work when the tablets haven't?\"","r":"\"That's a fair question after two disappointments. ECT works differently from tablets, and no treatment works better for severe depression, especially in people your age. Most improve, often within a few weeks.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will it give me epilepsy, or make me bite my tongue?\"","r":"\"Good to ask. ECT doesn't cause epilepsy. The seizure is brief and controlled, and a soft mouth guard protects your tongue and teeth.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"What about my blood pressure and kidneys? Is it safe for me?\"","r":"\"I'm glad you raised that. We'll do blood tests, an ECG and an anaesthetic check first. Your blood pressure rises briefly during treatment and the anaesthetist manages it. They'll advise about your ramipril.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I'm just a burden on everyone. Maybe I don't deserve fixing.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry you feel that way; that feeling is part of the depression talking. You do deserve to get better, and your family want you back. Let's find the treatment that suits you.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Workup before any treatment: FBC, U&Es and eGFR, ECG, and anaesthetic assessment","Anaesthetist to advise whether ramipril is taken or held on treatment mornings; BP checked before and after each treatment","If he agrees: usually twice weekly, often 6-12 treatments, right unilateral ultrabrief pulse first to protect memory","MoCA and depression rating at baseline and after every 2-3 treatments; continue an antidepressant afterwards"],"psych":["ECT leaflet and video today; visit to the ECT suite with the ECT nurse","Behavioural activation or CBT as he recovers"],"social":["Family meeting with his wife and daughter, with his consent","Dietitian and OT support while appetite is poor"],"safety":["Food and fluid chart and weekly weight while he decides; escalate if intake falls further","No driving during the course or until advised; nurse escort to and from ECT"]},"facts":[{"h":"Most people with severe depression improve","x":"ECT is the most effective acute treatment for severe depression. Older adults and those with psychotic features respond particularly well, and improvement often comes within two to four weeks."},{"h":"A course: usually twice weekly","x":"Most courses run twice weekly, often 6-12 treatments in total, reviewed after each session. Consent covers a stated number and can be withdrawn at any time."},{"h":"Memory: be specific, not reassuring","x":"Gaps for events around the course are common and mostly recover over weeks to months. A minority report lasting gaps. Right unilateral placement and ultrabrief pulse reduce effects."},{"h":"Death is very rare","x":"Risk is about the same as any brief general anaesthetic, roughly 2 deaths per 100,000 treatments, usually heart-related."},{"h":"Informal patients can refuse","x":"Valid consent is required and can be withdrawn at any time. Even if detained, a capacitous refusal of ECT stands except in a Section 62 emergency."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explore his beliefs, then explain ECT's rationale, procedure, memory effects and alternatives so he can decide."},"c-t-45":{"obj":["Kept control of the conversation and elicited his symptoms and why he stopped lithium","Assessed risks to money, work, driving, vulnerability and others, and screened for psychosis","Explained that this is a manic relapse and that two episodes mean bipolar disorder","Agreed a plan: antipsychotic with side effects explained, lithium discussed, home treatment, family, safety steps and admission as a fallback"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not ill, doc. I'm the best I've ever been.\"","r":"\"I can see you feel amazing, and I don't want to take that from you. Two hours' sleep and spending thousands is also how it started last time. Let's look at it together.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You lot just want to drug the creativity out of me.\"","r":"\"That's a real worry, and lots of creative people share it. The aim is to get you sleeping and thinking clearly, so your film actually gets made, not burned out in hospital.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going back on lithium. The shakes, the thirst, the blood tests.\"","r":"\"Fair enough; those are real side effects. We don't need to decide on lithium today. There are other medicines that work for the highs now, and we can talk long-term options when you're settled.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you going to section me again?\"","r":"\"I can hear that last time was frightening. I'd much rather treat you at home with your family's help. Hospital only comes up if the medicine isn't working or things get unsafe.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So what is it? Am I bipolar now?\"","r":"\"Good question. Having two episodes of mania means the diagnosis is bipolar disorder. It's common and treatable, and with the right plan many people stay well for years.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"I'll take the tablets if I can keep working on the film.\"","r":"\"I like that you're open to the tablets. Write the ideas down, by all means. Let's hold off on buying more or quitting your job for a few weeks until your sleep is back.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Olanzapine 10 mg at night, starting today (range 5-20 mg); warn about sedation, appetite and weight gain","Baseline today: FBC, U&Es, eGFR, LFTs, TFTs, calcium, HbA1c, lipids, weight, BP; ECG if indicated","Lorazepam 1 mg at night as needed for up to 5 nights if sleep stays under 4 hours","Once settled, discuss restarting lithium (e.g. Priadel 400 mg at night, level 12 h post-dose at 1 week, target 0.6-0.8 mmol/L) or another long-term option","No antidepressant; no caffeine or alcohol"],"psych":["Psychoeducation about bipolar disorder and early warning signs once calmer","Structured group psychoeducation or individual therapy for bipolar disorder after recovery","Relapse prevention plan written with him and his family"],"social":["Crisis Resolution and Home Treatment Team daily visits from tomorrow; care coordinator from the CMHT","Stays with parents; carers' assessment offered to them","Fit note for 2 weeks; no job resignation; credit card held by parents for now; try to return the equipment"],"safety":["Must stop driving and notify DVLA; advice documented","Admission, informal or via Mental Health Act assessment, if he stops medication or risk rises","Crisis line and NHS 111 option 2; family to call 999 in an emergency"]},"facts":[{"h":"Two manic episodes mean bipolar I","x":"A single manic episode is enough for a bipolar I diagnosis in ICD-11 and DSM-5. A second confirms the pattern and the need for long-term treatment."},{"h":"Stopping lithium suddenly triggers relapse","x":"Abrupt discontinuation sharply raises manic relapse risk within weeks to months. If lithium is stopped, reduce gradually over at least four weeks."},{"h":"NICE first-line for acute mania","x":"If not on an antipsychotic or mood stabiliser: haloperidol, olanzapine, quetiapine or risperidone. Stop any antidepressant."},{"h":"Lithium remains the best long-term option","x":"Lithium is first-line for preventing relapse. Levels 12 hours after the dose; usual target 0.6-0.8 mmol/L. Renal and thyroid tests are due six-monthly."},{"h":"Mania and driving","x":"People with mania must stop driving and notify the DVLA. Tell him, and document that you have."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Management plan","do":"Keep the lead with a manic patient, name mania and bipolar disorder, and agree medication, setting and safety steps."},"c-t-46":{"obj":["Gathered Tessa's account of Liam's usual self, the new police beliefs and missed risperidone","Explained the finding using all four abilities, showing that weighing up fails because of his belief","Agreed a best-interests plan for bloods, drip and endoscopy, starting with the least restrictive steps","Answered proxy consent, delay, legality and restraint, and named who will be consulted","Arranged psychiatric follow-up and confirmed Tessa's understanding of the plan"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"He knows exactly what they told him. How can you say he can't decide?\"","r":"\"You're right that he understands it, and that counts. But he can't weigh it up, because he's certain the tests are a police plot. That one belief blocks the balancing part.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"His mum's his next of kin. Shouldn't she be deciding?\"","r":"\"It's a natural thought. In England no relative can consent for an adult unless they hold a health and welfare power of attorney. We'll phone her today, though, because her view counts in deciding what's best.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can't it wait till the morning? He'll be calmer.\"","r":"\"I understand wanting to give him time. He's vomited blood twice and could bleed heavily again tonight, so the blood tests and drip can't wait. We'll go gently, with you there.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I can't watch them pin him down. He'll never trust me again.\"","r":"\"That fear makes complete sense, and it shows how much he matters to you. Holding him is the last resort. We start with you, pictures, and one step at a time.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it even legal to do it against what he says?\"","r":"\"Yes. When someone lacks capacity for a decision and treatment is urgent, the Mental Capacity Act lets staff act in his best interests, using only the force that's necessary and proportionate, and recording it.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I found his tablets in the bin. I should have told someone sooner.\"","r":"\"You noticed, and you've told us now, which really helps. Missing risperidone may explain the police worries, so that's something we can actually fix.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Best-interests treatment under the MCA now: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, clotting, group and crossmatch; IV access and fluids per the medical team","OGD within 24 hours (immediately after resuscitation if unstable), with sedation planned to reduce distress; PPI after endoscopy only if indicated","Restraint only if necessary and proportionate, as brief as possible, and recorded; DoLS urgent authorisation if he must be kept on the ward against his wishes","Once stable: review risperidone adherence and dose with his community LD psychiatrist; ECG, prolactin and metabolic bloods"],"psych":["Tessa and the learning disability liaison nurse present to explain each step in short words and easy-read pictures","Reassess capacity once bleeding is controlled and again before each further major decision; record each time","Liaison psychiatry review of mental state within 24 hours of stabilising; debrief with Liam afterwards"],"social":["Mother phoned today for his wishes and values; IMCA referral only if no appropriate person can be consulted","Social worker and supported housing manager informed today; plan for supervised medication at home","Community learning disability team follow-up after discharge"],"safety":["If he tries to leave while bleeding, ward staff may act under the MCA to keep him safe; call security and the medical registrar","Liaison psychiatry to review again tonight or first thing tomorrow"]},"facts":[{"h":"Understanding is not capacity","x":"He must also be able to use or weigh the information. A fixed false belief that blocks weighing the risk of death means he lacks capacity for that decision."},{"h":"Nobody can consent for an adult","x":"Relatives, carers and next of kin cannot consent or refuse for an adult, unless they hold a health and welfare LPA or are a court-appointed deputy."},{"h":"Unwise is not incapable","x":"If he could weigh the risks and still refused, the refusal would stand, however unwise. Say this out loud: it shows the law protects him too."},{"h":"MHA does not treat a stomach","x":"The Mental Health Act covers treatment for mental disorder. Bloods, drips and endoscopy for someone lacking capacity are given under the Mental Capacity Act."},{"h":"Emergency treatment does not wait","x":"An IMCA is needed for serious treatment when nobody suitable can be consulted, but urgent life-saving treatment is not delayed to find one."}],"type":"Capacity assessment + Management plan","do":"Explain why he lacks capacity for this decision and agree an urgent, least restrictive best-interests plan with his carer."},"c-t-47":{"obj":["Elicited Patricia's concerns: sterilisation, the baby's health, the burden on her, money and exhaustion","Clarified the exposing incidents and current support, and screened Patricia's mood and safety","Explained that sterilisation is Callum's own decision, with his rights and the role of a court if he lacked capacity","Answered the inheritance fear and explained parenting support, reassuring her she will not be made to raise the baby","Agreed a plan for Callum's behaviour and stress and for Patricia's own support, including a carer's assessment"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"He can't look after himself. How's he meant to look after a baby? Just stop it happening again.\"","r":"\"I can hear how frightened and worn out you are. I can't arrange an operation, but I can help with what's behind this: the baby, the support, and you.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I'm his mother. I've always made his decisions.\"","r":"\"You've cared for him for 26 years, and that matters enormously. For something permanent like this, though, the law says only Callum can decide. Your views still count in his care.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"The baby will be like him. I know it.\"","r":"\"That's a very understandable worry. Callum's disability came from lack of oxygen at birth. That's an injury, not something in his genes, so it isn't passed on.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Social services will hand that baby to me. I'm nearly sixty.\"","r":"\"That sounds like a real dread. You won't be expected to raise the baby. Social care's job is to help Callum and Jade parent, with support made for them.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Twenty-six years on my own. Nobody's ever asked how I am.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry nobody has asked. That's a long time to carry it alone. I'd like to make sure you get help in your own right, starting today.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What about the exposing? Is he going to end up in prison?\"","r":"\"I take it seriously. Both times he was very stressed. Our team can assess it properly and help him find safer ways to cope. We'll also make sure the police know about his disability.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Sterilisation only with Callum's own informed consent; if he lacked capacity, only a Court of Protection decision in his own best interests","Contraception for Callum and Jade to choose with their GP or sexual health clinic, with easy-read information","CLDT psychiatrist to assess Callum for anxiety or low mood; any medication for sexual impulsivity only after specialist forensic assessment","Patricia's GP to assess her mood and sleep; treat depression if present"],"psych":["CLDT psychology: assessment of the exposing (triggers, cognitions, risk), then stress-management and sexual behaviour work","Accessible sex and relationships education for Callum, with speech and language therapy input if communication under stress is a problem","Adapted parenting programme for Callum and Jade after the pre-birth assessment","Talking Therapies self-referral or a carers' support group for Patricia"],"social":["Callum's social worker to liaise with children's social care for a joint pre-birth assessment and support plan","Carer's assessment for Patricia under the Care Act 2014; carers' centre and Mencap for support and breaks","Benefits check and budgeting support for Callum and for Patricia","Police and solicitor told of his disability; appropriate adult for any further interview"],"safety":["Public safeguarding considered by the CLDT after the forensic risk assessment","Patricia given GP and NHS 111 option 2 if she feels unable to cope; review with her and Callum in 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Only Callum can consent","x":"No relative can consent to sterilisation for an adult. If he lacked capacity, it would in practice need a Court of Protection decision, in his interests only."},{"h":"Birth injury is not inherited","x":"Lack of oxygen at birth causes disability through injury, not genes. It does not raise the chance his own child is affected."},{"h":"Pre-birth assessment aims to support","x":"Children's social care assesses before birth. Parents with intellectual disability are entitled to adapted support; removal is considered only if the child is unsafe despite help."},{"h":"Carers are entitled to assessment","x":"Under the Care Act 2014, anyone who appears to need support as a carer has a right to a local authority assessment, whatever their income."},{"h":"Appropriate adult at interview","x":"Under PACE Code C, a vulnerable adult interviewed by police must have an appropriate adult. Make sure police know about his intellectual disability."}],"type":"Professional and ethical dilemma + Management plan","do":"Decline sterilisation with clear reasons, address each fear underneath, and agree support for Callum, Jade, the baby and her."},"c-t-48":{"obj":["Obtained a structured handover: sleep, eating, voices, behaviour, observations, withdrawal signs and drug testing","Identified the risk to others and to himself, with the reasons for each","Explained the likely diagnosis of psychosis, with substances as a possible factor and the limits of current information","Agreed investigations, observations, collateral and your own review today","Kept him on the healthcare unit, explained the route to hospital transfer, and agreed communication and escalation"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"It's GHB. Half my beds are drug cases. He needs to go back.\"","r":"\"You may be right that drugs are part of it, and I know how many you've had. But the voices began weeks before he came here, so let's not assume yet.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"He's been as good as gold. You're making a lot of nothing.\"","r":"\"Calm here is good news. But it's two days on a quiet unit. Voices telling him to defend himself, urges to hurt people and a violent offence could look very different on a wing.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"His urine was clean, though.\"","r":"\"Thanks for doing it. Standard screens usually don't test for GHB, and it clears in hours, so negative doesn't settle it. A repeat with bloods will help.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"It's relentless. I get it in the neck from everyone about beds.\"","r":"\"That sounds exhausting, and you're doing a hard job well. I'll speak to the healthcare manager myself, so the decision isn't sitting on you.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So when can he go back?\"","r":"\"Not until I've assessed him. If it settles and it was drug-related, maybe within days. If it's psychosis needing treatment, he'll need transfer to hospital.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can't you just give him an injection to calm him down?\"","r":"\"I understand the wish to settle him fast. We can't treat him against his will here, and he needs assessing first. If he's distressed, oral promethazine as needed for now.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods today: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, glucose, TFTs, CK; ECG; repeat urine drug screen; NEWS2 observations","Watch for late GHB withdrawal (tremor, sweats, agitation, confusion); diazepam only under medical advice if it recurs","If psychosis is confirmed after assessment: aripiprazole 10 mg once daily (or olanzapine 5–10 mg at night) with baseline weight, HbA1c, lipids and prolactin","Promethazine 25 mg orally as needed for distress or sleep, rather than repeated benzodiazepines"],"psych":["Forensic psychiatry full mental state and risk assessment today; daily review by the mental health in-reach team","Trauma screen once settled; supportive work after the assault he witnessed","Referral to the prison drug and alcohol team"],"social":["Remain on the healthcare unit; no return to the wing until reassessed","Section 47/49 transfer referral if psychosis needs inpatient treatment, aiming for 28 days","Collateral from previous wing staff, his family and previous mental health records; inform his offender manager"],"safety":["Need-to-know risk sharing with wing and security staff; consider two officers to unlock if risk rises","Consider ACCT given 'rather die than be attacked' and possible drug debt","Emergency response and on-call doctor at once if aggressive, confused or physically unwell; review by this afternoon"]},"facts":[{"h":"GHB hides from standard screens","x":"Most urine drug screens do not include GHB, and it clears within about 12 hours. A negative result does not exclude recent use."},{"h":"GHB withdrawal comes fast","x":"Withdrawal begins within hours of the last dose: tremor, sweats, agitation, delirium, sometimes psychosis. It can be dangerous; benzodiazepines are used under medical advice."},{"h":"No enforced treatment in prison","x":"Prison healthcare units are not hospitals under the MHA. Compulsory treatment needs transfer under section 47 (sentenced) or section 48 (remand, urgent need)."},{"h":"Threat plus control raises risk","x":"Feeling threatened together with feeling controlled by an outside force is linked to violence, especially with past violence and substance use."},{"h":"Transfer within 28 days","x":"NHS England guidance aims for transfer to hospital within 28 days of the first referral. Escalate any delay to commissioners."}],"type":"Handover and escalation + Risk assessment","do":"Take a structured handover, judge risk, and keep him on healthcare with a safe plan despite bed pressure."},"c-t-49":{"obj":["Gathered the current picture: mood, sleep, energy, spending, approaching strangers and irritability, while holding boundaries","Explored why and how he stopped lithium, and assessed practical risks including driving, money and work","Named mania and bipolar disorder using his own account, and linked the relapse to stopping lithium abruptly","Agreed an antipsychotic now and discussed lithium for the long term, with benefits and side effects","Agreed home treatment with family support, practical safeguards and admission as a fallback, confirmed by teach-back"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You'd be great in my video, actually. Want a part?\"","r":"\"That's kind of you, but I'll stick to being your doctor today. Tell me how you've been sleeping.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I've never felt better. This isn't illness, it's talent.\"","r":"\"Your talent is real, and I'm not taking that away. But two hours' sleep and four thousand pounds in a few weeks worry me, and they're exactly how it started last time.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going back on lithium. It made me shake and feel flat.\"","r":"\"That's a fair complaint, especially for a designer. We can start with something else now, and if we revisit lithium, the shake can often be eased with a lower dose or another tablet.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"You're going to section me again, aren't you?\"","r":"\"I'd much rather not, and I don't think we need to. If you take treatment and stay with your parents, the home team can visit daily. Hospital only if that isn't enough.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"So it's all just illness? Everything I've made?\"","r":"\"That's hard to hear, and I'm sorry. The ideas are yours. It's the speed and the no-sleep that are the illness, and settling them protects what you've built.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can't I just take a sleeping tablet and carry on?\"","r":"\"Sleep matters a lot, you're right. But a sleeping tablet won't calm the high itself. Quetiapine helps both, and it's usually for some weeks, then we review.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Quetiapine (immediate release) 50 mg twice daily on day 1, 100 mg twice daily on day 2, 150 mg twice daily on day 3, 200 mg twice daily on day 4; range 400–800 mg/day. Warn: sedation, dizziness, weight gain","Alternative if he wants less sedation: aripiprazole 15 mg once daily (licensed for mania; restlessness possible)","Baseline: weight, BP, pulse, FBC, U&Es, eGFR, LFTs, TFTs, calcium, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin; ECG","Lithium restart when agreed: e.g. Priadel 400 mg at night; level 12 hours post dose 4–7 days after starting, weekly until stable, target 0.6–0.8 mmol/L; tremor: reduce dose or add propranolol","No antidepressant; avoid alcohol and drugs; short-term promethazine 25 mg at night if sleep remains poor"],"psych":["Psychoeducation on bipolar disorder and the risk of stopping lithium suddenly (taper over at least 4 weeks if ever stopped)","Structured psychological intervention for bipolar disorder once euthymic, via the CMHT psychology team","Written relapse signature with family: sleep loss as the first warning sign"],"social":["Home treatment team daily visits from tomorrow; stays with his parents","Father holds credit and bank cards for a few weeks; debt advice via StepChange or Citizens Advice","Fit note for work; care coordinator allocated through the CMHT","Carers' assessment offered to his parents"],"safety":["Stop driving now and notify the DVLA","Crisis line or NHS 111 option 2; family to call 999 if he is at immediate risk","Review in 1 week; admission if he deteriorates or stops treatment"]},"facts":[{"h":"Stopping lithium suddenly invites mania","x":"Abrupt withdrawal raises the risk of manic relapse. If lithium is ever stopped, reduce it gradually over at least 4 weeks, ideally up to 3 months."},{"h":"NICE first choices for mania","x":"If not already on an antimanic drug: haloperidol, olanzapine, quetiapine or risperidone. Stop any antidepressant."},{"h":"Lithium levels: 12 hours post dose","x":"Check 4–7 days after starting or changing dose, weekly until stable, then every 3 months in year one. Usual target 0.6–0.8 mmol/L."},{"h":"Mania means no driving","x":"DVLA: people with mania or hypomania must not drive during the episode and must notify the DVLA."},{"h":"One manic episode is enough","x":"ICD-11 needs only one manic episode to diagnose bipolar type I. A second episode removes any doubt and strengthens the case for long-term treatment."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Management plan","do":"Name mania and bipolar disorder kindly, keep focus, and agree medication and home-based care he accepts."},"c-t-50":{"obj":["Elicited the onset of his depression, current symptoms and alcohol use","Assessed suicide risk, including plans, access to medicines at work and protective factors","Took a full medication history and identified the sexual side effect of mirtazapine","Answered the serotonin question honestly, separating how antidepressants work from whether they work","Agreed a plan covering a switch, next-step augmentation or rTMS, CBT, and a supported return to work"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"The serotonin theory's been debunked. So why would any of this work?\"","r":"\"You're right about the theory; the simple low-serotonin idea isn't supported. Whether antidepressants help is a separate question, and the large trials still show they do for many people.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"It's embarrassing, but I can't ejaculate properly since the mirtazapine.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. It's common, it's often why people give up on tablets, and it's fixable. Some options have much less of that effect.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I'm a consultant. I'm meant to fix things, and I can't fix myself.\"","r":"\"That sounds painful, and being a doctor can make it harder to be a patient. Depression isn't a failure of effort. It's an illness, and it's treatable.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Therapy? I had counselling. It was just chatting.\"","r":"\"That's a fair experience. CBT is different: structured, focused on how thoughts like 'I've failed' drive mood and behaviour, with tasks between sessions. Combined with medication, it works best at your level.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Do I have to tell the GMC?\"","r":"\"I can see why that worries you. Not just for having depression that's treated and that you're managing safely. What matters is following occupational health advice about your work.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Sometimes I think the kids would cope without me.\"","r":"\"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. It's important. Have you made any plans, or thought about how? And is there anything at work or home you could use?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Cross-taper: start vortioxetine 10 mg each morning and reduce mirtazapine over 1–2 weeks; increase to 20 mg after 2–4 weeks if tolerated. Warn: nausea in the first week","Alternative: agomelatine 25 mg at bedtime (up to 50 mg); LFTs at baseline and about 3, 6, 12 and 24 weeks","Baseline bloods: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, TFTs, B12, folate, HbA1c; alcohol to under 14 units a week","If no response after 4–6 weeks at full dose: add lithium (levels 12 hours post dose, eGFR, TFTs, calcium) or aripiprazole; rTMS as a further option","Short supplies only; no hypnotic unless essential (zopiclone 3.75–7.5 mg for no more than 2 weeks)"],"psych":["High-intensity individual CBT, about 16–20 weekly sessions, combined with medication","Behavioural activation while off work: a weekly timetable of exercise, time with his children and small tasks","Focused support around the divorce and separation"],"social":["Practitioner Health referral for confidential care outside his own hospital","Occupational health referral for a phased return without on-call and with review dates","BMA wellbeing support and peer support; legal and financial advice for the divorce","GP to continue the fit note until psychiatry and occupational health agree he is ready"],"safety":["Crisis plan: NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123, 999 in an emergency","No medicines from work stock; limited supplies at home; review in 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Mechanism and efficacy are different","x":"A 2022 umbrella review found no consistent evidence that low serotonin causes depression. Separately, large meta-analyses show antidepressants outperform placebo in acute depression."},{"h":"Vortioxetine after two failures","x":"NICE TA367 supports vortioxetine when two antidepressants have not worked in this episode. Start 10 mg daily; range 5–20 mg. Early nausea is common."},{"h":"Agomelatine needs liver checks","x":"LFTs before starting and at about 3, 6, 12 and 24 weeks, and after dose increases. Stop if transaminases exceed three times the upper limit."},{"h":"rTMS is a NICE-backed option","x":"NICE IPG542 supports rTMS for depression with normal arrangements. Courses are usually daily weekday sessions for 4–6 weeks; availability varies by trust."},{"h":"No automatic report to the GMC","x":"Doctors need not report depression that is treated and well managed. They must follow medical and occupational health advice if their work could be affected."}],"type":"Medication review + Management plan","do":"Answer the serotonin doubt honestly, review his medicines, and agree switch, therapy and return-to-work plans with him."},"c-t-52":{"obj":["Acknowledged Rhiannon's worry and confirmed the key facts with one safety question, without taking a history","Named refeeding syndrome and explained its cause and mechanism in plain words","Explained why Amira's hands and feet are swollen and why swallowing is hard, and how dangerous it can be","Arranged same-day paediatric assessment with ECG, bloods, electrolyte replacement, thiamine and a controlled feeding plan","Explained prevention and how to support Amira's mother, and agreed who does what and the warning signs"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Did I miss something? I've been seeing her every few days.\"","r":"\"You've done exactly the right thing by noticing and calling. This can develop in days. What matters now is that she's seen today, and you've made that happen.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So should her mum stop giving her food?\"","r":"\"No, and that's really important. Stopping food is dangerous too. She needs food coming in steadily, at a pace her body can handle, with the hospital checking her blood salts every day.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why are her hands and feet puffy?\"","r":"\"Good question. The surge of insulin makes the kidneys hold salt and water. Starving has weakened her heart muscle, and extra fluid puts it under pressure; swelling may be the first sign.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why would she struggle to swallow?\"","r":"\"Every muscle runs on phosphate. Hers is low, so they tire, including those for swallowing and breathing. No one should push food on her until the doctors check her.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Her mum will be furious. She thinks this means she's finally getting better.\"","r":"\"That's understandable. She's been terrified. Let's be clear it's not her fault; many parents feed faster out of love. The weight jumped mostly from fluid. The ward will show her the safe pace.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can't she just have a blood test at the GP tomorrow?\"","r":"\"I see why that seems simpler. But with swelling and swallowing trouble, her heart rhythm and salts need checking today, and treatment can start straight away. Tomorrow could be too late.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Same-day paediatric assessment unit: ECG (QTc, rhythm), lying and standing BP and pulse, temperature, daily weight, fluid balance","Bloods today and daily for the first week: phosphate, potassium, magnesium, calcium, sodium, glucose, U&E, LFTs, FBC","Replace phosphate (oral, or IV if very low or she cannot swallow), potassium and magnesium as per paediatric protocol","Thiamine and a vitamin B compound strong plus a multivitamin, started before and continued during feeding","Dietitian-led plan per MEED: start around 1400 to 2000 kcal a day and increase steadily; avoid both overfeeding and underfeeding"],"psych":["Psychoeducation for Amira and her mother on safe weight gain, 0.5 to 1 kg a week, and why","Family therapy for anorexia nervosa (FT-AN), 18 to 20 sessions over a year, by the CAMHS eating disorder team once she is stable"],"social":["Rhiannon phones Amira's mother now and arranges transport; CAMHS consultant and paediatric consultant agree a joint care plan","Rhiannon debriefs with her supervisor today; CAMHS eating disorder team visits the ward","School informed of absence with consent; Beat helpline details for the family"],"safety":["Call 999 if chest pain, palpitations, fainting, breathlessness, confusion or a fit before she is seen","Nothing pushed by mouth while swallowing is weak; nil by mouth decisions left to the paediatric team"]},"facts":[{"h":"Phosphate is the key marker","x":"Low phosphate after intake increases is the hallmark of refeeding syndrome. Check it daily for the first week of refeeding."},{"h":"3 kg in a week is fluid","x":"Safe gain is about 0.5 to 1 kg a week. A jump of 2 kg or more in a week usually means fluid retention and refeeding risk."},{"h":"Thiamine before food goes up","x":"Thiamine is used up as carbohydrate metabolism restarts. Give it with a vitamin B compound to prevent heart failure and Wernicke's encephalopathy."},{"h":"Underfeeding also harms","x":"MEED warns that overly cautious feeding causes further weight loss and harm. Feed steadily and monitor bloods closely."},{"h":"Highest risk in the first days","x":"Electrolytes fall most in the first 3 to 7 days of increased intake. A normal test a week ago does not reassure today."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Emergency management","do":"Explain refeeding syndrome to the nurse, answer her questions, and arrange same-day paediatric assessment and treatment."},"c-t-53":{"obj":["Engaged a guarded young man and clarified the recent change: irritability, staying in, and when it began","Elicited the restraint during his relapse, his fear of dying, and the TV reminder that set the symptoms off","Elicited re-experiencing, avoidance and hyperarousal with his own examples","Excluded psychotic relapse: voices, persecutory beliefs, olanzapine adherence and cannabis","Assessed mood and risk to self, to his family and to the police, and explained PTSD with the treatment that helps"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm fine. They worry about everything.\"","r":"\"Maybe they do. I'd still like to hear it from you. Staying in all day isn't much fun. What's made going out hard?\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You think I'm going mad again, don't you? That's why I'm here.\"","r":"\"I don't assume that. Something has changed, and I want to find out what. Sometimes it's the illness, sometimes it's something else entirely. Can I ask you about both?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"They pinned me down on the grass. I couldn't breathe. I thought that was it.\"","r":"\"That sounds terrifying. Thank you for telling me. Do you mind if I ask what happens when you remember it now?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I hear a siren and I'm back there. My chest goes tight.\"","r":"\"That must be exhausting, being dragged back like that. That's called a flashback, and it's a common reaction to something frightening. It can be treated.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"I hate the police. They nearly killed me.\"","r":"\"Your anger makes sense after what happened. I need to ask: have you had any thoughts of getting back at them, or anyone else?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So it's not my schizophrenia coming back?\"","r":"\"From everything you've told me, no. No voices, no feeling watched, and you're taking your tablet. This is a reaction to the arrest, called PTSD, and it responds well to the right therapy.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue olanzapine 10 mg at night unchanged; annual physical health check: weight, BP, HbA1c, lipids","No routine medication first-line for PTSD; if he prefers medication or cannot do therapy, sertraline 50 mg daily, review at 2 to 4 weeks, increase to 100 to 200 mg if needed","Avoid benzodiazepines for sleep; sleep hygiene and a nightmare plan first"],"psych":["Trauma-focused CBT, 8 to 12 weekly sessions with the psychology team, adapted for psychosis","EMDR as an alternative, 8 to 12 sessions with a trained therapist","Care coordinator teaches grounding techniques now while he waits"],"social":["Care coordinator: graded plan for going out, starting with short trips with his brother","Family session with consent: explain PTSD and how to help without pushing","Peer support or recovery college course once outings are easier; drug service if cannabis returns"],"safety":["Crisis plan: CMHT duty line in office hours, NHS 111 option 2 out of hours, 999 in an emergency","Care coordinator review in 2 weeks; clinic review in 4 weeks; recheck psychosis and risk at each contact"]},"facts":[{"h":"PTSD has three clusters in ICD-11","x":"Reliving in the present, avoiding reminders, and feeling under threat now (on edge, startling), for several weeks, with impaired functioning."},{"h":"Delayed onset is common","x":"Symptoms can start months after the event, often after a reminder. A gap does not exclude PTSD."},{"h":"Restraint can be trauma","x":"Many people with psychosis describe parts of their own care as traumatic: police, restraint, seclusion. Always ask how the admission happened."},{"h":"Therapy first, not tablets","x":"NICE NG116: trauma-focused CBT or EMDR first. Offer an SSRI or venlafaxine only if he prefers medication or cannot engage."},{"h":"Psychosis is not a barrier","x":"Trauma-focused therapy is safe and effective in stable psychosis. Do not wait for 'complete' recovery before referring."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Explain a diagnosis","do":"Exclude psychotic relapse, elicit PTSD after the police restraint, assess risk, and explain the diagnosis to him."},"c-t-54":{"obj":["Clarified what changed: both parents' deaths and everything they did for Martin","Gathered what Leah has seen, and screened other drugs, smoking, fits, physical health, risk and support","Explained a plain-language formulation linking his illness, the bereavement, lost care, drinking and missed clozapine, with his strengths","Explained the effects of alcohol on his mental and physical health, including dependence","Explained the clozapine risks, the 48-hour restart rule and the stockpile, and agreed next steps"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Mum did everything for him. Now there's no one, and I can't be there.\"","r":"\"You've lost your mum and dad, and you're carrying him on top of two small children. That's a huge amount. You aren't failing him; let's get the team doing more.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So what's actually happening to him?\"","r":"\"I think three things are feeding each other: his grief, the drink, and missed clozapine. Together they're letting his old illness creep back. Let me explain how they connect.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Could he be addicted?\"","r":"\"It's possible. If he needs a drink in the morning, gets shaky without it, or needs more for the same effect, that suggests dependence. The team will assess this properly.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have noticed the tablets sooner.\"","r":"\"You noticed, and you came. That's what matters. Counting tablets was your mum's job for years; nobody could expect you to step into that overnight.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will he end up like he was before clozapine? He was in hospital for a year.\"","r":"\"That's a real fear, and I understand it. The early signs are there, but early is good. Getting him back on clozapine safely and helping with the drink gives him the best chance.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Should I just tell him to take his usual tablets tonight?\"","r":"\"Please don't. After more than two days off clozapine, his usual dose could make him collapse or have a fit. The team needs to restart it at a low dose and build up.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Should he just stop drinking?\"","r":"\"Cutting down is the goal, but if he's dependent, stopping suddenly can cause fits, more likely on clozapine. The alcohol team will plan it safely.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Care coordinator visit within 48 hours: confirm last clozapine dose; if more than 48 hours, restart at 12.5 mg and retitrate with pulse, BP and temperature checks","Bloods: FBC with neutrophils, LFTs, GGT, MCV, U&E; clozapine plasma level once steady; ECG","Alcohol: AUDIT; thiamine 100 mg three times daily; if dependent, planned assisted withdrawal under specialist guidance, with caution combining benzodiazepines and clozapine","Ask about constipation at every visit; laxatives if needed; warn that changing how much he smokes alters clozapine levels"],"psych":["Bereavement support through Cruse or the CMHT psychologist","Motivational interviewing on alcohol with the care coordinator or local alcohol service","Family intervention including Leah, about 10 sessions over 3 to 12 months, with a relapse signature plan"],"social":["Pharmacy weekly blister pack, or supervised dispensing; daily prompt call from support worker","Ward or CMHT social worker: home support, benefits, probate and housing now his parents have died","Carers' assessment for Leah; Rethink Mental Illness carers' support"],"safety":["Spare clozapine returned to the pharmacy this week","If very drowsy, fitting, severely constipated, suicidal or very paranoid: 999 or NHS 111 option 2; CMHT duty line in hours"]},"facts":[{"h":"The 48-hour rule","x":"If clozapine is missed for more than 48 hours, restart at 12.5 mg and build up again. Restarting the old dose risks collapse, seizures and severe low blood pressure."},{"h":"Alcohol plus clozapine sedates","x":"Both depress the brain: more drowsiness, low blood pressure, falls, slowed breathing and choking on saliva or vomit."},{"h":"Two routes to a fit","x":"Clozapine lowers the seizure threshold, and alcohol withdrawal causes fits. Together the risk rises; never stop heavy drinking abruptly."},{"h":"Smoking changes the level","x":"Tobacco smoke speeds clozapine breakdown. Stopping or cutting down can push the level up steeply within a week or so; recheck it."},{"h":"Constipation can kill","x":"Clozapine slows the gut; dehydration from alcohol adds to it. Ask about bowels every visit; severe constipation is an emergency."},{"h":"A stockpile is an overdose","x":"After a break, tolerance is lost. Two weeks of spare clozapine taken at once could be fatal, especially with alcohol."}],"type":"Collateral history + Explain a diagnosis","do":"Explain a formulation of Martin's decline and the risks of alcohol, especially with clozapine, to his sister."},"c-t-55":{"obj":["Made her feel safe and explored her belief about her husband, including asking privately about real violence or abuse","Elicited the depressive syndrome, guilt and worthlessness, and the past episode, citalopram response and absence of mania","Elicited mood-congruent delusions and the bitter-taste hallucination, and screened voices, passivity and nihilism","Assessed suicide risk, self-neglect and any risk to her husband","Summarised her mental state and explained severe depression with psychotic symptoms and the need for admission"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"He's going to kill me when he gets home. I know he is.\"","r":"\"That sounds terrifying, and you're safe here. I want to understand. Can you tell me what makes you so sure?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I deserve it. I'm useless. I've ruined everything for him.\"","r":"\"I can hear how heavy that feeling is. When did you start feeling you were useless? Feelings like that are often part of a depression, not the truth about you.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"His food tastes bitter. He's putting something in it.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. How long has food tasted like that? Is it all food, or only what he makes? Have you been able to eat at all?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Can anything even help me? The tablets haven't done anything.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry they haven't helped yet. Depression this severe usually needs a stronger combination of treatment, and it responds well. I'm hopeful for you.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"Can I just go home? Nobody can help me.\"","r":"\"I understand you want to be somewhere familiar. Right now you're not eating and you're frightened at home. I'd really like you to stay in hospital for a while so we can get you better safely.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You think I'm making it up about Liam.\"","r":"\"No. I take it seriously, and I need to ask: has Liam ever hurt you, threatened you, or controlled you? I ask everyone, and your answer stays with me.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Admission today, informal if she agrees; otherwise MHA assessment","Switch citalopram to sertraline 50 mg in the morning, rising to 100 mg at 1 to 2 weeks; add olanzapine 5 mg at night, rising to 10 mg","Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFTs, TFTs, glucose, lipids, calcium, pregnancy test; ECG before the antipsychotic; weight and BMI","Food and fluid chart; if not eating or drinking for longer, medical review and consider ECT as a priority"],"psych":["Daily one-to-one time with her named nurse on the ward","Psychoeducation for her and, with consent, her husband about psychotic depression","High-intensity CBT for depression, 16 to 20 sessions, once psychotic features settle"],"social":["Ask privately about domestic abuse; if disclosed, safeguarding procedure and IDVA referral","Fit note and, with consent, contact her employer","Home treatment team for early supported discharge, then CMHT care coordinator"],"safety":["Enhanced observations on admission; remove means of harm; review observation level daily","Consultant informed today; husband told only what she agrees to"]},"facts":[{"h":"Mood-congruent means depression came first","x":"Delusions of guilt, worthlessness, punishment, ruin or disease, arising after the low mood, point to psychotic depression rather than schizophrenia."},{"h":"Ask about taste and smell","x":"Gustatory and olfactory hallucinations are easy to miss and often linked to poisoning beliefs. They also uncover not eating."},{"h":"Antidepressant plus antipsychotic","x":"NICE NG222: for psychotic depression, consider an antipsychotic added to an antidepressant. ECT if life-threatening or no response."},{"h":"Section 5(2) is for inpatients only","x":"In the emergency department the doctor's holding power does not apply. Use the MCA if she lacks capacity, and request an urgent MHA assessment."},{"h":"No plan is not low risk","x":"Psychotic depression carries high suicide risk. A belief that she deserves to die plus not eating is serious, even without a plan."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Mental state examination","do":"Elicit severe depression with mood-congruent psychotic features, assess risk, and explain the diagnosis and admission."},"c-t-56":{"obj":["Found the student's goals, what she already knows and what her consultant expects","Explained how common depression is after a heart attack, the two-way link and why it worsens adherence, rehab and survival","Set out what to assess next week, including suicide risk and deliberate missed heart tablets","Explained the plan: sertraline and its interactions, drugs to avoid, CBT with rehab, smoking cessation and the wider team","Checked her understanding with a summary she could present, and agreed supervision and confidentiality"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why would a psychiatrist care whether he goes to cardiac rehab?\"","r":"\"Good question, and lots of people wonder that. Depression is a big reason people drop out of rehab and stop their tablets. Treat the mood and the heart care usually follows.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I'm nervous about asking him if he wants to die. What if I make it worse?\"","r":"\"That worry is really common, and it's kind of you. Asking doesn't put the idea in his head; most people feel relieved. I'll be nearby and you can come and get me.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Which came first: his depression or the heart attack?\"","r":"\"Both, really. Depression is a risk factor for heart disease in its own right, partly through smoking and inactivity. And a heart attack is a big loss that can trigger depression.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Isn't it safer to avoid antidepressants after a heart attack?\"","r":"\"It's sensible to be careful. Sertraline has been studied in people just after a heart attack and looks safe. The ones to avoid are the older tricyclics, which affect heart rhythm and blood pressure.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What actually happens in CBT? I'll get asked.\"","r":"\"Fair, your consultant may ask. It's a talking therapy, usually 8 to 16 weekly sessions, looking at how thoughts, feelings and actions link. For him, it can build activity back up alongside rehab.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"My dad had a heart attack last year and went quite low too.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that must make this feel close to home. If anything next week feels hard, tell me. How is he doing now?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Restart sertraline 50 mg each morning; review at 2 to 4 weeks; increase to 100 mg if partial response; continue at least 6 months after remission","Before and after starting: sodium at baseline and 2 to 4 weeks (hyponatraemia), ECG, U&E, LFTs; warn about bruising, nausea and early agitation","SSRIs plus aspirin and clopidogrel or ticagrelor raise bleeding risk: add a PPI, choosing lansoprazole rather than omeprazole with clopidogrel","Avoid tricyclics (arrhythmia, postural drop); use citalopram and escitalopram cautiously because of QTc","Smoking: varenicline 0.5 mg once daily days 1 to 3, twice daily days 4 to 7, then 1 mg twice daily for 12 weeks, or combination NRT"],"psych":["NHS Talking Therapies long-term conditions pathway: CBT for depression, 8 to 16 sessions, linked to rehab goals such as activity and tablet diaries","Behavioural activation: graded daily walking set with the rehab team","Motivational interviewing about tablets and smoking: his own reasons to recover"],"social":["Cardiac rehab nurse: fresh start date, or a home-based programme if groups feel too much","Dietitian for heart-healthy eating; occupational therapist and occupational health for a phased return to work","Community pharmacist: medicines review and a dosette box; involve his family with his consent","Local stop smoking service: weekly behavioural support plus medication"],"safety":["Ask directly about suicide and deliberate missed heart tablets; crisis line NHS 111 option 2; GP told the same day if risk rises"]},"facts":[{"h":"About one in five","x":"Roughly 20% of people meet criteria for depression after a heart attack, and many more have milder symptoms. Screen everyone, not just those who look low."},{"h":"Sertraline has the best safety evidence","x":"Sertraline was tested in people soon after a heart attack or unstable angina and did not harm cardiac measures. Start 50 mg daily; few drug interactions."},{"h":"SSRI plus antiplatelet means bleeding","x":"SSRIs reduce platelet serotonin. With aspirin, clopidogrel or ticagrelor, add gastroprotection. Avoid omeprazole and esomeprazole with clopidogrel; lansoprazole is a usual choice."},{"h":"Missed tablets can be a risk sign","x":"In a depressed heart patient, stopping cardiac medicines may be passive suicidal behaviour. Ask: 'Have you stopped them on purpose, or not cared what happens?'"},{"h":"Depression drives drop-out","x":"Depressed patients are less likely to attend cardiac rehab, take medicines, stop smoking or exercise. Treating mood is part of secondary prevention."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Management plan","do":"Teach the student why post-MI depression matters, what to assess and the plan, then check she can present it."},"c-t-57":{"obj":["Adapted your communication: quiet room, short, literal questions asked singly, with time to reply","Established the incident, the timeline, changes in his routine and when and why he stopped risperidone","Elicited and characterised delusions, conviction, voices and thought broadcasting, and screened other first-rank symptoms","Separated lifelong autistic traits from new psychotic symptoms and screened mood and substances","Assessed insight and risk to others, self, absconding and non-adherence, and explained the next steps"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I need to go. My mission is coming.\"","r":"\"I can see it is important to you. I need to ask a few more questions first. It will take about twenty minutes. Then I will tell you what happens next.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why are you asking about trains? Trains are not the problem.\"","r":"\"You are right. I asked because you have always liked trains. I want to know what is the same as usual and what is new.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"The agents talk to me. They say, 'Wait, Jordan, wait.'\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. Do you hear them with your ears, like my voice? Where do they come from?\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I am not ill. The agents told me to stop the tablets.\"","r":"\"I hear that you don't feel ill. Your mum is worried because you changed after the tablets stopped. I think the voices are part of an illness that tablets help.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"The day centre shut. Now Tuesdays are wrong. Everything is wrong.\"","r":"\"That sounds very hard. Losing the day centre changed all your days. We will help you get a new timetable.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"That man was following me. I had to shout.\"","r":"\"It sounds like you felt in danger. If you think someone is following you again, what will you do?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Restart risperidone 2 mg at night, increasing over 1 to 2 weeks towards his previous effective dose (for example 4 mg)","Baseline: FBC, U&E, LFTs, glucose or HbA1c, lipids, prolactin; ECG; weight, BMI and BP; repeat at 12 weeks","If adherence remains a problem, discuss a long-acting injection such as paliperidone palmitate with Jordan and family","If very distressed: low-stimulus room first; oral lorazepam 1 mg as needed (max 4 mg in 24 hours)"],"psych":["Autism-adapted psychoeducation with written and visual explanations of his relapse signs","CBT for psychosis adapted for autism, at least 16 sessions, once settled","Relapse prevention plan made with Jordan and his mother using his own early signs"],"social":["Care (Education) and Treatment Review if admitted, to check reasonable adjustments","Hospital passport, quiet side room, a predictable daily timetable and consistent staff","Family meeting and carer's assessment by the local authority; care coordinator to find a replacement day service","If he refuses admission and risk is high, MHA assessment with an AMHP"],"safety":["Risk to strangers he believes are following him: ED staff aware, police information shared through proper channels","Absconding risk high: nurse with him in ED until the plan is settled"]},"facts":[{"h":"Ask 'Is this new?'","x":"Lifelong special interests and literal thinking are baseline. New beliefs held with full conviction, voices and decline in functioning point to psychosis. Check with family."},{"h":"Routine loss is a real trigger","x":"For an autistic person, a closed day centre or new support worker can destabilise everything, including medication routines. Treat it as a cause and fix it in the plan."},{"h":"Flat affect is not always negative symptoms","x":"Reduced eye contact, monotone speech and brief answers may be autism. Compare with his usual presentation before calling it a negative symptom."},{"h":"Section 136 in A&E","x":"Police can use section 136 outside a private home. A&E can be the place of safety. Up to 24 hours for assessment by a doctor and an AMHP."},{"h":"Reasonable adjustments are a duty","x":"Quiet room, short literal questions, extra time, one speaker. Under the Equality Act 2010, failing to adjust can be discrimination."}],"type":"Mental state examination + Risk assessment","do":"Using adapted communication, elicit psychotic symptoms, separate them from autistic traits, and assess insight and risk."},"c-t-58":{"obj":["Opened warmly and explored his specific fears about the wedding","Established fear of judgement across social and performance situations, with body symptoms and avoidance","Identified safety behaviours, pre-event drinking and the impact on work and relationships","Excluded panic disorder, agoraphobia, depression and avoidant personality, and checked for suicidal thoughts","Explored causes: temperament, family history, early experiences and what keeps it going, and explained the diagnosis"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I know it's stupid. It's meant to be the happiest day of my life.\"","r":"\"It's not stupid at all. Lots of people feel this, and big events bring it to a head. Let's understand it properly so the day can feel more like yours.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Everyone will see me go red and think I'm pathetic.\"","r":"\"That fear of being judged sounds exhausting. Can I ask what you picture them thinking, exactly? It helps us see what we're working with.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I have a couple of pints before anything social. Otherwise I can't walk in.\"","r":"\"Thanks for being honest; many people do that. It helps in the moment but keeps the fear going. How much, and how often?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can't I just have a tablet for the day?\"","r":"\"I understand wanting a quick fix with the date so close. Tablets like propranolol aren't recommended for this, and mixing sedatives with wedding drink is risky. Six weeks of CBT can make a real difference.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"What if I ruin it for Chloe?\"","r":"\"You clearly care about her a lot. Would it help to bring her in? Together we can plan a day you can manage, with breaks and a short speech.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is this just who I am? Was I born like this?\"","r":"\"Partly temperament, which runs in families, and partly what happened, like the teasing. But it's kept going by habits we can change, and that's the good news.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No medication first-line: NICE recommends individual CBT for social anxiety ahead of drugs","If he declines CBT or wants added help: escitalopram 10 mg in the morning (up to 20 mg) or sertraline 50 mg (up to 200 mg); review at 2 to 4 weeks; benefit over 6 to 12 weeks; warn about early jitteriness","Do not routinely offer beta-blockers or benzodiazepines; explain why if he asks","Alcohol: advise no drinking before events; AUDIT-C screen recorded"],"psych":["Individual CBT for social anxiety (based on the Clark and Wells or Heimberg model), up to 14 sessions over about 4 months, through NHS Talking Therapies","Drop safety behaviours, shift attention outward, video feedback, behavioural experiments","Wedding goals: short speech rehearsed with feedback; planned breaks; practice at smaller gatherings first"],"social":["Fiancée involved with consent to plan the reception layout and quiet breaks","Occupational health to plan a graded return to leading group activities","Self-help resources: NHS website and Anxiety UK"],"safety":["If mood drops or hopelessness appears: GP, NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans 116 123; review in 4 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"The core is fear of judgement","x":"Social anxiety is fear of being watched and negatively judged. If the fear is of having a panic attack with no escape, think agoraphobia; if attacks come out of the blue, panic disorder."},{"h":"Safety behaviours keep it going","x":"Rehearsing, avoiding eye contact, holding a drink and leaving early stop the person learning that the feared outcome would not happen. CBT drops them in planned steps."},{"h":"No routine beta-blockers","x":"NICE CG159 advises against routinely offering beta-blockers or benzodiazepines for social anxiety. First line is individual CBT; then an SSRI (escitalopram or sertraline)."},{"h":"Mini-SPIN screening","x":"Three questions on fear of embarrassment, avoidance and fear of being the centre of attention. A score of 6 or more suggests social anxiety disorder."},{"h":"Check for avoidant personality","x":"Pervasive feelings of inadequacy and avoidance of close relationships across all areas suggest avoidant personality disorder. Aaron has a fiancée and close friends: points against."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Elicit social anxiety with symptoms, avoidance and impact, exclude differentials, and identify causes and maintaining factors."},"c-t-59":{"obj":["Explained the purpose without pressure and named the specific decision","Took a brief history of the cut, its trigger and intent, his diagnosis, past self-harm and sentence","Tested understanding, retention, weighing and communication, and screened for depression, psychosis and intoxication","Assessed ongoing risk of further self-harm and suicide","Explained the outcome, gave advice once, offered alternatives and planned the return to prison"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Are you here to make me have the operation?\"","r":"\"No. I'm here to understand your decision and make sure it's yours to make. Can we talk it through?\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Every time I ask about the transfer, it's 'soon'. I'm sick of it.\"","r":"\"That sounds really frustrating, waiting weeks with no date. I'll ask the in-reach team to chase it. Can you tell me what happened just before you cut?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not staying here. It's noisy all night.\"","r":"\"Fair enough, wards are noisy. I'm not going to force you. Can you tell me what you think could happen to the cut if it isn't closed in theatre?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will this stop me getting on the DBT wing?\"","r":"\"I can't promise, but self-harm is often why people need DBT. I'll tell the in-reach team this is a priority for you.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"A scar's nothing. I've got loads.\"","r":"\"I hear you. You've lived with scars before. Can I check you know it might not close at all, or could get infected?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"If they mess me about again, I might do it again.\"","r":"\"Thank you for being honest. That helps us plan. What could help when that frustration builds, before you reach for something sharp?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Surgeons to offer closure under local anaesthetic as a day case, or wound dressings with a follow-up review","Check tetanus status; simple analgesia; antibiotics only if infection develops; prison nurse to check the wound daily for a week","No new psychotropic medication; review current prescriptions with the prison GP"],"psych":["In-reach team to request a transfer date to the DBT wing: weekly individual therapy, weekly skills group and phone coaching for about a year","Bridge with distress tolerance skills taught by the in-reach nurse now","Update his crisis plan with the trigger: uncertainty about the transfer"],"social":["Prison mental health in-reach team informed today","ACCT reviewed by the prison following self-harm","Escorting officers given a written handover of wound care and warning signs","Metalwork class: off-cut control reviewed by the prison"],"safety":["Return to hospital if the wound bleeds, becomes red, hot, swollen or discharges, or if self-harm urges rise; he can change his mind about surgery"]},"facts":[{"h":"Capacity is decision-specific","x":"Assess this decision, at this time: closure under general anaesthetic now. Someone can have capacity for one decision and not another."},{"h":"Unwise is not incapacity","x":"MCA principle 4: a person is not treated as unable to decide just because the decision seems unwise. Valuing therapy over a scar is a legitimate weighing."},{"h":"Functional test first","x":"Since the 2021 Supreme Court ruling, ask first whether he can understand, retain, weigh and communicate; then whether any inability is because of an impairment of mind or brain."},{"h":"MHA does not treat the wound","x":"Section 63 covers treatment for mental disorder. Repairing a self-inflicted cut in someone with capacity cannot be forced under the MHA."},{"h":"Retention can be brief","x":"He only needs to retain information long enough to make the decision. Ask him to repeat it a few minutes later; formal memory testing is unnecessary."}],"type":"Capacity assessment + Risk assessment","do":"Assess capacity to refuse surgery using the four tests, screen mood and risk, and explain the outcome."},"c-t-62":{"obj":["Established the time course of relapse, its triggers and missed doses","Elicited the exact content of the voices, any commands and beliefs about the colleague","Assessed thoughts, intent, plans, means and past violence, plus risk to self and protective factors","Agreed a management plan: medication, crisis team input, sick leave and when admission would be needed","Agreed a written safety plan with warning signs, contacts and limits of confidentiality"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I don't want to hurt anyone. I'm scared of what's in my head.\"","r":"\"I can hear how frightening this is. Being scared of the thoughts, and telling me, is a good sign. Let's work out together how to keep you and Gary safe.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Are you going to tell the police?\"","r":"\"Not from what you've told me so far. If I thought Gary was in serious danger I'd have to act to protect him, and I'd try to tell you first.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I can't take time off. I'm the supervisor.\"","r":"\"I know work matters a lot to you. Right now the voices are about someone you see every day. A short break protects both of you and your job. We'll plan the return.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"He really did put something in my tea. I'm not making it up.\"","r":"\"I believe this feels completely real. Some of this may be your illness flaring up. Can we treat the voices and see how sure you feel in a couple of weeks?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going into hospital.\"","r":"\"I don't think you need to today. The crisis team will come to your house daily. If the voices push harder, or you feel you might act, hospital is the safe place.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I skipped the tablets some nights. They knock me out for early shifts.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that's really common. Being off work helps for now, and we'll find a dose and timing that suits your shifts afterwards.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Restart quetiapine MR 500 mg nightly at about 8 pm; consultant to consider 600 mg, then up to 800 mg (BNF max) if needed","Short-term lorazepam 0.5-1 mg up to twice daily for agitation or sleep, no longer than 2 weeks","FBC, U&E, LFT, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin, urine drug screen; ECG before any dose increase","If adherence stays poor, discuss a long-acting injectable"],"psych":["Care coordinator: relapse signature and staying-well plan built from this episode","CBT for psychosis, at least 16 sessions, psychology team, once settled","Family intervention with his partner, 10 or more sessions, if he agrees"],"social":["Crisis resolution and home treatment team daily visits from today","Fit note for 2 weeks; occupational health and a phased return on a different shift later","Partner informed with consent; offer a carer's assessment"],"safety":["Written plan: no work, no contact with Gary, partner holds tablets and prompts doses","Warning signs: voices louder, urge to act, sleep under 4 hours; then call the crisis line","Crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, 999 or A&E if he feels he may act","Consultant review within the week; disclosure to employer or police if risk to Gary becomes serious"]},"facts":[{"h":"Command plus a named target","x":"Voices commanding harm, a persecutory belief about a specific person and ongoing contact with them together raise violence risk. Remove the contact first."},{"h":"Quetiapine MR is once daily","x":"Give quetiapine MR in the evening; usual range in schizophrenia 400-800 mg daily. Morning drowsiness is a common reason for skipped doses, so ask."},{"h":"Disclosure to protect others","x":"GMC: disclose without consent if it is needed to prevent serious harm to others. Share only what is needed, with the right person, and record why."},{"h":"Thought is not intent","x":"Record thoughts, intent, plan and preparation separately. 'I've imagined it' differs from 'I've gone looking for him'."},{"h":"Fit notes cover mental illness","x":"Any doctor can issue a fit note. 'Unwell, not fit for work' is enough; the diagnosis need not go to the employer."}],"type":"Risk assessment + Safety plan","do":"Assess command voices and risk to a named colleague, then agree treatment and a written safety plan."},"c-t-63":{"obj":["Established the circumstances of Frank's sudden death and her early reactions","Characterised the perceptions of Frank, including insight, timing and emotional tone","Screened for a depressive episode, psychosis and alcohol use","Asked about wishes to join Frank, suicidal thoughts and plans, and protective factors","Explained normal grief, why sudden death intensifies it, and agreed support and follow-up"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"So am I going mad?\"","r":"\"No, Bernie, you're not. What you describe is something many people experience after losing someone close, especially suddenly. Let me explain why it happens.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"He said it was indigestion. I should have made him go to the doctor.\"","r":"\"Lots of people blame themselves after a sudden death; it's part of how grief works. Chest pain is often mistaken for indigestion, even by doctors. You couldn't have known.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Some days I just want to be with him.\"","r":"\"That's a very natural feeling when you miss someone so much. I do need to ask: have you thought about taking your own life, or planned anything?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Should I be taking something for it?\"","r":"\"For grief like this, tablets don't help and can get in the way. Talking support is what helps most. If your mood becomes low all the time, we'd look again.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I like it when I see him. Is that wrong?\"","r":"\"Not at all. Many people find it comforting. It's one way the mind holds on to someone while it adjusts. It usually fades gradually over time.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Everyone says it gets easier. It doesn't.\"","r":"\"Three months is still very early, and hearing that can feel dismissive. Grief comes in waves. The waves don't disappear, but they usually get less frequent.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No antidepressant or hypnotic for normal grief; reassess if a depressive episode develops","Sleep: regular rise time, limit daytime naps, keep alcohol low","GP to review her physical health; offer an appointment if appetite or weight change"],"psych":["Bereavement support through Cruse: helpline or local volunteer sessions","If intense grief persists beyond 6 months with impairment, refer for grief-focused CBT"],"social":["Cruse helpline 0808 808 1677; local bereavement groups","Citizens Advice or GOV.UK for Bereavement Support Payment and probate","Keep work, friends and family routines; involve her children if she wishes"],"safety":["If thoughts of dying increase: GP, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123, or 999","Clinic review in 6 weeks; GP letter today"]},"facts":[{"h":"Grief perceptions are common","x":"Many widowed people sense, see or hear their partner in the first months. Brief, with insight, often comforting: not psychosis and needs no medication."},{"h":"Hypnopompic is normal","x":"Seeing someone at the bedside on waking that fades as you wake fully is a hypnopompic experience. It occurs in healthy people, more often when grieving or sleep-deprived."},{"h":"Prolonged grief needs 6 months","x":"ICD-11 prolonged grief disorder: intense longing or preoccupation with the dead person, with impairment, lasting at least 6 months. Three months is ordinary grief."},{"h":"Grief or depression?","x":"Grief comes in waves linked to reminders, and self-esteem stays intact. Depression is pervasive low mood, loss of pleasure and worthlessness unrelated to the loss."},{"h":"Sudden deaths hit harder","x":"No warning and no goodbye increase guilt, disbelief and the intensity of grief. Name this; it helps people make sense of their reaction."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Explain a diagnosis","do":"Characterise her perceptions, exclude depression and psychosis, assess risk, and explain normal grief in plain words."},"c-t-64":{"obj":["Explored her daily routine, motivation, enjoyment and social contact","Screened for depression, clozapine sedation, residual psychosis and physical causes","Explained negative symptoms in plain words as part of the illness, with hope","Discussed specific psychological and social options and how each would help her","Agreed her goals and a first concrete step, with follow-up and staff involvement"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm fine. I just like my room.\"","r":"\"That's fair; your room is your space. I'm wondering whether there's anything you'd like more of in your week. What did you used to enjoy?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Everyone thinks I'm lazy.\"","r":"\"I don't. Finding it hard to get going is part of the illness itself, like the beliefs were. It's not a choice, and there are ways to make it easier.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I'll just make a fool of myself in a group.\"","r":"\"That worry makes sense after a hard few years. We can start small, maybe one session with someone you know. CBT can also help with those thoughts.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What's the point? Nothing works out for me.\"","r":"\"It sounds like things have been really tough. Small steps often work better than big plans. Could we pick one thing for this week and see how it goes?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How is drawing meant to help my illness?\"","r":"\"Arts therapy gives you a way to express things without lots of talking, and it builds motivation and confidence. It's recommended for exactly these symptoms.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I'm always tired in the afternoon.\"","r":"\"Thank you, that's really useful. Clozapine often causes that. I'll check your level and ask my consultant about the dose, which might give you more energy.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Clozapine level (12 h post-dose), FBC per monitoring schedule, TFTs, ferritin, weight and bowel check","Consultant to review clozapine dose if level is high and afternoon sedation persists","Continue sertraline 200 mg; no increase without a depressive episode"],"psych":["Arts therapy group, weekly, for at least 12 weeks, via the arts therapies service","CBT for psychosis, about 16 sessions, psychology team: defeatist beliefs and activity scheduling","Family intervention with her sister, if both agree"],"social":["OT: weekly timetable with graded steps and a residential staff key worker","Recovery college art course; walking or exercise group","IPS supported employment when she feels ready; benefits check"],"safety":["Staff to tell the team if mood drops, old beliefs return or she stops eating","Review in 4 weeks with her care coordinator"]},"facts":[{"h":"Arts therapies target negative symptoms","x":"NICE advises considering arts therapies for people with psychosis, particularly for negative symptoms. Usually groups, delivered by an HCPC-registered arts therapist."},{"h":"Five A's of negative symptoms","x":"Avolition, anhedonia, asociality, alogia and affective blunting. Anticipatory anhedonia (no looking forward) is more typical than losing pleasure in the moment."},{"h":"Exclude secondary causes first","x":"Depression, sedation, residual psychosis, side effects, hypothyroidism, anaemia and an empty environment all mimic negative symptoms and are easier to fix."},{"h":"Clozapine level guides sedation","x":"Take the trough level about 12 hours after the night dose. Response is usually seen above 0.35 mg/L; high levels add sedation and seizure risk."},{"h":"Social skills training: not routine","x":"NICE does not recommend routine social skills training. Use OT-led, goal-based activity and supported employment instead."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Elicit negative symptoms, exclude other causes, and agree specific psychological and social options linked to her goals."},"c-t-65":{"obj":["Reviewed his current depressive symptoms and assessed suicide risk","Explained the diagnosis of bipolar disorder and how the mania and depression connect","Explained why sertraline and risperidone are not lifting his mood","Discussed medication options with dosing, monitoring and weight effects, plus psychological and lifestyle options","Agreed his preferred option, a relapse plan and next steps"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So what can I take that won't make me balloon?\"","r":"\"That's a fair worry, and we'll take it seriously. Lamotrigine doesn't usually cause weight gain and works well for bipolar lows. Let me explain how it's started.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will this keep happening for the rest of my life?\"","r":"\"Bipolar disorder tends to come back without treatment, but many people stay well for years with the right medicine and by knowing their early signs. You have real control here.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I'm so embarrassed about what I did when I was high.\"","r":"\"That's really common, and it shows how unwell you were, not who you are. Would it help to plan together what you say to people at work?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why didn't the sertraline work?\"","r":"\"Antidepressants on their own work less well in bipolar depression, and can make mood less stable. It wasn't the wrong idea, but it's not the best fit for bipolar disorder.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Some days I think everyone'd be better off without me.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. That sounds really painful. Have you thought of ending your life, or made any plans?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not having blood tests every week for lithium.\"","r":"\"That's understandable. The frequent tests are mostly at the start, then every three months. It's your choice; lamotrigine needs no regular levels. Let's weigh it up.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Taper sertraline over 1-2 weeks (e.g. 100 mg for a week, 50 mg for a week, then stop)","Lamotrigine: 25 mg daily for 2 weeks, 50 mg for 2 weeks, then 100 mg; usual 200 mg; stop and seek help for any rash","Lithium for prevention: target 0.6-0.8 mmol/L 12 h post-dose; baseline U&E, eGFR, TFT, calcium, weight, ECG if cardiac risk","Continue risperidone 4 mg nocte for now; weight, HbA1c and lipids at 3 months"],"psych":["CBT or IPT for bipolar depression, about 16 sessions, psychology team","Bipolar psychoeducation group; mood diary and early warning sign plan with his girlfriend"],"social":["Care coordinator on discharge; girlfriend and family involved with consent","DVLA notification; phased return to work with occupational health","Regular sleep, no drugs, limit alcohol; Bipolar UK peer support"],"safety":["Crisis plan for leave: NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123, 999","Medicines in weekly supply while low; review mood and risk before each leave"]},"facts":[{"h":"One manic episode means bipolar I","x":"A single manic episode is enough for bipolar I disorder. Most people with it will also have depressive episodes; say this clearly."},{"h":"Lamotrigine: slow start, watch for rash","x":"25 mg daily for 2 weeks, 50 mg for 2 weeks, then 100 mg. Rash risk, including Stevens-Johnson syndrome, rises with fast titration. Restart titration if over 5 days missed."},{"h":"Lithium levels 12 hours after dose","x":"Aim 0.6-0.8 mmol/L. Check weekly after starting until stable, then 3-monthly in year one; renal, thyroid and calcium 6-monthly. Avoid NSAIDs and dehydration."},{"h":"NICE first choices for bipolar depression","x":"Quetiapine, olanzapine, fluoxetine with olanzapine, or lamotrigine. Weigh each against the person's priorities, such as weight gain."},{"h":"Antidepressants alone risk switching","x":"In bipolar disorder an antidepressant without an antimanic drug can trigger mania or rapid cycling. Stop if mania emerges."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Explain a treatment","do":"Explain bipolar depression and why current treatment failed, and agree options respecting his weight concerns."},"c-t-66":{"obj":["Gathered Chloe's symptoms, the weight jump and its cause, purging and water loading, observations and missing bloods","Named refeeding syndrome and explained its mechanism and dangers in plain words","Checked physical risk against MEED flags and screened self-harm","Agreed same-day assessment with named tests and treatments, keeping feeding going","Agreed prevention, who does what, and the same-day escalation rule, confirmed by teach-back"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Have I missed something? I should have rung someone two days ago.\"","r":"\"You've spotted something important, and you've raised it. Next time abnormal obs go to the duty doctor the same day. Right now, let's make sure Chloe gets what she needs today.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Isn't putting weight on what we want?\"","r":"\"It is, but steadily: between half a kilo and one kilo weekly. Three kilos in a week after starvation is mostly fluid, and that tells me her body is struggling.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Her BP was better today though. Can't it wait for her review on Friday?\"","r":"\"I can see why that's reassuring. But blood salts can be dangerously low while she feels a bit better. Only bloods and an ECG will tell us, so it needs to be today.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"She was trying so hard. She'll be devastated if we tell her to slow down.\"","r":"\"She has worked hard, and that's worth telling her. We'll frame it as making recovery safe, not as her doing anything wrong.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Should she stop the meal plan until the results come back?\"","r":"\"Good question, and no. Stopping food makes things worse. We keep her eating at a steady, set amount and the dietitian adjusts it once her salts are corrected.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"What if she won't go to A&E? She hates hospitals.\"","r":"\"That's understandable. I'll talk to her myself, explain the danger, and check she understands it. If she still says no, I'll involve the consultant today.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Same-day: lying and standing BP, pulse, temperature, ECG (rhythm, QTc); U&E, phosphate, magnesium, calcium, glucose, LFT, FBC","Oral thiamine 100 mg three times daily plus vitamin B compound strong 1-2 tablets three times daily and a multivitamin; IV Pabrinex if admitted","Phosphate, potassium and magnesium replaced orally or IV per local protocol; admission if levels are low, ECG abnormal or she is breathless","Bloods daily until stable, then twice weekly for two weeks; daily weight and fluid balance while being corrected","Dietitian keeps intake at a steady, set amount (not stopped), then builds it up; target gain 0.5-1 kg a week"],"psych":["Explain to Chloe that the swelling is fluid, not fat, to stop her cutting back","Continue eating-disorder-focused therapy (MANTRA or CBT-ED) with her therapist; extra meal support while the plan is adjusted"],"social":["Care coordinator links with the acute medical team and liaison psychiatry if admitted","Consultant and GP told today; shared physical monitoring plan written into her care plan","Flatmate or family, with Chloe's consent, support meals and notice deterioration"],"safety":["999 or A&E for chest pain, collapse, fits, confusion or marked breathlessness","Team rule: abnormal observations escalated to the duty doctor the same day; delay logged as a learning incident, no blame"]},"facts":[{"h":"Fast gain is fluid","x":"After starvation, true tissue gain runs at about 0.5-1 kg a week. A jump of 3 kg in a week is mostly retained salt and water."},{"h":"Phosphate is the key blood","x":"A falling phosphate is the hallmark of refeeding syndrome. Check it with potassium, magnesium and glucose before and daily during early refeeding."},{"h":"Thiamine before more food","x":"Feeding uses up thiamine. Give 200-300 mg daily orally, with vitamin B compound strong, to prevent Wernicke's encephalopathy and heart problems."},{"h":"Never stop feeding","x":"MEED warns that underfeeding is also dangerous. Correct the salts and keep a steady intake rather than stopping food."},{"h":"MEED adult flags","x":"BMI below 13 is red, 13-14.9 amber. Pulse below 40 is red, 40-50 amber. Abnormal ECG or salts need same-day medical review."}],"type":"Emergency management + Handover and escalation","do":"Identify refeeding syndrome from the nurse's account, then agree same-day tests, treatment, prevention and escalation."},"c-t-67":{"obj":["Set the agenda without retaking history and drew out his goals, worries and what he had already tried","Completed pre-medication checks: cardiac history, family sudden death, BP, pulse, weight, tics, fits, substances and other medicines","Explained lisdexamfetamine: how it works, start dose, weekly titration and maximum","Covered side effects, monitoring schedule, controlled-drug rules, driving and second-line options","Agreed non-drug and university support and confirmed the plan by teach-back"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will it make me a different person? I don't want to be a zombie.\"","r":"\"That's a really common worry. At the right dose most people feel like themselves, just able to stay on task. Feeling flat or wired means the dose is wrong, and we change it.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Isn't it basically speed? People at uni take it to cram.\"","r":"\"Fair question. It's related to amphetamine, which is why the rules are strict. In ADHD, at a steady prescribed dose, it corrects a shortfall rather than giving a high. Please never share it.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How quickly will I know if it's working?\"","r":"\"Usually within the first days. We go up a step each week, so within about a month we should know the right dose.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can I just take it on study days and skip weekends?\"","r":"\"Some people do that once they're settled, and it can be reasonable. While we find your dose, take it every morning so we can judge it properly.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I've already tried everything at uni. Why do I need therapy too?\"","r":"\"You've clearly put a lot in already. The tablet helps you focus; it doesn't plan your week. A few sessions on planning and procrastination make the medicine go much further.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Honestly, a mate gave me one of his tablets before an essay once.\"","r":"\"Thanks for telling me; it helps. Did you notice any effects, good or bad? From now on, only take what's prescribed for you, and keep yours locked away.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Baseline: BP 118/74, pulse, weight, height; ECG only if cardiac history, murmur, raised BP or family sudden death","Lisdexamfetamine 30 mg each morning, increased by 20 mg weekly to effect, maximum 70 mg; take before 10 a.m. to protect sleep","BP, pulse and weight at each dose change, then every 6 months; specialist review at least yearly","If ineffective after 6 weeks at optimal dose: methylphenidate MR (e.g. 18 mg mane, up to 108 mg daily); atomoxetine 40 mg rising to 80 mg if stimulants unsuitable","Warn: lower appetite, poor sleep, headache, dry mouth, anxiety, raised BP and pulse; stop and seek help for chest pain or palpitations"],"psych":["Psychoeducation with the ADHD specialist nurse, 2-3 sessions","CBT for adult ADHD, about 12 weekly sessions, on planning, procrastination and self-esteem","Study-skills or ADHD coaching through the university"],"social":["University disability service: Disabled Students' Allowance, extra time, quiet room, deadline flexibility","Personal tutor told, with his consent, so earlier failed modules can be reviewed","GP takes over prescribing under shared care once the dose is stable; letter for travel with controlled medicine"],"safety":["Store tablets securely; never share or sell; no early or lost-script replacements","Tell the DVLA if ADHD or its treatment affects driving; review in 2 weeks by phone and 4 weeks in clinic"]},"facts":[{"h":"Two first-line choices","x":"NICE NG87: offer lisdexamfetamine or methylphenidate first for adults. Switch to the other after an adequate 6-week trial; atomoxetine if neither suits."},{"h":"Lisdexamfetamine numbers","x":"Start 30 mg each morning, increase by 20 mg weekly, maximum 70 mg daily. It is a prodrug, giving smooth all-day cover and less misuse potential."},{"h":"ECG only with a reason","x":"Before stimulants, ask about heart disease, exertional symptoms and family sudden death, and check BP and pulse. ECG only if any of these raise concern."},{"h":"Controlled-drug rules","x":"Schedule 2: script valid 28 days, normally 30 days' supply at most, no early repeats. Lost or shared tablets are not replaced."},{"h":"Adjustments come first","x":"In adults, medication is offered when ADHD still causes significant impairment after environmental and study adjustments. Ask what he has tried and say so."}],"type":"Explain a treatment + Management plan","do":"Agree lisdexamfetamine with pre-treatment checks, titration, monitoring and controlled-drug rules, plus non-drug support, without retaking history."},"c-t-71":{"obj":["Established last night's overdose: trigger, amount, alcohol, intent and how she got help","Elicited the lasting pattern with examples: cutting for relief, fast mood shifts, anger, emptiness, unstable relationships, fear of being left, shifting self-image and impulsivity","Excluded a depressive episode, hypomania, psychosis, PTSD and an eating disorder, and placed stress-linked detachment and suspiciousness correctly","Asked gently about childhood adversity and assessed current intent, tablets at home, supports and violence in her relationship","Explained the working diagnosis in plain, hopeful words and agreed a crisis plan, CMHT referral and GP letter"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Are you here to tell me off as well?\"","r":"\"Not at all. I'm glad you rang for help last night. I want to understand what's been going on, because the help so far clearly hasn't fitted.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Everyone here's sick of me.\"","r":"\"That sounds a lonely way to feel in a hospital. I'm not sick of you. Coming back this often tells me things are hard, and that's what I want to hear about.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Why do I have to tell my whole life story again?\"","r":"\"That's fair, and I'm sorry you've had to repeat it. I'll keep it focused. What you tell me goes in one letter, so the next team doesn't start from scratch.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want to talk about my dad.\"","r":"\"That's absolutely fine. Thank you for telling me that much. We can leave it there, and it's there if you ever want to come back to it.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"So is that it, I'm just broken?\"","r":"\"You're not broken. This is a recognised pattern, it usually improves with the right help, and most people get much better over time.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can't you just give me something to calm me down?\"","r":"\"I understand wanting something quick. There isn't a tablet that treats this pattern. What helps most is therapy that teaches skills for strong feelings, and I'll refer you today.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Sometimes it's like I'm watching myself from outside.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; lots of people feel that under stress. Does it pass once things calm down? That helps me tell it apart from other problems.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No medication for the personality pattern itself (NICE CG78); avoid starting an antidepressant or sedative today","GP to prescribe in weekly supplies only; alcohol brief advice today and referral to the local alcohol service if she wants it"],"psych":["CMHT personality disorder pathway: assessment for DBT (weekly individual therapy, skills group and phone coaching for about a year) or MBT","While waiting: structured clinical management with a named care coordinator, plus written information on EUPD"],"social":["GP letter today with diagnosis, risk summary and crisis plan; shared A&E care plan so staff respond consistently","Offer domestic abuse support (IDVA or local service) if the relationship becomes unsafe"],"safety":["Written crisis plan: warning signs (rows, alcohol), what helps, Lily and Mia, Samaritans 116 123, NHS 111 option 2, 999","Mia to hold the spare paracetamol; CMHT duty worker to phone within 72 hours of discharge"]},"facts":[{"h":"Duration separates EUPD from bipolar","x":"Shifts lasting minutes to hours, set off by people, point to EUPD. Hypomania lasts at least four days, with reduced need for sleep and no tiredness."},{"h":"Frequent attendance raises long-term risk","x":"Repeated self-harm is one of the strongest predictors of later suicide. Every attendance earns a full psychosocial assessment, not a shorter one."},{"h":"No tablet for the disorder itself","x":"NICE advises against drug treatment for borderline personality disorder or its individual features. Treat a coexisting depressive episode or psychosis on its own merits."},{"h":"Paracetamol pack limits","x":"UK shops sell up to 16 tablets per pack, pharmacies up to 32. Moving stockpiles out of the home is a simple, effective safety step."},{"h":"Most people improve","x":"Many people with this diagnosis no longer meet the criteria within a few years, especially with structured therapy. Say so: hope is part of the treatment."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"Elicit the emotionally unstable pattern with examples, exclude other disorders, assess current risk, and explain the working diagnosis kindly."},"c-u-01":{"obj":["Elicited the night-time intruder belief with his evidence, who he suspects, and a certainty score out of ten","Asked about thoughts put in through food and about voices, without using the words 'delusion' or 'hallucination'","Screened mood, enjoyment, guilt, sleep and energy, and stated why primary psychosis is likelier than psychotic depression","Recorded cannabis amount, past history and the uncle's illness, and asked about suicide, self-neglect and wanting to retaliate","Told A&E a working diagnosis of first-episode psychosis with named actions: bloods and ECG, senior discussion, EIP referral, friends updated with consent"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is this about my body? Something's wrong with me and nobody will check.\"","r":"\"I can hear something feels very wrong, and I want to understand it. We'll make sure your body is checked tonight too. Tell me what you've noticed.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You think I'm making it up, don't you?\"","r":"\"No. I can see this feels completely real and frightening to you. I'm not here to judge it, just to understand it. What happened the last time?\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Voices? I'm not mad. Why does everyone ask that?\"","r":"\"Fair enough, and I'm sorry if it sounded like that. I ask everyone going through something this stressful, because the mind can play tricks under strain. It's not a judgement.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Maybe my flatmates know about it. Maybe that's why they brought me.\"","r":"\"That must make it hard to trust anyone. They told me they're worried because you've stopped eating. Have they ever done anything to harm you?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not eating that. It puts things in my head.\"","r":"\"I understand you want to protect yourself. Your body still needs fluids, though. Would a sealed bottle of water or a drink you open yourself feel safer?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I called the police but they did nothing. There are too many of them to fight.\"","r":"\"It sounds like you've felt very alone with this. Have you ever thought about getting back at them yourself, or protecting yourself with anything?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Tonight: U&E, FBC, LFTs, TFTs, glucose, CK if dehydrated; urine drug screen; ECG; weight and BMI","Fluids by mouth with a sealed bottle he opens himself; IV fluids only if U&E or obs say so","No antipsychotic started in A&E: the admitting or EIP team chooses one after baseline checks, e.g. aripiprazole 5 mg once daily, rising to 10 mg","Lorazepam 1 mg as needed (max 4 mg in 24 hours) only for severe distress or no sleep"],"psych":["Engage without collusion: acknowledge the fear, do not argue with the belief","EIP team later: CBT for psychosis (at least 16 sessions) and family intervention with the people he chooses","Cannabis: brief advice now; motivational work with EIP"],"social":["EIP referral tonight (email or electronic referral) for assessment within two weeks","Consultant decides informal admission versus home treatment team, given poor intake and suspicion of flatmates","With consent: flatmates told the plan; next of kin contacted","Later: university wellbeing service for interruption or extension of studies"],"safety":["Nurse in charge told he must not leave without review; call liaison if he tries","If he refuses help and risk rises: senior review and Mental Health Act assessment via AMHP","Liaison note filed on the A&E record before you leave the department"]},"facts":[{"h":"Mood screen settles the differential","x":"Psychotic depression brings low mood, guilt and mood-matching beliefs (ruin, sin, decay). Mood 'okay', no guilt and bizarre content point to a primary psychosis."},{"h":"Score the conviction","x":"Ask 'How sure are you, out of ten?' and 'Could there be another explanation?' Full certainty despite weak evidence is the core of a delusion; record the score."},{"h":"Thought insertion needs one more question","x":"'Do those thoughts feel like yours, or put there by something outside you?' Only alien thoughts count as insertion, a first-rank symptom."},{"h":"EIP: two-week standard","x":"NHS England expects a first episode of psychosis to start treatment with an EIP team within two weeks of referral. Refer the same night."},{"h":"Section 5(2) does not work in A&E","x":"Holding powers apply only to admitted inpatients. In A&E use capacity and the MCA for urgent care, or request a Mental Health Act assessment."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"In ten minutes, separate psychosis from depression, assess risk, and give A&E a working diagnosis and next step."},"c-u-02":{"obj":["Established the current yellow-skin fear, at least one earlier fear, and her certainty out of ten","Recorded numbers for checks per day, hours online and GP or A&E visits in the past year","Linked onset to her mother's death and screened mood, a fixed belief and suicidal thoughts","Explained health anxiety through her own worry-check-relief cycle and confirmed it by teach-back","Agreed a named plan: Talking Therapies CBT referral, one named GP with planned visits, no new tests without new signs, review date"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I don't know why I've been sent to a psychiatrist. I'm not mad.\"","r":"\"You're not, and I'm glad you came. Dr Ellison sent you because the worry itself is making you ill, and that's something we know how to treat.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"But what if they've missed something? It happens.\"","r":"\"That fear makes sense after what happened to your mum. You've had more checks than most people ever have, all normal. The worry is now the thing hurting you most.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can't you just do one more scan, to put my mind at rest?\"","r":"\"I understand why you'd want that. But look at the pattern: each scan settles you for a few hours, then the worry is back. Another one would keep the cycle going.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"My husband says I'm a hypochondriac. He's sick of me.\"","r":"\"That must hurt, when you're the one suffering. It isn't attention-seeking; it's an anxiety problem. With your permission, I'd like to help him support you differently.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What does the therapy actually involve?\"","r":"\"Weekly sessions, around 12 to 16, with a therapist. You'll test what happens when you check less and search less, and learn how normal body feelings get misread.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Could I keep checking just once a day while I wait?\"","r":"\"That's a good first step. Write down each urge to check and what happens to the worry if you wait. Bring that to your first session.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No new investigations unless there are new objective findings; record the reason in the letter","Medicine is not first-line. If CBT is declined or not enough, or depression or OCD coexist: sertraline 50 mg once daily, review at 2-4 weeks, up to 200 mg","If sertraline is started: warn of nausea and jitteriness in weeks 1-2; review suicidal thoughts at 1-2 weeks in the first month"],"psych":["CBT for health anxiety: 12-16 weekly sessions, NHS Talking Therapies high-intensity therapist (self-referral today)","Homework now: urge diary; checking cut to an agreed once a day; no searching after 8 pm","Guided self-help book or online CBT while waiting"],"social":["GP letter: one named GP, planned appointments every 2-4 weeks instead of urgent ones","Husband (with consent): stop giving repeated reassurance, kindly redirect her to her plan","Copy of the clinic letter to her; Anxiety UK details for peer support"],"safety":["If mood drops or hopelessness appears: GP the same week, or NHS 111 option 2","New objective signs (visible jaundice confirmed by others, weight loss, bleeding) still go to the GP as normal"]},"facts":[{"h":"Reassurance feeds the fear","x":"Normal results bring short relief, so the brain learns to check again. Each test or reassurance strengthens the cycle. Planned, limited contact breaks it."},{"h":"Fixed belief means something else","x":"If she cannot accept any chance it is worry, consider delusional disorder (somatic type) or psychotic depression. A doubting belief points to health anxiety."},{"h":"CBT is the treatment","x":"CBT for health anxiety, usually 12-16 sessions, is effective. Target checking, searching, reassurance seeking and avoidance; challenge catastrophic readings of body sensations."},{"h":"One named GP helps","x":"Regular, planned appointments with one GP, regardless of symptoms, cut urgent visits and duplicate tests."},{"h":"Health anxiety is common","x":"It is common in general practice, often starts after a loved one's serious illness, and can last years if untreated."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Explain a diagnosis","do":"In ten minutes, take a focused history, explain health anxiety in her own terms, and agree CBT and testing limits."},"c-u-03":{"obj":["Got him seated within two minutes by agreeing to help with the records and using short questions","Described appearance, behaviour, speech and affect with at least one concrete example each","Elicited the grandiose belief, the reason for the records, and asked about persecution and voices","Asked about sleep, spending, driving, substances, sexual behaviour and harm to others or himself","Presented a structured mental state to the registrar naming probable mania, bloods and drug screen, no driving, and the plan if he leaves"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I don't need a psychiatrist. I need my records. Now.\"","r":"\"I hear you, and I'll explain how to get them. Give me about ten minutes so I understand what's going on, then we'll sort the next step.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Stop interrupting me! You're not listening!\"","r":"\"Sorry, you're right, I'll let you finish. Then I need to ask a couple of quick things.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why are you asking about sleep? I don't need sleep.\"","r":"\"Lots of people with big plans tell me that. Going three nights without sleep can wear the body down even when you feel great, so I need to know.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"Blood tests? Absolutely not. I've got investors waiting.\"","r":"\"I understand you're busy. The tests are quick and check nothing physical is speeding you up. It protects you and your plans.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Right, so can I go now? I've got investors waiting.\"","r":"\"I can see how much energy you have for this, and I don't want you to lose what you've built. I'm worried about you, and I'd like my senior to see you first.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"I'll just drive home and sleep. Happy?\"","r":"\"Sleep is a good idea. Driving isn't safe right now, with no sleep for three nights. Could Sofie collect you, or the keys stay with her?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Now: FBC, U&E, LFTs, TFTs, calcium, glucose, urine drug screen; CT head only if neurological signs or head injury","If an antidepressant is found on his record, stop it","Ward team: olanzapine 10 mg at night (range 5-20 mg) after baseline weight, BP, glucose, lipids and ECG","Lorazepam 1 mg as needed (max 4 mg in 24 hours) for agitation or no sleep","Later, once settled: discuss long-term lithium with level, renal and thyroid monitoring"],"psych":["Short, calm sentences; don't argue with the app or the 'chosen' belief","After recovery: psychoeducation and relapse-signature work for bipolar disorder, with his partner if he agrees"],"social":["Consultant decides admission versus home treatment; MHA assessment via AMHP if he refuses and risk is high","With consent: partner Sofie to hold car keys and bank cards; bank can add spending limits","Explain the subject access request: hospital records team, free, within one month","Fit note from the treating team; GP letter"],"safety":["Nurse in charge and security told he may try to leave; call liaison at once if he does","Advise no driving; record the advice in the notes","Risks: spending, driving, disinhibition, conflict if thwarted"]},"facts":[{"h":"Section 5(2) is not for A&E","x":"Holding powers only apply to admitted inpatients. In A&E, use the MCA if he lacks capacity and is at serious risk, and request a Mental Health Act assessment."},{"h":"First mania: rule out organic causes","x":"Check for stimulants, steroids, thyroid disease, head injury and neurological signs. Send TFTs, calcium, glucose and a urine drug screen."},{"h":"Mania: ask about money, driving, sex","x":"Spending, reckless driving and sexual disinhibition are the commonest serious harms. Ask each one directly and record the answers."},{"h":"Records: subject access request","x":"Patients can request their records free of charge; the organisation normally replies within one month. Explain the route instead of promising them now."},{"h":"Short questions settle pressured speech","x":"Long questions lose a manic patient. Use one short question at a time, listen briefly, then steer firmly but kindly."}],"type":"Mental state examination + Risk assessment","do":"In ten minutes, settle him, examine his mental state, assess risks, and present probable mania with a lawful plan."},"c-u-04":{"obj":["Allowed silences of at least ten seconds, used single short questions and obtained a mood rating","Recorded dry lips, soiled clothes, retardation and when he last ate or drank","Elicited nihilistic, guilt and poverty beliefs and the voice, stating its person and content","Asked directly about suicide and about refusing food and drink, and recorded his exact words","Presented psychotic depression with high physical risk and three same-day actions: bloods with fluid chart, held ramipril, ECT discussion"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Oh dear... oh dear.\"","r":"\"I can see you're in a lot of distress, Stuart. I'm not going anywhere. Take your time.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I've ruined them. My family. There's no money left because of me.\"","r":"\"That sounds like a terrible weight to carry. Your wife has told the team the money is all right. Depression can make things feel far worse than they are.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"No point eating. My insides have rotted away.\"","r":"\"That must be frightening to believe. Your body still needs water to keep going, and I'd like to check it with some blood tests. Will you have a few sips?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why bother? Nothing can be done for someone like me.\"","r":"\"I know it feels that way, Stuart, but this kind of depression does get better, and you've recovered before. We'll help you through it.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"There's a voice. It says I'm worthless.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that must be horrible to hear. Is it talking to you, or about you? Does it tell you to do anything?\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I can't kill myself. I'm already dead.\"","r":"\"Thank you for being honest. It sounds like you see no reason to keep going. Have you been not eating or drinking on purpose, to let yourself die?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Today: U&E, creatinine, CK, FBC, LFTs, glucose, TFTs, calcium; ECG; NEWS2 4-hourly; fluid balance and food charts; daily weight; VTE risk assessment","Hold ramipril while intake is poor and until U&E are back; discuss with the on-call medical team if creatinine has risen","Urgent ECT referral given past response and life-threatening intake; pre-ECT anaesthetic review, ECG and bloods","Restart sertraline 150 mg once he will take it; add an antipsychotic such as olanzapine 5 mg at night, rising to 10 mg","Later: consider lithium augmentation, target level 0.6-0.8 mmol/L with renal and thyroid monitoring"],"psych":["Named nurse: short, frequent supportive contact; prompt fluids at every contact","Once improving: behavioural activation or CBT; relapse prevention with his wife"],"social":["With consent: contact his wife about his usual self and the family finances","Ward social worker: flat cleaning and benefits check before discharge","HTT and care coordinator to plan follow-up within 72 hours of discharge"],"safety":["Enhanced observations agreed with the nurse in charge (at least intermittent, every 15 minutes); remove ligature risks","Consultant review today: capacity for treatment and food, and whether to request a Mental Health Act assessment","Call the on-call medical team if urine output falls, NEWS2 rises or he stops drinking entirely"]},"facts":[{"h":"Fluid refusal kills before depression","x":"Days of little fluid cause kidney injury, rhabdomyolysis and VTE. Check U&E and CK, chart fluids and NEWS2 from day one."},{"h":"ACE inhibitors and dehydration","x":"Ramipril with poor fluid intake raises the risk of acute kidney injury. Hold it until U&E are back and agree a plan with the medical team."},{"h":"ECT works fastest here","x":"NICE supports ECT for severe, life-threatening depression and when a rapid response is needed. Previous good response makes it more likely to work again."},{"h":"Ask about the delusions by name","x":"Nihilism (body rotting, already dead), guilt (sin, punishment), poverty (ruin) and illness (cancer) are the depressive delusions. Ask about each."},{"h":"'Already dead' is not a denial","x":"It means hopelessness and no reason to live. Record it verbatim and treat suicide and self-starvation risk as high."}],"type":"Mental state examination + Risk assessment","do":"In ten minutes, examine his mental state at his pace, gauge physical and suicide risk, and present three same-day actions."},"c-u-05":{"obj":["Described appearance and behaviour, naming at least one observed sign of responding to voices","Recorded the voices' number, person, content and any commands, and asked about thought withdrawal, insertion, broadcast and passivity","Explored the poisoning belief with evidence, a certainty score and what he has done about it","Recorded the switch date, doses missed, restlessness and cannabis use","Asked about harm to the manager and himself, and presented relapse with a medication plan and the hostel informed"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"They made me come. They're worried about nothing.\"","r":"\"Thanks for staying to talk anyway. Help me understand this week from your side; then I'll tell you what I think.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"The voices are personal. I don't want to talk about them.\"","r":"\"That's fair, and you don't have to tell me everything. It helps me know how much they're bothering you. Are they speaking to you, or about you?\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"He's putting stuff in my food. If he carries on, he'll be sorry.\"","r":"\"It sounds like you've felt really unsafe, and angry. What do you mean by 'he'll be sorry'? Have you thought about doing something to him?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it the new tablet's fault? What are you going to do?\"","r":"\"It may well be linked to the change, and the restlessness too. I'll speak to my consultant tonight about going back to what worked for you.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"They take my thoughts out. Everyone can tell what I'm thinking.\"","r":"\"That sounds very unsettling. Thank you for telling me. Does anything ever move your body, or make you do things against your will?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You're going to tell the hostel, aren't you? Then they'll kick me out.\"","r":"\"I understand that worry. I'll tell them only what keeps everyone safe, and that you're getting help. The aim is to keep your place, not lose it.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Tonight: urine drug screen, FBC, U&E, LFTs, glucose, prolactin; ECG; weight","Consultant decision: restart risperidone 2 mg, rising to his previous effective dose (often 4 mg) over a few days, and stop aripiprazole","If he prefers to stay on aripiprazole: raise to 15 mg with supervised doses, and treat akathisia","Akathisia: rate it (Barnes scale); propranolol 10 mg three times a day if no asthma or heart block","Lorazepam 1 mg as needed (max 4 mg in 24 hours) for acute distress"],"psych":["Explain the likely link between the switch, restlessness, missed doses and relapse","Update his relapse prevention plan with the care coordinator; CBT for psychosis once settled"],"social":["Home treatment team tonight if risk allows; admission if risk to the manager rises or he won't take medicine","Hostel key worker to supervise doses, with his consent","Email the CMHT care coordinator for review within two working days"],"safety":["Tell the hostel of the risk to the manager tonight; consider moving the manager off his corridor; record the disclosure and reasons","If he refuses help and risk is high: Mental Health Act assessment through the AMHP","Crisis line: NHS 111 option 2; hostel staff to call 999 if threats escalate"]},"facts":[{"h":"Aripiprazole switches can destabilise","x":"Aripiprazole is a partial agonist with a long half-life. Straight swaps from risperidone, with missed doses, risk relapse and akathisia. Cross-tapering is safer."},{"h":"Akathisia drives non-adherence","x":"Inner restlessness and inability to sit still are common with aripiprazole. Ask directly, rate it, and treat with dose reduction or propranolol."},{"h":"Third-person voices are first-rank","x":"Voices discussing him or commenting on his actions, and thought withdrawal, are Schneiderian first-rank symptoms. Record the person and whether there are commands."},{"h":"Named target, named action","x":"A persecutory belief about a named person plus anger raises risk of violence. Ask what he might do, and share the minimum with those at risk."},{"h":"What worked before guides now","x":"If he was stable on risperidone, returning to it is often the quickest route back to stability. Check why he was switched first."}],"type":"Mental state examination + Risk assessment","do":"In ten minutes, examine his mental state, check the switch, assess risk to the manager and present a plan."},"c-u-06":{"obj":["Asked what he knows about lithium and recorded his top priority and at least two worries","Explained why lithium suits him and stated the baseline tests, 400 mg at night, a 12-hour level at one week and the 0.6-0.8 target","Named at least four toxicity signs, three interacting drug groups and the dehydration rule, with the action to take","Stated the long-term monitoring schedule, the risk of stopping suddenly and the olanzapine taper plan","Checked understanding by teach-back and agreed baseline bloods today with a decision at a review in two weeks"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I read lithium's basically a poison. Isn't that why you need all the blood tests?\"","r":"\"I can see why that's frightening. It is strong, and too much does harm. That's exactly why we check levels: they keep you in the safe range, and you'll know the warning signs.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will I lose the weight if I switch?\"","r":"\"I hope you'll lose some once olanzapine is tapered, but I won't promise. Lithium can add a little weight too, so we'll weigh you and get dietitian help early.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Can't I just stop the olanzapine now and start lithium tomorrow?\"","r":"\"I understand wanting it gone. You were manic six weeks ago, so stopping now risks a relapse. We'll get lithium to the right level first, then reduce olanzapine slowly over a few weeks.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"My partner and I want kids. Does lithium affect that?\"","r":"\"Good that you asked. Lithium taken by a man isn't known to harm a baby he fathers. The cautions in pregnancy apply to women taking it.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"Blood tests every week? I've got a job, I can't keep taking time off.\"","r":"\"That's fair, work matters. Weekly tests are only for the first few weeks. After that it's every three months, and your GP surgery can usually do them early morning.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What if I just want to come off it one day?\"","r":"\"That's always your choice. The key is never to stop suddenly, because that can trigger mania. We'd reduce it over at least four weeks with you.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Baseline: U&E, eGFR, TFTs, calcium, FBC, weight and BMI; ECG if heart disease or cardiac risk factors","Priadel (lithium carbonate, prescribe by brand) 400 mg at night; 12-hour level 7 days after the first dose and 7 days after any dose change; target 0.6-0.8 mmol/L","Levels weekly until stable, then every 3 months for the first year, then every 6 months; U&E, eGFR, TFTs, calcium and weight every 6 months","Once two levels are therapeutic, taper olanzapine over about 4 weeks; never stop lithium abruptly (reduce over at least 4 weeks)","Avoid NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, ARBs and diuretics unless a prescriber has checked; paracetamol for pain"],"psych":["Bipolar psychoeducation group: relapse signature, sleep protection, stress","Written relapse plan with early warning signs, shared with his partner"],"social":["Lithium pack: booklet, record book and alert card; record each level in the book","Shared-care agreement with the GP before monitoring transfers; care coordinator tracks bloods","Early-morning blood slots so he loses little work time"],"safety":["Toxicity signs (vomiting, diarrhoea, coarse tremor, unsteadiness, slurred speech, confusion): stop lithium, same-day level, A&E if unwell","Drink more in heat, exercise or illness; if vomiting or diarrhoea for more than a day, seek advice about pausing lithium"]},"facts":[{"h":"Level timing is 12 hours","x":"Take the level 12 hours after the last dose. Bedtime dosing means a morning blood test. A level taken too soon after a dose reads falsely high."},{"h":"Ibuprofen raises lithium","x":"NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, ARBs and thiazides reduce lithium clearance and can push levels into toxicity. Tell him to use paracetamol and to mention lithium at every pharmacy."},{"h":"Fine tremor fine, coarse tremor danger","x":"A fine hand tremor is common and often settles. A coarse tremor with vomiting, diarrhoea, unsteadiness or confusion suggests toxicity: stop and get an urgent level."},{"h":"Stopping suddenly invites mania","x":"Abrupt withdrawal raises the risk of manic relapse. If stopping, reduce gradually over at least four weeks, and watch for relapse signs for months afterwards."},{"h":"Kidneys and thyroid are long-term watch","x":"Lithium can lower thyroid function, raise calcium and reduce kidney function over years. Six-monthly U&E, eGFR, TFTs and calcium catch these early."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"In ten minutes explain lithium's purpose, checks, dosing, toxicity and monitoring, then agree a dated next step."},"c-u-07":{"obj":["Recorded what he believes is wrong, when it began, hours preoccupied per day and his conviction out of 100","Identified at least three checking or hiding behaviours, past surgical consultations and the impact on work and social life","Screened for depression, OCD, eating disorder, psychotic symptoms and substance use","Asked directly about suicidal thoughts, self-surgery or DIY procedures and anger towards surgeons, and stated a risk level","Named likely body dysmorphic disorder, agreed a follow-up date and gave a named crisis route"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Just look at it. You can see it's bent, can't you?\"","r":"\"I can see how much it distresses you. I see your face differently, but I'm not here to argue. I want to understand what this worry is doing to your days.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not mental. I just need a surgeon who'll do it properly.\"","r":"\"I hear that, and you're not 'mental'. Three surgeons have said no, and you're still suffering. Can we look at what's going on, so you've more options than surgery?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Sometimes I think if it can't be fixed, what's the point of anything.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that takes courage. Can I ask more? Have you thought of ending your life, or made any plans?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Everyone says I look fine. So why can't I see it?\"","r":"\"That's a really good question. In this condition, the brain zooms in on one feature and magnifies it. It's real distress, but the picture you see isn't what others see.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"These surgeons just take your money and then fob you off.\"","r":"\"That sounds really frustrating, and expensive. Has it ever made you so angry you've thought of getting back at any of them?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If you write to Mr Hale, he'll never operate on me.\"","r":"\"I understand that worries you. I'd only write with your agreement. Surgery tends not to fix this worry, and I'd rather we treat the part that's making life so hard.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["CBT first; if moderate to severe or CBT not enough, fluoxetine 20 mg each morning, increasing over weeks towards 40-60 mg if tolerated","Allow up to 12 weeks at a good dose; under 25: review within 1 week of starting an SSRI for agitation or suicidal thinking","Avoid benzodiazepines; antipsychotic augmentation only on specialist advice"],"psych":["CBT with exposure and response prevention for BDD, around 16 weekly sessions, delivered by the CMHT psychologist","Targets: phone-camera checking, covering his nose, taping, comparing online, reassurance from his sister","Specialist BDD service if two adequate treatments fail"],"social":["With consent: letter to Mr Hale and the GP advising against cosmetic procedures now","Phased return to customer-facing shifts as therapy progresses","BDD Foundation information and peer support"],"safety":["Crisis plan: Samaritans 116 123, NHS 111 option 2, 999 if he might act on suicidal thoughts","No DIY or online cosmetic products; seek help if urges grow","Repeat suicide enquiry at each contact; consultant review within 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Measure conviction as a number","x":"Ask 'how sure, out of 100?' and 'could you be wrong?' This sets the insight specifier and guides treatment. Recheck it later: it often drops as therapy works."},{"h":"Poor insight is still BDD","x":"ICD-11 classes absent insight as BDD 'with poor to absent insight', not delusional disorder. Treat with CBT and an SSRI, not an antipsychotic alone."},{"h":"Surgery rarely cures BDD","x":"Most people with BDD stay preoccupied after cosmetic procedures, and some shift to a new feature or become more distressed and angry. Advise against procedures while untreated."},{"h":"Ask about DIY by name","x":"Ask about home filler pens, online kits, overseas clinics and self-surgery. These are specific BDD risks a general risk screen misses."},{"h":"Suicide risk is high","x":"Suicidal thoughts are common in BDD, often driven by hopelessness about the defect. Ask directly, document it, and give a named crisis route."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"In ten minutes take a focused appearance history with conviction and risk, and name the likely diagnosis."},"c-u-08":{"obj":["Identified the side effect precisely, separating desire, arousal and orgasm, and placed it on a timeline against depression and sertraline","Checked current mood, past episodes and suicidal thoughts, and screened at least four other causes","Offered at least three options with one benefit and one drawback each, and explained why not to stop suddenly","Agreed a named plan with a review date, the wording of the GP letter and a crisis route, confirmed by teach-back"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"This is so embarrassing. I couldn't even say it to Dr Kaur.\"","r":"\"That's completely understandable; it's a hard thing to bring up. I talk about this every week, so nothing will shock me. Is it to do with sex?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I just can't... finish. It takes forever, or it doesn't happen.\"","r":"\"Thanks for trusting me with that. It's a very common effect of sertraline, and it's treatable. Can I ask a few more specific questions so we get it right?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"My boyfriend thinks I've gone off him. Maybe I have.\"","r":"\"That sounds painful for you both. From what you've said, your feelings for him haven't changed; the tablet is affecting your body's response. Would it help if he understood that?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So do I have to choose between being depressed and having a sex life?\"","r":"\"No, you don't. We can lower the dose, switch to a different medicine, or adjust things over time. Let me go through them with you.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm better now. I'd rather just stop it this weekend.\"","r":"\"I understand wanting it gone. Stopping suddenly can cause dizziness and electric-shock feelings, and after one month the depression could return. Can we change it in a way that keeps you well?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will it go back to normal if I come off it?\"","r":"\"For most people it does, usually within weeks of lowering or stopping. Very rarely it lingers, which is another reason we'll review you closely.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Reduce sertraline 100 mg to 50 mg once daily; review mood (PHQ-9) and sexual function at 4 weeks; return to 100 mg if mood slips","If the side effect persists: switch to mirtazapine 15 mg at night (up to 45 mg); warn of weight gain and drowsiness","Do not stop abruptly; continue an antidepressant for at least 6 months after remission, longer as this is a recurrent episode","Pregnancy test and contraception check before any switch if relevant"],"psych":["Continue NHS Talking Therapies CBT to protect against relapse","Psychoeducation for her, and her boyfriend if she wishes, that the problem is the medicine, not the relationship","Psychosexual or couples counselling via the GP if difficulties remain after the medication change"],"social":["GP letter the same day, wording agreed with Shona","Boyfriend may join a future appointment if she chooses"],"safety":["If mood drops or suicidal thoughts appear: GP same day, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123, or 999","Written plan: what to do if she misses doses or feels discontinuation symptoms"]},"facts":[{"h":"Ask about the three phases","x":"Desire, arousal and orgasm fail in different ways. SSRIs mostly delay or block orgasm and blunt arousal; depression mostly lowers desire. Separate them to find the cause."},{"h":"Solo versus partner tells you something","x":"If the same problem occurs alone and with a partner, a drug or physical cause is more likely than relationship difficulty."},{"h":"Lower dose before switching","x":"Sexual side effects are partly dose-related. If mood has responded, a lower dose may keep the benefit and ease the side effect. Review at about 4 weeks."},{"h":"Mirtazapine has fewer sexual effects","x":"Mirtazapine causes much less sexual dysfunction than SSRIs, but weight gain and sedation are common. Start 15 mg at night."},{"h":"Stopping early risks relapse","x":"NICE advises continuing for at least 6 months after remission. Stopping an SSRI abruptly can cause dizziness, electric-shock sensations, irritability and poor sleep; taper over weeks."}],"type":"Medication review","do":"In ten minutes identify the side effect, check mood and other causes, and agree a safe, dated plan."},"c-u-09":{"obj":["Elicited at least three inattentive and two hyperactive or impulsive symptoms in adult life, each with an example","Established symptoms before age 12 in at least two settings and named a collateral source","Screened depression, mania, anxiety, autism traits, sleep, thyroid and substance use, and stated why each is less likely","Asked about suicidal thoughts, driving, impulsive spending, family history and cardiac history","Stated adult ADHD as the initial impression and named three next steps: screener, specialist assessment with collateral, occupational health letter"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I've worked so hard to get here. I can't lose it now.\"","r":"\"That sounds frightening, especially after all you've achieved. Let's work out what's really going on, because that's the first step to keeping the job.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Everyone has ADHD these days. Isn't it just a fashionable excuse?\"","r":"\"I understand the scepticism. It's a real, lifelong condition, and the assessment is careful precisely so it isn't over-diagnosed. Your story has some strong pointers.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"If it is ADHD, why didn't anyone notice when I was a kid?\"","r":"\"That's very common, especially in girls who are bright. Your reports said 'daydreams' rather than 'disruptive', and good grades hide a lot.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can you just give me the tablets today?\"","r":"\"I can see why you want help fast. First the diagnosis must be confirmed, and before any medicine we check your heart, blood pressure and weight. Meanwhile, work adjustments can start now.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will my boss have to know?\"","r":"\"Only what you agree. I can write to occupational health, and we'll decide together what that letter says.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"Maybe I'm just lazy, like my teachers said.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry you heard that so often. Lazy people don't win features awards. What you describe sounds like a brain that struggles with structure, not a lack of effort.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Confirm diagnosis first: specialist adult ADHD assessment with a structured interview, school reports and a parent's account","TFTs via the GP to exclude thyroid disease","If confirmed, before any medicine: BP, pulse, weight, personal and family cardiac history; ECG if cardiac concerns"],"psych":["Psychoeducation about ADHD for her and, if she wishes, her partner","Practical systems now: one calendar, phone reminders, written task lists, short timed work blocks"],"social":["Occupational health letter, content agreed with her: written instructions, protected focus time, quieter workspace","Equality Act: adjustments need not wait for the diagnosis; Access to Work for coaching or software","Inform the DVLA if her ADHD affects her ability to drive safely"],"safety":["If mood drops under the work pressure: GP, NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans 116 123"]},"facts":[{"h":"Before 12, in two settings","x":"ADHD needs several symptoms present before age 12, in two or more settings, with clear impairment. Ask for school reports and a parent who knew her then."},{"h":"Structure hides, change reveals","x":"Flexible, deadline-driven work can mask ADHD for years. Promotion, parenthood or office working often unmask it. Ask how she coped before."},{"h":"ADHD is constant, bipolar episodic","x":"Distractibility and restlessness in ADHD are lifelong and steady. In bipolar disorder they come in discrete episodes with elated mood and less need for sleep."},{"h":"Screener is not a diagnosis","x":"The 6-item adult self-report screener takes two minutes and supports a referral. Diagnosis needs a full specialist interview and collateral."},{"h":"Ask about driving","x":"Adults with ADHD have more speeding offences and road accidents. Ask directly, and advise telling the DVLA if ADHD affects safe driving."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"In ten minutes take a focused ADHD history with childhood onset, mimics and risk, and explain next steps."},"c-u-10":{"obj":["Recorded the trigger, planning, tablets and alcohol taken, precautions against discovery, final acts and her stated intent","Recorded how she was found, her feelings about surviving, current suicidal thoughts and hopelessness","Asked about depressive and psychotic symptoms, past self-harm, alcohol, home safety and at least two protective factors","Stated a needs-based formulation to her and the A&E doctor without using a risk score","Agreed a written safety plan with two named contacts, removal of tablets and a crisis number, plus follow-up and a GP letter"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I feel so stupid. Everyone's wasting their time on me.\"","r":"\"You're not wasting anyone's time. Something made things feel unbearable last night, and that matters. Can you tell me what happened?\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"It was a silly mistake. I'm fine now, honestly.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you feel safer now. I'd still like to understand how you got to that point, so we can make sure it doesn't build up again.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So what happens now? Can I go home once the drip's done?\"","r":"\"Good question. Once the medical team are happy with your bloods, yes, with a plan we make together and someone checking in with you in the next few days.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you going to section me?\"","r":"\"I can hear that's a worry. No: you've talked openly, and we're making a plan together. Sectioning is for people who can't stay safe any other way.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"Please don't tell my sister how bad it's been.\"","r":"\"I'll respect that; it's your decision. She's clearly someone who helps you. Could we agree one or two things she should know, so she can support you?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Ryan shouts a lot when we argue. But he's never hit me.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. Shouting can still be frightening. Do you feel safe at home? Has he ever stopped you seeing people or controlled your money?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Complete acetylcysteine; repeat LFTs, INR, creatinine and paracetamol level before medical clearance (A&E team)","GP to consider sertraline 50 mg daily for depression, reviewed within 1 week (under 30: watch early agitation and suicidal thoughts)","Prescriptions in small quantities; no stockpile of paracetamol at home","Brief alcohol advice now; GP to recheck intake"],"psych":["Written safety plan before she leaves: warning signs, coping steps, people and places, crisis numbers, means removal","NHS Talking Therapies CBT for depression; self-harm-specific therapy if episodes recur"],"social":["With consent, sister helps with safety plan and stays the first nights","Crisis team contact within 72 hours of discharge; GP letter the same day","Domestic abuse screen repeated in private at follow-up; IDVA details offered"],"safety":["In A&E: tell the nurse in charge; if she tries to leave before treatment ends, assess capacity under the MCA","Crisis numbers: Samaritans 116 123, NHS 111 option 2, 999 if she might act","Sister removes spare tablets from the flat before Chloe returns"]},"facts":[{"h":"No risk scores to decide care","x":"NICE NG225: do not use risk tools or a global 'low/medium/high' label to predict suicide or decide who gets care. Assess needs and risks, and formulate."},{"h":"Before, during, after","x":"Before: trigger, planning, final acts, precautions. During: what, how much, alcohol, expectation. After: how found, help-seeking, regret, current thoughts."},{"h":"5(2) does not work in A&E","x":"Section 5(2) applies only to inpatients on a ward. In A&E, if she tries to leave treatment, assess capacity under the MCA and act in her best interests if she lacks it."},{"h":"Medical fitness comes first","x":"Psychiatric discharge waits until the medical team clear her: acetylcysteine finished and LFTs, INR, creatinine and paracetamol level acceptable."},{"h":"Means restriction saves lives","x":"Agree who removes spare tablets from home and limit prescription sizes. Pharmacy pack-size limits on paracetamol were brought in for the same reason."}],"type":"Risk assessment + Safety plan","do":"In ten minutes take the overdose history, assess current risk, and agree a written safety plan and follow-up."},"c-u-12":{"obj":["Asked about at least three aura features (stomach, smell or taste, déjà vu or fear) and awareness during the spell","Obtained the witness account of automatisms and the post-episode confusion, with frequency and triggers","Asked about febrile convulsions, head injury, brain infection and family history of epilepsy","Screened mood, suicidal thoughts and the impact on work, social life and driving","Named likely temporal lobe seizures in plain words, listed EEG, MRI and neurology referral, and gave DVLA and water safety advice"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I looked it up. Is it a brain tumour?\"","r":"\"That's a frightening thing to read, and I'm glad you asked. Nothing you've told me points to a tumour, and the MRI scan will check properly. Most people with these spells have no tumour.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why am I seeing a psychiatrist? Do you think I'm mad?\"","r":"\"I can see why that worried you. No, I don't. We see people whose symptoms sit between the mind and the brain, and yours sound like they come from the brain.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I still drive? I need the car for work.\"","r":"\"I know that's a real problem for you. Until the neurologist has seen you, the law says you mustn't drive, and you need to tell the DVLA. Let's think about lifts or travel support for work.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I've stopped going out. I feel like my life's shrinking.\"","r":"\"That sounds lonely and exhausting. Losing confidence like that is really common with these spells. Once they're treated, most people get their life back. Have things ever felt so bad you've thought about not being here?\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"It's probably just stress, isn't it? I've been tired.\"","r":"\"Tiredness can make them more likely, you're right. But the smell, the lip movements and the confusion afterwards point to something the brain is doing, and that's worth testing.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What if the EEG comes back normal?\"","r":"\"Good question. A normal EEG between spells is common and doesn't rule it out. The neurologist puts together your story, the video and the tests.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Urgent referral to neurology or the epilepsy clinic; suggest EEG (sleep-deprived if routine is normal) and MRI brain with epilepsy protocol","Bloods: FBC, U&E, glucose, calcium, magnesium; 12-lead ECG to exclude a cardiac cause","Antiseizure medicine is started by neurology, e.g. lamotrigine with slow titration from 25 mg daily (warn about rash); avoid starting valproate (MHRA)","If on a combined pill, flag that it lowers lamotrigine levels and that enzyme-inducing drugs weaken the pill"],"psych":["Plain-language explanation now, with an Epilepsy Action leaflet","Mood follow-up in clinic; CBT through Talking Therapies if low mood or anxiety persist after treatment","Epilepsy specialist nurse for seizure diary, triggers and first-aid teaching"],"social":["Stop driving and inform the DVLA; record the advice in the letter","Flatmate to film an episode on her phone and learn seizure first aid (with Funmi's consent)","Fit note or workplace adjustments if needed while she is assessed"],"safety":["Showers not baths, no swimming alone, avoid heights and unguarded heat","Call 999 if a seizure lasts more than 5 minutes, she is injured, or one follows another without recovery"]},"facts":[{"h":"The aura is the seizure starting","x":"A rising stomach feeling, odd smell or taste, déjà vu or sudden fear before a spell is a focal seizure in the temporal lobe. Ask for it specifically."},{"h":"Automatisms and confusion decide it","x":"Lip smacking, chewing or picking, unresponsiveness and post-episode confusion are not features of depersonalisation disorder or panic. Ask the witness."},{"h":"Normal EEG does not exclude epilepsy","x":"A routine EEG between seizures is often normal. Sleep-deprived EEG and a video of an episode add yield; the diagnosis rests on the history."},{"h":"Suspected seizures: no driving","x":"Advise her to stop driving and tell the DVLA, and document it. The neurologist advises when she may return; it is usually after a seizure-free year."},{"h":"Valproate: not a first choice","x":"MHRA 2024: do not start valproate in anyone under 55 unless two specialists independently agree. Women need the pregnancy prevention programme."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Explain a diagnosis","do":"In ten minutes: take a before-during-after seizure history, name temporal lobe epilepsy, give tests and driving advice."},"c-u-15":{"obj":["Established onset, extent and progression and asked at least five red flags (pain on eye movement, flashes, floaters, headache, neurological symptoms)","Asked about recent stresses, mood and suicidal thoughts and recorded protective factors","Checked acuity in each eye, pupils including the swinging light test, and colour vision","Performed fundoscopy on both eyes: red reflex, disc, vessels in four quadrants, macula last","Explained the normal findings without labelling the cause and named same-day ophthalmology and a driving restriction"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Am I going blind?\"","r":"\"That's a terrifying thought, and it makes sense you're scared. The back of your eye looks healthy, which is reassuring. The eye team will see you today to find out more.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"The doctors think I'm making it up, don't they?\"","r":"\"I can see why it feels like that, and I'm sorry. I don't think you're making anything up. Your sight loss is real, and my job is to help find out why.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why is a psychiatrist looking at my eyes?\"","r":"\"Fair question. I've been asked to help, and nobody's checked the back of your eyes yet, so I'm doing that first. I'll also ask how you've been coping.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Since I lost my job I sometimes think I'd rather not wake up.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. That sounds like a heavy week. Have those thoughts ever gone further, to plans or anything you've done?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So is there something wrong with my eye or not?\"","r":"\"The back of your eye and your pupils look healthy, which is good news. The eye team have special equipment to look further, and they'll see you today.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Same-day ophthalmology: slit-lamp, visual fields and OCT; neurology to consider MRI brain and orbits","No dilating drops without the eye team's agreement; no psychotropic medication now","Bloods if not done: FBC, CRP, glucose, B12; pregnancy test if MRI or treatment planned"],"psych":["Liaison review tomorrow: mood, sleep, coping after the job loss, and repeat risk screen","If functional loss is confirmed: joint explanation with neurology and neurosymptoms.org information; therapy for functional symptoms if it persists"],"social":["With consent, include her husband in explanations","Fit note from the medical team; signpost Citizens Advice for benefits after redundancy"],"safety":["Report at once any eye pain, new headache, weakness or change in the right eye","No driving until the eye team say it is safe; tell the DVLA if the loss persists","If thoughts of not waking up turn into plans: tell the nurse; Samaritans 116 123 or NHS 111 option 2 after discharge"]},"facts":[{"h":"No RAPD with a blind eye","x":"Total loss from an optic nerve lesion causes a relative afferent pupillary defect. Normal swinging-light responses with no light perception point strongly to functional loss."},{"h":"A normal disc can still be optic neuritis","x":"In retrobulbar optic neuritis the disc looks normal early. Ask about pain on eye movement and colour washout; RAPD is usually present."},{"h":"Your right eye, her right eye","x":"Use your right eye and right hand for her right eye, and vice versa. Approach from about 15 degrees to the side to land on the disc."},{"h":"Macula last","x":"Asking her to look straight into the light is uncomfortable and constricts the pupil, so check the macula last, after disc and vessels."},{"h":"Indifference proves nothing","x":"Calm detachment about a symptom is seen in organic illness too and is not a reliable sign of functional disorder. Base the diagnosis on positive examination signs."}],"type":"Physical examination + History to a diagnosis","do":"In ten minutes: take a visual-loss history with mood screen, check acuity and pupils, perform fundoscopy, escalate."},"c-u-22":{"obj":["Explained the purpose, gained verbal consent, and checked glasses, hearing, first language and schooling before the first test","Took a brief history covering onset, long-term memory, mood and alcohol","Tested at least one item in each of six domains (orientation, attention, memory, language, visuospatial, executive) and recorded each raw result","Named the impaired domains (place orientation, delayed recall, writing) and the preserved ones","Answered 'how did I do?' honestly without a diagnosis, and named three next steps: bloods, scan and his wife's account"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is this a test? Am I going to fail?\"","r":"\"There's no pass or fail. They're tasks that show which parts of memory and thinking are working well and which aren't, so we know where to look. Some are meant to be hard.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Sorry, my mind's gone blank. This is so embarrassing.\"","r":"\"Please don't apologise; lots of people feel the same. Let's leave that one and try something different.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why are you asking me the date? I'm not stupid.\"","r":"\"You're clearly not, and I'm sorry if it feels basic. I ask everyone the same questions so the answers can be compared fairly. It'll get more interesting, I promise.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So how did I do? Did I do well?\"","r":"\"How do you feel it went? ... You did well on several parts. Remembering the words and where we are were harder, so we'll do some tests to find out why.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is it Alzheimer's? My uncle had that.\"","r":"\"I can see why that's on your mind, especially after your uncle. It's too early to say. Low mood, vitamins or thyroid can all affect memory, so we'll check those first.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want my wife knowing how badly I did.\"","r":"\"That's your choice, and I'll respect it. Her view of day-to-day memory often helps us get the answer right, though. Would you be happy for me to ask her a few questions without sharing your scores?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods (NICE NG97): FBC, ESR or CRP, U&E, calcium, HbA1c, LFT, TFT, B12, folate","MRI brain (CT if MRI unsuitable) for vascular change, atrophy or a lesion","Review medicines for anticholinergic load; record alcohol units","If Alzheimer's is later diagnosed: donepezil 5 mg at night for 4 weeks then 10 mg; check pulse first, warn about nausea, diarrhoea and vivid dreams"],"psych":["Mood screen (GDS-15) today; NHS Talking Therapies if anxiety or low mood is significant","Results appointment in 4-6 weeks, with his wife if he agrees","Memory aids now: phone reminders, a calendar for birthdays, a notebook for names"],"social":["Informant history from his wife today, with his consent (structured questionnaire such as the IQCODE)","Ask whether he drives; if dementia is diagnosed he must inform the DVLA and his insurer","If a diagnosis follows: dementia adviser, Lasting Power of Attorney and a carer's assessment for his wife"]},"facts":[{"h":"One item per domain fits ten minutes","x":"A full ACE-III takes 15-20 minutes. In a short slot, cover orientation, attention, registration, language, visuospatial, fluency and delayed recall with one or two items each."},{"h":"Distract before delayed recall","x":"Put at least two other tasks (language, clock, fluency) between registering the words and asking for them back, or you test attention, not memory."},{"h":"Cues tell you which memory failed","x":"If a category cue brings the word back, storage is intact and retrieval is the problem; if cues don't help, think hippocampal, as in early Alzheimer's."},{"h":"Check what lowers scores","x":"Poor hearing or sight, few years of schooling, testing outside the first language, anxiety and depression all lower scores. Record them beside the result."},{"h":"Mini-ACE when you need a number","x":"The Mini-Addenbrooke's takes about five minutes and is scored out of 30: orientation, a name and address, animal fluency, clock and recall."}],"type":"Cognitive assessment","do":"In ten minutes, test orientation, attention, memory, language, visuospatial and executive function with one item each, then feed back honestly."},"c-u-23":{"obj":["Stated the limits of confidentiality and offered to see her alone within the first minute","Recorded the number, time, source, planning, intent, lethality belief, precautions and what she did afterwards","Elicited at least five depressive symptoms, including hopelessness, and the forearm scratching","Asked about current suicidal thoughts, plans and access to medicines, and named at least two protective factors","Agreed tonight's plan with her: medical treatment, overnight paediatric stay, CAMHS review and what her parents will be told"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I've already told everyone. I'm not going through it again.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry, you've had to tell it three times tonight and that's exhausting. I'll keep it short, and I'll only ask what the others didn't. You can stop me any time.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you going to tell my parents everything?\"","r":"\"Not everything. Most of what you say stays with the team. If I'm worried about your safety I'll share that part, and I'll tell you first. What would you want them to know?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"It's never going to stop. They'll just find another way online.\"","r":"\"That sounds so tiring, feeling there's no way out of it. It makes sense you felt desperate. Can we think about who could actually stop it, starting with school?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Mum hasn't stopped crying. I've ruined everything.\"","r":"\"She's upset because she loves you, and she's here. You haven't ruined anything; you told her when you felt ill, and that took courage.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Do I have to stay here? What happens to me now?\"","r":"\"Good question. Tonight is about keeping your body safe: you'll stay on the children's ward for treatment. Tomorrow the CAMHS team will see you and your parents about what's been happening.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've been scratching my arm with a pen. Only when it's really bad.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that's not easy. Can I have a look so we can clean it? I'd like to understand what those bad moments are like.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Paracetamol level at 4 hours (23:00) with LFT, INR, U&E, glucose; acetylcysteine if on or above the treatment line, per paediatrics","Stay on the paediatric ward until medically fit and CAMHS have assessed her","Clean and dress the forearm scratches; check tetanus status if the skin is broken","No antidepressant tonight; if moderate to severe depression persists after psychological therapy, CAMHS may add fluoxetine 10 mg daily, rising to 20 mg"],"psych":["Full CAMHS psychosocial assessment with her alone and with her parents before discharge (NICE NG225)","CBT for depression with CAMHS, weekly, or family therapy if family factors emerge","Written safety plan before discharge: triggers, coping steps, who to call"],"social":["With her agreement, parents involved in the plan tonight","School designated safeguarding lead to act on the bullying, with her consent","Report abusive posts to the platform; police if there are threats or sexual images","Childline 0800 1111, Shout text 85258, Papyrus HOPELINE247 0800 068 4141"],"safety":["General observations on the paediatric ward; nurses to report any new disclosure","Parents to lock away all medicines and sharp items before she goes home","Safeguarding team informed via the hospital referral form tonight","Call 999 or come to A&E if she takes anything else or feels she cannot stay safe"]},"facts":[{"h":"One treatment line for paracetamol","x":"Since 2012 the UK uses a single line starting at 100 mg/L at 4 hours. On or above it, give acetylcysteine. Levels before 4 hours can't be interpreted."},{"h":"Regret is not low risk","x":"Regret about upsetting family does not cancel high intent. Ask what she wants now, separately from how others feel."},{"h":"No risk scales to decide care","x":"NICE NG225: risk scales and tools should not be used to forecast suicide or to decide who is treated or discharged. Formulate risk from the history."},{"h":"Fluoxetine is the first antidepressant under 18","x":"Only alongside psychological therapy, started by a specialist: 10 mg daily, can rise to 20 mg after a week. Warn about early agitation and new suicidal thoughts."},{"h":"See young people alone first","x":"Offer time alone at every assessment. Self-harm, abuse or online exploitation are often only disclosed without parents present."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"In ten minutes, map the overdose before, during and after, assess mood and current risk, and agree tonight's plan."},"c-u-24":{"obj":["Asked what he already understood before giving information, and used plain words and a picture","Gave four key facts (the break, the operation, its main risks, what happens without it) and checked them by teach-back","Tested retention after a gap of several minutes and recorded a specific answer for each of the four functional parts","Removed the pressure about his mother and recorded why faith alone was not the reason for the finding","Told him the outcome in plain words and named who makes the best-interests decision and whose views will count"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I go home now? My mum needs me.\"","r":"\"I know you want to be with your mum, and that shows how much you care. Let's make sure she's looked after first; I'll ask the social worker to sort help for her today.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want them cutting my leg.\"","r":"\"That's okay to say, and it sounds scary. Can I show you a picture of what they'd do? Then you can tell me what you think.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"God will fix it. It already hurts less.\"","r":"\"Your faith clearly matters a lot, and I respect that. The pain is less partly because of the painkillers. What do you think happens to the bone if it isn't joined?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What's a blood supply?\"","r":"\"Good question. Blood is like food for the bone. The break can cut off the food, and then the top of the bone can die. The operation helps stop that.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will it hurt? Will I wake up?\"","r":"\"Lots of people worry about that. You'll be asleep and won't feel it. Afterwards you'll have medicine for pain, and the nurse who helps people like you will be there.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"So you're saying I'm stupid.\"","r":"\"No, not at all. This is a really hard decision, with a lot to remember. Lots of people need help with decisions this big. Your views still matter, and I'll make sure they're heard.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Outcome recorded on the hospital capacity form: lacks capacity for this decision today","Best-interests decision by the orthopaedic consultant (MCA s4), today, given the risk to the hip","Pain: regular paracetamol 1 g four times daily, fascia iliaca block, oral morphine as needed per surgical chart","Avoid unnecessary sedation; any restraint must be necessary, proportionate and the last resort"],"psych":["Explain the outcome to him with easy-read pictures; repeat it later","Hospital learning disability liaison nurse to prepare him for theatre and stay with him","Hospital chaplain or his own faith leader, so prayer and treatment sit side by side"],"social":["Best-interests meeting today: surgeon, anaesthetist, liaison psychiatry, LD liaison nurse, mother by phone","IMCA only if no appropriate family or friend can be consulted","Ward social worker to arrange urgent care for his housebound mother","Reassess capacity if things change; if he regains it, his refusal stands"],"safety":["If he tries to leave before the decision: talk first; any step to stop him must be proportionate under MCA s5-6, recorded, and escalated to the consultant","LD liaison nurse or a familiar person with him before theatre to reduce distress"]},"facts":[{"h":"Retaining briefly is enough","x":"Under MCA s3(3), holding information for a short time is enough if it lasts long enough to decide. Losing it within minutes, even with pictures, is not."},{"h":"Faith is not incapacity","x":"Refusing on religious grounds can be a capacitous choice. The question is whether he can hold and use the risks, not whether he believes."},{"h":"Functional test first","x":"Since the Supreme Court in JB (2021), test the four functional parts first, then show the impairment is what causes the failure."},{"h":"The surgeon makes the decision","x":"The person proposing treatment is the best-interests decision-maker. They must consult family and consider his wishes, feelings, values and beliefs."},{"h":"When an IMCA is needed","x":"For serious medical treatment, instruct an IMCA if there is no family member or friend appropriate to consult. His mother can be consulted by phone."}],"type":"Capacity assessment","do":"In ten minutes, test understand, retain, weigh and communicate with pictures, link any failure to his disability, and explain plainly."},"c-u-25":{"obj":["Explained confidentiality and its two limits (risk of serious harm, serious crime) before he disclosed","Recorded the age and sex of children he is drawn to, the onset, and a direct answer on whether he has touched a child","Established the babysitting event, the images he holds and his access to children at work","Asked about suicidal thoughts, mood, alcohol and drugs, and named at least two protective factors","Told him that police, children's services and the LADO would be informed tonight or next day, why, and gave Stop It Now and Samaritans numbers"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is this private? I can't have anyone knowing.\"","r":"\"I can see how frightened you are. Most of what you say stays with the team. If a child or anyone might be at risk, I'd have to share that, and I'd tell you first.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I'm disgusting. I hate myself for even thinking it.\"","r":"\"I can hear how much shame you're carrying. Coming here to stop harm was the right thing to do. Let's work out how to keep children, and you, safe.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What happens now? Are you going to call the police?\"","r":"\"I'll be honest with you, because you've been honest with me. Yes: because of the images and your access to children, I have to inform the police and children's services tonight.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can't you just give me tablets and leave the police out of it?\"","r":"\"I understand why you'd want that. Treatment can help with urges, and I'll make sure you get it. But I can't keep this from the people whose job is to protect children.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"There's a memory stick at home. Pictures. I hate that it's there.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; I know that was hard. Please don't destroy it or move it; the police will need it. Can I ask how often you've been looking at them?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"My life's over. I'll lose my job, everything.\"","r":"\"That's a huge fear, and some of it may be real. When people feel this way, they sometimes think of ending their life. Is that happening for you?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No medicine cures this. A specialist may use an SSRI such as fluoxetine 20 mg daily, rising to 40-60 mg, to reduce intrusive urges","In high-risk cases a forensic psychiatrist may consider antilibidinal treatment, with full consent and ECG, bone and liver monitoring","Screen for and treat depression, anxiety or alcohol misuse that lowers self-control"],"psych":["Referral to a specialist forensic or sexual behaviour service for CBT-based work on urges and relapse prevention","Stop It Now confidential helpline (0808 1000 900) from tomorrow","Supportive work on shame and hopelessness, which raise both suicide and offending risk"],"social":["Police via 101 tonight; children's services emergency duty team tonight for the 8-year-old","LADO referral next working day because he works at a school","Trust safeguarding lead informed; GMC-compliant note of what was shared, with whom and why","GP letter, with his knowledge"],"safety":["Agreed: no unsupervised contact with any child, no babysitting, from tonight","Written safety plan before he leaves; Samaritans 116 123, NHS 111 option 2, 999 if he feels unable to stay safe","Follow-up call from the crisis team within 24 hours, as police contact raises suicide risk"]},"facts":[{"h":"Paedophilia is not the same as offending","x":"A paedophilic interest is a disorder; abusing a child or holding images is a crime. Assess both, separately."},{"h":"GMC allows disclosure to protect children","x":"You may share without consent to prevent serious harm or a serious crime. Tell the patient first unless that would increase risk, and record your reasons."},{"h":"LADO for anyone working with children","x":"If an adult who works with children may pose a risk to them, the Local Authority Designated Officer must be told, usually within one working day."},{"h":"Suicide risk peaks after disclosure","x":"People facing police inquiry for indecent images are at high suicide risk. Ask before and after you tell him, and arrange next-day contact."},{"h":"Stop It Now helps people worried about themselves","x":"Confidential helpline 0808 1000 900 for adults worried about their sexual thoughts towards children. It does not replace your safeguarding duty."}],"type":"Risk assessment + Professional and ethical dilemma","do":"In ten minutes, elicit his concern, assess risk to children and himself, and explain the duty to share honestly."},"c-u-26":{"obj":["Named his long wait and anger aloud, sat with a clear exit, and did not promise to fetch Dr Fenwick","Recorded the belief's evidence, his certainty, how he explains the ignored friend request, and that it has lasted over three months","Asked about four pursuit behaviours: repeat visits, online searching, her home address and the Venice plan","Asked directly whether he would use force, about martial arts and weapons, and screened mood, voices, drugs and self-harm","Gave the nurse in charge a three-point plan: security present, no lone working, police if he leaves; and named the consultant and Mental Health Act assessment"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"I've been here five hours. Just tell me when she's coming.\"","r":"\"Five hours is a long time, and I'd be fed up too. She won't be coming down tonight. I'm here now and I want to hear what's going on.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You think I'm making it up, don't you?\"","r":"\"I can see you feel this very strongly, and I'm not here to argue. Help me understand: what happened when you met that told you how she felt?\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Two minutes. Let me see her for two minutes and I'll go.\"","r":"\"I understand why you're asking. That isn't something I can arrange. What I can do is listen properly and work out the next step with you.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why won't anyone let me see her?\"","r":"\"Staff can't call colleagues in to meet patients personally; that's the same for everyone. I know that's hard to hear. Can we talk about what you'd planned for tonight?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"If security try to stop me, they'll regret it. I do MMA.\"","r":"\"Thank you for being straight with me. I take that seriously, and I want everyone safe, you included. Have you ever hurt anyone before, or carried anything as a weapon?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not staying for any more doctors.\"","r":"\"I hear you're done with waiting. My senior and I are worried about tonight, so we'd like to keep talking with you here. Can I get you a drink while we sort it?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["If agitation rises: offer oral lorazepam 1-2 mg or oral promethazine 25-50 mg first (NICE NG10); IM only if oral is refused and risk is high","Once assessed: an antipsychotic for delusional disorder, e.g. aripiprazole 5 mg daily rising to 10 mg, or risperidone 1-2 mg; baseline ECG, weight, HbA1c and lipids","Urine drug screen and basic bloods to exclude stimulant or organic causes"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis once settled, focused on the belief and its consequences, through the forensic or early intervention psychology team","Engagement and psychoeducation with a named care coordinator; stalking-focused work with a forensic psychologist"],"social":["Trust security informed tonight; his record flagged so future attendances never route to Dr Fenwick","Dr Fenwick's clinical lead tells her personally and arranges support; police may consider a Stalking Protection Order","Mental Health Act assessment by AMHP and Section 12 doctor if he will not accept informal admission"],"safety":["No lone working with him; security outside the door; 999 if violence is threatened or he leaves intent on finding her","Personal safety advice for Dr Fenwick, including at home and online; Datix incident report"]},"facts":[{"h":"Erotomania is delusional disorder","x":"ICD-11 delusional disorder: a fixed false belief for at least three months, no prominent hallucinations, mood episode or substance cause. Erotomania is the 'loved by a stranger' theme, often someone in a caring role."},{"h":"Rejection is the danger point","x":"Risk peaks when the belief meets reality: being refused, blocked or told she will not come. Plan who tells him, with security close, before you say it."},{"h":"Section 5(2) is not for A&E","x":"A holding power needs an inpatient. In A&E, if he leaves while high risk, call the police, who can use Section 136 in A&E or act on a threat of violence."},{"h":"You may warn her","x":"GMC guidance allows disclosure without consent to protect someone from serious harm. Share only what is needed, with her clinical lead and security, and record your reasons."},{"h":"Ask about the next step","x":"Pursuit escalates: visits, searching, address, letters, turning up at home, travel plans. A booked trip and a stated willingness to use force are imminent-risk markers."}],"type":"Risk assessment + History to a diagnosis","do":"In ten minutes, calm him, establish the erotomanic belief and pursuit, grade risk, and hand over a safe plan."},"c-u-27":{"obj":["Answered her question about her hands and stated confidentiality and its one limit before asking about the fire","Recorded her feelings before, during and after the fire, including relief and how she felt watching the fire engines","Asked about past fires, age when fire first interested her, and whether she carries a lighter","Asked about revenge, self-harm intent, voices, high mood, low mood and alcohol or drugs","Named likely pyromania and the risk to housemates, and agreed two safety actions plus a GP and psychology referral"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you going to tell the police?\"","r":"\"It's a fair worry. What you tell me stays within the health team. I'd only share if someone were at serious risk, and I'd tell you first.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Someone could have died. I'm a horrible person.\"","r":"\"You're clearly shaken by what could have happened, and that matters. Feeling this badly tells me you care. Let's work out how to stop it happening again.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"It was just the wine. It won't happen again.\"","r":"\"The wine may well have played a part. Can I ask: has a fire ever happened before, at a time you hadn't been drinking?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Watching the fire engines, I felt... excited. Is that sick?\"","r":"\"I'm grateful you trusted me with that; it's hard to say. It isn't unusual in people who have urges like yours, and it helps me understand what's going on.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is there something wrong with me?\"","r":"\"You've been carrying a lot. What you describe, tension that only eases when you set a fire, is a recognised problem called pyromania, and talking treatment helps.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want to give up my lighter. It calms me down.\"","r":"\"I understand it's been your way to cope. Right now it's also the thing that makes a fire possible. Could we swap it for something else that calms you, and talk again in a week?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No medicine is licensed for pyromania; treatment is psychological","If a depressive or anxiety disorder emerges at follow-up, sertraline 50 mg daily, reviewed at 2-4 weeks","Burns: dressings and paracetamol 1 g up to four times daily; burns clinic or practice nurse review as advised by A&E","No alcohol for now: it removed her brakes tonight"],"psych":["GP referral to a forensic psychology service for CBT on fire-setting: triggers, tension, alternatives; usually around 12-20 weekly sessions","Distress-tolerance skills (from DBT) for urges when tension builds","University counselling or NHS Talking Therapies for stress and her father's illness"],"social":["University wellbeing team and personal tutor for coursework extensions, with her consent","Honest advice that the fire service may investigate; suggest legal advice if contacted","Macmillan carer support for her father's cancer","GP review within one week"],"safety":["Lighter handed over today; no matches or lighters in her room for now","If the urge returns: tell a named friend, leave the house, call NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans 116 123","Consultant informed; no police disclosure at present, reasoning recorded"]},"facts":[{"h":"Pyromania: tension, then relief","x":"ICD-11: repeated fire-setting with no clear motive, tension or arousal before, pleasure or relief during and after, and fascination with fire. Not better explained by intoxication, psychosis, mania or learning disability."},{"h":"Ask the meaning of the fire","x":"Fires are set for revenge, to self-harm, to cover a crime, for money, to get attention or rescue, for excitement, or to relieve tension. The meaning drives the risk and the treatment."},{"h":"Before, during, after","x":"Map thoughts and feelings at each stage, plus triggers, alcohol and what she did next. Watching the fire service with excitement points to pyromania."},{"h":"No duty to report past crime","x":"GMC guidance: confidentiality holds unless disclosure is needed to prevent serious harm or to help detect serious crime. Discuss with a senior, record the reasoning, and tell the patient."},{"h":"Who is in the building?","x":"Fire-setting risk is about people, not only property. Ask who lives there, whether the fire was near doors or bins by windows, and whether she raised the alarm."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"In ten minutes, map her fire-setting before, during and after, exclude other causes, and agree a safety plan."},"c-u-28":{"obj":["Recorded six methadone details: dose, prescriber, pharmacy, supervised or not, time of last dose, and any heroin on top","Asked about alcohol in units, tobacco, benzodiazepines, cannabis, stimulants, other opioids and novel drugs, each with amount, route and last use","Asked about injecting, needle sharing, blood-borne virus testing and past overdoses","Scored his withdrawal on COWS and asked what he would do if he got no methadone today","Explained in one sentence why the dose must be verified, and agreed a named plan: who will be phoned, symptom relief, naloxone kit, and tomorrow's dose"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"I'm not leaving till I get my methadone. Simple.\"","r":"\"You're clearly feeling rough, and I want to help you. To do that safely I need ten minutes of questions. Shall we start?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why all these questions? I just told you my dose.\"","r":"\"Fair question. I ask everyone the same. The wrong dose of methadone can stop someone breathing, so I need the full picture to keep you safe.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I don't get something, I'll end up scoring. I know I will.\"","r":"\"Thank you for being honest. Two years clean is huge, and I don't want you to lose it today. Let's get you something for the symptoms now while we confirm your script.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can't you just ring my GP?\"","r":"\"Your drug service writes the script, and your GP's closed today. I'll try your pharmacy's out-of-hours line and the drug service's on-call number. That usually works.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"Ten mil? That won't touch it.\"","r":"\"I hear you; it's less than you're used to. It's a safe start while we can't confirm. We'll recheck you in an hour, and we can give more if you still need it.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Years ago I shared needles. I never got the results back.\"","r":"\"Thanks for telling me. That's really common. Let's take a blood test today for hepatitis B, C and HIV, and check whether you've had the hepatitis B jab.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Verify first: dispensing pharmacy (out-of-hours line), drug service on-call, or prescription records; record who confirmed and when","Confirmed and fewer than 3 days missed: usual dose (60 mg) observed in A&E. Not confirmed: e.g. 10 mg observed if objective withdrawal, reassess at 1-2 hours, per trust policy and senior advice","Symptom relief: loperamide 4 mg then 2 mg after each loose stool (max 16 mg/day); metoclopramide 10 mg up to three times daily; paracetamol 1 g; hyoscine butylbromide 20 mg for cramps","Urine drug screen; avoid prescribing benzodiazepines or other sedatives alongside"],"psych":["Brief motivational work: name his two years without heroin and his plan for urges today","Keyworker review this week; peer support through SMART Recovery or Narcotics Anonymous"],"social":["Call or message the drug service and pharmacy today with the dose given, so tomorrow's is not doubled","Ask the keyworker to plan bank holiday pick-ups (take-home doses or an open pharmacy) in future","Blood-borne virus tests and hepatitis B vaccination status; stop smoking service referral"],"safety":["Naloxone kit given with a demonstration; he knows to call 999 first","Warn: reduced tolerance, don't use alone, don't mix with alcohol or sedatives","No driving while in withdrawal or sedated"]},"facts":[{"h":"Never prescribe on his word","x":"Confirm the dose and last collection with the pharmacy or drug service before giving methadone. Record who confirmed it, and tell them what you gave so the next dose is not doubled."},{"h":"Three missed days: reassess","x":"After about three or more missed days tolerance may have fallen; the prescriber should reassess before the full dose restarts. One missed day is usually safe to resume once verified."},{"h":"Methadone withdrawal is slower","x":"Starts about 24-48 hours after the last dose, versus 6-12 hours for heroin, and lasts longer. Score it with COWS: 13 or more is moderate, 25 or more moderately severe."},{"h":"Withdrawal hurts, overdose kills","x":"Uncomplicated opioid withdrawal is rarely life-threatening. Overdose is. When in doubt, give less, observe, and treat symptoms."},{"h":"Naloxone goes home with him","x":"Offer take-home naloxone to anyone at risk of opioid overdose: injectable or nasal kit, with a demonstration, and advice to call 999 first."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"In ten minutes, take a full substance history, score withdrawal, and agree a verified, safe plan for today."},"c-u-29":{"obj":["Recorded a PHQ-9 score and asked directly about suicidal thoughts, alcohol and psychotic symptoms","Asked about all four IPT areas (grief, role dispute, role transition, isolation) and named the two that apply to him","Explained IPT in three points: mood and relationships link, here-and-now focus, and 12-16 weekly sessions in three phases","Answered 'why not IPT first?' by saying both are NICE-recommended and no therapy suits everyone","Checked four suitability points (links mood to events, motivation, weekly attendance, no contraindication) and agreed referral, GP review and a crisis number"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"If IPT is better, why wasn't I offered it first?\"","r":"\"That's a fair thing to ask. It isn't that one is better: both work, and CBT is usually offered first. It didn't fit what's driving this for you, and that helps us choose.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Forty years of teaching, and now I'm just... in the way at home.\"","r":"\"That's a huge change, and it sounds like you've lost a big part of who you were. That's exactly the sort of thing IPT works on.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it just talking about my childhood?\"","r":"\"No. IPT stays mostly in the here and now: the changes and relationships in your life today, and how they link to your mood.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I feel I failed at CBT. I did the homework.\"","r":"\"You worked hard at it, and that counts for a lot. Not every therapy suits every person. Your effort tells me you'll engage well with the next one.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will my wife have to come?\"","r":"\"Only if you'd like her to. Some people invite their partner to one session. It's your choice.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Do I need tablets as well?\"","r":"\"Not necessarily. At your level, therapy alone is a reasonable choice. Some people do better with both. You can decide, and your GP can review it any time.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Optional alongside therapy: sertraline 50 mg once daily, reviewed at 2-4 weeks; he may reasonably choose therapy alone","If an SSRI is started: sodium before and 2-4 weeks after, as over-60s are prone to hyponatraemia","GP bloods if not done: FBC, TFTs, B12, folate, glucose, calcium"],"psych":["IPT: 12-16 weekly 50-minute sessions with a trained IPT therapist (CMHT or Talking Therapies)","Early phase: mood timeline, interpersonal inventory, agree focus: role transition, with role dispute as second strand","Middle phase: mourn the old role, build a new one, and renegotiate expectations with his wife","Ending phase: review gains, warning signs and a relapse plan; PHQ-9 each session"],"social":["Ideas for a new role: University of the Third Age, volunteering, STEM mentoring or occasional teaching","Invite his wife to one session if he wishes","GP to review mood monthly while he waits"],"safety":["If thoughts of self-harm start: NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans 116 123; tell the GP or therapist"]},"facts":[{"h":"Four IPT focus areas","x":"Grief (a death), role disputes, role transitions, and interpersonal deficits or isolation. Find all that apply, then agree one, at most two, to work on."},{"h":"IPT is time-limited","x":"Usually 12-16 weekly sessions in three phases: assessment and focus, working on the area, and ending with a relapse plan. Mood is scored each session."},{"h":"Both are NICE options","x":"NICE NG222 includes CBT, behavioural activation, IPT, counselling and short psychodynamic therapy among options for depression. No one therapy is best for everyone."},{"h":"Grief means a death","x":"In IPT, losing a job or role is a role transition, not grief. Grief is reserved for bereavement."},{"h":"When IPT is a poor fit","x":"Active psychosis, heavy substance use, or risk he cannot manage between sessions make outpatient therapy unworkable until addressed."}],"type":"Therapy assessment + Explain a treatment","do":"In ten minutes, find his IPT focus area, check safety and suitability, and explain IPT practically."},"c-u-30":{"obj":["Recorded a full day's intake, daily exercise and each compensatory behaviour by name, with the laxative number","Asked about weight history, fear of weight gain and how she sees her body now","Asked about at least five physical red flags: dizziness or faints, palpitations, chest pain, weakness rising from a chair, periods","Asked directly about suicidal thoughts and self-harm, and recorded the passive death wish with no plan","Explained the danger in plain words, asked her to stay, and gave the medical team a five-point plan: refeeding, bloods, ECG, laxatives stopped, eating disorder referral"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm fine, really. I just eat healthily.\"","r":"\"I hear that, and lots of people tell me the same. Could you walk me through yesterday, from when you woke up, so I can see what healthy looks like for you?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I take laxatives. Every night. Quite a few.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that can't have been easy. It helps explain your potassium. Roughly how many do you take?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Everyone says I'm thin. I just feel huge.\"","r":"\"That sounds exhausting, feeling one way when everyone tells you another. That gap is part of the illness. And if your weight went up, what do you fear would happen?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Sometimes I wish I just wouldn't wake up.\"","r":"\"I'm really glad you told me. Have you thought of ending your life, or made any plans? Have you ever hurt yourself?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"Can I just go home now my potassium's back to normal?\"","r":"\"I can see you want to get home. Your potassium was fixed by a drip, and it will drop again. Your heart is at risk while that happens, so I'd like you to stay while we make eating safe.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you going to make me eat everything?\"","r":"\"That's a really common worry. A dietitian will build a plan with you, step by step, and nurses will support you at meals. We'll go at a pace that's safe.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Refeeding per MEED with the dietitian: start around 1,400-2,000 kcal/day and increase; avoid underfeeding","Daily potassium, phosphate, magnesium, glucose and U&E for at least the first week, then twice weekly; replace as needed","Thiamine 100 mg three times daily plus a multivitamin before and during early refeeding","ECG for QTc and bradycardia; lying and standing BP, temperature; stop laxatives with a bowel plan"],"psych":["Daily liaison psychiatry reviews; specialist eating disorder team referral today","Supported mealtimes by trained nurses, with 30-60 minutes' supervision after meals","Motivational work now; later eating-disorder-focused CBT (CBT-ED, around 40 sessions) or MANTRA through the eating disorder service"],"social":["With consent, involve her family and point them to Beat for carer support","Fit note or university medical leave","Specialist eating disorder unit if no progress on the medical ward"],"safety":["Passive death wish: agree she tells a nurse if it worsens; review daily","With consent, check her belongings for laxatives","Medical team escalate falling potassium or phosphate, pulse under 40, chest pain, fainting or confusion"]},"facts":[{"h":"MEED replaced MARSIPAN","x":"RCPsych MEED (2022) is the UK guide for medically unwell people with eating disorders. Risk is judged on BMI, rate of loss, observations, bloods, ECG and muscle power, not BMI alone."},{"h":"Underfeeding is a risk too","x":"MEED advises starting refeeding at about 1,400-2,000 kcal/day with close electrolyte monitoring, rather than very low calories, which prolong starvation."},{"h":"Refeeding syndrome: watch phosphate","x":"Falls in phosphate, potassium and magnesium in the first days of eating can cause arrhythmias, heart failure and confusion. Check daily for the first week; give thiamine first."},{"h":"Laxatives explain low potassium","x":"Unexplained hypokalaemia in a young woman with low weight suggests laxative misuse or vomiting. Ask about both by name, with numbers and frequency."},{"h":"Section 5(2) works on medical wards","x":"Any informal inpatient can be held for up to 72 hours by the doctor or approved clinician in charge, or their nominated deputy, while a Mental Health Act assessment is arranged."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"In ten minutes, take an eating disorder history, grade medical and suicide risk, and give the medics a plan."},"c-u-31":{"obj":["Explained refeeding syndrome in plain words, naming phosphate, potassium and magnesium, and why the starting rate is low","Gave the plan with timings: thiamine before the first feed, pH check before each feed, daily bloods for a week, ECG and four-hourly NEWS2","Named at least four call-now triggers and who to bleep, day and night","Stated the legal authority (MHA Section 63) and a least restrictive plan for tube pulling or leaving the ward","Got Callum to repeat back the triggers and agreed a review time"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why such a tiny amount? She's skin and bone, surely she needs more?\"","r":"\"It looks wrong, I know. After starving, her body can't cope with a big load: minerals rush into the cells and her heart can fail. We build up over days.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I'm not comfortable holding her down to feed her.\"","r":"\"That's a fair worry, and nobody should be doing it alone. Talking and calm explanation come first. Holding is a last resort, by trained staff, and recorded every time.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What do I actually do if she pulls the tube out?\"","r":"\"Good to plan now. Stop the pump, make sure she's safe, tell the doctor and me. Don't re-pass it until we've agreed how, and check position before any feed.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"She's an adult. Can we really feed her if she says no?\"","r":"\"Good question to ask. She's detained, and feeding counts as treatment for her anorexia under the Mental Health Act. We still explain every step and ask for her help.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"She was sobbing, saying we're making her fat. It was horrible.\"","r":"\"That sounds really hard to watch. It's the illness talking, and she's very frightened. Stay calm and kind, don't argue about weight, and focus on her being safe.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What if she walks off the ward?\"","r":"\"She's detained, so she'd be absent without leave. Tell the nurse in charge at once, call the on-call psychiatrist, and the police if she's at risk. Section 18 lets us bring her back.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Dietitian's starting rate, very low because of BMI 13 and past refeeding syndrome (NICE CG32: 5-10 kcal/kg/day); rises over about 4-7 days only on dietitian advice","Thiamine 200-300 mg daily (or IV Pabrinex), vitamin B compound strong and a multivitamin: before the first feed, then for 10 days","Phosphate, potassium, magnesium, glucose and U&E before starting, then daily for the first week; replace per hospital protocol","Baseline ECG, repeated if electrolytes fall or pulse changes; NEWS2 at least four-hourly; capillary glucose; strict fluid balance; weight as the plan sets","NG position: aspirate pH 5.5 or below before every feed and after coughing, retching or vomiting; X-ray if no aspirate or pH above 5.5"],"psych":["Calm, consistent approach; explain each step to Imogen and avoid any talk about weight or numbers in front of her","Mental health nurse one-to-one, especially during feeds and for an hour after (vomiting, exercise, tampering)","Eating disorder unit nurse to visit and advise; liaison psychiatry review daily"],"social":["Legal: NG feeding under Section 63; record each decision and any holding","IMHA: make sure she knows how to contact one, and that her rights have been explained again on this ward","Family updated with her agreement where possible; offer carer support"],"safety":["Call the medical team at once: phosphate under 0.5 mmol/L, palpitations or new arrhythmia, chest pain, confusion, seizure, rapid oedema or breathlessness","Tube pulled out: stop the feed, inform the doctor, do not re-pass without a plan; holding only as last resort and recorded","Leaves the ward: AWOL procedure, nurse in charge, on-call psychiatrist; police if at risk"]},"facts":[{"h":"Why refeeding kills","x":"Feeding triggers insulin, which pulls phosphate, potassium and magnesium into cells. Blood levels crash in the first days, risking arrhythmia, heart failure, weakness, confusion and seizures."},{"h":"Thiamine goes before the feed","x":"Give thiamine 200-300 mg daily, vitamin B compound strong and a multivitamin before the first feed and for 10 days. Carbohydrate without thiamine can tip someone into Wernicke's encephalopathy."},{"h":"pH 5.5 or below before feeding","x":"Check NG position by aspirate pH before every feed and after coughing or vomiting. pH 5.5 or below confirms gastric placement. No aspirate or higher pH: do not feed, escalate."},{"h":"Feeding is treatment under Section 63","x":"For a detained patient, NG feeding for anorexia counts as treatment for the mental disorder, so consent is not required. Holding stays a last resort and is recorded."},{"h":"Watch for hidden behaviours","x":"Expect tube pulling, turning the pump down, hiding or vomiting feed, exercising and water loading before weighing. One-to-one during and after feeds catches them early."}],"type":"Management plan + Handover and escalation","do":"In ten minutes, brief the nurse on refeeding risk, monitoring, escalation triggers and the tube-out plan; check teach-back."},"c-u-32":{"obj":["Confirmed her methadone dose, any on-top use or injecting, cravings, hepatitis C and blood-borne virus status, and midwife booking","Explained that staying on a steady methadone dose is safest, giving at least two risks of stopping suddenly","Explained neonatal withdrawal (signs, timing, observation, treatment) and that breastfeeding is encouraged","Screened mood, relapse risk and domestic abuse, and gave naloxone, safe storage and crisis routes","Agreed a plan naming the specialist midwife, prescriber, GP letter and review date, checked by teach-back"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I feel awful. I've done this to my baby before it's even born.\"","r":"\"I can hear how much you care. You've been off heroin four years and stopped smoking and drinking already. Staying steady on methadone is part of protecting your baby, not harming it.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can't I just come off it quickly and get it over with?\"","r":"\"I understand wanting it gone. Stopping fast risks withdrawal, cravings and going back to heroin, which is far riskier for you both. If you still want to cut down later, we do it slowly, with support.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I read babies come out withdrawing. Is that true?\"","r":"\"It's a fair question. Some do: crying, poor feeding, sneezing, poor sleep, usually in the first few days. The team watch for it, and it's very treatable. Cuddles and breastfeeding help a lot.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will social services take my baby off me?\"","r":"\"That's a really common fear, and I'll be straight with you. They'll probably be involved before the birth, to plan support. Being on methadone and doing well is not a reason to remove a baby.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I breastfeed on methadone? And with my hep C?\"","r":"\"Yes, usually. Only a little methadone gets into milk, and it can ease withdrawal. Treated hepatitis C isn't a reason to stop; we'd only pause on a side if a nipple cracked and bled.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why would my dose need to go up later? I want less, not more.\"","r":"\"I get that. Late in pregnancy your body clears methadone faster, so the same dose can feel weaker. If you feel shaky or crave, a small increase or a split dose keeps you and the baby steady.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue methadone 30 mg daily; no sudden stop; inform her prescriber of the pregnancy today","Third trimester: review for withdrawal symptoms; a small increase or split dose if needed","If she still wants to reduce: small, slow steps, usually in the second trimester, led by the drug service with obstetric input; stop reducing if cravings or use return","Booking bloods: hepatitis C RNA, hepatitis B, HIV, syphilis; folic acid 400 micrograms daily until 12 weeks","After birth: baby observed for neonatal withdrawal for several days (often about five for methadone); breastfeeding encouraged"],"psych":["Drug service keyworker sessions continued for relapse prevention","Perinatal team review in 4 weeks; mood check again in late pregnancy and after birth given past depression","Antenatal session on caring for a baby with withdrawal: skin-to-skin, quiet room, responsive feeding"],"social":["Specialist substance misuse midwife; birth planned in a unit with neonatal care","Pre-birth planning with children's social care explained honestly as support","Involve Dan; health visitor antenatal contact","Safe storage: methadone in a locked box out of reach; naloxone kit for the household"],"safety":["Any lapse: tell the keyworker at once; tolerance drops quickly, so overdose risk is high","999 for suspected overdose; 111 option 2 if mood or coping worsens"]},"facts":[{"h":"Stay steady on methadone","x":"Methadone is not known to cause birth defects. A stable dose in pregnancy protects against withdrawal, relapse and overdose; sudden stopping carries more risk for mother and baby."},{"h":"Reduce slowly, if at all","x":"If a woman insists on reducing, use small steps, usually in the second trimester, led by the drug service with obstetric input. Stop reducing if cravings or use return."},{"h":"Third trimester may need more","x":"Methadone is cleared faster late in pregnancy. New shakiness, yawning or cravings may need a small increase or a split dose."},{"h":"Neonatal withdrawal is treatable","x":"Signs include high-pitched crying, poor feeding, sneezing, tremor, loose stools and poor sleep, usually starting within days. Most babies settle with comfort care; some need oral morphine."},{"h":"Breastfeeding is encouraged","x":"Only small amounts of methadone pass into milk, and breastfeeding can ease withdrawal. Hepatitis C alone is no bar; pause that side if a nipple cracks and bleeds."}],"type":"Explain a treatment + Management plan","do":"In ten minutes, check her drug use and health, explain staying on methadone, answer her questions, agree a shared plan."},"c-u-33":{"obj":["Established at least five depressive symptoms, with onset about two weeks after birth and duration over two weeks","Asked about feelings for the baby, care of the baby and unwanted thoughts of harm, separating them from intent","Screened baby blues, psychosis, mania, OCD and physical causes (anaemia, thyroid)","Asked about the previous postnatal period, family history, alcohol, support and domestic abuse","Assessed suicidal thoughts, plans and protective factors and risk to both children, then named postnatal depression and gave a crisis route"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should be happy. I've got a healthy baby. What's wrong with me?\"","r":"\"Lots of mums feel exactly this, and it isn't your fault. Feeling low after a baby is an illness, not a failing. Can I ask a bit more so we can help?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"When I hold him I feel nothing. Like he's someone else's.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that takes courage. It's a very common part of postnatal depression, and it usually comes back as you recover. How do you manage his feeds and care?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I tell you everything, will they take my children away?\"","r":"\"That's a really common worry. My job is to help you get better. From what you've said, you're caring for both boys well. Being honest helps us support you.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Sometimes I think they'd all be better off without me.\"","r":"\"I'm really glad you said that. It sounds exhausting. Can I ask more: have you thought of ending your life, or made any plans?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it just the baby blues? My friend said it passes.\"","r":"\"Good question. Baby blues start in the first few days and settle by about two weeks. Yours started later and has lasted a month, so it sounds like postnatal depression, which needs treatment.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why are you asking if I'd hurt the baby? I'd never!\"","r":"\"I'm sorry, I can see that's upsetting. I ask every mum I see, because some get frightening thoughts and feel unable to say. It helps me make sure you get the right help.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["GP bloods this week: FBC and ferritin (blood loss at delivery), TFTs (postpartum thyroiditis), vitamin D","If an antidepressant is chosen: sertraline 50 mg once daily is usually first choice while breastfeeding; review at 2 weeks"],"psych":["High-intensity CBT through NHS Talking Therapies, prioritised in the perinatal period","Parent-infant work with the perinatal team to rebuild the bond"],"social":["Urgent referral to the specialist perinatal mental health team (previous postnatal low mood, detachment from baby)","Health visitor informed with consent for extra visits","Rhys to cover some night feeds (expressed milk) to protect her sleep; ask her mother to help in the day"],"safety":["Discuss with the consultant before she leaves; crisis plan shared with Ffion and Rhys: 111 option 2, or 999 if she feels unable to keep herself or the boys safe","Same-day review if thoughts of harming the baby, unusual beliefs, confusion or a plan to end her life appear"]},"facts":[{"h":"Blues settle by two weeks","x":"Baby blues start within days of birth and settle by about two weeks. Symptoms beginning later, or lasting beyond two weeks, suggest postnatal depression."},{"h":"Detachment from baby is a red flag","x":"New, persistent feelings of being estranged from the baby or useless as a mother call for urgent specialist perinatal assessment, not routine waiting."},{"h":"OCD thoughts versus intent","x":"Unwanted, horrifying images of the baby being harmed, which she resists and avoids, suggest perinatal OCD. They are not intent and do not mean she is dangerous."},{"h":"Past postnatal illness predicts recurrence","x":"A previous postnatal episode, or a mother with one, raises the chance of illness after this birth. Ask about both every time."},{"h":"Sertraline is usually breastfeeding-friendly","x":"Sertraline passes into milk in very small amounts and is usually the first choice when breastfeeding. Watch the baby's feeding and sleep."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"In ten minutes, take a focused postnatal history, exclude key differentials, assess risk to her and both children."},"c-u-34":{"obj":["Noted and named aloud her appearance, behaviour and speech (agitation, guardedness, pressured speech)","Elicited the swapped-baby belief, persecutory ideas about midwives and auditory hallucinations, plus mood, sleep and appetite","Tested orientation and attention (months backwards) and recorded insight","Asked directly what she plans to do to find her 'real' baby, including harm to others, and about harm to her daughter and herself","Told her the urgent plan (staying, one-to-one, consultant, Mental Health Act assessment, Mother and Baby Unit) and ensured the baby is never alone with her"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why? Who sent you? Are you with them?\"","r":"\"I can see you don't know who to trust, and that's frightening. I'm a doctor from the hospital's mental health team. I'm here to help you, not to take anything away.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you going to lock me up again? I just want my baby.\"","r":"\"I can hear how much you love your daughter. I'm worried you're very unwell after the birth. The aim is to get you well, ideally in a unit where mums and babies stay together.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"That's not my baby. They swapped her. She looks different, she sounds different.\"","r":"\"That must be terrifying. Tell me more: when did you first feel she wasn't yours, and who do you think did it?\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Don't tell me I'm imagining the whispers. I hear them as clearly as you!\"","r":"\"I believe you're hearing them, and they sound awful. I'm not going to argue. What do they say, and are they there now?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Once I can walk properly, I'm getting my real baby, whatever it takes.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you're being honest with me. When you say whatever it takes, what might that mean? Could anyone get hurt?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"There's nothing wrong with me. I won't take tablets.\"","r":"\"I hear you, and I know this feels unfair. I think the fear, the whispers and the lack of sleep are an illness that can follow a birth, and it's very treatable.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Exclude delirium and physical causes today: FBC (after haemorrhage), CRP, U&E, calcium, glucose, LFTs, TFTs; temperature and wound review; review opioids and antiemetics","Antipsychotic once agreed with the consultant, e.g. olanzapine 5 mg at night, titrating to 10 mg; baseline ECG, weight, lipids, HbA1c","Lorazepam 0.5-1 mg orally for severe agitation (max 4 mg in 24 hours), with obstetric agreement","No antidepressant (previous switch to mania); mood stabiliser such as lithium may follow on the MBU","Feeding plan for the baby agreed with the obstetric pharmacist for each medicine"],"psych":["Calm, consistent approach: acknowledge fear, never argue with or confirm the belief","Explain to Arjun what postpartum psychosis is and that recovery is usual"],"social":["Urgent MHA assessment (AMHP and two doctors) as she lacks insight and risk is high","Mother and Baby Unit as first choice, via the perinatal team","Safeguarding: baby cared for by Arjun or staff, never alone with her; children's social care and safeguarding midwife informed","Action on Postpartum Psychosis information for the family"],"safety":["Constant one-to-one observation; ward security and neonatal unit alerted to abduction risk","Section 5(2) by the doctor in charge if she tries to leave before assessment; consultant told today; 999 if she leaves with a baby"]},"facts":[{"h":"Postpartum psychosis is an emergency","x":"Onset is typically in the first fortnight after delivery, and the picture shifts hour by hour. Risks include suicide and harm to the baby. Same-day senior review; Mother and Baby Unit is first choice."},{"h":"Past mania raises the risk","x":"Bipolar disorder, including antidepressant-triggered mania, sharply raises the risk of postpartum psychosis. Avoid antidepressants alone in these women."},{"h":"Screen for delirium in a minute","x":"After surgery and haemorrhage, test orientation and attention (months backwards). Fluctuating attention or disorientation points to delirium: check infection, Hb, sodium, calcium, glucose and drugs."},{"h":"Section 5(2) works on any ward","x":"Any hospital inpatient can be held for up to 72 hours by the doctor in charge or a nominated deputy, pending MHA assessment. Midwives cannot use the nurse's holding power."},{"h":"Think of other babies","x":"A mother who believes her real baby is elsewhere may try to take another. Alert ward security and the neonatal unit, and keep her on one-to-one."}],"type":"Mental state examination + Risk assessment","do":"In ten minutes, elicit her psychotic and mood symptoms, test orientation and insight, and assess risk to baby and others."},"c-u-35":{"obj":["Stated the limits of confidentiality before the history and established what he intended last night and why then","Elicited the jealous belief with its evidence, conviction and checking, plus hallucinations, mood, alcohol and insight","Asked at least twice what the knife was for and what he would have done if refused, and his current intent","Asked about risk to his wife, the rival, bystanders, his daughter and himself, and about past violence","Told him the plan (not released, Mental Health Act assessment, police and wife informed, no driving) and named who will be told"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why am I seeing a shrink? I'm not mad.\"","r":"\"Fair question. The officers were worried about you overnight, so I've come to hear your side. I'm not here to judge. Can you tell me what happened?\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Everyone takes her side. I KNOW she's sleeping with him.\"","r":"\"I can hear how sure you are, and how much it's tearing you up. I'm not taking sides. Tell me what's made you so certain.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"The knife? I don't know. It was just there.\"","r":"\"I know it's hard to talk about. It matters, so I'll ask again: when you put it in the car, what was it meant for?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I'd never hurt Kerry. I love her. Him, I don't know.\"","r":"\"Thank you for being honest. If you'd found them together and he laughed at you, with the knife there, what do you think would have happened?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So what are you going to do with me?\"","r":"\"I'll be straight. I think you're unwell, and I'm worried for your safety and other people's. I'd like you assessed for hospital today, not released home.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"You can't tell her what I said. That's private.\"","r":"\"I understand why you'd want that, and I said at the start I might have to share. Because someone could be seriously hurt, the police need to know, so Kerry can be kept safe.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Admission for assessment; urgent MHA assessment in custody if he declines","Antipsychotic once assessed, e.g. risperidone 1 mg rising to 2-4 mg daily; baseline ECG, FBC, U&E, LFTs, GGT, glucose, lipids, prolactin; urine drug screen","Alcohol withdrawal: CIWA-Ar by the custody healthcare practitioner now; chlordiazepoxide if dependent; thiamine (oral 100 mg three times daily, or IV Pabrinex if at risk of Wernicke's)","Organic screen if any neurological signs or atypical features"],"psych":["Engagement before any challenge to the belief","Later: CBT for psychosis targeting checking and jealous beliefs; alcohol work with the addictions team"],"social":["Police informed of risk to Kerry and the rival so she is warned (GMC public interest disclosure)","Safeguarding referral for Ellie; DASH risk assessment and MARAC referral via police","Police keep the knife; check access to other weapons","Liaison and diversion team; forensic advice; geographical separation advised on discharge"],"safety":["Not to be released home before MHA assessment; consultant informed today","No driving; DVLA notification advised; Kerry given police contact and 999 if he approaches her"]},"facts":[{"h":"Jealousy delusions carry real risk","x":"Delusional jealousy is linked with serious violence, most often towards the partner, sometimes the supposed rival. Checking, following, threats and weapons all raise the risk."},{"h":"Ask the knife question twice","x":"The first answer is often 'I don't know'. Ask calmly what it was for, then what would have happened if the rival refused or laughed."},{"h":"Disclose to prevent serious harm","x":"GMC guidance allows sharing without consent when needed to protect others from death or serious harm. Share the minimum, with the right people, and record why."},{"h":"No Section 136 after arrest","x":"Section 136 is for people found in a public place, not those already arrested. In custody, request an AMHP-led MHA assessment if admission is needed and declined."},{"h":"Drinkers withdraw in custody","x":"Ask about the last drink and shakes. Alert the custody healthcare practitioner to monitor with CIWA-Ar; withdrawal can cause seizures and delirium within 6-72 hours."}],"type":"Mental state examination + Risk assessment","do":"In ten minutes, map his jealous beliefs and psychopathology, press on the knife and plans, and decide next steps."},"c-u-36":{"obj":["Elicited the food-poisoning and depot beliefs and recorded his conviction as a number out of 100","Screened reference, thought interference, passivity and hallucinations in hearing, smell and taste, including what the voice tells him","Asked directly about suicide, harm to staff and plans to leave the accommodation, and graded each risk","Rated his insight and offered at least one treatment option he could accept","Agreed a dated next visit, gave the crisis line, and named who you will tell today"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not having that injection again. You lot used it to mess with my head.\"","r":"\"I hear you, Kofi, and I won't force anything today. Can you tell me what the last one felt like, and what happened after?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"They put stuff in the kitchen food. That's why I'm thin. You'd do the same.\"","r":"\"That sounds really frightening, and it makes sense you'd want to protect yourself. Would sealed food you open yourself feel safer for now?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why should I trust you? You're with them.\"","r":"\"Fair question. I work with your team, I won't pretend otherwise. What I can do is listen, tell you honestly what I think, and not do anything behind your back.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"There's a voice. It says don't let the nurse in.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me, that takes trust. Does the voice say anything else? Does it ever tell you to hurt anyone, or yourself?\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not ill. I'm just tired because I can't sleep here.\"","r":"\"Not sleeping is hard on anyone. You may not see it as illness, and that's okay. Could we work on the sleep together, as a start?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"If I don't have the injection, what then?\"","r":"\"That's a fair question. I'd rather find a treatment you're comfortable with than force anything. Some people prefer tablets they can see come out of a sealed pack. Would you consider that?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Offer oral aripiprazole 10 mg each morning, in blister packs he opens himself; usual range 10-30 mg","Weight, BMI and waist this week; bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, glucose, lipids, prolactin; ECG if he agrees","If intake stays very low, check phosphate, magnesium and potassium and ask a dietitian about refeeding risk"],"psych":["Same nurse visits daily, short and predictable; no debate about the beliefs","CBT for psychosis (at least 16 sessions, psychology team) once he is calmer"],"social":["Support staff offer sealed or self-prepared food and record intake daily","Care coordinator increases contact; home treatment team if he deteriorates","Family involved only with his consent; advocacy offered"],"safety":["Consultant and care coordinator told today; CTO status confirmed","Request AMHP for a Mental Health Act assessment if intake falls, weight keeps dropping, or voices command harm","He and staff have the crisis line and NHS 111 option 2"]},"facts":[{"h":"Ask conviction as a number","x":"'How sure, out of 100?' turns a belief into a recordable fact. Repeat it at each visit: falling conviction is an early sign of response."},{"h":"Poisoning fears: test taste and smell","x":"Someone who thinks food is tampered with may report odd tastes or smells. Ask directly; olfactory and gustatory hallucinations change the picture and the risk."},{"h":"Delusional perception is first-rank","x":"A normal perception (a painful injection) followed by a sudden delusional meaning ('they're controlling me') is a Schneiderian first-rank symptom. Record the sequence in his words."},{"h":"CTO recall: Section 17E","x":"If he is on a CTO, the responsible clinician can recall him to hospital for up to 72 hours when he needs treatment and there is risk without it."},{"h":"Real sounds or voices?","x":"Staff chatting through a wall are real; believing they discuss him is reference. A voice with no one there is a hallucination. Clarify which before you record it."}],"type":"Mental state examination + Risk assessment","do":"In ten minutes, elicit and grade his psychotic symptoms, risk and insight, then agree a dated next contact."},"c-u-37":{"obj":["Recorded the tablet number and source, planning, being alone, any note, and how she was found","Asked how she felt on waking and whether thoughts of dying and plans continue now","Documented the dates and triggers of all three overdoses and any other self-harm","Asked about mood, alcohol, drugs, trauma and who supports her","Agreed admission, home treatment or discharge with her, gave the reason, and named the legal fallback and who you will tell"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"So can I go home, or what?\"","r":"\"I understand you want to get out of here. I'd like to understand last night first, so whatever we decide is right for you. Can we take ten minutes?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Everyone thinks I just do it for attention.\"","r":"\"That must feel awful on top of everything. I don't see it that way. Three overdoses tells me you're in a lot of pain. Can you help me understand it?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Honestly, I wish I hadn't woken up.\"","r":"\"Thank you for being honest, that's really important. Are you still having those thoughts now, while we're talking? Have you thought about how?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"He just left. Two years, and he packed a bag.\"","r":"\"That's a huge loss, and so sudden. No wonder you've been struggling. How have you been coping since, day to day?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going on a psych ward. That's for crazy people.\"","r":"\"I hear that, and lots of people worry about it. It would be short, with a clear plan, and you can leave the ward informally. What worries you most about it?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can't I just go to my mum's?\"","r":"\"Your mum sounds important. Right now you're still thinking of dying, and I'm worried a night at home isn't enough. Could she visit you on the ward instead?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No benzodiazepines prescribed; mother removes or locks away her diazepam with Leah's consent","On the ward: CIWA-Ar alcohol withdrawal monitoring; bloods FBC, U&E, LFT, GGT","If a depressive episode is confirmed: sertraline 50 mg each morning, weekly supply; warn about agitation and suicidal thoughts in under-25s"],"psych":["Ward team completes the full psychosocial assessment and a written safety plan with her","Alcohol brief intervention; consider DBT referral if emotional instability is confirmed"],"social":["Mother involved with her consent; visits on the ward","GP informed within 24 hours; CMHT or home treatment follow-up booked before discharge"],"safety":["1:1 nursing in A&E until transfer; MHA assessment via AMHP if she withdraws consent and risk persists","Crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123; 999 in an emergency"]},"facts":[{"h":"No risk scores to decide care","x":"NICE NG225: never let a risk tool or score decide who gets treatment or goes home; none can predict suicide. Assess needs and risks in a full psychosocial assessment."},{"h":"Repeat self-harm raises suicide risk","x":"Self-harm is a strong predictor of later suicide. Increasing frequency or lethality is a warning sign, even if earlier episodes were judged impulsive."},{"h":"5(2) does not work in A&E","x":"Section 5(2) needs an inpatient. In A&E, use the MCA if she lacks capacity and is at risk, or arrange a Mental Health Act assessment."},{"h":"Under 25 on an SSRI","x":"Antidepressants may increase suicidal thoughts and agitation in under-25s early on. Review within one week, prescribe small quantities, and warn her and family."},{"h":"Safety planning beats no-harm contracts","x":"A written plan naming warning signs, coping steps, people, services and means restriction is useful. A 'promise not to harm yourself' is not."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"In ten minutes, assess this overdose, intent and current risk, then agree admission, home treatment or discharge."},"c-u-38":{"obj":["Recorded his usual drink, daily amount, years of drinking and the time of the last drink","Tested orientation and attention with one task (months backwards) and recorded the result","Elicited the content of visual and tactile hallucinations and any persecutory ideas","Recorded tremor, sweating, pulse and temperature, and named delirium tremens with at least three differentials","Handed over a specific plan: CIWA-Ar, benzodiazepine choice, IV Pabrinex before glucose, bloods and escalation triggers"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Get them off the wall! They're crawling everywhere!\"","r":"\"That sounds terrifying. You're safe here, Desmond. I can't see them, but I believe you can. It's part of an illness we can treat.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Who are you? Are you with them?\"","r":"\"I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the doctors. I'm here to help you, not to hurt you. You're in hospital after your fall.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not an alcoholic. I just like a drink.\"","r":"\"I'm not judging, lots of people enjoy a drink. I ask because stopping suddenly can make people feel like this. Roughly how much would you have on a normal day?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"What's wrong with me? Make it stop.\"","r":"\"I can see how frightened you are. Your body is reacting to going without alcohol. We'll give you medicine to calm it and vitamins to protect your brain.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm going home. Let me out.\"","r":"\"I understand you want to get away from this. Right now you're not well enough to be safe at home. Stay with us today, and the nurse will stay close.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["CIWA-Ar hourly; symptom-triggered lorazepam (e.g. 0.5-1 mg oral) because of his age and raised LFTs; chlordiazepoxide at reduced doses only if liver function is acceptable","IV Pabrinex 2 pairs three times daily for 3-5 days, given before any glucose","Bloods: U&E, magnesium, phosphate, glucose, LFT, clotting; replace magnesium and phosphate","Septic screen (chest, urine, wound) and ECG; repeat CT head if focal signs or confusion persists"],"psych":["Calm, well-lit side room, clock and glasses in reach, same staff where possible","After recovery: alcohol brief intervention, then community alcohol team"],"social":["Wife contacted for collateral and to support reorientation","Alcohol care team review on the ward; GP letter; DVLA advice before driving"],"safety":["Falls and seizure precautions; 1:1 nursing if he tries to climb out of bed","Escalate to registrar and outreach if NEWS rises, seizure, or reduced consciousness"]},"facts":[{"h":"DTs: 48-72 hours after the last drink","x":"Withdrawal tremor starts within hours, seizures peak around 24-48 hours, and delirium tremens usually appears from about 48-72 hours. Without treatment it can be fatal."},{"h":"Thiamine before glucose","x":"In anyone with alcohol misuse and confusion, presume Wernicke's. Give IV Pabrinex before glucose, as glucose without thiamine can precipitate it."},{"h":"Older or liver-impaired: shorter-acting","x":"Long-acting benzodiazepines accumulate in older people and liver impairment. Lorazepam or oxazepam are safer choices; use symptom-triggered dosing."},{"h":"Months backwards tests attention","x":"Inattention is the core of delirium. Ask for the months backwards from December: stopping early or missing months is abnormal. Record exactly how far he got."},{"h":"Antipsychotics only as add-on","x":"Haloperidol does not treat withdrawal and lowers the seizure threshold. Use only alongside a benzodiazepine for severe hallucinations, after an ECG."}],"type":"Mental state examination + Emergency management","do":"In ten minutes, take a focused alcohol history, assess attention and perceptions, diagnose, and hand over a plan."},"c-u-39":{"obj":["Stated the limits of confidentiality before taking details, and he confirmed he understood","Recorded planning, arousal, masturbation, alcohol, previous episodes and use of child sexual images","Screened for mania, psychosis, depression, learning disability and organic causes, and recorded each as present or absent","Graded the risk of repetition, escalation to contact offending, access to children and suicide","Gave the police a stated opinion on mental disorder and appropriate adult, and named the safeguarding referral and custody suicide alert"],"snip":[{"tag":"Shame","p":"\"I'm disgusting. I can't even look at you.\"","r":"\"I can see how ashamed you feel. I'm not here to judge you. Talking honestly is the first step to stopping this. Can you tell me what happened?\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"It was a mistake. I was just having a pee.\"","r":"\"I understand it's hard to talk about. The police report says the children saw you clearly. What was going through your mind before you got there?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you going to tell the police everything I say?\"","r":"\"That's a fair question. I won't pass on everything, but anything that puts children at risk, I have to share. I'd rather be honest with you now.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've looked at pictures. Young girls. Online.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me, I know that was hard. How long has that been going on? Have you ever touched a child, or wanted to?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What happens now? Can anyone help me stop this?\"","r":"\"Wanting help matters, Kieran. There is treatment: specialist talking therapy and sometimes medication to reduce urges. The police will also be involved. I'll write down where help is.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"My life's over. I might as well end it.\"","r":"\"It sounds like you're in a really dark place. I'm glad you told me. Have you thought about how you might do it? I'll make sure the custody nurse knows.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No mental illness requiring admission; return to custody with a written opinion","Specialist forensic or psychosexual service may consider an SSRI (e.g. fluoxetine 20 mg, rising to 40-60 mg) to reduce urges","Antilibidinal treatment only through a specialist, with informed consent and baseline bloods"],"psych":["Specialist psychosexual assessment and CBT focused on urges, alcohol and relapse prevention","Stop It Now confidential helpline and self-help resources"],"social":["Children's social care safeguarding referral today; police lead on the criminal process and devices","GP letter; alcohol service referral"],"safety":["Tell the custody nurse about suicidal thoughts; enhanced observation advised","Crisis line and Samaritans 116 123 on release"]},"facts":[{"h":"Exposure can escalate","x":"Most people who expose themselves do not go on to contact offences, but planning, arousal, targeting children, repetition and child sexual images raise concern and need specialist assessment."},{"h":"Ask about arousal directly","x":"Arousal and masturbation separate a paraphilic act from disinhibition due to illness, drink or dementia. Ask plainly: 'Were you sexually aroused? Did you touch yourself?'"},{"h":"Rule out new-onset causes","x":"A first episode in later life, or with personality change, needs mania, dementia (especially frontotemporal), head injury, epilepsy and drug effects excluded."},{"h":"Suicide after sexual-offence arrest","x":"People arrested for sexual offences, especially involving children, have a raised suicide risk in the days afterwards. Ask, and tell custody staff."},{"h":"Appropriate adult: answer the question","x":"Under PACE, police need an appropriate adult for a mentally vulnerable detainee. State clearly whether he needs one and why."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"In ten minutes, explore causes of the exposure, grade risk to children and himself, and advise police."},"c-u-40":{"obj":["Asked her name and pronouns at the start and used them throughout","Recorded the age of onset, childhood and puberty experience, and duration of living as a woman","Asked about sexual arousal with clothing, psychotic beliefs and body-focused preoccupation, and recorded each as present or absent","Asked about mood, suicidal thoughts, self-harm and self-sourced hormones","Explained GP referral to a Gender Dysphoria Clinic, who prescribes, and two supports while she waits"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I've waited long enough. I don't need someone checking if I'm trans.\"","r":"\"That's completely fair, and I'm not here to judge your identity. I want to make sure nothing else needs help, and get your referral moving. Can I ask a few questions?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So will you start me on oestrogen today?\"","r":"\"I understand why you want to start. I can't prescribe it myself today, Sophie, but I can explain the route and what can help while you wait.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've looked at buying hormones online. People do it.\"","r":"\"I hear how hard the wait is. Unregulated hormones can cause clots and liver problems, and doses are unknown. If you ever do start, please tell your GP so they can do safety bloods.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"My parents won't use my name. Mum says it's a phase.\"","r":"\"That sounds really painful, especially from your own family. How has that been affecting your mood? There are support groups for families too, if they're ever open to it.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why are you asking if I get turned on by the clothes?\"","r":"\"Good question, and I ask everyone in this situation. It helps me separate gender identity from other things, so the referral letter is clear and quick to act on.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will hormones mean I can't have kids?\"","r":"\"They can reduce fertility, sometimes permanently. Many people store sperm before starting. I'll ask the GP to discuss fertility preservation as part of the referral.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Hormones are started through the Gender Dysphoria Clinic, sometimes with GP shared care; not by general psychiatry","If she self-sources: GP safety bloods (FBC, LFT, lipids, HbA1c, prolactin, oestradiol, testosterone) and BP; stop smoking advice","Fertility preservation (sperm storage) discussed before any hormone treatment"],"psych":["Talking Therapies self-referral for low mood; supportive, identity-affirming approach","Voice and communication therapy via speech and language therapy, if wished"],"social":["GP referral to the adult Gender Dysphoria Clinic; NHS record name update","Trans peer support groups and LGBT+ helplines; workplace support under the Equality Act"],"safety":["If mood drops or suicidal thoughts arise: GP, crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123","Report hate incidents to the police; harassment support via LGBT+ services"]},"facts":[{"h":"Not a mental disorder in ICD-11","x":"ICD-11 moved gender incongruence out of mental disorders into conditions related to sexual health. Psychiatry's role is co-existing mental health, not deciding identity."},{"h":"Arousal separates the differential","x":"Cross-dressing for sexual arousal, without a wish to live as a woman, suggests fetishistic transvestism. Ask neutrally and record the answer."},{"h":"Online hormones carry real risk","x":"Unregulated oestrogen has unknown doses and contents. Clot risk is higher with smoking and oral ethinylestradiol. Harm reduction: GP monitoring if she self-medicates."},{"h":"Fertility before hormones","x":"Oestrogen and anti-androgens can reduce sperm production, sometimes permanently. Offer a conversation about sperm storage before treatment starts."},{"h":"Waits are long: plan support","x":"NHS gender clinic waits are often several years. Offer mood support, peer groups and voice therapy information rather than leaving the wait empty."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"In ten minutes, take a gender history, exclude key differentials, check mood and risk, and explain the pathway."},"c-u-41":{"obj":["Confirmed she was alone and safe to talk, and explained confidentiality and its limits, including the children","Elicited at least four specific controlling behaviours, including economic abuse, and screened for physical and sexual abuse and strangulation","Asked the key risk questions (escalation, threats, fear, children) and her own suicide and harm-to-others risk","Named the abuse as coercive control with emotional and economic abuse, and said it is a crime and not her fault","Agreed a safe contact route, gave the helpline and 999 then 55, and recorded her decision on an IDVA referral"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"He's never hit me. I'm probably making a fuss about nothing.\"","r":"\"I can hear you doubting yourself, and that's very common. Abuse isn't only hitting. Taking your money, your phone and your friends is abuse too. You're not making a fuss.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If he finds out I said any of this, it'll be so much worse.\"","r":"\"That fear makes complete sense. Nothing goes to your house, and we'll only ring when you say it's safe. Let's agree that now, before anything else.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Maybe it's me. I'm useless at everything, he's right.\"","r":"\"After years of hearing that, anyone would start to believe it. But it's his behaviour that's wrong, not you. You got yourself here today, alone. That isn't useless.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you going to take my kids off me?\"","r":"\"I can see that's your biggest worry. No. Children's services aim to support you and the children together. You're protecting them by talking to me. I'll make the referral with you.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So what do I do? Just leave him?\"","r":"\"You don't have to decide that today. Leaving needs a careful plan, because it can be a risky time. A specialist adviser can plan it with you, at your pace.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I've got to go, I have to get Zofia. He'll check the time.\"","r":"\"Of course, her pickup comes first. Two quick things before you go: the helpline number, hidden as a contact name, and how we can safely reach you.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No medication for the abuse itself; screen for depression, anxiety and PTSD, which often follow","If moderate depression persists at review, discuss sertraline 50 mg daily with a 2-week review and small supplies","GP to rebook asthma review, contraception and cervical screening at times she can safely attend"],"psych":["IDVA for one-to-one safety planning and emotional support","Freedom Programme or a similar group to understand controlling behaviour, when safe to attend","Trauma-focused CBT later, once she is safe, if PTSD symptoms persist"],"social":["IDVA completes the full DASH checklist; 14 or more ticks or professional concern leads to MARAC","Children's social care referral through the trust safeguarding lead; school and nursery informed by them","Clare's Law 'right to ask' via police; legal advice about non-molestation orders through the IDVA"],"safety":["Safe contact: withheld number, weekdays 10:00-14:00, no letters home; record marked sensitive","In danger: 999, and if she cannot speak, press 55 when prompted (Silent Solution)","Review in 2 weeks, timed to her nursery run"]},"facts":[{"h":"Coercive control is a crime","x":"Section 76 of the Serious Crime Act 2015 makes repeated coercive or controlling behaviour in an intimate or family relationship an offence. No physical violence is needed."},{"h":"Always ask about strangulation","x":"Non-fatal strangulation sharply raises the risk of later homicide and is its own offence under the Domestic Abuse Act 2021. Ask plainly: 'Has he ever put his hands round your neck?'"},{"h":"DASH: 14 ticks means MARAC","x":"The SafeLives DASH checklist has 27 questions. 14 or more 'yes' answers, or your professional judgement, or escalation, means referral to MARAC, the multi-agency risk meeting."},{"h":"999 then 55","x":"If she calls 999 and cannot speak, she should cough or tap, then press 55 when prompted. The call is put through to the police as an emergency."},{"h":"Children are victims too","x":"Under the Domestic Abuse Act 2021, a child who sees, hears or experiences the effects of abuse is a victim. Discuss with your safeguarding lead and refer, telling the mother."},{"h":"National helpline is free, 24 hours","x":"National Domestic Abuse Helpline: 0808 2000 247, free, day and night. Suggest she saves it under a harmless name on her phone."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Explain a diagnosis","do":"In 10 minutes: elicit the controlling behaviours, ask key risk questions, name coercive control, and agree safe contact."},"c-u-42":{"obj":["Explained she was not speaking to the police, the limits of confidentiality, and that the appropriate adult stays","Completed an ABC analysis of this fire, including whether she raised the alarm and how she feels now","Established the motive, linked it to named psychotic symptoms, and asked about past fires and fire interest","Screened mood, substances, care history and learning difficulty, and stated a working diagnosis with two differentials","Assessed the risk of another fire and of suicide, and told her and the sergeant the next step and her fitness for interview"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Am I going to prison for this?\"","r":"\"I can see how frightening that is. I can't promise what the police will decide. But you seem unwell, and I'll tell them that clearly. Getting you treated comes first.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not saying anything else. You'll just tell them.\"","r":"\"That's fair, and you can stop any time. I'm not here to build a case. I only share what affects your safety or someone else's. Can you tell me how you're feeling right now?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"The voice says it's not finished. The poison's still there.\"","r":"\"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. It sounds terrifying. Is the voice telling you to light another fire? That helps me keep you and others safe.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Everyone had to run outside. I could have killed someone.\"","r":"\"It matters that you care about that. You also shouted 'fire' and got out, which kept people safe. Let's work out what was driving you, so it doesn't happen again.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I'm not mad. The staff really are filming me.\"","r":"\"I'm not saying you're mad. You're clearly under huge strain and haven't slept. Whatever is going on, you need rest and help. Would you come into hospital for a few days?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is this because of the weed?\"","r":"\"Good question. Cannabis can trigger this, but you stopped a month ago and it's still going, so it may be an illness of its own. A urine test and time in hospital will help us tell.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Urine drug screen, FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, glucose and HbA1c, lipids, prolactin, weight, BP and ECG on admission","If first-episode psychosis is confirmed, aripiprazole 10 mg each morning, chosen with her; review response at 4-6 weeks","Warn about restlessness (akathisia) and sleep problems; short-term promethazine 25 mg at night if needed"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis, at least 16 one-to-one sessions, once settled","Forensic psychology formulation of the fire-setting: triggers, warning signs, safer coping"],"social":["Liaison and diversion worker updates police and CPS on her illness to inform charging decisions","Leaving-care personal adviser (statutory support to 25) and hostel manager involved in a safe placement plan","Early intervention in psychosis team referral, with a care coordinator"],"safety":["Not fit for interview now; written advice in the custody healthcare record","Close observation in custody until the MHA assessment; no lighters or matches on the ward","Fire-setting risk and warning signs written into her care plan before any return to the hostel"]},"facts":[{"h":"ABC every fire","x":"Antecedents (mood, voices, conflict, substances), Behaviour (what, where, how, alone, planned) and Consequences (alarm raised, stayed or fled, feelings after). Do it for this fire and any earlier ones."},{"h":"Pyromania is a diagnosis of exclusion","x":"It needs repeated deliberate fires, fascination with fire, and tension or arousal before with relief after, and no other motive such as psychosis, revenge or profit."},{"h":"Raising the alarm matters","x":"Shouting a warning, staying nearby and showing concern for others lower the immediate risk. Watching with excitement, fleeing unseen or planning escalate it."},{"h":"Appropriate adult for mental vulnerability","x":"Under PACE Code C, anyone who may be mentally vulnerable must have an appropriate adult present when the police interview them or carry out key procedures."},{"h":"MHA assessment can happen in custody","x":"If she needs admission and refuses, ask the AMHP. An AMHP and two doctors, one section 12 approved, can assess her in the custody suite for section 2."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"In 10 minutes: complete an ABC of the fire, find the motive, screen for illness, and advise custody."},"c-u-43":{"obj":["Established in under two minutes her difficulties, past treatment and why now, and checked current self-harm and suicidal thoughts","Stated that talking therapy, not medication, is the recommended treatment, and named one alternative therapy","Explained acceptance plus change and named all four DBT skill modules, each linked to one of her difficulties","Gave the structure: weekly individual session, weekly skills group, phone coaching, diary card, about 12 months, and the pre-treatment assessment","Gave a clear recommendation, answered the individual-only request honestly without promising it, and agreed a crisis plan and referral"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Would you actually recommend it for me, or are you just saying that?\"","r":"\"Fair to ask. Yes, I'd honestly recommend it. It was designed for fast-changing emotions, self-harm to cope and strained relationships, which is exactly what you've told me.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not doing a group. I'd die of embarrassment.\"","r":"\"Lots of people feel that at first. The group is more like a class: you learn skills, you don't share your story. I can ask about one-to-one, but I can't promise it.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"What if I mess it up? I always quit things.\"","r":"\"That worry makes sense. DBT expects slips and plans for them. The first weeks are about building your commitment, and your therapist helps you stick with it when it's hard.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can't I just have a better tablet instead?\"","r":"\"I understand wanting something quicker. Tablets haven't helped you, and no medicine treats this pattern directly. Therapy has the best evidence. We'll review your sertraline with your GP too.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I cut again last week. I didn't tell anyone.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you told me; it's not easy to say. Can I check the cut has healed, and whether that night you thought about killing yourself?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"A year? That's ages. Why so long?\"","r":"\"It is a commitment, you're right. Each of the four skills needs time to learn and practise in real life. Many people notice changes within the first few months.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No medication for the core difficulties (NICE); review sertraline 100 mg with GP: continue only if a separate depression, otherwise taper over 4 weeks","Avoid benzodiazepines; in a crisis, short-term promethazine 25 mg at night for up to one week","Small supplies of any tablets at home because of self-harm risk"],"psych":["DBT, about 12 months: weekly one-to-one (about an hour) plus weekly skills group (about 2 hours)","Four modules: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, interpersonal effectiveness","Daily diary card of urges, self-harm and spending, reviewed each session; phone coaching between sessions","Alternative if DBT is not suitable or available: mentalisation-based therapy"],"social":["Referral to the DBT service through the CMHT; named care coordinator while she waits","Debt advice through StepChange or Citizens Advice","Optional joint session with Jordan, with her consent"],"safety":["Written crisis plan: distraction skills, Jordan, crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, A&E for serious injury","Wound care advice; review at CMHT in 4 weeks or sooner if self-harm escalates"]},"facts":[{"h":"No drug for the core problem","x":"NICE advises against medication specifically for borderline personality disorder or its individual symptoms. Use medicines only for a separate condition, or short-term sedation in a crisis."},{"h":"Four modules, one line each","x":"Mindfulness: notice without judging. Distress tolerance: survive a crisis without making it worse. Emotion regulation: understand and calm feelings. Interpersonal effectiveness: ask for what you need and say no."},{"h":"Standard DBT has four parts","x":"Weekly individual therapy, a weekly skills group, telephone coaching between sessions, and a weekly consultation team for the therapists. Usually about 12 months."},{"h":"The diary card drives sessions","x":"Each day she records urges, self-harm, spending and skills used. Sessions start with it, and any self-harm is the first thing discussed."},{"h":"Commitment comes first","x":"DBT starts with a pre-treatment phase: assessment, agreeing goals, learning how DBT works, and committing to the programme and to working on self-harm."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"In 10 minutes: check risk, explain DBT's idea, four modules and structure, recommend it, and handle the group question."},"c-u-44":{"obj":["Characterised the voice: through the ears, recognised speaker, second person, abusive content, commands asked, onset and frequency, and his explanation","Excluded visual and tactile hallucinations and other psychotic features, and confirmed clear consciousness with an orientation and attention check","Quantified his drinking in units per day, the time of his last drink and at least three dependence features","Screened current withdrawal, Wernicke's signs, head injury, drugs and epilepsy","Assessed risk to the neighbour and on the road, advised no driving and DVLA notification, and explained the cause and admission plan"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"It's not in my head. He really said it. I heard him.\"","r":"\"I believe you heard it, as clearly as you hear me. That's what makes it so upsetting. Can I ask: was he actually there when you heard it?\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"I'm going to go round and sort him out.\"","r":"\"I can see how angry it's made you, being called those names. Going round could get you into real trouble, and he may not have said it. Can you agree not to go tonight?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"So I'm going mad, aren't I?\"","r":"\"That's a really frightening thought. No, you're not. Heavy drinking can cause voices like this while you're fully awake. It's recognised, and it usually settles with treatment.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Sian's had enough. This is all my fault.\"","r":"\"It's clear you care about her a lot. Blaming yourself won't help you get better, but getting treatment will. Would it help if I spoke to her too, with your OK?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I can't stop driving. It's my job.\"","r":"\"I know this is a huge worry, and it's your livelihood. But driving while dependent and hearing voices isn't safe, and the law says the DVLA must be told. We'll get you a fit note.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can't I just stop drinking at home?\"","r":"\"Good that you want to stop. But stopping suddenly at your level can cause fits or a dangerous confusion. It's much safer with medication and monitoring, so I'd like you to stay in.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Admit for medically assisted withdrawal: chlordiazepoxide reducing regimen, symptom-triggered top-ups guided by CIWA-Ar","Pabrinex IV before any glucose: one pair of ampoules daily for 3-5 days as prophylaxis (two to three pairs three times daily if Wernicke's suspected); then oral thiamine","Bloods: FBC (MCV), LFT, GGT, U&E, magnesium, phosphate, glucose, clotting; ECG if an antipsychotic is needed","If the voice persists or distress is severe after withdrawal is covered, short course of olanzapine 5 mg at night, reviewed daily","After detox: naltrexone 50 mg daily or acamprosate 666 mg three times daily to prevent relapse"],"psych":["Brief motivational interviewing on the ward to build his own reasons to stop","Community alcohol service relapse prevention, weekly for about 12 weeks; mutual aid such as AA or SMART Recovery"],"social":["Stop driving now; he notifies the DVLA; fit note for work","Alcohol care team review on the ward and community alcohol service referral before discharge","Sian offered carer support and Al-Anon, with his consent"],"safety":["Nurses: CIWA-Ar and observations hourly at first; escalate if seizure, confusion, visual hallucinations, fever or pulse over 120","Agreed not to confront the neighbour; review risk if the voice persists"]},"facts":[{"h":"Clear consciousness is the key","x":"Alcoholic hallucinosis happens with orientation and attention intact. If he is disoriented, cannot do months backwards, or sees things, think delirium tremens and treat as an emergency."},{"h":"Withdrawal timeline","x":"Tremor and sweating start 6-12 hours after the last drink; seizures peak at 24-48 hours; delirium tremens usually at 48-72 hours. Always record the last drink time."},{"h":"Thiamine before glucose","x":"Glucose without thiamine can tip a deficient person into Wernicke's encephalopathy. Give Pabrinex IV first. Watch for confusion, unsteady gait and eye movement problems."},{"h":"Units: the quick sums","x":"Units = volume in ml times ABV percent, divided by 1,000. 70 cl vodka at 40% = 28 units. A pint of 5% lager = about 2.8 units."},{"h":"DVLA and alcohol dependence","x":"Drivers with alcohol dependence must tell the DVLA and stop driving. For a car or van licence, it is usually refused until at least a year free from dependence."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"In 10 minutes: characterise the voice, confirm clear consciousness, quantify drinking, screen withdrawal and Wernicke's, address driving."},"c-u-45":{"obj":["Established core, biological and cognitive symptoms, duration and severity using her PHQ-9 score, and screened for psychosis and past highs","Asked about suicidal thoughts, plans and protective factors","Asked about all four IPT areas and named which are present (role transition, role dispute) and which are not","Checked suitability: outpatient, moderate severity, link between mood and relationships, motivation and weekly availability","Explained IPT's length and three phases, agreed one or two focus areas, and gave the referral route and a crisis line"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I'm a terrible daughter. I resent her for being here.\"","r":"\"That sounds like a really heavy feeling to carry. Resentment and love often sit side by side when caring takes over your life. It doesn't make you a bad daughter. It's exactly what IPT can help with.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Iain just thinks it's my job because she's my mum.\"","r":"\"That sounds frustrating, feeling it's all landed on you. Disagreements like that, about who does what, are one of the areas IPT works on directly.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How is talking about relationships going to fix depression?\"","r":"\"Good question. Mood and relationships feed each other. When a big change or conflict is sorted, mood often lifts. Studies show IPT works about as well as other talking therapies for depression.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Do I have to stop my tablets?\"","r":"\"No. They've started to help your sleep, so carry on. Therapy and tablets together often work better than either alone. Your GP will review the dose.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I can't leave Mum on her own every week.\"","r":"\"That's a real practical worry. Let's see who could cover. A carer's assessment from the council might also get some help at home, which would ease things anyway.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I used to sing every week. Now I can't be bothered with anything.\"","r":"\"It sounds like you've lost a part of yourself that mattered. That loss of enjoyment is a key sign of depression, and getting back to things like choir is part of recovery.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue sertraline 50 mg each morning; GP to review at 4 weeks and consider 100 mg if partial response","Continue for at least 6 months after recovery; warn about stopping suddenly","GP bloods if not done: FBC, TFT, B12, folate, to exclude physical contributors"],"psych":["IPT, about 12-16 weekly one-hour sessions through NHS Talking Therapies (high-intensity therapist)","Focus areas: role transition into caring and role dispute with her husband","Phases: map relationships first, work on the focus in the middle, plan for the future at the end; PHQ-9 at each session","Alternative if IPT is not available or she prefers: CBT, about 16 sessions"],"social":["Carer's assessment by the local authority (Care Act 2014): day centre or home care for her mother","Carers UK and the local carers' centre for support and benefits advice","Flexible working request to her employer; sister to cover weekly session time"],"safety":["If mood worsens or thoughts of self-harm appear: GP, NHS 111 option 2, or Samaritans 116 123","Review in clinic in 8 weeks; Talking Therapies to report PHQ-9 progress"]},"facts":[{"h":"The four IPT areas","x":"Grief (complicated bereavement), role disputes (conflict with someone important), role transitions (a major life change) and interpersonal deficits (long-standing isolation or difficulty forming relationships)."},{"h":"Pick one or two, not four","x":"IPT is time-limited. Map all four areas, then agree one or two focus areas linked to the onset of the depression."},{"h":"Who suits IPT best","x":"Outpatients with mild to moderate depression, a clear interpersonal trigger, no psychosis or bipolar disorder, and the motivation to attend weekly."},{"h":"PHQ-9 bands","x":"0-4 minimal, 5-9 mild, 10-14 moderate, 15-19 moderately severe, 20-27 severe. Always check item 9 separately for thoughts of death or self-harm."},{"h":"IPT in three phases","x":"Initial: interpersonal inventory and agreeing focus. Middle: work on the focus areas. Ending: review gains, plan for warning signs. Usually 12-16 weekly sessions."}],"type":"Therapy assessment","do":"In 10 minutes: assess depression and risk, check all four IPT areas, judge suitability, and agree a referral."},"c-u-46":{"obj":["Recorded every feature she dislikes, the age it began and a number for daily preoccupation in hours","Rated her insight (good or fair, poor, or absent) by asking whether others might see her differently","Listed at least three safety behaviours and their effect on work and dating","Asked about cosmetic consultations, plans to change her eyes herself and suicidal thoughts, and stated a risk level","Screened for depression, social anxiety, OCD, an eating disorder and psychosis, named body dysmorphic disorder and agreed one next step"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Just look at me. You can see it, can't you?\"","r":"\"I can see how much this is hurting you, and I'm not going to argue about it. What I'd like to understand is how much of your life it's taking up.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you saying I'm imagining it? That I'm mad?\"","r":"\"Not at all. You're not mad and you're not vain. Many people feel exactly this way about a feature, and the worry itself is something we know how to treat.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"If I had the surgery, all this would stop, wouldn't it?\"","r":"\"I understand why that feels like the answer. Sadly, most people with this kind of worry still feel the same afterwards, or the worry moves somewhere else. There's treatment that works better, and I'd like to tell you about it.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Some days I don't see the point in anything. I'm so tired of it.\"","r":"\"That sounds exhausting, and I'm glad you said it. I need to ask more: has it got as far as thinking about suicide, or making plans?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've watched videos of people taping their eyes, or doing more. I've thought about it.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you told me, because that matters for your safety. Have you bought anything or tried anything? What has stopped you so far?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want tablets. It's my face that's the problem, not my head.\"","r":"\"That's fair, and you don't have to decide today. Talking therapy is the main treatment and works well on its own for many people. Medicine is an option we can talk about later.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Fluoxetine 20 mg each morning if she agrees; review at 2 weeks for agitation and suicidal thoughts; BDD often needs higher doses, up to 60 mg, over 12 weeks","No routine bloods needed for fluoxetine; warn about nausea, early restlessness and sexual side effects"],"psych":["CBT including exposure and response prevention for BDD, from a specialist OCD/BDD service or NHS Talking Therapies","Early targets: cut mirror and phone checks, go out without the tinted glasses in graded steps"],"social":["GP to note BDD and avoid onward cosmetic or ophthalmic referrals while treatment starts","BDD Foundation for information and peer support"],"safety":["Crisis plan: NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123, 999 if in danger; ask about suicidal thoughts at every contact","Advise her not to attempt any procedure on herself; agree she will call the team if the urge grows"]},"facts":[{"h":"Insight decides the label, not the diagnosis","x":"ICD-11 codes BDD with fair-to-good or poor-to-absent insight. Even a belief held with full conviction stays BDD; it does not become delusional disorder."},{"h":"Surgery rarely helps BDD","x":"Most people with BDD who have cosmetic procedures report no improvement, or the concern moves to another feature. Advise against procedures until treated."},{"h":"Ask the self-surgery question","x":"'What will you do if no one will operate?' is the key risk question. Do-it-yourself procedures cause real injury and need a safety plan."},{"h":"Suicide risk is high","x":"Suicidal thoughts are common in BDD and attempts are markedly raised compared with the general population. Ask directly at every review."},{"h":"Treatment is CBT with ERP, or an SSRI","x":"NICE CG31: CBT with ERP, or an SSRI such as fluoxetine; offer both when impairment is severe. Judge the SSRI only after 12 weeks at a good dose."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"In 10 minutes, map her concern, conviction, rituals, surgery plans and suicide risk, then name the diagnosis."},"c-u-48":{"obj":["Asked about adherence, side effects, alcohol and at least three hypothyroid symptoms, and arranged TFTs","Asked directly about suicidal thoughts and recorded the answer","Recommended venlafaxine 225 mg daily as the next step, with BP today and at 2 weeks","Offered CBT alongside medication and named augmentation as the fallback, with ECT only if all else fails","Checked his understanding by teach-back and booked a review within 4 weeks with a crisis route"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"I've done everything the GP asked. Why isn't anything working?\"","r":"\"That's really frustrating after six months. You've done all the right things, and the venlafaxine is starting to help. Let's work out why the rest hasn't followed and what comes next.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is this it? Am I always going to feel like this?\"","r":"\"I can hear how worn down you are. Most people in your position do get better with the next steps. You've recovered three times before, and that matters.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why not just switch to something new?\"","r":"\"Good question. You've had some benefit from venlafaxine, so building on it usually works better than starting again. A higher dose is the next step.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will a higher dose mean more side effects?\"","r":"\"It can. Sweating and nausea may increase for a week or two, and blood pressure can rise, so we'll check it. If it's not tolerable, we can step back.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"My mate had electric shock treatment. Is that next for me?\"","r":"\"I understand that worry. ECT is a very effective treatment, but it's for severe depression when other things haven't worked. We're nowhere near that for you.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I can't take time off for therapy. I'll lose my job.\"","r":"\"Keeping your job matters, and work is often good for recovery. Sessions can be early or by video, and occupational health can help with flexible hours.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["TFTs, U&E, FBC today; BP and pulse today","If TFTs normal: venlafaxine XL 225 mg each morning with food; BP at 2 weeks and after any further increase; review response at 4-6 weeks","Warn: sweating, nausea, insomnia, raised BP; never stop suddenly (discontinuation symptoms)","If no response at 225 mg: add aripiprazole 2-5 mg (off-label) or lithium (aim for a level of 0.4-1.0 mmol/L; TFTs, U&E, calcium first); avoid mirtazapine first given his weight concern","If TFTs low: GP to start levothyroxine; review mood once euthyroid before further changes"],"psych":["CBT via NHS Talking Therapies, 16-20 sessions, alongside medication","Behavioural activation now: short bike rides, scheduled pleasant activities"],"social":["Occupational health referral (with consent) for flexible start and reduced load for 4-6 weeks","Involve his partner in the plan if he agrees"],"safety":["Crisis route: NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans 116 123 if suicidal thoughts appear","Clinic review in 4 weeks; specialist mood service, rTMS or ECT discussion only if further steps fail"]},"facts":[{"h":"Two failed drugs defines resistance","x":"Treatment-resistant depression usually means no adequate response to two antidepressants from different classes, each taken properly for 4-6 weeks."},{"h":"Check the four reasons first","x":"Before adding drugs, check adherence, alcohol and drugs, physical illness such as hypothyroidism, and ongoing stress or another diagnosis."},{"h":"Venlafaxine raises blood pressure","x":"BNF: measure BP before and during titration, especially at higher doses. Avoid in uncontrolled hypertension or high-risk arrhythmia. Get an ECG if cardiac risk factors."},{"h":"Partial response: raise the dose","x":"NICE NG222: with some response and good tolerability, increasing the dose is a reasonable next step before switching or adding a second drug."},{"h":"Combined treatment works best","x":"For depression that has not responded fully, NICE advises combining medication with a high-intensity psychological therapy such as CBT."}],"type":"Explain a treatment + Management plan","do":"In 10 minutes, rule out reasons for non-response, then recommend one next step with monitoring and therapy."},"c-u-49":{"obj":["Set up the room safely before starting (colleague present, alarm, clear exit) and stated the limits of confidentiality","Recorded how he met her, what he believes she feels, his evidence and whether the belief is fixed","Asked what he knows about her and what he has done (waiting, following, messages, social media)","Asked directly what he will do if refused contact and whether he has access to weapons","Stated a risk level and named at least four actions: consultant, police, the nurse and her manager, incident report"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Where is she? I'm not leaving until I see her.\"","r":"\"I can see how much this matters to you, and I want to hear about it. Let's sit somewhere quieter so we can talk properly.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You don't believe me, do you? You think I'm making it up.\"","r":"\"I believe these feelings are very real and very strong for you. I'd like to understand what makes you so sure, so tell me more.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Just give her a note from me. That's all I'm asking.\"","r":"\"I know that feels like a small thing to ask. I can't do that, Lewis; it wouldn't be fair to her. What I can do is help with how much this is taking over your life.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've waited outside her ward a couple of times. Just to see her.\"","r":"\"Thank you for being honest about that. What happened those times? Did you speak to her, or follow her when she left?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"People will have to notice me. I'll do whatever it takes.\"","r":"\"That sounds like a lot of anger. I need to ask clearly: what do you mean by 'whatever it takes'? Are you thinking of hurting anyone?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"She's the only person who's ever been kind to me.\"","r":"\"That sounds really lonely, and I'm sorry. It makes sense that her kindness meant so much. I'd like to help you find support that doesn't depend on one person.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["If acutely agitated: offer oral lorazepam 1 mg (NICE NG10); avoid parenteral unless needed and with monitoring","Urine drug screen if he agrees; physical observations","If erotomanic delusional disorder confirmed later: antipsychotic (e.g. aripiprazole 10 mg daily) with baseline weight, BP, glucose, lipids, prolactin and ECG"],"psych":["Consultant review tonight; MHA assessment via AMHP if he refuses assessment and risk remains high","Collateral from GP and the A&E record from three months ago"],"social":["Nurse's line manager and A&E nurse in charge informed now; escort to her car; consider shift change","Police informed; they can advise the nurse on a stalking protection order","Incident report (Datix) and security log; receptionist offered debrief"],"safety":["Never interview alone; alarm room; you nearest the exit; security outside","Call 999 if he produces a weapon or leaves stating intent to find her"]},"facts":[{"h":"Actions predict risk more than feelings","x":"Knowing her address or shifts, waiting, following and repeated contact raise risk far more than the intensity of love he describes."},{"h":"Rejection is the danger point","x":"Anger and violence in erotomania most often follow rejection or a perceived rival. Always ask how he will react if she refuses contact."},{"h":"Ask about weapons, every time","x":"Ask what he carries now and what he has at home. A veiled threat plus weapon access means police involvement today."},{"h":"Disclosure to protect is lawful","x":"GMC guidance allows disclosure without consent when needed to protect others from serious harm. Share the minimum necessary and record your reasons."},{"h":"Stalking protection orders exist","x":"Under the Stalking Protection Act 2019, police can apply for an order restricting contact. Point the nurse to the police for advice."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"In 10 minutes, assess the belief, his actions, rejection response and weapons, then grade risk and act."},"c-u-50":{"obj":["Recorded why lorazepam started, the current dose and timing, and at least two features of dependence","Screened mood, suicidal thoughts, alcohol, other sedatives or opioids, and physical health","Told her she is dependent and gave at least two reasons to reduce that apply to her","Agreed a written taper: diazepam equivalent, step size, interval, never stopping suddenly, in the community","Named follow-up: single prescriber, review in 2 weeks, CBT referral, and when to seek urgent help"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"Why can't I just stay on it? It works for me.\"","r":"\"That's a fair question, and it has helped you. The trouble is your body has got used to it, so it's doing less while making you tired and foggy. A slow reduction often leaves people feeling clearer.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I can't face feeling like that again. The anxiety was unbearable.\"","r":"\"That sounds like a really hard time, and I don't want you back there. We'll go slowly, you'll start therapy for anxiety, and we can pause any step that feels too much.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Would I have to go into hospital to stop?\"","r":"\"No. You're well, you have support at home, and a slow reduction is safest done in your own life. We'll see you regularly instead.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How do you know I won't just get anxious again?\"","r":"\"I can't promise you won't feel some anxiety. If it returns, we'll treat it properly, with CBT and, if needed, a medicine like sertraline that isn't habit-forming.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I'm so busy. I don't have time for withdrawal symptoms.\"","r":"\"That's understandable. The steps are small so most people keep working. You choose when each step happens, so you can avoid busy weeks.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I feel like an addict. I only took what the doctor gave me.\"","r":"\"You've done nothing wrong. You took it exactly as prescribed. Dependence is a known effect of the medicine, not a failing on your part.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Switch lorazepam 1 mg QDS to diazepam 10 mg QDS over about a week, one dose at a time (1 mg lorazepam ≈ 10 mg diazepam)","Then reduce by about a tenth of the daily dose every 2-4 weeks (e.g. 2 mg steps, slowing to 1 mg then 0.5 mg near the end); expect several months","Never stop suddenly; hold a step if withdrawal is marked; avoid alcohol, opioids and other sedatives","If anxiety disorder re-emerges: sertraline 25-50 mg daily (up to 200 mg) per NICE GAD guidance; no new benzodiazepine or z-drug"],"psych":["CBT for generalised anxiety via NHS Talking Therapies, starting early in the taper","Withdrawal and anxiety diary to show symptoms rise and settle after each step"],"social":["GP letter with the full schedule; weekly scripts from the GP only","Plan steps around her work calendar; partner informed if she agrees"],"safety":["Seek urgent help (999) for a fit, confusion or hallucinations; NHS 111 option 2 for a mental health crisis","Do not drive if drowsy, especially during the switch week; review in 2 weeks, then every 2-4 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Lorazepam 1 mg is diazepam 10 mg","x":"BNF approximate equivalence. Diazepam's long half-life smooths levels between doses, so switching from short-acting lorazepam usually makes a taper easier."},{"h":"Small steps, her pace","x":"NICE CKS: reduce gradually, about 5-10% of the dose every 2-4 weeks, guided by symptoms. Pausing is fine; going back up should be rare."},{"h":"Abrupt stopping can cause fits","x":"After long-term use, sudden withdrawal can cause seizures, confusion and severe anxiety. Never stop or sharply cut a long-standing benzodiazepine."},{"h":"Benzodiazepines plus opioids can kill","x":"MHRA warns that combining benzodiazepines with opioids risks profound sedation and fatal breathing suppression. Ask about painkillers and alcohol."},{"h":"Community taper is the norm","x":"Most people with prescribed benzodiazepine dependence taper safely at home. Inpatient withdrawal is for very high doses, mixed substance use or serious health problems."}],"type":"Medication review + Management plan","do":"In 10 minutes, confirm dependence, screen risk, and agree a written gradual taper she feels in control of."},"c-u-52":{"obj":["Characterised the voices (onset, number, content, location, no commands) and screened for persecutory ideas, passivity, mood and sleep","Identified missed olanzapine doses with his reasons (grogginess, weight) and recent skunk use, without judgement","Screened stress, sleep, smoking and physical triggers, and compared this with his last relapse and early warning signs","Asked about thoughts of harming himself or others, command voices and driving","Named a likely relapse with its causes and agreed one medication change, follow-up within a week and a crisis route"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"Leah worries too much. I'm fine.\"","r":"\"It sounds like she cares a lot. I'd still like to hear it from you, because you know best what's been going on. How have the last few weeks been?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've been skipping it on work nights. I can't get up at five feeling like that.\"","r":"\"Thanks for being honest; lots of people do the same. Feeling groggy before an early shift is a real problem. Let's look at how to make the tablet fit your life.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"I've put on a stone on that stuff. Would you take it?\"","r":"\"That's a fair point, and weight gain is a common reason people stop. It matters to me too. There are options, including other medicines that affect weight less. Can we talk about them?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Skunk calms me down. It's not the problem.\"","r":"\"I can see why it feels calming. The tricky thing is that strong cannabis tends to bring voices back in people with your illness. Would you be open to looking at that with some support?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I don't want to end up in hospital again.\"","r":"\"I understand; last time was hard. We've caught this early, and the plan is to treat it at home with more support. Staying on the tablet is the best way to keep it that way.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Are you going to tell Leah all this?\"","r":"\"Not without your say-so. It might help if she knows your early warning signs and the crisis number, so she can help. What would you be happy for me to share?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Olanzapine every night at 7 pm from tonight to ease morning grogginess; consultant to consider 10 mg at review if voices persist","If weight stays the barrier, discuss switching to aripiprazole (start 10 mg daily) with weight, BMI and waist review at 12 weeks","If doses keep being missed, offer a long-acting injection, e.g. aripiprazole 400 mg monthly once oral tolerability is shown, with 14 days of oral overlap","Bloods today: FBC, U&E, LFT, HbA1c, lipids; weight and BMI; urine drug screen with consent","Smoking: tobacco (including in joints) lowers olanzapine levels; if he stops, review dose and sedation"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis, at least 16 sessions, psychology team","Relapse-signature work with his care coordinator: his own early warning signs written down","Family intervention with Leah, at least 10 planned sessions over 3-12 months","Motivational interviewing about skunk, linked to his goal of staying out of hospital"],"social":["Care coordinator to see him weekly during the relapse","Referral to the local drug and alcohol service, which he can accept or decline","Talk to his manager or occupational health about shift times if early starts remain a barrier","Carers' assessment for Leah from the local authority; crisis plan shared with his consent"],"safety":["Crisis plan: CMHT duty line in hours; NHS 111 option 2 out of hours; 999 if anyone is in immediate danger","Not to drive while unwell; home treatment team if symptoms worsen; review in 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Normalise before you ask about adherence","x":"Patients over-report adherence. Ask 'How many nights a week do you miss?' after saying most people miss some. Check pharmacy collection records too."},{"h":"Cannabis drives relapse","x":"Continuing cannabis after a psychotic illness, especially high-potency skunk, is linked with more relapses and admissions. Explore it with motivational interviewing, not a lecture."},{"h":"Smoke speeds up olanzapine clearance","x":"Tobacco smoke induces CYP1A2 and lowers olanzapine and clozapine levels. Stopping smoking can raise levels and side effects; review the dose."},{"h":"Move the dose earlier, not out","x":"Morning hangover from olanzapine often eases if the dose is taken early in the evening, around 7 pm, instead of at bedtime."},{"h":"Long-acting injections need an oral trial","x":"Before aripiprazole depot, confirm he tolerates oral aripiprazole. After the first 400 mg injection, continue oral aripiprazole for 14 days."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"In ten minutes, confirm relapse symptoms and risk, identify adherence, skunk and stress as causes, and agree next steps."},"c-u-53":{"obj":["Explained his role and the limits of confidentiality before asking about the knife","Asked directly what he intended with the knife and what he would have done if they denied the affair","Established the evidence, duration and fixity of the jealous belief and at least three checking or controlling behaviours","Recorded past violence to Donna and previous partners, police contact, brief alcohol use, weapons at home, children and suicidal thoughts","Told him plainly that the risk was high and named the next steps: custody sergeant informed, MHA assessment, no contact with Donna, safeguarding"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why do I need a psychiatrist? My wife is the problem, not me.\"","r":"\"I can hear you're angry, and today's been a lot. I'm not here to judge. I want to understand what's been going on between you and Donna, from your side.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You don't believe me either. Nobody does.\"","r":"\"It sounds lonely to feel nobody believes you. I'm not here to argue about it. Tell me what has made you so sure.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I only took the knife to scare him off.\"","r":"\"Thanks for telling me straight. I need to understand that properly. If he'd laughed at you, or Donna denied it, what do you think would have happened?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I haven't slept properly in months. I can't stop thinking about it.\"","r":"\"That sounds exhausting, and it must be wearing you down. When it's that bad, some people start to feel life isn't worth it. Has that crossed your mind?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Are you going to tell the police what I said?\"","r":"\"I'll be honest with you. Because of the knife and what you've told me, I'm worried someone could be hurt, so I do need to share that with the custody team. I'd rather you knew.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"As soon as I'm out I'm going to get the truth out of her.\"","r":"\"I can see how badly you need an answer. Going to her now could end with someone hurt and you in far more trouble. That's why I'm asking for more help for you tonight.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Full assessment in hospital: physical health check, bloods (FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, B12, glucose); brain imaging if neurological signs or atypical features","If delusional disorder is confirmed: antipsychotic, e.g. risperidone 1 mg at night, titrated to 2-4 mg over 1-2 weeks; baseline weight, prolactin and ECG","Treat co-existing depression and alcohol misuse, which add to risk"],"psych":["Structured violence risk assessment (e.g. HCR-20) by the forensic or inpatient team","Individual CBT for jealous thinking once more settled; no couples therapy while risk to Donna remains"],"social":["Custody sergeant and officer in the case informed; police to contact Donna, complete DASH and refer to MARAC; IDVA for Donna","Children's social care referral for Ellie, 14","AMHP to arrange Mental Health Act assessment in custody","Appropriate adult for interview; liaison and diversion report to court if charged"],"safety":["No contact with Donna or her manager; police to consider bail conditions and removing knives and blades from the home","Close observation in custody for suicide risk; custody nurse informed"]},"facts":[{"h":"Ask the 'what if they denied it' question","x":"Intent is the core of this assessment. Ask why he took the knife and what he would have done if confronted with denial. 'I don't know' is a worrying answer."},{"h":"Morbid jealousy can be lethal","x":"Delusional jealousy is a recognised risk factor for serious violence and homicide of a partner, and sometimes of the supposed rival. Past violence to partners raises the risk further."},{"h":"Checking behaviours signal escalation","x":"Phone checking, smelling clothes, following and night-time interrogation show rising preoccupation and controlling behaviour, which is itself an offence."},{"h":"MARAC protects the partner","x":"Police or professionals complete the DASH checklist; high-risk cases go to MARAC, where agencies share information and an IDVA supports the victim."},{"h":"Separation is often the safest step","x":"In morbid jealousy, keeping the couple apart is often the only reliable protection while treatment starts. Never offer couples therapy while risk is high."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"In ten minutes, assess his jealous beliefs, intent with the knife, past violence and current risk, then state next steps."},"c-u-54":{"obj":["Reassured her that Leo was safe and established a timeline: well for about four weeks, then days of little sleep and rapid change","Elicited the belief about Leo, the voice and manic or perplexed features, and screened fever, headache, blood pressure and substances","Asked about family history and identified her mother's admission after childbirth","Asked directly about harming herself or Leo, acting on the voice, self-neglect and leaving with Leo","Named likely postpartum psychosis, said it is treatable, and stated next steps: Leo with paediatrics, supervised contact, mother and baby unit, Kwame involved"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Where's Leo? Is he safe?\"","r":"\"Leo is just next door with the children's nurses, and he's being looked after. It's clear he means the world to you. Can we talk for a few minutes, then I'll update you on him?\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"There's nothing wrong with me. He doesn't need milk; he's pure light.\"","r":"\"I can hear how strongly you feel that, and I'm not going to argue. What helped you to be sure of it?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"A lady's voice tells me to keep him from food so he stays pure.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that helps me understand. When she speaks, how does it make you feel? And has she ever told you to do anything else?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Am I going to lose him? What happens to me now?\"","r":"\"That's a frightening thought, and it's natural to worry about it. Our aim is to keep you and Leo together and safe. There are special units where mums are treated with their babies.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I just want to take him home now.\"","r":"\"I understand you want to be home with him. Right now I'm worried you're both not safe at home. I'd like you to stay while we get you somewhere you can be together.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Are you saying I'm a bad mother?\"","r":"\"Not at all. This is an illness. It isn't anyone's fault and any mother could get it. You've done nothing wrong. Getting help now is the best thing for you both.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Now: temperature, pulse, BP, urine dip for protein and infection; bloods FBC, CRP, U&E, LFT, calcium, glucose, TFT; CT or MRI head if headache, seizures or focal signs","Olanzapine 5 mg at night, increasing to 10 mg if needed; compatible with breastfeeding with the baby watched for drowsiness (check with the perinatal pharmacist)","Lorazepam 0.5-1 mg as needed for severe agitation or to restore sleep, short term only","Avoid valproate (MHRA: not for women who could become pregnant unless a pregnancy prevention programme is in place); later mood stabiliser choice with the perinatal team"],"psych":["Calm, simple explanation to Amara and Kwame: what postpartum psychosis is and that most women recover fully","Mother-baby interaction work with nursery nurses on the unit, building confidence in feeding and care","Later, perinatal psychology to process the experience and pre-conception planning for any future pregnancy"],"social":["Perinatal team to find a mother and baby unit bed today; informal admission if she agrees, Mental Health Act assessment if not","Hospital safeguarding team and children's social care referral; health visitor and GP informed","Kwame: paternity leave, family help; Action on Postpartum Psychosis peer support"],"safety":["Leo stays with paediatrics; supervised contact only until she is well","1:1 nursing in A&E; security aware of absconding risk"]},"facts":[{"h":"Postpartum psychosis is an emergency","x":"About 1 to 2 in 1,000 births; most start within the first two weeks. NICE: sudden symptoms after birth need a specialist assessment within 4 hours."},{"h":"Family history is a major warning","x":"A mother or sister with postpartum psychosis or bipolar disorder sharply raises a woman's own risk. Always ask, and record it for any future pregnancy."},{"h":"Rule out the physical first","x":"Check temperature, BP and urine: infection, postpartum pre-eclampsia (up to six weeks), thyroiditis and venous sinus thrombosis can all cause postnatal confusion or psychosis."},{"h":"The risk is delusional neglect","x":"Most mothers with postpartum psychosis have no wish to harm their baby. The danger is acting on beliefs, like not feeding. Ask whether she would follow the voice."},{"h":"Mother and baby unit by default","x":"NICE: women needing admission within 12 months of birth should usually go to a mother and baby unit with their baby."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"In ten minutes, elicit postpartum psychosis features, screen physical causes, assess risk to her and Leo, and explain next steps."},"c-u-56":{"obj":["Recorded a finding for appearance, speech, mood, thought, perception, cognition and insight, including the grandiose delusion and the confirming voice","Checked orientation and attention and named delirium as the key physical differential, with obs and named bloods","Graded risk to Ellis (neglect, leaving with him), to herself (asked about suicide) and to staff (suspicion)","Told her clearly she should stay, without agreeing to her leaving with or without Ellis","Agreed a named plan: Ellis supervised by midwives, 1:1 for Leanne, perinatal consultant called today, MHA route if she leaves"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't need a psychiatrist. I need to get him home.\"","r":"\"I hear how much you want to be home with him. I'm here because the midwives care about you both. Give me a few minutes so I understand what's going on for you.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You don't believe me about Ellis, do you?\"","r":"\"I can see how strongly you feel it, and I want to understand it. What I'm noticing is that you've hardly slept, and that worries me for you.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"The nurses keep watching him. They want to take him.\"","r":"\"That must feel frightening. Nobody here is planning to take him from you. They're watching because they want him fed and safe, and you rested.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why won't anyone let me go home?\"","r":"\"Because I think you're unwell right now, and it's an illness that can follow birth and does get better with treatment. Leaving today wouldn't be safe for you or Ellis.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Everyone thinks I'm a bad mother.\"","r":"\"I don't think that at all. You love him, that's clear. Being unwell isn't your fault, and our job is to get you well so you can care for him.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"If my partner watches me at home, can I go?\"","r":"\"I'm glad he's there for you. But this needs specialist care, round the clock, with Ellis beside you. I'd like him to come in so we can talk to him too.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Today: temperature, pulse, BP, urine dip for protein and infection; FBC, CRP, U&E, LFT, calcium, glucose, TFT. Review delivery drugs.","Olanzapine 5 mg at night, agreed with the perinatal consultant, rising to 10 mg as needed; can be used while breastfeeding with infant monitoring","Lorazepam 0.5-1 mg orally as needed for agitation or to restore sleep, short term only","No valproate (pregnancy prevention rules); lithium is a specialist decision and not advised if breastfeeding"],"psych":["Calm, low-stimulus side room; one consistent midwife and nurse","Brief, clear explanation of postpartum psychosis to Leanne and her partner, repeated once she is calmer","Supervised time with Ellis to protect bonding; perinatal psychology later to plan future pregnancies"],"social":["Perinatal consultant: mother and baby unit bed today","Children's social care referral today for a safety plan for Ellis; maternity safeguarding midwife informed","Partner contacted with her knowledge; GP, health visitor and community midwife informed in writing"],"safety":["Ellis never alone with Leanne; midwives feed him and document every feed","1:1 nursing for Leanne; ward and security aware she may try to leave with Ellis","If she leaves: police informed at once; Mental Health Act assessment arranged","Review again before 16:00"]},"facts":[{"h":"Onset is fast and early","x":"Postpartum psychosis usually starts in the first two weeks after birth and can escalate within hours. Treat it as a psychiatric emergency: same-day specialist review."},{"h":"Mother and baby unit, not general ward","x":"NICE CG192: a woman needing admission within 12 months of birth should normally go to a specialist mother and baby unit, unless there are specific reasons against it."},{"h":"Rule out delirium first","x":"Infection, eclampsia, haemorrhage and drugs can cause acute confusion after birth. Check orientation, obs, urine and bloods before calling it purely psychiatric."},{"h":"Family history raises the risk","x":"A personal or family history of bipolar disorder or postpartum psychosis greatly raises the risk. Ask, and record it for any future pregnancy."},{"h":"Section 5(2) works on any ward","x":"It applies to any hospital in-patient, including maternity. The doctor in charge of her care or their nominated deputy signs it; it lasts up to 72 hours."}],"type":"Mental state examination + Risk assessment","do":"In 10 minutes, record a finding per MSE domain, grade risk to Ellis, and keep both safely on the ward."},"c-u-57":{"obj":["Explained confidentiality and its limits before asking about the overdose","Established the planning, setting, concealment, help-seeking and intent of the overdose","Named at least three causes: the bullying and its online spread, depressive symptoms, and limited support","Asked about current suicidal thoughts, self-harm, substances and safeguarding","Agreed a named plan: medicines locked away, safety plan with mum, school safeguarding lead, CAMHS within 48 hours"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I've told the nurses everything already.\"","r":"\"That must be tiring, sorry. I'm the person who helps plan what happens next, so I need to hear it from you. We'll keep it short.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Please don't tell my mum about the photos.\"","r":"\"I understand you're scared about that. I won't share more than I need to. But keeping you safe means mum needs to know some things. Can we decide together what to say?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"It's my fault. I should've just ignored them.\"","r":"\"It's not your fault. What they did is bullying, and it's wrong. Anyone would struggle with that every day, at school and on their phone.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't go back there. Everyone will know.\"","r":"\"That sounds terrifying. You won't be going back to the same situation. We'll talk to school before you return.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So what happens now? Do I have to go back to school?\"","r":"\"Good question. Not straight away. Today we make a plan with you and mum, someone from CAMHS sees you within a couple of days, and school's safeguarding lead gets involved before you go back.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm fine now. I just want to go home.\"","r":"\"I'm really glad you're feeling better. Going home is the plan. I just want to make sure home feels safe for you first.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Paediatric team confirms LFTs and INR have recovered before discharge","No medication for self-harm itself","If moderate to severe depression persists, CAMHS may add fluoxetine 10 mg daily alongside therapy, rising to 20 mg after a week if needed; review weekly at first for agitation or new suicidal thoughts"],"psych":["CAMHS follow-up within 48 hours of discharge","Psychological therapy for depression (CBT, or IPT for adolescents) with family sessions","DBT for adolescents if self-harm recurs"],"social":["School designated safeguarding lead contacted on the first school day; anti-bullying plan and phased return","Report the images to the platform and get police advice; Report Remove (Childline and IWF) if any are sexual","Childline 0800 1111 and Papyrus HOPELINE247 0800 068 4141 given to Chloe","Children's social care referral if any abuse or exploitation is disclosed"],"safety":["Mum locks away all medicines, including paracetamol, and removes sharps","Written safety plan: triggers, things that help, people to contact, crisis line, 999 if in immediate danger","Copy of plan to GP and CAMHS; paediatric team informed of the outcome"]},"facts":[{"h":"No risk scores for discharge","x":"NICE NG225 advises against risk tools or scales for predicting suicide or deciding who goes home. Base the decision on a full psychosocial assessment and a plan."},{"h":"Ambivalence isn't low intent","x":"Contacting someone after an overdose shows mixed feelings. Judge intent by planning, concealment, what she expected and what she did beforehand."},{"h":"Pack limits were bypassed","x":"Buying from more than one shop to get around sales limits shows planning. Record it as evidence of intent."},{"h":"Online abuse is everywhere","x":"Bullying on social media follows the young person home. Ask about images; sexual images of a child are a police and safeguarding matter."},{"h":"Fluoxetine is the only first-line SSRI","x":"NICE NG134: for moderate to severe depression in 12-18s, fluoxetine with psychological therapy, started by CAMHS. Warn about early agitation and new suicidal thoughts."}],"type":"Risk assessment + History to a diagnosis","do":"In 10 minutes, take the overdose history and its causes, assess current risk, and agree a discharge safety plan."},"c-u-58":{"obj":["Established where the belief came from and whether his church and family share it","Tested conviction with at least one alternative view and recorded how he responded","Asked about voices, passivity, thought interference, ideas of reference, mood and substances","Assessed risk, including refused care for himself or his child and anger at non-believers","Stated an impression of an overvalued idea, not psychosis, with reasons, and agreed a blood test plan with the GP"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Most people laugh at me.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry that's happened. I'm not here to laugh. I'd like to understand how you see it.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So do you think I'm mad?\"","r":"\"No. I see no sign of a mental illness. This is a firmly held belief that makes sense within your faith, and others share it.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"My GP sent me to a psychiatrist for my religion.\"","r":"\"I can see why that felt insulting. Your GP was unsure and wanted to be careful. My job is to make sure nothing's being missed, and so far nothing is.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What about my blood tests? I'm not touching those tubes.\"","r":"\"That makes sense, given how you feel. The test is about your health. I'll ask your GP whether the nurse can label the tube after you leave.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I do worry about it more than I'd like.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. If the worry starts taking over your days, there's talking help through your GP. Would you like that information?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You're not religious, are you? You won't get it.\"","r":"\"I may not share your faith, but I respect it. You can help me understand it, and that's what matters today.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No antipsychotic: no psychotic illness","GP bloods to go ahead with an adjustment: tube labelled out of his sight","Routine physical health care continues with his consent"],"psych":["Plain explanation of a strong belief versus an illness","NHS Talking Therapies (self-referral) if the worry becomes distressing","Re-assess if new symptoms appear: voices, wider suspicion, or the belief spreads"],"social":["Letter to GP today with impression and suggested adjustment","Discharge from CMHT with a clear route back through the GP","Support from his minister or church if he wishes"],"safety":["Seek help through the GP, or NHS 111 option 2 in a crisis, if beliefs widen or he feels unsafe","GP to note any future refusal of essential care for his daughter"]},"facts":[{"h":"Delusion versus overvalued idea","x":"A delusion is held with absolute conviction, is not explained by culture and resists all evidence. An overvalued idea is understandable and shared, but held beyond reason and drives behaviour."},{"h":"Shared beliefs are not delusions","x":"If his church teaches it and others hold it, it is culturally congruent. Ask the community question before deciding anything."},{"h":"Test conviction by linking","x":"Use his own words: 'Tubes help diagnose illness. Could some barcodes not be linked to evil?' A person who can consider it is unlikely to be deluded."},{"h":"Screen beyond the belief","x":"Psychosis rarely gives one isolated symptom. Ask about voices, passivity, thought interference and ideas of reference before you decide."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Explain a diagnosis","do":"In 10 minutes, explore the belief, test its conviction, screen for psychosis, and state your impression with reasons."},"c-u-59":{"obj":["Established onset, duration and at least five symptoms of depression, including guilt and hopelessness","Covered Isha's birth, bonding, this pregnancy and a family history of postnatal illness","Screened for mania and psychosis, including beliefs about Isha being ill","Graded risk to herself, to Isha and to the pregnancy after asking about each directly","Agreed a plan naming treatment, the midwife, health visitor and GP, and a crisis line"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I'm a terrible mother. Isha deserves better.\"","r":"\"I'm so sorry you're feeling that. Feeling like a bad mother is one of the commonest signs of depression. It's not the truth about you, and it can get better.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I tell you I've had dark thoughts, will you take Isha away?\"","r":"\"I understand why you'd worry. Having thoughts doesn't mean you'll act on them, and many mums have them. Our job is to support you and keep you together.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is there anything I can take while I'm pregnant?\"","r":"\"Yes. Sertraline is used a lot in pregnancy. There are small risks, like the baby being jittery for a few days after birth, and there are risks from untreated depression too. Let's weigh it together.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I didn't even want this pregnancy. What kind of person thinks that?\"","r":"\"An honest one, who's exhausted. Mixed feelings about an unplanned pregnancy are very common. Thank you for telling me.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Sometimes I think it would be easier if I didn't wake up.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you told me. Have you thought about acting on it, or how? What holds you back?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"My husband works so much. Should I tell him?\"","r":"\"I think it would help, and many partners want to know. Would you like him at your next appointment, so we can plan together?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline 50 mg each morning after a balanced risk discussion; review at 2 weeks; can increase in 50 mg steps (max 200 mg)","Bloods: TFT, FBC, ferritin; continue folic acid 400 micrograms daily until 12 weeks","Put the medication in the birth plan and tell the paediatric team: watch for neonatal adaptation symptoms after delivery","No valproate; no other changes without perinatal advice"],"psych":["CBT through the perinatal psychology team or priority NHS Talking Therapies, around 12-16 sessions","Parent-infant support if bonding with Isha stays flat","Husband invited to the next review"],"social":["Midwife, health visitor and GP informed today with her consent","Home-Start volunteer referral; PANDAS Foundation helpline","Husband to ask about flexible working; grandmother's help agreed for nights"],"safety":["Crisis plan: NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123, 999 in an emergency","Urgent review if suicidal thoughts increase, she feels unable to care for Isha, or she feels detached from her","Perinatal review in 2 weeks; pre-birth planning meeting at about 32 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Ask about both babies","x":"Perinatal risk covers the child at home and the pregnancy. Ask about harm to Isha, neglect, and whether she is attending antenatal care."},{"h":"Red flags need urgent review","x":"Recent significant change in mental state, new thoughts of violent self-harm, or persistent feelings of estrangement from the baby need urgent perinatal assessment."},{"h":"Screen for bipolar first","x":"Bipolar disorder sharply raises the risk of postpartum psychosis. Ask about elation, reduced need for sleep and family history before starting an antidepressant."},{"h":"Intrusive thoughts are common","x":"Many new mothers have unwanted thoughts of their baby being harmed. They are distressing and not acted on. Ask kindly; distinguish them from intent."},{"h":"Sertraline in pregnancy","x":"Widely used; small risks include neonatal adaptation symptoms and a small rise in persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn. Balance against untreated depression."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"In 10 minutes, gauge perinatal depression severity, grade risk to her, Isha and the pregnancy, and agree a plan."},"c-u-60":{"obj":["Drew out the trigger, a specific guilty thought, his withdrawal, and one link to his early life","Explained CBT as a cycle of situation, thoughts, feelings, body and behaviour, using his own example","Named at least two techniques (thought records, behavioural activation or graded tasks) and how each fits his problems","Gave the practical details: 12-20 weekly sessions, trained therapist, homework, possible early discomfort, and to continue mirtazapine","Checked understanding by teach-back and agreed a named referral route"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"So how is talking actually going to fix this?\"","r":"\"Fair question. It's more than talking: you learn to catch the thoughts that drag you down, test them, and change what you do. It's practical, with tasks between sessions.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I let those people down. That's just a fact.\"","r":"\"I can hear how much you cared about your team. CBT wouldn't tell you it doesn't matter. It would help you check whether 'I betrayed them' is the whole story.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Do I have to go through my childhood?\"","r":"\"Not in depth. CBT focuses on now. Your past helps make sense of why this hit so hard, but the work is on today's thoughts and habits.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Should I stop the mirtazapine if I'm doing therapy?\"","r":"\"No, please carry on. It's helping your sleep, and that will make therapy easier. Any change should be planned with your GP.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"What if it makes me feel worse?\"","r":"\"That's a sensible worry. Some people feel a bit worse early on, because they're looking at painful thoughts. It usually eases, and your therapist will pace it with you.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will my employer find out?\"","r":"\"Not from therapy. It's confidential unless there's a serious safety concern. Anything for work, like occupational health, only happens with your agreement.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue mirtazapine 30 mg at night; GP review now: little mood response at two months, so consider 45 mg (BNF maximum) or a switch, such as sertraline 50 mg","Check weight at review: mirtazapine increases appetite","Continue an effective antidepressant for at least 6 months after recovery"],"psych":["High-intensity individual CBT through NHS Talking Therapies, 12-20 weekly sessions","Behavioural activation early: a weekly activity plan, starting with one contact with friends","Thought records on guilt about the redundancies; later work on perfectionism and over-responsibility"],"social":["GP continues fit notes; occupational health review with consent; phased return plan","Involve his wife if he wishes: she can support homework","NHS and Mind CBT leaflets given today"],"safety":["If mood worsens or thoughts of self-harm appear: GP same day, NHS 111 option 2, or Samaritans 116 123"]},"facts":[{"h":"Use his words in the cycle","x":"Situation: telling the team. Thought: 'I betrayed them.' Feeling: guilt, low. Body: drained. Behaviour: withdrawing. Drawing his own loop makes the model stick."},{"h":"Behaviour change comes first","x":"In depression, CBT usually starts with behavioural activation: scheduling small, valued activities to rebuild energy and mood before deeper thought work."},{"h":"Effective and lasting","x":"CBT works about as well as antidepressants for many people with depression, and gains tend to last longer after treatment ends, lowering relapse risk."},{"h":"Self-referral saves weeks","x":"NHS Talking Therapies accepts self-referral online or by phone. Give him the local link today."},{"h":"Mirtazapine maximum is 45 mg","x":"Usual dose 15-45 mg at night. If mood hasn't lifted after an adequate trial at a good dose, increase or switch, with GP review."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"In 10 minutes, explain CBT through his own thought-feeling-behaviour cycle, give the practical details, and confirm understanding."},"c-u-61":{"obj":["Elicited his main fear and at least two effects of the rows at home, including refused medication, without blaming him","Explained family therapy: who attends, at least 10 sessions over 3 to 12 months, and three things sessions cover","Explained in plain words that both criticism and over-protection add stress that raises relapse risk, and that both come from love","Asked directly whether he will attend and agreed a named next step: referral to EIP family intervention and inviting his wife","Offered a carer's assessment and gave a named crisis route for Ffion"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"So how will sitting in a room together actually help her?\"","r":"\"Fair question. When the whole family understands the illness and agrees how to respond, home gets calmer, and calmer homes see fewer relapses and steadier medication taking.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"This is going to end up being my fault, isn't it?\"","r":"\"I can hear that worry. It isn't about fault. Every family under this pressure falls into patterns. The work is about spotting them together and finding what helps Ffion.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"My wife wraps her in cotton wool. That's the real problem.\"","r":"\"You both want the same thing for her and see the route differently. That's exactly what family sessions are for: getting you on the same page.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"She could try harder. She's just lying in bed all day.\"","r":"\"It looks like that from outside, I know. Low energy and pulling away are part of the illness itself, not laziness. The sessions explain that, so pushing can be paced.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How long does it go on for? I work shifts.\"","r":"\"Usually ten or more sessions, spread across three months to a year, often fortnightly. We can look at evening slots or seeing you at home where the team can.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"Ffion won't come. She won't even come downstairs.\"","r":"\"That's common at the start. We begin with whoever can come, keep inviting her, and she can join for part of a session when she's ready.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"My brother had this. He never worked again. I can't watch that happen to her.\"","r":"\"That must make every bad day feel terrifying. Ffion is getting help much earlier than your brother may have, and families being involved is part of what improves the outlook.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Ffion continues aripiprazole as prescribed by the EIP team (for example 10 mg each morning); care coordinator notes any doses refused after rows","EIP physical health checks: weight, BMI, BP, glucose and lipids at baseline, 12 weeks and then annually; prolactin if symptoms","Missed doses discussed with Ffion directly, not policed by her parents"],"psych":["Family intervention per NICE CG155: a minimum of 10 planned sessions spread across 3 to 12 months, delivered by trained EIP family intervention practitioners, Ffion included when practical","Content: psychoeducation, communication skills, problem solving, early warning signs and a relapse plan","Ffion offered CBT for psychosis individually alongside family sessions"],"social":["Care coordinator links with school for a graded return and education support","Carer's assessment for both parents via the local authority (Care Act 2014)","Carers' groups and information from Rethink Mental Illness; young carers' support if the younger son is affected"],"safety":["If violence at home emerges: same-day safeguarding referral to children's social care","Crisis route: EIP duty line in hours, NHS 111 option 2 out of hours, 999 if immediate danger"]},"facts":[{"h":"At least ten sessions","x":"NICE CG155: young people with psychosis and their families should be offered family intervention, a minimum of 10 planned sessions across 3 to 12 months, with the young person joining where practical."},{"h":"It reduces relapse","x":"Family intervention in psychosis lowers relapse and admission rates and helps people keep taking medication. Say this plainly when a parent asks 'does it work?'"},{"h":"Stress, not blame","x":"Criticism, hostility and over-involvement in the home are linked with higher relapse. Explain them as stress that any loving family can fall into, never as fault."},{"h":"Carers get their own assessment","x":"Under the Care Act 2014 the local authority must offer a carer's assessment to anyone who appears to need support, whatever their income. Refer, don't just mention it."},{"h":"Violence changes everything","x":"A 15-year-old at home: any physical violence during rows means a safeguarding referral to children's social care the same day, recorded in the notes."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"In 10 minutes: hear his fears, explain family therapy with numbers and without blame, and agree whether he attends."},"c-u-62":{"obj":["Took a brief history covering mood, sleep, appetite, lost interests, a trigger, the psychology sessions and thoughts of self-harm","Gave sertraline information in small plain chunks: what it is for, how to take it, weeks to work, two common side effects, not stopping suddenly","Tested all four abilities with open questions, including recall after a pause and what happens if he does not take it","Answered his worry about the tablet changing him and told him directly that he can make this decision","Agreed the start dose, an easy-read leaflet, a 2-week review and who to tell if he feels worse"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will it make me weird? Will I be a different person?\"","r":"\"That's a good thing to ask. No, it won't change who you are. It may help the sad feeling so you feel more like you again.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Do I have to take it forever?\"","r":"\"Not forever. If it helps, you keep taking it for at least six months after you feel better. Then we decide together about stopping slowly.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will it make me sick? I hate being sick.\"","r":"\"I understand. Some people feel a bit sick in the first week or two. It usually goes away. Taking it with breakfast helps. Tell Jade if it doesn't settle.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What if I forget one?\"","r":"\"Good question. If you forget one, just take the next one at the normal time. Don't take two together. Staff can help remind you.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can't I just keep seeing the psychologist instead?\"","r":"\"You can keep seeing her, yes. The tablet is extra, not instead. Some people do best with both. It's your choice.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Nan used to help me when I was sad. Now she's gone.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry, Liam. Missing your nan is really hard. It makes sense you've felt sad since. Let's find things that can help you now.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"(support worker) Shouldn't his mum decide? She usually does.\"","r":"\"It's kind that his mum is involved. In law, Liam decides for himself if he can, and we help him. With his agreement, we can let her know what he's chosen.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline 25 mg each morning with food for 7 days, then 50 mg if tolerated (BNF usual 50 mg; lower start is a reasonable adjustment); maximum 200 mg","Review at 2 weeks: nausea, diarrhoea, headache, sleep change, agitation, worse mood","Check TFTs, FBC and U&E if not done in the last 3 months; review other medicines for interactions","Continue for at least 6 months after recovery; never stop suddenly"],"psych":["Continue psychology: adapted CBT or behavioural activation with pictures and short sessions","Simple faces mood chart completed with staff each evening, brought to review","Grief work for his nan's death, at his pace"],"social":["CLDT named nurse to coordinate; support staff help him back to drama group","Reasonable adjustments: longer appointment, easy-read letters, same clinician where possible","With his consent, tell his mum the plan; GP letter including capacity outcome; STOMP medication review at 3 months"],"safety":["Staff report worse mood, agitation or talk of self-harm to the CLDT the same day; NHS 111 option 2 out of hours","Medicines kept and given by staff under the supported living medication policy"]},"facts":[{"h":"Capacity is presumed","x":"MCA 2005: an intellectual disability alone never shows incapacity. Take all practicable steps to support him first, then assess the specific decision at that time."},{"h":"Four abilities, all needed","x":"Understand the information, retain it long enough to decide, use or weigh it, and communicate a choice by any means. Failing one, because of the impairment, means lacking capacity."},{"h":"Avoid yes/no questions","x":"People with intellectual disability may agree to please. Test understanding with 'tell me in your words' or by pointing to pictures in the easy-read leaflet."},{"h":"Sertraline settles in 2 weeks","x":"Nausea, diarrhoea, headache and sleep change are common early and usually ease. Full mood benefit takes 4 to 6 weeks. Don't stop suddenly."},{"h":"STOMP: review psychotropics regularly","x":"NHS England's STOMP programme asks that every psychotropic in people with learning disability has a clear reason, a review date and an exit plan."}],"type":"Capacity assessment","do":"In 10 minutes: brief history, plain-words sertraline information, test all four abilities, and tell Liam the outcome."},"c-u-63":{"obj":["Checked her current voices and beliefs, olanzapine adherence, self-harm thoughts, and what she already knows and fears about clozapine","Explained why clozapine is offered after two failed antipsychotics and how it starts at home: 12.5 mg, a slow rise, daily HTT checks","Stated the baseline tests and the blood schedule: weekly bloods for 18 weeks, then two-weekly to a year, then monthly, and why","Named at least four common side effects with their fix and three red flags for same-day contact: infection signs, chest pain or breathlessness, no bowel opening for 3 days","Covered the 48-hour missed-dose rule and smoking, confirmed the red flags by teach-back, and gave the HTT and crisis numbers"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I read online that it can kill your white blood cells.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you told me. It can lower white cells in about 3 in 100 people, and seriously in under 1 in 100. That's exactly what the weekly bloods catch early.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why can't I just go up on the olanzapine?\"","r":"\"Fair question. You're already on the top licensed dose, and two different tablets haven't quietened the voices. Clozapine is the one that works best when others haven't.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I just want the voices to stop. I'm so tired of them.\"","r":"\"That sounds exhausting, and I'm sorry you've lived with them so long. Clozapine gives you the best chance of them easing. Let's make it as safe and easy as we can.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"Weekly blood tests? I hate needles.\"","r":"\"That's a real worry. Many clinics use a finger-prick machine, which is quicker. And after the first year it's only monthly.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I've already put on loads of weight with olanzapine.\"","r":"\"I hear you, and weight matters. Clozapine can add weight too, so we'll weigh you regularly, offer a healthy lifestyle group, and can add metformin if needed.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will I have to stop smoking?\"","r":"\"No, but please don't change it without telling us. Smoking speeds up how fast your body clears clozapine. Stopping suddenly can push levels up, so we'd check a level.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Could I still have a baby one day?\"","r":"\"Yes, that's a really important question. Plan it with us first. Don't stop clozapine suddenly if you get pregnant; ring us and we'll involve the perinatal team.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Baseline: FBC with neutrophils, U&E, LFT, lipids, HbA1c, troponin, CRP, ECG, weight, BMI, waist and BP; register with the monitoring service","Day 1: clozapine 12.5 mg once or twice, then rise by 25 to 50 mg a day as tolerated towards 300 mg/day over 2 to 3 weeks; olanzapine reduced gradually as clozapine rises","HTT: pulse, BP and temperature daily for 2 weeks; troponin and CRP weekly for 4 weeks (myocarditis)","FBC every week for 18 weeks, two-weekly to 1 year, then monthly; plasma level about 6 to 8 weeks, aim at least 350 micrograms/L","Senna 7.5 to 15 mg at night from day 1, plus docusate if needed; hyoscine hydrobromide 300 micrograms at night for drooling"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis from the psychology team, at least 16 sessions, once clozapine is settled","HTT nurse psychoeducation during titration: what to expect, side effects, red flags","Relapse prevention plan with her care coordinator"],"social":["HTT daily visits during titration, then handover to the CMHT care coordinator","Clozapine clinic and pharmacist for results, dispensing and interaction checks","Stop smoking service only with the team's knowledge, with a level check; healthy lifestyle group","Carer's assessment for her mother, who can help spot side effects"],"safety":["Same-day bloods for sore throat, fever or flu-like illness","999 or A&E for chest pain, breathlessness, a fit, or severe abdominal pain with vomiting","More than 48 hours of missed doses: do not restart alone; team restarts at 12.5 mg"]},"facts":[{"h":"Clozapine after two failed antipsychotics","x":"NICE: offer clozapine when schizophrenia has not responded to adequate trials of at least two antipsychotics, one a non-clozapine second-generation drug. Around half improve."},{"h":"Blood counts: 18 weeks weekly","x":"UK monitoring: an FBC every week for 18 weeks, two-weekly to a year, then monthly. Green continue, amber repeat more often, red stop. No green, no dispensing."},{"h":"Constipation can kill","x":"MHRA: clozapine slows the gut and can cause obstruction and death. Ask about bowels every contact; start a stimulant laxative early; no opening for 3 days needs review."},{"h":"Myocarditis comes early","x":"Highest risk in the first 4 to 8 weeks. Fever, chest pain, breathlessness or pulse over 120 need same-day troponin and CRP, and stopping if raised."},{"h":"Stopping smoking raises levels","x":"Tobacco smoke (not nicotine) induces CYP1A2. Stopping can raise clozapine levels by about half within a week. Check a level and reduce the dose."},{"h":"48 hours missed: restart low","x":"If more than 48 hours of doses are missed, restart at 12.5 mg and titrate again, because tolerance to cardiovascular and sedative effects is lost."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"In 10 minutes: explain why clozapine, the home start, blood monitoring and red-flag side effects, then confirm by teach-back."},"c-u-65":{"obj":["Gained consent, offered a chaperone, exposed both legs and checked for joint pain before moving them","Tested tone and power at hip, knee, ankle and big toe on both sides, grading each on the MRC 0 to 5 scale","Tested knee and ankle reflexes with reinforcement and both plantar responses","Tested light touch, pin prick, vibration and joint position from the toes upward, plus heel-shin, gait, heel-toe walking and Romberg's test","Stated whether there is a deficit and its pattern, and named next steps: bloods, thiamine, falls plan and medical referral"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is it something in my spine? Am I going to end up in a wheelchair?\"","r":"\"I can hear how frightening that is. I've found no signs of a spine problem. It looks like the nerves to your feet, which usually improve or stop getting worse with treatment.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"It's the drink, isn't it? I've done this to myself.\"","r":"\"Alcohol can affect these nerves, yes, along with low vitamins. Blaming yourself won't help your legs. Vitamins, cutting down and physio can. Would you like help with the drinking?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't even walk properly now. I feel so useless.\"","r":"\"That sounds really hard on top of everything else. You're not useless: this has a physical cause, and there's a lot we can do to make you steadier.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want to walk in front of everyone.\"","r":"\"That's fair. We'll do it here with the door closed, and I'll be right beside you. It tells me a lot about why you've been tripping.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why do you keep asking me to close my eyes?\"","r":"\"Good question. With your eyes shut I can test the feeling in your feet on its own, without your eyes helping. That's how we find where the nerves are struggling.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is this why I keep tripping at night?\"","r":"\"Very likely. In the dark you rely on feeling in your feet to balance, and that's reduced. A night light and good shoes will help straight away.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods: FBC (MCV), B12, folate, HbA1c, TFT, U&E, LFT, GGT, CK, serum protein electrophoresis","Thiamine 100 mg three times a day orally now; Pabrinex IV if any confusion, ataxia or eye signs (Wernicke's)","Refer to the general medical team for review and nerve conduction studies; urgent spinal MRI only if upper motor neurone signs or a sensory level appear"],"psych":["Brief alcohol intervention and referral to the alcohol liaison nurse","Link the physical cause to her low mood and self-blame in the next review"],"social":["Physiotherapy for balance and strength; OT for footwear and walking aids","Update the ward falls care plan: night light, call bell in reach, low bed if needed"],"safety":["Tell the nurse in charge today: high falls risk, especially at night","Urgent medical review if new back pain, bladder or bowel problems, numbness between the legs, or rapidly worsening weakness"]},"facts":[{"h":"Stocking loss means neuropathy","x":"Symmetrical sensory loss from the toes up, absent ankle jerks and distal weakness is a length-dependent peripheral neuropathy. Commonest causes: diabetes and alcohol."},{"h":"Cord signs need urgent imaging","x":"Brisk reflexes, raised tone, clonus, extensor plantars or a sensory level suggest spinal cord compression: urgent medical review and MRI the same day."},{"h":"Absent ankles, upgoing toes: think B12","x":"Absent ankle jerks with extensor plantars and loss of vibration and joint position suggests subacute combined degeneration. Check B12 before giving folic acid."},{"h":"Thiamine for every heavy drinker","x":"NICE CG100: offer oral thiamine to people drinking harmfully or dependently if poorly nourished or at risk of it. Suspected Wernicke's needs parenteral thiamine immediately."},{"h":"Romberg tests the feet, not cerebellum","x":"Steady with eyes open but sways or falls with eyes closed means poor joint position input from the feet. Stand close with arms ready."}],"type":"Physical examination","do":"In 10 minutes: examine tone, power, reflexes, coordination and sensation in both legs, then state any deficit and its pattern."}});
